window.FLOW_DOCS_BUNDLE = {"assert.md":"# Pipeline Assertions in Flow 🚨\n\nFlow pipelines support native `\u003cassert\u003e` tags to perform data quality checks, environment checks, and business rule validations. With assertions, you can gracefully halt execution on critical issues or run fallback pipelines to recover or log warnings.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Configuration Syntax\n\nThe `\u003cassert\u003e` tag supports the following attributes:\n\n*   **`var`**: The name of the variable inside the pipeline registry to evaluate. (Required)\n*   **`equals`** / **`value`**: The expected value to compare against (case-insensitive string comparison).\n*   **`message`**: A custom error/warning message to log when the assertion fails.\n*   **`on_failure`**: Behavior on assertion failure. Options:\n    *   `halt` (default): Immediately stops pipeline execution and returns a non-zero exit code.\n    *   `warn` / `continue`: Logs a warning to the execution results but allows the pipeline to continue.\n*   **`fail_var`**: (Optional) Pipeline variable name to set when the assertion fails.\n*   **`fail_val`**: (Optional) The value to set in `fail_var` (defaults to `\"true\"` if omitted).\n*   **`\u003con_failure\u003e`**: (Optional) A nested child element containing sequence nodes (e.g., `\u003cscript\u003e`, `\u003csql\u003e`, `\u003chttp_client\u003e`) to execute only if the assertion fails.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Practical Examples\n\n### Example 1: Basic Environment Check (Fail-Fast)\nEnsure the active pipeline environment variable matches the production expectation. If not, halt the pipeline immediately.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ENV\" type=\"string\" value=\"staging\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- This will halt execution since ENV is staging instead of production --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"check_production\" \n                var=\"ENV\" \n                equals=\"production\" \n                message=\"Pipeline must be run in production environment!\" \n                on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n                \n        \u003c!-- Subsequent nodes will not run --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"prod_deploy\" language=\"powershell\"\u003e\n            Write-Host \"Deploying to production...\"\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: API Status Verification with Warnings\nRun a warning-only assertion that checks an HTTP client\u0027s status code, logging a warning and setting a failure flag, but allowing execution to continue.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003chttp_client id=\"fetch_health\" \n                     url=\"https://api.example.com/health\" \n                     status_code_var=\"STATUS_CODE\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Log a warning and set PIPELINE_DIRTY to true if status is not 200 --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"assert_api_health\" \n                var=\"STATUS_CODE\" \n                equals=\"200\" \n                message=\"Health check returned non-200 code!\" \n                on_failure=\"warn\"\n                fail_var=\"PIPELINE_DIRTY\" \n                fail_val=\"true\" /\u003e\n                \n        \u003cscript id=\"log_results\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                isDirty := vars.GetString(\"PIPELINE_DIRTY\")\n                if isDirty == \"true\" {\n                    fmt.Println(\"Warning: Pipeline marked as dirty.\")\n                } else {\n                    fmt.Println(\"Health checks passed successfully!\")\n                }\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Fallback and Cleanup on Failure (`\u003con_failure\u003e`)\nUse the nested `\u003con_failure\u003e` block to run fallback scripts or cleanup actions when an assertion fails.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"temp_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./temp.db\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"RECORD_COUNT\" type=\"int\" value=\"0\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Check if we ingested any records. If not, trigger the \u003con_failure\u003e block --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"verify_ingested_records\" \n                var=\"RECORD_COUNT\" \n                equals=\"1000\" \n                on_failure=\"halt\" \n                message=\"Ingested record count does not match expected threshold!\"\u003e\n            \u003con_failure\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Run a SQL cleanup script before halting --\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"rollback_staging\" db=\"temp_db\"\u003e\n                    DELETE FROM staging_records;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n                \u003cscript id=\"notify_slack\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                    package main\n                    import \"fmt\"\n                    func main() {\n                        fmt.Println(\"Sending Slack alert: Ingestion failed, staging table rolled back.\")\n                    }\n                \u003c/script\u003e\n            \u003c/on_failure\u003e\n        \u003c/assert\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","BUILDER_SLIDESHOW.md":"\u003c!-- slide --\u003e\n\u003c!-- Slide 1: Title \u0026 Mascot Introduction --\u003e\n# Flow Builder: Visual Pipeline Designer \u0026 Runtime Orchestration\n### *Modern, Zero-Dependency ETL \u0026 Workflow Engine in Go*\n\n![Flow Pirate Mascot](../flow-mascot.jpg)\n\n\u003e **\"Slashing ETL Madness with Go, HTMX, and Declarative Pipelines\"**\n\n* **Single-Binary Engine:** No Python runtimes, Celery workers, or JVM footprint.\n* **Embedded HTMX Visual Builder:** Interactive web studio served directly from the Go binary (`flow -builder`).\n* **Live Bidirectional XML Sync:** Visual drag-and-drop synchronized in real time with Git-friendly declarative XML.\n* **Built-in Quality Gates \u0026 Telemetry:** Full preflight validation, row-level telemetry, and compliance logging out of the box.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 2: What is Flow \u0026 The Builder UI? --\u003e\n# What is Flow Builder Doing?\n### *An Embedded Visual Studio for Declarative Data Pipelines*\n\nFlow Builder bridges the gap between raw XML configuration and visual workflow orchestration. It runs an embedded local web server backed by an embedded SQLite database (`flow_builder.db`), allowing data engineers to visually compose, validate, test, and execute pipelines without writing boilerplate code.\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                               Flow Web Builder UI                                 |\n|                                                                                   |\n|  +--------------------+  +-----------------------------+  +--------------------+  |\n|  | Component Palette  |  |     Interactive Canvas      |  |  Live XML Preview  |  |\n|  | - Variables        |  | 1. Variables (\u003cvariables\u003e)  |  |                    |  |\n|  | - Databases        |  | 2. Databases (\u003cdatabases\u003e)  |  | \u003cpipeline\u003e         |  |\n|  | - Preflight Checks |  | 3. Preflight (\u003cpreflight\u003e)  |  |   \u003cvariables\u003e...   |  |\n|  | - Control Flow     |  | 4. Main Flow (\u003cflow\u003e)       |  |   \u003cdatabases\u003e...   |  |\n|  | - Database \u0026 SQL   |  |                             |  |   \u003cpreflight\u003e...   |  |\n|  | - Key-Value Store  |  | [Drag / Reorder / Edit / Del]  |   \u003cflow\u003e...        |  |\n|  +--------------------+  +-----------------------------+  +--------------------+  |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                          |\n                         +---------------------------------+\n                         |  Embedded Execution Engine      |\n                         |  - Preflight Quality Gates      |\n                         |  - Real-Time Stream Telemetry   |\n                         +---------------------------------+\n```\n\n#### Core Capabilities at a Glance:\n1. **Interactive Component Toolbox:** Instant drag-and-drop or click-to-add for variables, database connections, loops, scripts, and SQL tasks.\n2. **Visual Hierarchy Canvas:** Organizes pipelines into 4 distinct phases: Setup, Connections, Preflight Quality Gates, and Execution Flow.\n3. **Real-time Live XML Preview:** Instant validation, bidirectional feedback, copy, and export.\n4. **Execution Dashboard:** Run entire pipelines or preflight checks in isolation with live stream logs and row-count metrics.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 3: Unified 3-Panel Designer (Slate Theme) --\u003e\n# The Builder UI: Unified 3-Panel Studio\n### *Default Slate Theme with Live Synchronization*\n\n![Flow Builder Default Slate Theme](screenshots/Builder_Default_Theme.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Left Panel (Component Palette):** Provides organized categories of pipeline primitives:\n  * **Pipeline Setup:** `\u003cvariable\u003e`, `\u003cdatabase\u003e`, `\u003cpreflight\u003e`\n  * **Control Flow:** `\u003cscript\u003e`, `\u003cgroup\u003e`, `\u003cif\u003e`, `\u003cforeach\u003e`, `\u003cwhile\u003e`, `\u003cparallel\u003e`\n  * **Database \u0026 SQL:** `\u003csql\u003e`, `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`\n  * **Key-Value \u0026 Cache:** `\u003ckv\u003e`, `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`\n  * **Templates \u0026 Storage:** `\u003ctemplate\u003e`, `\u003cexcel_write\u003e`\n* **Center Panel (Interactive Canvas):** Displays the active pipeline split into clear execution stages:\n  1. `\u003cvariables\u003e`: Configuration variables like `TargetTable` and connection strings (`log_db_cs`).\n  2. `\u003cdatabases\u003e`: Configured connection pools (`local_sqlite`, `log_db`).\n  3. `\u003cpreflight\u003e`: Pre-execution diagnostic checks (`pre_select_from_db_1`, `PRE_TABLE_RUN_CHECK`).\n  4. `\u003cflow\u003e`: Main execution tasks (`GET_LOGS`).\n* **Right Panel (Live XML Preview):** Renders the exact schema-compliant declarative XML in real time with copy and export controls.\n* **Top Bar Controls:** Fast pipeline switching, tab navigation (`scripts.xml`, `Config Overrides`, `CLI Options`, `Pipeline Runner`), and database management (`Purge DB`).\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 4: Multi-Theme Support (VS Light) --\u003e\n# Theme Customization: Visual Studio Light\n### *High-Contrast Readability for Daytime Workflows*\n\n![Flow Builder VS Light Theme](screenshots/Builder_VS_Light_Theme.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Instant Dynamic Theme Switching:** Powered by HTMX and CSS variables without page reloads.\n* **Clean Daytime Ergonomics:** Crisp borders, high-contrast typography, and accessible syntax coloring in the Live XML pane.\n* **Identical Functional Parity:** Full access to component insertion, drag reordering, and runner telemetry across all visual themes.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 5: Multi-Theme Support (VS Dark) --\u003e\n# Theme Customization: Visual Studio Dark\n### *Low-Glare Visual Studio Dark Aesthetic*\n\n![Flow Builder VS Dark Theme](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Theme.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Developer-Centric Dark Mode:** Modern dark charcoal surfaces with vibrant neon status indicators (blue, cyan, emerald, amber).\n* **Code Editor Aesthetics:** Matches standard VS Code dark styling for data engineers spending extended sessions designing complex ETL pipelines.\n* **Syntax-Highlighted Live Code:** Right pane provides clear, syntax-highlighted XML markup reflecting every card modification instantly.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 6: Component Search \u0026 Active Pipeline Editing --\u003e\n# Component Search \u0026 Pipeline Editing\n### *Real-Time Filtering \u0026 Active Pipeline Context*\n\n![Flow Builder VS Dark Loaded Saved with Search](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Loaded_Saved_if_testing_with_search.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Instant Palette Search:** Typing `\"sql\"` into the component search bar dynamically filters available components down in real time.\n* **Pipeline Selector:** Active pipeline `if_testing` loaded from SQLite storage (`flow_builder.db`).\n* **Visualizing Complex Control Flow:**\n  * Shows a conditional branch card (`\u003cif\u003e PRE_IF_TABLE_COUNT_GT_0`).\n  * Condition expression: `var: PRE_TABLE_AVAIL == \u00271\u0027`.\n  * Distinct colored sub-branches:\n    * **Green Branch:** `\u003cthen\u003e` containing `PRE_SUCCESS_STEP`\n    * **Red Branch:** `\u003celse\u003e` containing `PRE_FAIL_STEP` with template fallback `{{.PRE_TABLE_AVAIL}} Failed`.\n* **Reordering \u0026 Editing:** Every card features drag handles, up/down arrows (`▲` `▼`), in-line `Edit` dialog, and `✕` removal.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 7: Nested Step Insertion Modal --\u003e\n# Nested Step Insertion: Dynamic Containers\n### *Inserting Primitives Inside Conditional \u0026 Loop Blocks*\n\n![Add Step to IF THEN Block](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Add_Step_To_IF_THEN.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Scoped Component Insertion:** The `Add Step to \u003cthen\u003e` modal allows targeted insertion of any task directly inside nested branch containers.\n* **Searchable Step Catalog:** Instant filter bar (`Filter components (e.g. assert, sql, http, template)...`).\n* **Supported Nested Primitives:**\n  * `\u003cscript\u003e`: Execute embedded Go, Shell, PowerShell, Bash, Cmd, or C# (.NET CSX) code.\n  * `\u003cgroup\u003e`: Nest tasks with independent retry policies, transaction rollback boundaries, and error policies.\n  * `\u003cif\u003e`: Multi-level nested conditional logic.\n  * `\u003cforeach\u003e` / `\u003cwhile\u003e`: Iterative streaming data loops over SQL result sets.\n  * `\u003cparallel\u003e`: Concurrent multi-goroutine execution with synchronized joins.\n  * `\u003csql\u003e` / `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`: High-throughput query execution and direct table-to-table stream copying.\n  * `\u003ckv\u003e` / `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`: Key-value operations on embedded BBolt/Badger or external Redis/etcd.\n  * `\u003ctemplate\u003e` / `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e`: Contextual template rendering with automatic escaping.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 8: Database Connection Pooling \u0026 Secrets --\u003e\n# Database Connection Management\n### *Native Multi-Engine Dialect Pooling \u0026 Secret Interpolation*\n\n![Edit Database Connection](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Edit_Database_Connection.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Named Connection Definition:** Configures connection pool `log_db` assigned to the `\u003cdatabases\u003e` section.\n* **Cross-Dialect Drivers:** Built-in support for `sqlserver`, `postgres`, `mysql`, `sqlite`, `oracle`, and `kv`.\n* **Dynamic Secret Interpolation:**\n  * Supports variable expansion syntax `{{log_db_cs}}`.\n  * Resolves secrets up to 3 levels deep across base XML, environment overrides (`-config`), and CLI arguments (`-vars`).\n  * Protects sensitive production credentials from hardcoded Git commits.\n* **Workload Tuning Profiles:** Optimizes connection pooling for `oltp`, `bulk`, `analytics`, or `batch`.\n* **Connection Lifecycle Limits:**\n  * Max Open Connections (`25`)\n  * Max Idle Connections (`10`)\n  * Max Lifetime Duration (`300` seconds)\n* **Custom Dynamic Attributes:** Expandable key-value pairs (e.g. `description: Logging Database`).\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 9: Pipeline Catalog \u0026 Script Management --\u003e\n# Pipeline Catalog \u0026 Storage Management\n### *Embedded SQLite Storage for Drafts and Production Scripts*\n\n![Manage Loaded Pipelines](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Manage_Import_New_Scripts.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Centralized Pipeline Catalog:** The `Manage Loaded Pipelines` modal lists all pipelines stored in the builder\u0027s local SQLite database (`flow_builder.db`).\n* **Multi-Pipeline Switching:**\n  * `default_pipeline`: Default pipeline draft.\n  * `github_billing`: Pipeline fetching GitHub billing usage and loading into SQL Server.\n  * `if_testing`: Active pipeline imported from `if_testing.xml`.\n* **Pipeline Lifecycle Operations:**\n  * **`Switch To`:** Instant one-click activation of another pipeline in the visual designer.\n  * **`+ New`:** Spin up clean empty pipeline configurations.\n  * **`Import`:** Ingest existing XML pipeline files from the local filesystem.\n  * **`Delete` / `Purge DB`:** Remove obsolete drafts or reset builder state.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 10: CLI Options File Selector --\u003e\n# CLI Options File Navigator\n### *Binding Pre-Baked Profiles to Interactive Runs*\n\n![Select CLI Options File](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Select_Options_File_Runner.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Interactive Directory Navigation:** Built-in modal filesystem browser traversing folders (such as `.private/`).\n* **Pre-Baked Execution Profiles:**\n  * Selects `-options` XML files containing pre-configured flags, runtime variables, and connection overrides.\n  * Examples: `options.xml`, `options_gcloud_billing.xml`, `options_github_ai_credit_usage.xml`.\n* **Zero CLI Friction:** Eliminates the need to manually memorize or copy lengthy CLI command flags when testing pipelines in the browser.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 11: Live Execution \u0026 Telemetry Dashboard --\u003e\n# Live Execution \u0026 Real-Time Telemetry\n### *Interactive Pipeline Runner with Step-by-Step Audit Metrics*\n\n![Execute Pipeline with Live Stream](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Execute_Options_File_Runner.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Dual Execution Modes:** Select between executing a `Filesystem Script` or the in-memory `Builder Draft`.\n* **Configuration Binding:** Combines pipeline script (`scripts.xml`), config overrides (`CONFIG.xml`), and CLI options (`options_github_ai_credit_usage.xml`).\n* **Execution Summary Banner:** Immediate execution outcome and benchmark timing: `Execution Finished Successfully (536.275ms)`.\n* **Per-Step Execution Grid:**\n  | Step / Node ID | Kind | Status | Return Code | Duration | Output Summary |\n  | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n  | `CREATE_GITHUB_COPILOT_BILLING_TABLE` | SQL | `SUCCEEDED` | `0` | 43ms | Completed successfully |\n  | `GET_GITHUB_BILLING` | SCRIPT | `SUCCEEDED` | `0` | 374ms | Completed successfully |\n  | `LOAD_GITHUB_COPILOT_BILLING_TO_SQL` | SQL | `SUCCEEDED` | `0` | 33ms | **Affected: 9** |\n* **Console Stream Logs:** Emits real-time RFC3339 audit telemetry rows with sequence numbers, run IDs, and accurate row tracking (`rows_read`, `rows_written`, `rows_affected`).\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 12: Standalone Preflight Validation Runner --\u003e\n# Preflight Validation: Quality Gates\n### *Non-Destructive Fail-Fast Environment Testing*\n\n![Preflight Runner](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_PreFlight_Runner.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Dedicated Preflight Execution (`-preflight`):**\n  \u003e *\"Execute preflight validation checks only without executing the main flow\"*\n* **Non-Destructive Guarantee:** `\u003cflow\u003e` data loading nodes are completely bypassed. Only diagnostic checks, database ping tests, and assertions run.\n* **Rapid Diagnostic Results (`172.2539ms`):**\n  * `pre_select_from_db_1` (SQL, Succeeded, 26ms, Read: 1)\n  * `PRE_TABLE_RUN_CHECK` (SQL, Succeeded, 3ms, Read: 1)\n  * `PRE_RETURN_CHECK_STEP` (TEMPLATE, Succeeded, 5ms)\n  * `f9e974065d0397f58ab45429e3f5db78` (IF conditional branch, Succeeded, 28ms, Read: 1)\n* **Fail-Fast Safety:** Catches missing environment variables, unreachable databases, or schema mismatches before executing heavy data transfers.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 13: Options-Driven Preflight \u0026 Assertions --\u003e\n# Options-Driven Preflight \u0026 Assertions\n### *Automated Structural Assertions via CLI Profiles*\n\n![Preflight From Options File](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_PreFlight_From_Options_File_Runner.png)\n\n### What It\u0027s Doing:\n* **Configuration Profile Validation:** Executes preflight checks specified inside `options_github_ai_credit_usage.xml`.\n* **Automated `\u003cassert\u003e` Quality Gates:**\n  * `preflight_check` (SQL check, 30ms, Read: 1)\n  * `preflight_check_2` (SQL check, 12ms, Read: 1)\n  * `database_check` (**ASSERT**, 11ms, Completed successfully)\n  * `user_check` (**ASSERT**, 12ms, Completed successfully)\n* **Production Readiness Sign-off:** Verifies database availability and permission rights in 211ms before scheduled cron jobs or worker nodes take over.\n\n---\n\n\u003c!-- Slide 14: Summary \u0026 Quick Reference --\u003e\n# Summary \u0026 Command Reference\n### *Everything You Need to Run Flow and the Visual Builder*\n\n![Flow Pirate Mascot](../flow-mascot.jpg)\n\n### Key Capabilities Summary:\n* **Visual HTMX Studio:** Launch with `flow -builder` on an ephemeral or designated port.\n* **Declarative XML Schema:** Full pipeline reproducibility, version control, and CI/CD friendly.\n* **Multi-Engine Dialects:** Native translation for SQL Server, Postgres, MySQL, Oracle, and SQLite.\n* **Two-Stage Execution:** Standalone `\u003cpreflight\u003e` quality gates prevent failed batch runs.\n* **Full Compliance Telemetry:** Automatic logging to `pipeline_runs` and `pipeline_events` tables.\n\n### Quick Start Commands:\n```bash\n# 1. Launch the Visual Builder web interface\nflow -builder -builder-port 8080\n\n# 2. Run Preflight quality validation only (non-destructive)\nflow -file pipeline.xml -preflight\n\n# 3. Execute full pipeline with pre-baked options profile\nflow -file pipeline.xml -options options.xml\n\n# 4. Run pipeline with runtime variable overrides\nflow -file pipeline.xml -vars \"TargetTable=prod_orders,BatchSize=10000\"\n```\n","BUSINESS_SUMMARY.md":"## Executive Overview\r\n\r\n**Flow** is an enterprise data integration and pipeline orchestration engine designed to automate, validate, and monitor complex ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) workflows. Built as a lightweight, low-overhead Go application, Flow enables organizations to define data processing jobs in structured XML or via an embedded web builder, execute parallel and conditional logic, automatically log operational telemetry to centralized databases, and auto-generate workflow documentation.\r\n\r\nBy combining traditional relational database support with high-performance Key-Value (K/V) stores and a visual web interface, Flow reduces operational overhead, accelerates job design, and enforces standardized auditing across heterogeneous enterprise data environments.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Core Business Features\r\n\r\n### 1. Interactive Web Builder \u0026 Low-Code Orchestration\r\n\r\n* **Embedded HTMX Web Builder:** Features a standalone visual management web interface (`-builder`) running on a dynamically assigned ephemeral port (or user-configured via `-builder-port`) backed by an embedded metadata storage engine (`flow_builder.db`). Data engineers and analysts can visually design, inspect, and configure data pipelines without hand-crafting XML.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Declarative XML Workflows:** Pipelines, scripts, connection strings, and variables are stored in structured XML definitions, enabling seamless version control and automated deployment pipelines.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Advanced Flow Control:** Supports conditional evaluation (`if`), iterative looping (`foreach`, `while`), and concurrent execution branches (`parallel`) to maximize throughput for large-scale data processing.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Preflight \u0026 AST Validation:** Performs pre-execution semantic schema (XSD) and AST validation passes to catch configuration errors before running production workloads.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n### 2. Multi-Engine Storage: Relational \u0026 Key-Value (K/V) Databases\r\n\r\n* **Hybrid Storage Support:** Integrates seamlessly across standard relational platforms (PostgreSQL, Microsoft SQL Server, MySQL, SQLite, and Oracle) as well as Key-Value (K/V) database stores.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **High-Speed Lookups \u0026 State Caching:** Utilizes Key-Value data stores for rapid key-based state persistence, intermediate row caching, reference lookups, and fast deduplication during active pipeline execution.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Dialect \u0026 Syntax Abstraction:** Dynamically handles connection pooling, dialect detection, and database-specific query and parameter placeholders across diverse database platforms.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n### 3. Enterprise Auditability \u0026 Operational Telemetry\r\n\r\n* **Centralized Audit Logging:** Automatically logs pipeline runs and individual step events to standardized audit tables (`pipeline_runs` and `pipeline_events`).\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Granular Telemetry:** Tracks real-time status, run durations, timestamps, error classifications, and exact row counts read, written, and affected.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Multi-Sink Fanout:** Supports concurrent event streaming to console streams and database sinks simultaneously for real-time observability.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n### 4. Dynamic Configuration \u0026 Environment Portability\r\n\r\n* **Multi-Tiered Parameter Management:** Merges base XML configurations with environment-specific override files (`-config`) and command-line overrides (`-vars`).\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Environment Promotion:** Promotes data pipelines across Development, Staging, and Production environments without modifying core source logic.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **XML CLI Load Profiles:** Pre-loads flag options from reusable configuration files (`-options`) to simplify scheduled job runs.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n### 5. Automated Documentation \u0026 Visual Management\r\n\r\n* **Auto-Generated Flowcharts:** Dynamically renders XML pipeline structures into visual Mermaid.js diagrams (`flowchart TD`) for documentation and monitoring.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **XSLT Stylesheet Processing:** Includes an integrated XSLT 3.0 transformation engine to convert XML configurations into custom enterprise reports or formatted documentation packages.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n### 6. Flexible Output Formatting\r\n\r\n* **Multi-Format Output Engine:** Generates output in raw JSON, formatted JSON, Markdown tables, CSV, plain text, or live-streamed console events.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Feature Matrix\r\n\r\n| Functional Area | Business Capability | Operational Benefit |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| **Pipeline Builder** | Embedded HTMX web UI (`-builder`) backed by `flow_builder.db`\u003cbr\u003e | Speeds up pipeline construction and lowers technical barriers for non-developer teams.|\r\n| **Multi-Engine Storage** | Native support for Relational DBs (Postgres, SQL Server, Oracle, etc.) and Key-Value stores| Combines structured SQL processing with high-speed key-value state lookups.|\r\n| **Pipeline Execution** | Parallel processing, conditional branching, loops (`parallel`, `if`, `foreach`)| Minimizes batch execution windows and streamlines complex dependency handling.\r\n| **Audit \u0026 Logging** | Real-time event tracking (`pipeline_events`) with row-level metrics| Ensures enterprise compliance readiness and provides instant root-cause failure diagnostics.|\r\n| **Configuration** | Variable inheritance and runtime parameter overrides (`-vars`)| Simplifies CI/CD integration and multi-environment deployment.|\r\n| **Governance** | Auto-generated Mermaid.js visual diagrams \u0026 XSLT transformation engine| Keeps pipeline architecture self-documenting and auditable.|\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Strategic Value \u0026 ROI Impact\r\n\r\nFlow delivers a versatile, enterprise-ready data execution framework that eliminates the complexity of managing custom, brittle ETL scripts. By uniting an intuitive visual Web Builder with dual-mode storage support (relational and key-value) and automated compliance tracking, Flow enables organizations to scale their data operations efficiently while maintaining strict audit controls.","database.md":"# Database Operations in Flow Pipelines 🗄️\r\n\r\nFlow features native, high-performance integration with both relational SQL databases and modern Key-Value (KV) stores—enabling direct query execution, transactional grouping, rapid bulk data replication, key-value lookups/streaming, and styled multi-tab spreadsheet generation.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Supported Databases \u0026 Storage Engines\r\n\r\n| Engine / Store | Driver Identifier(s) | Type | Connection String Example | Primary Workload \u0026 Capabilities |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **PostgreSQL** | `postgres`, `postgresql`, `pq` | Relational SQL | `postgresql://user:pass@localhost:5432/dbname?sslmode=disable` | OLTP, high-throughput bulk ETL, JSON analytics |\r\n| **SQLite** | `sqlite`, `sqlite3` | Embedded SQL | `file::memory:?cache=shared` or `./app.db` | Local development, testing, caching, zero-config persistence |\r\n| **MySQL** | `mysql` | Relational SQL | `user:pass@tcp(localhost:3306)/dbname?parseTime=true` | Web OLTP, read replicas, batch ingestion |\r\n| **SQL Server** | `sqlserver`, `mssql` | Relational SQL | `sqlserver://sa:pass@localhost:1433?database=dbname` | Enterprise OLTP, analytics, fast bulk copies (`tablock`) |\r\n| **Oracle** | `oracle`, `godror` | Relational SQL | `oracle://user:pass@localhost:1521/service_name` | Mission-critical enterprise OLTP, data warehouses |\r\n| **bbolt** | `bbolt`, `bolt` | Embedded KV | `./data/cache.db` | ACID transactions, zero-dependency embedded KV caching |\r\n| **BadgerDB** | `badger`, `badgerdb` | Embedded KV | `./data/badger_dir` | LSM-tree architecture, high-write-throughput embedded KV |\r\n| **Redis / Valkey** | `redis`, `valkey` | Server KV | `redis://:password@localhost:6379/0` or `127.0.0.1:6379` | In-memory caching, pub/sub, pipelined bulk replication |\r\n| **etcd** | `etcd`, `etcdv3` | Distributed KV | `http://127.0.0.1:2379,http://127.0.0.1:22379` | Distributed configuration, metadata consensus, prefix scans |\r\n\r\n\u003e [!TIP]\r\n\u003e For an exhaustive reference of all driver options, SSL/TLS certificates, query pragmas, network packet sizing, and multi-scenario connection string examples across all supported engines, see the dedicated [Database Connection Strings \u0026 Driver Reference Guide](database_connection_strings.md).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Database Connection Tuning `\u003cdatabase\u003e`\r\nDefined under `\u003cdatabases\u003e` as child `\u003cdatabase\u003e` elements.\r\nConnection parameters live on the `\u003cdatabase\u003e` definition itself, not on each SQL or KV step.\r\n\r\n- **`name`**: unique logical database identifier used by SQL and KV nodes.\r\n- **`driver`**: database driver (e.g., `sqlite`, `postgres`, `mysql`, `sqlserver`, `oracle`, `bbolt`, `badger`, `redis`, `etcd`).\r\n- **`connection_string`**: driver-specific DSN, file path, or endpoint list. Supports variable interpolation (e.g. `{{DB_URL}}`).\r\n- **`max_open_conns`**: max number of simultaneous open connections in the pool (SQL engines).\r\n- **`max_idle_conns`**: number of idle connections retained ready for reuse (SQL engines).\r\n- **`conn_max_lifetime_seconds`**: maximum age of a pooled connection before it is recycled (SQL engines).\r\n- **`workload`**: optional tuning profile label such as `oltp`, `bulk`, or `analytics` (SQL engines).\r\n\r\nA workload is the operational pattern of a database connection. It describes how a connection is expected to be used at runtime so the pool can be tuned for the right trade-off between concurrency, latency, and throughput. In practice, Flow uses the workload label as a hint to choose sane defaults for connection count, idle retention, and lifetime without hardcoding one-size-fits-all values.\r\n\r\nCommon workload types:\r\n- **`oltp`**: short, latency-sensitive reads and writes; keep a moderate number of open connections and shorter idle time to reduce cold-start latency.\r\n- **`bulk`**: large ETL or ingestion jobs; favor larger pools and more throughput, with a shorter connection lifetime to handle high churn.\r\n- **`analytics`**: read-heavy reporting workloads; balance concurrency and reuse to support many query workers without oversubscribing the server.\r\n- **`batch`**: scheduled, non-interactive jobs that may run bursts; a tuned pool can temporarily scale higher but still recycle stale connections.\r\n\r\nThese labels are metadata, not required switches: they are optional and can be omitted. When omitted, the runtime falls back to the safe defaults already used by the registry.\r\n\r\nSupported database engines and typical workload fit:\r\n- **SQLite**: best for local dev, embedded apps, testing, and lightweight single-user workflows; commonly used with `oltp` or `batch`.\r\n- **PostgreSQL**: strong fit for `oltp`, `analytics`, and `bulk` patterns; widely used for transactional apps, staging pipelines, and reporting.\r\n- **MySQL**: commonly used for `oltp` and `batch` workloads; also suitable for bulk ingestion when configured with a larger pool.\r\n- **SQL Server / MSSQL**: strong fit for `oltp`, `analytics`, and `bulk`, including large ETL copy operations with `tablock` and other MSSQL-specific bulk options.\r\n- **Oracle**: commonly used for mission-critical `oltp` and `analytics` workloads with tuned connection reuse and larger query fan-out.\r\n\r\nThese workload labels are not limited to a single database engine; they describe the application behavior of the connection, so all supported drivers can use the same tuning model.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n\u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabase name=\"analytics_db\"\r\n              driver=\"postgres\"\r\n              connection_string=\"host=localhost port=5432 user=app password=secret dbname=analytics sslmode=disable\"\r\n              max_open_conns=\"25\"\r\n              max_idle_conns=\"10\"\r\n              conn_max_lifetime_seconds=\"300\"\r\n              workload=\"oltp\" /\u003e\r\n\u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Example: Pool tuning for bulk ETL\r\nUse a larger pool for high-throughput bulk workloads and a shorter idle lifetime for transient jobs.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n\u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabase name=\"warehouse_db\"\r\n              driver=\"postgres\"\r\n              connection_string=\"host=warehouse.internal port=5432 user=loader password=secret dbname=warehouse sslmode=disable\"\r\n              max_open_conns=\"80\"\r\n              max_idle_conns=\"20\"\r\n              conn_max_lifetime_seconds=\"180\"\r\n              workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\r\n\u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n\u003cflow\u003e\r\n    \u003csql_bulk id=\"bulk_copy_orders\"\r\n              db=\"orders_db\"\r\n              target_db=\"warehouse_db\"\r\n              target_table=\"orders_fact\"\r\n              batch_size=\"5000\"\u003e\r\n        SELECT order_id, customer_id, amount, created_at FROM orders WHERE created_at \u003e= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL \u00277 days\u0027;\r\n    \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\r\n\u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n## Key AST Nodes\r\n\r\n### 1. `\u003csql\u003e`\r\nUsed for executing standard SQL scripts (DDL, DML, multi-statement batches, and analytical queries).\r\n- **`db` / `database`**: Name of the configured database connection to execute against.\r\n- **`id`**: Unique identifier for this step.\r\n- **`output_var` / `var` / `variable`**: (Optional) Pipeline variable where query results (with column headers) or affected row counts will be stored as a string.\r\n- **Intelligent Query vs. DML Classification**: Flow employs a token-aware query classifier (`isDMLQuery`) that strips SQL line comments (`--`), block comments (`/* ... */`), and XML `\u003c![CDATA[...]]\u003e` tags.\r\n  - **Query Mode**: Statements starting with `SELECT`, `WITH`, `SHOW`, `PRAGMA`, `DESCRIBE`, or containing row-returning clauses like `RETURNING` or `OUTPUT` are executed as queries. Results are formatted as a tabular text block into `output_var`. Columns named `deleted_at`, `is_deleted`, `insert_ts`, or `update_count` are recognized as regular projected columns without false DML classification.\r\n  - **DML / DDL Mode**: Pure mutating statements (`INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `MERGE`, `DROP`, `CREATE`, `ALTER`, `TRUNCATE`) are executed as updates, and the affected row count is recorded.\r\n\r\n### 2. `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`\r\nOptimized for streaming massive datasets directly from a source database query into a target table across heterogeneous database engines (e.g., PostgreSQL to SQL Server, MySQL to SQLite), bypassing CPU and memory bottlenecks with constant $O(1)$ RAM usage.\r\n- **`db` / `database`**: The source database connection name.\r\n- **`target_db` / `target_database`**: The destination database connection name (defaults to the source database if omitted).\r\n- **`target_table`**: The destination table to bulk insert records into.\r\n- **`batch_size`**: The row batch size (defaults to `10000`).\r\n- **`tablock`**: Acquire a table lock for minimal logging on SQL Server (`true` / `false`, defaults to `true`).\r\n- **`check_constraints`**: Evaluate table constraints during bulk insert (`true` / `false`).\r\n- **`fire_triggers`**: Execute target table triggers during insert (`true` / `false`).\r\n- **`keep_nulls`**: Preserve explicit NULL values (`true` / `false`).\r\n\r\n\u003e [!TIP]\r\n\u003e For in-depth guidance on choosing between `\u003csql\u003e`, `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`, and `\u003cforeach\u003e`, refer to the dedicated [SQL \u0026 SQL Bulk Guide](sql_sql_bulk.md).\r\n\r\n### 3. `\u003ckv\u003e`\r\nExecutes individual atomic operations (`get`, `put`/`set`, `delete`/`del`, `scan`/`list`) against Key-Value stores (`bbolt`, `badger`, `redis`, `etcd`).\r\n- **`db` / `database`**: Name of the configured KV database connection handle.\r\n- **`id`**: Unique identifier for this step.\r\n- **`bucket`**: Target bucket name (`bbolt`, `badger`) or namespace key prefix (`redis`, `etcd`). Defaults to `\"default\"`.\r\n- **`op`**: Operation type: `get`, `put` / `set`, `delete` / `del`, or `scan` / `list`.\r\n- **`key`**: Target key name.\r\n- **`value`**: Value payload to store (for `put` / `set`).\r\n- **`output_var` / `var` / `variable`**: (Optional) Pipeline variable receiving the operation result or returned values.\r\n- *Inline DSL*: Operations, keys, and values may also be written inside the node body (e.g. `set mykey myval` or `get mykey`).\r\n\r\n### 4. `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`\r\nStreams records directly from a SQL query source into a target Key-Value store bucket or namespace in high-throughput batches.\r\n- **`db` / `database`**: Name of the source SQL database connection executing the query.\r\n- **`target_db`**: Target Key-Value database connection handle.\r\n- **`target_bucket`**: Target bucket or key namespace prefix in the destination KV store.\r\n- **`batch_size`**: Number of records committed per chunk (defaults to `10000`). Automatically flushes Badger write batches and submits transactional commits in Etcd.\r\n- **`output_var` / `var`**: (Optional) Pipeline variable receiving the total count of transferred records.\r\n- *Query Body*: The enclosed SQL query must project at least two columns: `key` (col 1) and `value` (col 2).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Practical Examples\r\n\r\n### Example 1: Schema Setup and Data Ingestion (`\u003csql\u003e`)\r\nInitialize a database table structure and load initial seed data.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"analytics_db\"\r\n                  driver=\"sqlite\"\r\n                  connection_string=\"file::memory:?cache=shared\"\r\n                  max_open_conns=\"25\"\r\n                  max_idle_conns=\"10\"\r\n                  conn_max_lifetime_seconds=\"300\"\r\n                  workload=\"oltp\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"setup_analytics_schema\" db=\"analytics_db\"\u003e\r\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS page_views (\r\n                id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\r\n                url TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n                visitor_ip TEXT,\r\n                viewed_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\r\n            );\r\n            \r\n            INSERT INTO page_views (url, visitor_ip) VALUES \r\n            (\u0027/home\u0027, \u0027192.168.1.1\u0027),\r\n            (\u0027/products\u0027, \u0027192.168.1.100\u0027),\r\n            (\u0027/checkout\u0027, \u0027192.168.1.100\u0027);\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 2: Dynamic Querying with Parameter Interpolation (`\u003csql\u003e`)\r\nFilter database records dynamically utilizing pipeline environment variables.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"production_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/prod\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"MIN_RATING\" type=\"float\" value=\"4.5\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"LIMIT_COUNT\" type=\"int\" value=\"10\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"fetch_top_products\" db=\"production_db\" output_var=\"RECOMMENDED_PRODUCTS\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT product_id, title, rating \r\n            FROM store.products \r\n            WHERE rating \u003e= {{MIN_RATING}} \r\n            ORDER BY rating DESC \r\n            LIMIT {{LIMIT_COUNT}};\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 3: High-Performance Data Archival (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`)\r\nBulk replicate records from a production database directly into a separate analytics cold storage target.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"prod_db\" driver=\"mysql\" connection_string=\"root:secret@tcp(localhost:3306)/prod\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"archive_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./archive.db\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Stream rows in batches of 5000 directly from source to target --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql_bulk id=\"archive_historical_logs\" \r\n                  db=\"prod_db\" \r\n                  target_db=\"archive_db\" \r\n                  target_table=\"historical_logs\" \r\n                  batch_size=\"5000\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT log_id, event, severity, created_at \r\n            FROM system.logs \r\n            WHERE created_at \u0026lt; DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 90 DAY);\r\n        \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 4: Exporting Queries to Multiple Excel Tabs (`\u003cexcel_write\u003e`)\r\nConsolidate multiple database reports into separate tabs inside a single `.xlsx` workbook using consecutive `\u003cexcel_write\u003e` nodes.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"retail_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/retail\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Tab 1: Store Sales Overview --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"write_sales_overview\" \r\n                     file=\"./reports/Retail_Dashboard.xlsx\" \r\n                     sheet=\"Sales Summary\" \r\n                     db=\"retail_db\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT date_trunc(\u0027month\u0027, sale_date) AS sale_month, SUM(revenue) AS total_revenue\r\n            FROM sales.daily_ledger\r\n            GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1 DESC;\r\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Tab 2: Best Performing Products --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"write_top_inventory\" \r\n                     file=\"./reports/Retail_Dashboard.xlsx\" \r\n                     sheet=\"Top Products\" \r\n                     db=\"retail_db\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT sku, product_name, units_sold, stock_on_hand\r\n            FROM sales.product_performance\r\n            WHERE units_sold \u003e 500\r\n            ORDER BY units_sold DESC;\r\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 5: Combined Ingestion, Bulk Replication, and Multi-Tab Reporting\r\nBuild a complete end-to-end data staging, bulk copy, and multi-tab Excel dashboard export workflow.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"crm_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/crm\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"reporting_warehouse\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./warehouse.db\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- 1. Staging tables creation --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"create_staging_structures\" db=\"reporting_warehouse\"\u003e\r\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS stg_leads (\r\n                lead_id INTEGER, \r\n                company TEXT, \r\n                deal_size REAL, \r\n                stage TEXT\r\n            );\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 2. Bulk stream dynamic leads data directly to staging database --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql_bulk id=\"stage_crm_leads\" \r\n                  db=\"crm_db\" \r\n                  target_db=\"reporting_warehouse\" \r\n                  target_table=\"stg_leads\" \r\n                  batch_size=\"1000\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT id AS lead_id, company, deal_size, status AS stage \r\n            FROM public.leads \r\n            WHERE status IN (\u0027Qualified\u0027, \u0027Proposal Sent\u0027);\r\n        \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 3. Export Summary to Sheet 1 --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"report_summary\" \r\n                     file=\"./reports/CRM_Leads_Report.xlsx\" \r\n                     sheet=\"Summary Overview\" \r\n                     db=\"reporting_warehouse\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT stage, COUNT(lead_id) AS total_leads, SUM(deal_size) AS pipeline_value\r\n            FROM stg_leads\r\n            GROUP BY stage;\r\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 4. Export Raw Details to Sheet 2 --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"report_raw_details\" \r\n                     file=\"./reports/CRM_Leads_Report.xlsx\" \r\n                     sheet=\"Lead Details\" \r\n                     db=\"reporting_warehouse\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT lead_id, company, deal_size, stage\r\n            FROM stg_leads\r\n            ORDER BY deal_size DESC;\r\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 6: Reading Excel Data and Writing to a Database\r\nExtract data from a spreadsheet using `\u003cexcel_read\u003e` and write it to a database using a Go interpreter script.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"inventory_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/inventory\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- 1. Extract spreadsheet data into a pipeline variable containing JSON string --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_read file=\"./uploads/inventory_updates.xlsx\" sheet=\"New_Arrivals\" header=\"true\" output_var=\"EXCEL_JSON\" /\u003e\r\n        \r\n        \u003c!-- 2. Loop over extracted data programmatically and insert/update in database --\u003e\r\n        \u003cscript lang=\"go\"\u003e\r\n            package main\r\n            import (\r\n                \"encoding/json\"\r\n                \"fmt\"\r\n                \"host/vars\"\r\n                \"host/db\"\r\n            )\r\n            \r\n            type Item struct {\r\n                SKU   string  `json:\"sku\"`\r\n                Name  string  `json:\"name\"`\r\n                Price float64 `json:\"price\"`\r\n                Stock int     `json:\"stock\"`\r\n            }\r\n            \r\n            func main() {\r\n                jsonData := vars.GetString(\"EXCEL_JSON\")\r\n                var items []Item\r\n                if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(jsonData), \u0026items); err != nil {\r\n                    panic(err)\r\n                }\r\n                \r\n                // Retrieve the database connection\r\n                conn, err := db.Get(\"inventory_db\")\r\n                if err != nil {\r\n                    panic(err)\r\n                }\r\n                \r\n                for _, item := range items {\r\n                    _, err := conn.Exec(`\r\n                        INSERT INTO products (sku, name, price, stock) \r\n                        VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4) \r\n                        ON CONFLICT (sku) \r\n                        DO UPDATE SET price = EXCLUDED.price, stock = EXCLUDED.stock`,\r\n                        item.SKU, item.Name, item.Price, item.Stock,\r\n                    )\r\n                    if err != nil {\r\n                        panic(err)\r\n                    }\r\n                }\r\n                fmt.Printf(\"Successfully imported %d items from Excel to DB\\n\", len(items))\r\n            }\r\n        \u003c/script\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 7: Wrapping SQL Script Blocks inside Transactions\r\nUse `\u003cgroup\u003e` with `transaction=\"true\"` to wrap multiple SQL operations inside an atomic transaction, ensuring automatic rollback on any failure.\r\n\r\nFlow implements a thread-safe transaction stack (`activeTxs`):\r\n- **Nested Transactions**: If an inner `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` executes against the same database as an outer transaction, Flow automatically provisions a dialect-aware savepoint: ANSI `SAVEPOINT` / `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` / `RELEASE SAVEPOINT` for PostgreSQL, SQLite, and MySQL; T-SQL `SAVE TRANSACTION` / `ROLLBACK TRANSACTION` (with automatic release omission) for Microsoft SQL Server; and `SAVEPOINT` for Oracle. If the inner group completes successfully, its savepoint is committed/released; if it fails, it rolls back to the savepoint without corrupting the outer transaction.\r\n- **Fail-Safe Defer Rollback**: Any unhandled error, pipeline cancellation, or runtime panic triggers an immediate rollback, popping the transaction stack cleanly and preventing hanging database locks.\r\n- **Transaction Timeout**: You can specify `timeout=\"30s\"` on the `\u003cgroup\u003e` node to ensure a stuck transaction does not block connection pools indefinitely.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"finance_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/finance\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Outer transaction boundary with a 30s deadline --\u003e\r\n        \u003cgroup id=\"transfer_funds_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"finance_db\" timeout=\"30s\" description=\"Transfers balance safely between accounts\"\u003e\r\n            \r\n            \u003c!-- Step 1: Withdraw from Account A --\u003e\r\n            \u003csql id=\"withdraw_acc_a\" db=\"finance_db\"\u003e\r\n                UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 250.00 WHERE account_id = \u0027ACC-001\u0027 AND balance \u003e= 250.00;\r\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n            \r\n            \u003c!-- Step 2: Nested transaction block using ANSI SAVEPOINT --\u003e\r\n            \u003cgroup id=\"nested_audit_log\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"finance_db\"\u003e\r\n                \u003csql id=\"log_txn_event\" db=\"finance_db\"\u003e\r\n                    INSERT INTO transactions_ledger (from_account, to_account, amount) VALUES (\u0027ACC-001\u0027, \u0027ACC-002\u0027, 250.00);\r\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n            \u003c/group\u003e\r\n            \r\n            \u003c!-- Step 3: Deposit into Account B --\u003e\r\n            \u003csql id=\"deposit_acc_b\" db=\"finance_db\"\u003e\r\n                UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 250.00 WHERE account_id = \u0027ACC-002\u0027;\r\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n            \r\n        \u003c/group\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 8: Inserting excel_read JSON Output directly using SQL JSON Parsing\r\nIf your database engine supports native JSON processing functions, you can pass the JSON output variable from `\u003cexcel_read\u003e` directly into your `\u003csql\u003e` node for inline database insertion.\r\n\r\n#### Method A: PostgreSQL (Using `json_populate_recordset`)\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"store_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/store\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- 1. Extract spreadsheet rows to a variable containing a JSON array string --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_read file=\"./uploads/catalog.xlsx\" sheet=\"Products\" header=\"true\" output_var=\"CATALOG_JSON\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 2. Deserialize and insert directly inside SQL using postgres JSON engine functions --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"import_catalog\" db=\"store_db\"\u003e\r\n            INSERT INTO store.inventory (sku, name, price)\r\n            SELECT sku, name, price \r\n            FROM json_populate_recordset(NULL::store.inventory, \u0027{{CATALOG_JSON}}\u0027);\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### Method B: SQLite (Using `json_each`)\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"local_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./local.db\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- 1. Extract worksheet data into a variable containing a JSON array string --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_read file=\"./uploads/users.xlsx\" sheet=\"Sheet1\" header=\"true\" output_var=\"USER_JSON\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 2. Parse and insert using SQLite\u0027s built-in json_each function --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"import_users\" db=\"local_db\"\u003e\r\n            INSERT INTO users (username, role)\r\n            SELECT \r\n                json_extract(value, \u0027$.username\u0027) AS username,\r\n                json_extract(value, \u0027$.role\u0027) AS role\r\n            FROM json_each(\u0027{{USER_JSON}}\u0027);\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### Method C: Microsoft SQL Server / MSSQL (Using `OPENJSON`)\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"mssql_db\" driver=\"sqlserver\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://sa:secret@localhost:1433?database=store\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- 1. Extract spreadsheet rows into a JSON array variable string --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_read file=\"./uploads/catalog.xlsx\" sheet=\"Products\" header=\"true\" output_var=\"CATALOG_JSON\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 2. Parse and insert using SQL Server\u0027s native OPENJSON function --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"import_catalog_mssql\" db=\"mssql_db\"\u003e\r\n            INSERT INTO dbo.inventory (sku, name, price)\r\n            SELECT sku, name, price\r\n            FROM OPENJSON(\u0027{{CATALOG_JSON}}\u0027)\r\n            WITH (\r\n                sku VARCHAR(50) \u0027$.sku\u0027,\r\n                name VARCHAR(100) \u0027$.name\u0027,\r\n                price DECIMAL(10, 2) \u0027$.price\u0027\r\n            );\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 9: Key-Value Single Operations (`\u003ckv\u003e`)\r\nDemonstrates interacting with embedded stores (`bbolt`, `badger`) and server instances (`redis`, `etcd`) using both XML attributes and inline DSL syntax.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"local_cache\" driver=\"bbolt\" connection_string=\"./data/cache.db\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"redis_pub\" driver=\"redis\" connection_string=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Attribute syntax: write and read from bbolt --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"set_cfg\" db=\"local_cache\" bucket=\"settings\" op=\"put\" key=\"max_workers\" value=\"64\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"get_cfg\" db=\"local_cache\" bucket=\"settings\" op=\"get\" key=\"max_workers\" output_var=\"WORKER_COUNT\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Inline DSL syntax: Redis commands --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"redis_cache_session\" db=\"redis_pub\" bucket=\"sessions\"\u003e\r\n            set sess:12345 user_session_payload\r\n        \u003c/kv\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"redis_get_session\" db=\"redis_pub\" bucket=\"sessions\" output_var=\"SESSION_DATA\"\u003e\r\n            get sess:12345\r\n        \u003c/kv\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Prefix Scan --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"scan_sessions\" db=\"redis_pub\" bucket=\"sessions\" op=\"scan\" key=\"sess:\" output_var=\"ACTIVE_SESSIONS\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Key Deletion --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"delete_session\" db=\"redis_pub\" bucket=\"sessions\" op=\"del\" key=\"sess:12345\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 10: SQL to Key-Value High-Throughput Bulk ETL (`\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`)\r\nStreams key-value projections from a relational SQL database straight into a Key-Value target bucket in high-speed batches.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"sql_catalog\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/store\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"fast_redis\" driver=\"redis\" connection_string=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"embedded_badger\" driver=\"badger\" connection_string=\"./data/product_cache\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Replicate product prices directly into Redis cache in chunks of 5000 --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv_bulk id=\"bulk_cache_prices\"\r\n                 db=\"sql_catalog\"\r\n                 target_db=\"fast_redis\"\r\n                 target_bucket=\"product_prices\"\r\n                 batch_size=\"5000\"\r\n                 output_var=\"RECORDS_CACHED\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT sku, CAST(price AS TEXT) FROM store.products WHERE active = true\r\n        \u003c/kv_bulk\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Stream lookup values into an embedded BadgerDB LSM storage --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv_bulk id=\"bulk_seed_badger\"\r\n                 db=\"sql_catalog\"\r\n                 target_db=\"embedded_badger\"\r\n                 target_bucket=\"sku_inventory\"\r\n                 batch_size=\"10000\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT sku, CAST(quantity AS TEXT) FROM store.inventory\r\n        \u003c/kv_bulk\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor dedicated details, driver characteristics, and DSL syntax, refer to the [Key-Value Documentation](kv.md).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Database-Backed Resource Storage \u0026 Encryption 🔒\r\n\r\nFlow supports loading pipelines, options, and configuration overrides directly from database tables using standard SQL URIs:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nsql://\u003cdriver\u003e@\u003cdsn\u003e#SELECT ...\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Encrypted Storage at Rest\r\nSensitive database rows containing pipeline logic, connection strings, or variables can be encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM. Because Flow uses a text-safe armored envelope (`FLOWENC:v1:\u003cbase64(...)\u003e`), encrypted payloads can be stored directly into standard database string columns without schema modifications:\r\n\r\n* `dbo.flow_pipeline_content` (`[PipelineXML] NVARCHAR(MAX)`)\r\n* `dbo.flow_options_content` (`[OptionsXML] NVARCHAR(MAX)`)\r\n* `dbo.flow_config_content` (`[ConfigXML] NVARCHAR(MAX)`)\r\n\r\n### Executing Encrypted Database Pipelines\r\nAt runtime, specify the `-encrypted` flag and provide the decryption key via `-secure-key` or the `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` environment variable:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -encrypted -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\" \\\r\n         -file \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=master#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name = \u0027production_daily_etl\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf the stored record begins with `FLOWENC:v1:`, Flow will automatically detect and decrypt the payload before parsing and execution.\r\n\r\nFor detailed table schemas, SQL Server merge queries, and tooling, refer to:\r\n* [Database-backed Resource Storage Guide](README_DATABASE_XML.md)\r\n* [Cross-Platform Encryption Guide](ENCRYPTION.md)\r\n* [Database Importer Documentation](../db_importer/README_DB_IMPORTER.md)\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n","database_connection_strings.md":"# Database Connection Strings \u0026 Driver Reference Guide 🔌\n\nThis guide provides a comprehensive reference of all supported database drivers in `go-flow`, detailing every available connection string format, query parameter, security setting, and performance tuning option. It includes practical examples for local development, enterprise production, cloud-managed instances, mutual TLS, and high-throughput ETL pipelines.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Driver Overview \u0026 Comparison Matrix\n\nThe table below outlines each database and storage engine supported by `go-flow`, the registered driver aliases, default network ports, URI schemes, and supported operational capabilities.\n\n| Database / Engine | Driver Aliases | Underlying Go Library | Default Port | Primary DSN Schemes | Key Capabilities | Safe Default Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **PostgreSQL** | `postgres`\u003cbr\u003e`postgresql`\u003cbr\u003e`pq` | `github.com/lib/pq` | `5432` | `postgresql://`\u003cbr\u003e`postgres://`\u003cbr\u003e`host=...` (Key-Value) | Full SSL/TLS (mTLS), schema `search_path`, session timeouts, binary parameters, connection keepalives | `postgresql://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb?sslmode=disable` |\n| **MySQL \u0026 MariaDB** | `mysql` | `github.com/go-sql-driver/mysql` | `3306` | `user:pass@tcp(host:port)/db`\u003cbr\u003e`user:pass@unix(socket)/db` | Datetime parsing (`parseTime`), TLS/SSL, bulk parameter interpolation (`interpolateParams`), multi-statements, packet sizing | `user:pass@tcp(localhost:3306)/mydb?parseTime=true\u0026loc=UTC` |\n| **Microsoft SQL Server \u0026 Azure SQL** | `sqlserver`\u003cbr\u003e`mssql`\u003cbr\u003e`azuresql` | `github.com/microsoft/go-mssqldb`\u003cbr\u003e(incl. `/azuread`) | `1433` | `sqlserver://`\u003cbr\u003e`server=...;` (ADO.NET) | TLS encryption, self-signed cert trust, named instances, Kerberos/AD auth, Microsoft Entra (`fedauth`), MultiSubnetFailover (Always On AG), packet sizing (`packet size`), MARS | `sqlserver://sa:pass@localhost:1433?database=mydb\u0026encrypt=disable` |\n| **SQLite** | `sqlite`\u003cbr\u003e`sqlite3` | `modernc.org/sqlite` | N/A | `file:...`\u003cbr\u003e`:memory:`\u003cbr\u003e`/path/to/file.db` | Pure Go (zero CGO), WAL journal mode, busy timeouts, RAM caches, shared in-memory caches, read-only immutable mounts | `file:app.db?cache=shared\u0026mode=rwc\u0026_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)` |\n| **Oracle Database** | `oracle`\u003cbr\u003e`godror` | `github.com/sijms/go-ora/v2` | `1521` | `oracle://`\u003cbr\u003e`user/pass@host:port/svc` | Pure Go (zero Oracle Client/OCI), Service Names \u0026 SIDs, Oracle Cloud Wallets (mTLS), bulk prefetch tuning | `oracle://user:pass@localhost:1521/XEPDB1` |\n| **Redis \u0026 Valkey** | `redis`\u003cbr\u003e`valkey` | `github.com/redis/go-redis/v9` | `6379` | `redis://`\u003cbr\u003e`rediss://` (TLS)\u003cbr\u003e`host:port` | In-memory key-value, pipelines, TLS verification, connection pooling, DB indexes | `redis://:password@localhost:6379/0?dial_timeout=5s` |\n| **bbolt** | `bbolt`\u003cbr\u003e`bolt` | `go.etcd.io/bbolt` | N/A | `/path/to/cache.db`\u003cbr\u003e`./store.db` | Embedded ACID transactions, lock timeouts, POSIX file permissions | `./data/cache.db?timeout=1s` |\n| **BadgerDB** | `badger`\u003cbr\u003e`badgerdb` | `github.com/dgraph-io/badger/v4` | N/A | `/path/to/dir`\u003cbr\u003e`badger://:memory:` | Pure Go LSM-tree KV, fast sequential writes, in-memory mode, Snappy compression | `./data/badger_data?sync_writes=false` |\n| **etcd (v3)** | `etcd`\u003cbr\u003e`etcdv3` | `go.etcd.io/etcd/client/v3` | `2379` | `http://host:port,...`\u003cbr\u003e`https://host:port` (TLS) | Distributed consensus KV, multi-endpoint failover, client certificate authentication | `http://127.0.0.1:2379,http://127.0.0.1:22379` |\n\n\u003e [!IMPORTANT]\n\u003e **XML Entity Escaping**: When defining connection strings inside XML pipeline files (`\u003cdatabase connection_string=\"...\" /\u003e`) or options files (`\u003coptions\u003e\u003cdsn\u003e...\u003c/dsn\u003e\u003c/options\u003e`), all literal ampersands (`\u0026`) separating URL query parameters **must be escaped as `\u0026amp;`**.\n\u003e * Example: `postgresql://user:pass@localhost:5432/db?sslmode=disable\u0026amp;connect_timeout=10`\n\n---\n\n## 2. Common Parameter Comparison Matrix\n\nThe following table summarizes common operational categories and how they are configured across the relational SQL drivers:\n\n| Category | PostgreSQL (`lib/pq`) | MySQL (`go-sql-driver/mysql`) | SQL Server (`go-mssqldb`) | SQLite (`modernc.org/sqlite`) | Oracle (`go-ora`) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **SSL / TLS Mode** | `sslmode=disable`\u003cbr\u003e`sslmode=require`\u003cbr\u003e`sslmode=verify-full` | `tls=false`\u003cbr\u003e`tls=true`\u003cbr\u003e`tls=skip-verify` | `encrypt=disable`\u003cbr\u003e`encrypt=true`\u003cbr\u003e`encrypt=false` | N/A (Local / In-Process) | `SSL=FALSE`\u003cbr\u003e`SSL=ENABLE`\u003cbr\u003e`SSL VERIFY=FALSE` |\n| **Trust Self-Signed Certs** | `sslmode=require` | `tls=skip-verify` | `TrustServerCertificate=true` | N/A | `SSL VERIFY=FALSE` |\n| **Client Certificate (mTLS)** | `sslcert=...`\u003cbr\u003e`sslkey=...`\u003cbr\u003e`sslrootcert=...` | Registered via Go code or `tls=custom` | `certificate=...` | N/A | `WALLET=/path/to/wallet.zip` |\n| **Connection Timeout** | `connect_timeout=10` *(seconds)* | `timeout=10s`\u003cbr\u003e`readTimeout=30s` | `connection timeout=15` *(seconds)* | `_pragma=busy_timeout(5000)` *(ms)* | `CONNECTION TIMEOUT=10` *(seconds)* |\n| **Timezone \u0026 Datetime** | `timezone=UTC` | `parseTime=true`\u003cbr\u003e`loc=UTC` | Driver parses `DATETIME2` to UTC | Native epoch / ISO8601 strings | Converted via session time zone |\n| **Application Identity** | `application_name=flow_app` | Handled via connection ID | `app name=flow_app`\u003cbr\u003e`workstation id=host1` | N/A | `CLIENT IDENTIFIER=flow_app` |\n| **Batch / Bulk Tuning** | `binary_parameters=yes` | `interpolateParams=true`\u003cbr\u003e`maxAllowedPacket=67108864` | `packet size=32767` | `_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)`\u003cbr\u003e`_pragma=synchronous(NORMAL)` | `PREFETCH_ROWS=1000` |\n| **Federated / Cloud Identity** | IAM authentication tokens | IAM authentication tokens | Microsoft Entra Integrated (`fedauth=ActiveDirectoryIntegrated`)\u003cbr\u003eEntra Default (`fedauth=ActiveDirectoryDefault`)\u003cbr\u003eMSI (`fedauth=ActiveDirectoryMSI`) | N/A | OCI IAM / Wallets |\n| **High Availability \u0026 Failover** | `target_session_attrs=primary` (multi-host) | Multi-host connection strings | `MultiSubnetFailover=true`\u003cbr\u003e`ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly`\u003cbr\u003e`failoverpartner=mirror` | N/A (Replicated / distributed disk) | Oracle RAC / SCAN Listeners |\n| **Schema Isolation** | `search_path=staging,public` | Specified in database path | `database=catalog` | Attach secondary databases | Specified in table query |\n\n---\n\n## 3. PostgreSQL\n\nThe PostgreSQL driver (`github.com/lib/pq`) supports two distinct connection string formats: **URI format** (`postgresql://...`) and **Key-Value format** (`host=... port=...`).\n\n### Available Options Reference\n\n| Parameter | Type | Allowed Values / Format | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| `sslmode` | string | `disable`, `require`, `verify-ca`, `verify-full` | Determines TLS verification rigor. `disable` skips encryption; `require` encrypts without CA validation; `verify-full` validates server certificate hostname against CA. |\n| `sslrootcert` | path | Absolute or relative path to CA certificate | Root CA certificate file (PEM format) used to verify server identity. |\n| `sslcert` | path | Absolute or relative path to client cert | Client certificate file (PEM format) for mutual authentication (mTLS). |\n| `sslkey` | path | Absolute or relative path to client private key | Client private key file (PEM format, must have restricted POSIX permissions `0600`). |\n| `connect_timeout` | integer | Number of seconds (e.g. `10`) | Maximum wait time for connection establishment before failing. `0` disables timeout. |\n| `statement_timeout` | integer | Milliseconds (e.g. `30000`) | Aborts any SQL statement that takes longer than the specified number of milliseconds. |\n| `application_name` | string | Text identifier (e.g. `flow_etl_worker`) | Sets the application name displayed in `pg_stat_activity` for auditing and DBA monitoring. |\n| `fallback_application_name` | string | Text identifier | Fallback application name if `application_name` is not specified. |\n| `search_path` | string | Comma-separated schemas (e.g. `analytics,public`) | Configures the schema search order for unqualified table and view references. |\n| `timezone` | string | Time zone identifier (e.g. `UTC`, `America/New_York`) | Sets the session `TimeZone` configuration variable. |\n| `client_encoding` | string | Character encoding (e.g. `UTF8`) | Configures the character encoding of SQL statements and returned results. |\n| `binary_parameters` | string | `yes`, `no` | When set to `yes`, sends parameters in binary format for improved transmission speed. |\n| `keepalives` | integer | `1` (enable) or `0` (disable) | Controls whether client-side TCP keepalives are enabled. |\n| `keepalives_idle` | integer | Seconds | Number of seconds of inactivity before sending a keepalive probe. |\n| `keepalives_interval` | integer | Seconds | Interval in seconds between keepalive retransmissions. |\n| `keepalives_count` | integer | Integer count | Number of unacknowledged keepalive probes before dropping connection. |\n| `sslsni` | integer | `1` (default) or `0` | Enables or disables Server Name Indication (SNI) during TLS handshake. |\n\n### PostgreSQL Connection Examples\n\n#### Example 1: Local Development (Plaintext / No SSL)\n```text\npostgresql://postgres:localDevPass123@localhost:5432/app_db?sslmode=disable\n```\n*Equivalent Key-Value format:*\n```text\nhost=localhost port=5432 user=postgres password=localDevPass123 dbname=app_db sslmode=disable\n```\n\n#### Example 2: Production Cloud Database (AWS RDS / GCP Cloud SQL / Neon / Supabase)\nRequires TLS encryption with connection timeout and UTC session timezone:\n```text\npostgresql://app_user:StrongProdPass2026!@db.internal.company.com:5432/analytics_prod?sslmode=require\u0026connect_timeout=10\u0026timezone=UTC\u0026application_name=flow_pipeline\n```\n*XML pipeline syntax (note the escaped `\u0026amp;`):*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"analytics_db\"\n          driver=\"postgres\"\n          connection_string=\"postgresql://app_user:StrongProdPass2026!@db.internal.company.com:5432/analytics_prod?sslmode=require\u0026amp;connect_timeout=10\u0026amp;timezone=UTC\u0026amp;application_name=flow_pipeline\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 3: High-Security Mutual TLS (mTLS) with Root CA Verification\nValidates the server certificate against a custom root certificate authority and authenticates the client using client certificates:\n```text\nhost=secure-db.internal port=5432 user=etl_service dbname=finance sslmode=verify-full sslrootcert=/etc/ssl/certs/internal_ca.pem sslcert=/etc/ssl/certs/etl_client.pem sslkey=/etc/ssl/certs/etl_client_key.pem connect_timeout=15\n```\n\n#### Example 4: Schema Isolation with Search Path \u0026 Statement Guardrails\nDirects queries to the `staging` schema first and aborts long-running statements after 60 seconds:\n```text\npostgresql://loader:SecretPass@db.internal:5432/warehouse?sslmode=require\u0026search_path=staging,shared,public\u0026statement_timeout=60000\u0026application_name=batch_loader\n```\n\n#### Example 5: High-Speed Local Unix Domain Socket\nAvoids loopback TCP stack overhead on Linux / macOS servers:\n```text\nhost=/var/run/postgresql user=postgres dbname=app_db sslmode=disable\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. MySQL \u0026 MariaDB\n\nThe MySQL driver (`github.com/go-sql-driver/mysql`) uses Data Source Names (DSNs) structured as:\n```text\n[username[:password]@][protocol[(address)]]/dbname[?param1=value1\u0026param2=value2]\n```\n\n### Available Options Reference\n\n| Parameter | Type | Allowed Values / Format | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| `parseTime` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `false`) | Automatically parses `DATE` and `DATETIME` columns into Go `time.Time` values instead of raw `[]byte`. **Essential for ETL pipelines.** |\n| `loc` | string | `Local`, `UTC`, or URL-encoded IANA location (e.g. `America%2FNew_York`) | Sets the timezone location used when parsing datetime values. |\n| `tls` | string | `false`, `true`, `skip-verify`, `preferred`, or custom profile name | `false` disables TLS; `true` requires valid server cert; `skip-verify` enables TLS but skips certificate chain verification (useful for self-signed certificates). |\n| `timeout` | duration | Duration string (e.g. `5s`, `10s`) | Timeout for establishing the network connection (dial timeout). |\n| `readTimeout` | duration | Duration string (e.g. `30s`, `1m`) | I/O read timeout on established connections. |\n| `writeTimeout` | duration | Duration string (e.g. `30s`, `1m`) | I/O write timeout on established connections. |\n| `interpolateParams` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `false`) | Interpolates placeholders client-side into a single query string instead of preparing separate statements. **Significantly accelerates bulk inserts and batch updates.** |\n| `multiStatements` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `false`) | Allows executing multiple semicolon-delimited SQL statements in a single query call (e.g. DDL scripts, multi-table transactions). |\n| `collation` | string | MySQL Collation name (e.g. `utf8mb4_unicode_ci`, `utf8mb4_general_ci`) | Sets the character set collation for client communication. |\n| `charset` | string | Charset name (e.g. `utf8mb4`, `utf8`) | Sets the connection character set. |\n| `maxAllowedPacket` | integer | Size in bytes (e.g. `67108864` for 64MB) | Sets maximum packet size accepted from the MySQL server. |\n| `clientFoundRows` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `false`) | Returns matching rows instead of changed rows for `UPDATE` statements. |\n| `allowNativePasswords` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `true`) | Allows the `mysql_native_password` authentication method. |\n| `checkConnLiveness` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `true`) | Performs connection health check before executing queries. |\n| `autocommit` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `true`) | Controls automatic transaction commitment. |\n\n### MySQL Connection Examples\n\n#### Example 1: Standard Local Development (TCP with Datetime Parsing)\n```text\nroot:localPassword123@tcp(localhost:3306)/flow_data?parseTime=true\u0026loc=Local\n```\n\n#### Example 2: High-Performance Bulk ETL Pipeline\nEnables parameter interpolation for rapid bulk insertions, 64MB packet buffers, and explicit I/O timeouts:\n```text\netl_writer:ETLSecretPass2026!@tcp(db-cluster.internal:3306)/sales_warehouse?parseTime=true\u0026loc=UTC\u0026interpolateParams=true\u0026maxAllowedPacket=67108864\u0026timeout=10s\u0026readTimeout=60s\u0026writeTimeout=60s\n```\n*XML pipeline syntax (with escaped `\u0026amp;`):*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"sales_warehouse\"\n          driver=\"mysql\"\n          connection_string=\"etl_writer:ETLSecretPass2026!@tcp(db-cluster.internal:3306)/sales_warehouse?parseTime=true\u0026amp;loc=UTC\u0026amp;interpolateParams=true\u0026amp;maxAllowedPacket=67108864\u0026amp;timeout=10s\u0026amp;readTimeout=60s\u0026amp;writeTimeout=60s\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 3: Cloud Database with Managed SSL / Self-Signed Skip-Verify\nSuitable for Amazon RDS, Google Cloud SQL, or DigitalOcean Managed MySQL when intermediate CA certs are not locally configured:\n```text\napp_service:ProdPassword99@tcp(mysql-master.c7xyz.us-east-1.rds.amazonaws.com:3306)/production?parseTime=true\u0026loc=UTC\u0026tls=skip-verify\u0026timeout=15s\n```\n\n#### Example 4: Local High-Speed Unix Domain Socket\nDirectly connects to local daemon via Unix socket without TCP networking:\n```text\nflow_user:Pass123!@unix(/var/run/mysqld/mysqld.sock)/production_db?parseTime=true\u0026loc=UTC\n```\n\n#### Example 5: Migration Runner (Multi-Statement Support)\nEnables running multi-statement schema migration files containing semicolons:\n```text\nadmin_user:AdminPass123@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/app_schema?multiStatements=true\u0026parseTime=true\n```\n\n#### Example 6: IPv6 Loopback Connection\n```text\nflow_app:MyPassword@tcp([::1]:3306)/ipv6_test?parseTime=true\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Microsoft SQL Server \u0026 Azure SQL\n\nThe Microsoft SQL Server driver (`github.com/microsoft/go-mssqldb`) supports both **URL format** (`sqlserver://...`) and **ADO.NET / ODBC semi-colon key-value format** (`server=...;database=...;`).\n\n### Available Options Reference\n\n| Parameter (URL / ADO.NET) | Type | Allowed Values / Format | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| `database` | string | Database name | Target database / catalog name to switch to upon connecting. |\n| `encrypt` | string / bool | `true`, `false`, `disable` | `true` enforces TLS/SSL encryption; `disable` or `false` allows unencrypted connections. |\n| `TrustServerCertificate`\u003cbr\u003e`trustservercertificate` | boolean | `true`, `false` (default `false`) | When set to `true`, trusts self-signed server certificates without CA validation. **Essential for local Docker or test instances.** |\n| `connection timeout` | integer | Seconds (e.g. `15`) | Connection establishment timeout. |\n| `app name`\u003cbr\u003e`application name` | string | String identifier (e.g. `Flow_ETL_Service`) | Surfaces client application name in SQL Server dynamic management views (`sys.dm_exec_sessions`). |\n| `workstation id` | string | Hostname (e.g. `ETL-NODE-01`) | Workstation identifier logged in SQL Server connection telemetry. |\n| `packet size` | integer | Bytes (`512` to `32767`, default `4096`) | Network Packet Size (TDS buffer). **Setting to `32767` significantly accelerates high-volume ETL bulk copies.** |\n| `multisubnetfailover`\u003cbr\u003e`MultiSubnetFailover` | boolean | `true`, `false`, `Enabled`, `Disabled` | When `true`, connects in parallel to all IP addresses resolved for an Always On Availability Group listener across multi-subnet clusters. |\n| `applicationintent`\u003cbr\u003e`ApplicationIntent` | string | `ReadOnly`, `ReadWrite` | Directs read-only queries to secondary replicas in Always On Availability Group configurations (requires `database` parameter). |\n| `MultipleActiveResultSets`\u003cbr\u003e`mars` | boolean | `true`, `false` | Enables Multiple Active Result Sets (MARS) on a single connection. |\n| `failoverpartner` | string | Hostname or IP of mirror server | Specifies the database mirroring or Always On Availability Group failover partner. |\n| `keepAlive` | integer | Seconds (default `30`) | Frequency of TCP keep-alive packets sent to detect dead connections. |\n| `log` | integer | Integer bitmask (e.g. `63`) | Enables internal driver logging to standard error for troubleshooting connection negotiation. |\n| `fedauth` | string | `ActiveDirectoryIntegrated`\u003cbr\u003e`ActiveDirectoryDefault`\u003cbr\u003e`ActiveDirectoryMSI`\u003cbr\u003e`ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal`\u003cbr\u003e`ActiveDirectoryInteractive`\u003cbr\u003e`ActiveDirectoryPassword` | Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory) authentication workflow. |\n| `applicationclientid` | string | UUID (Client / Application ID) | Azure Client ID required for `ActiveDirectoryPassword` and `ActiveDirectoryInteractive`. |\n| `clientcertpath` | path | File path to `.pem` certificate | Certificate path for certificate-based Service Principal authentication. |\n| `resource id` | string | Azure Resource ID | Azure resource ID when using User-Assigned Managed Identity (`ActiveDirectoryMSI`). |\n| `additionallyallowedtenants` | string | Comma-separated tenant IDs | Allowed additional tenant IDs for multi-tenant Microsoft Entra authentication. |\n| `integrated security` | string / bool | `true`, `sspi` | Uses Windows Integrated Authentication (SSPI / Kerberos) under on-premises Windows Active Directory. |\n\n### SQL Server Connection Examples\n\n#### Example 1: Local Development / Docker (Plaintext Encryption Disabled)\n```text\nsqlserver://sa:YourStrongPassword123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026encrypt=disable\n```\n*Equivalent ADO.NET key-value format:*\n```text\nserver=localhost;port=1433;user id=sa;password=YourStrongPassword123!;database=master;encrypt=disable;\n```\n\n#### Example 2: Local Staging with Self-Signed Certificate\nEnables TLS encryption while instructing the client to trust the self-signed certificate:\n```text\nsqlserver://sa:YourStrongPassword123!@localhost:1433?database=telemetry\u0026encrypt=true\u0026TrustServerCertificate=true\n```\n*XML pipeline syntax (note the escaped `\u0026amp;`):*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"telemetry_db\"\n          driver=\"sqlserver\"\n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://sa:YourStrongPassword123!@localhost:1433?database=telemetry\u0026amp;encrypt=true\u0026amp;TrustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 3: Azure SQL Database (SQL Authentication)\nAzure SQL requires encryption enabled and authenticates using username and server suffix:\n```text\nsqlserver://appadmin@my-sql-srv:ComplexAzurePass999!@my-sql-srv.database.windows.net:1433?database=enterprise_db\u0026encrypt=true\u0026connection+timeout=30\u0026app+name=Flow_Pipeline\n```\n\n#### Example 4: Named Instance Connection\nConnects to a specific named instance (e.g. `SQLEXPRESS` or `REPORTING`):\n```text\nsqlserver://sa:StrongPass123!@db-host\\SQLEXPRESS?database=orders\u0026encrypt=disable\n```\n*ADO.NET format:*\n```text\nserver=db-host\\SQLEXPRESS;user id=sa;password=StrongPass123!;database=orders;encrypt=disable;\n```\n\n#### Example 5: High-Throughput Bulk ETL (Max TDS Packet Size)\nTunes network TDS buffers from the 4KB default to 32KB (`32767` bytes) to maximize network throughput during large data warehouse bulk copies, and logs workstation identity:\n\n*URL format (spaces encoded as `+` or `%20`):*\n```text\nsqlserver://etl_operator:SecretPass2026!@sql-cluster.internal:1433?database=staging_area\u0026encrypt=true\u0026TrustServerCertificate=true\u0026packet+size=32767\u0026app+name=Flow_Bulk_Loader\u0026workstation+id=ETL-HOST-01\n```\n\n*Equivalent ADO.NET semi-colon format:*\n```text\nserver=sql-cluster.internal;port=1433;user id=etl_operator;password=SecretPass2026!;database=staging_area;encrypt=true;TrustServerCertificate=true;packet size=32767;app name=Flow_Bulk_Loader;workstation id=ETL-HOST-01;\n```\n\n*XML pipeline definition (note the escaped `\u0026amp;`):*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"bulk_staging_db\"\n          driver=\"sqlserver\"\n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://etl_operator:SecretPass2026!@sql-cluster.internal:1433?database=staging_area\u0026amp;encrypt=true\u0026amp;TrustServerCertificate=true\u0026amp;packet+size=32767\u0026amp;app+name=Flow_Bulk_Loader\u0026amp;workstation+id=ETL-HOST-01\"\n          max_open_conns=\"50\"\n          max_idle_conns=\"10\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 6: On-Premises Windows Integrated Authentication (Kerberos / NTLM)\nUses current on-premises Windows domain logon credentials without storing passwords in configuration files:\n```text\nserver=localhost;database=finance;integrated security=true;\n```\n\n#### Example 7: Multi-Subnet Failover (Always On Availability Groups)\nIn SQL Server Always On Availability Group (AG) architectures spanning multiple subnets or disaster recovery data centers, an AG listener registers multiple IP addresses in DNS. Setting `MultiSubnetFailover=true` instructs the driver to attempt parallel TCP connections to all resolved IP addresses simultaneously. Whichever replica answers first (the currently active primary or secondary node) wins the connection immediately—slashing failover discovery time from standard 20–30 second TCP timeout delays to milliseconds.\n\nCombine with `ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly` to offload analytical or reporting queries directly to secondary read-only replicas:\n\n*URL format:*\n```text\nsqlserver://app_etl:SecurePassword2026!@ag-listener.corp.internal:1433?database=enterprise_reporting\u0026multisubnetfailover=true\u0026applicationintent=ReadOnly\u0026encrypt=true\u0026TrustServerCertificate=true\n```\n\n*Equivalent ADO.NET semi-colon format:*\n```text\nserver=ag-listener.corp.internal;database=enterprise_reporting;user id=app_etl;password=SecurePassword2026!;MultiSubnetFailover=True;ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly;encrypt=true;TrustServerCertificate=true;\n```\n\n*XML pipeline definition:*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"ag_reporting_db\"\n          driver=\"sqlserver\"\n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://app_etl:SecurePassword2026!@ag-listener.corp.internal:1433?database=enterprise_reporting\u0026amp;multisubnetfailover=true\u0026amp;applicationintent=ReadOnly\u0026amp;encrypt=true\u0026amp;TrustServerCertificate=true\"\n          max_open_conns=\"20\"\n          max_idle_conns=\"5\"\n          workload=\"analytics\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 8: Multiple Active Result Sets (MARS)\n**Multiple Active Result Sets (MARS)** allows executing multiple batches or interleaving queries across a single physical database connection without buffering the entire result set in client memory before issuing another command on that connection.\n\n*URL format:*\n```text\nsqlserver://flow_app:OrderPass2026!@sql-srv.internal:1433?database=order_processing\u0026MultipleActiveResultSets=true\u0026encrypt=true\u0026TrustServerCertificate=true\n```\n\n*Equivalent ADO.NET semi-colon format:*\n```text\nserver=sql-srv.internal;database=order_processing;user id=flow_app;password=OrderPass2026!;MultipleActiveResultSets=True;encrypt=true;TrustServerCertificate=true;\n```\n\n*XML pipeline definition:*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"orders_mars_db\"\n          driver=\"sqlserver\"\n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://flow_app:OrderPass2026!@sql-srv.internal:1433?database=order_processing\u0026amp;MultipleActiveResultSets=true\u0026amp;encrypt=true\u0026amp;TrustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n```\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e **Go Concurrency vs. MARS**: In Go\u0027s `database/sql`, concurrent queries dispatched across goroutines automatically receive separate physical connections managed by the connection pool (`max_open_conns`). For single queries or stored procedures returning multiple result sets (e.g. `SELECT * FROM headers; SELECT * FROM lines;`), Go processes each result set sequentially using `rows.NextResultSet()` natively. Specifying `MultipleActiveResultSets=True` is primarily utilized when porting legacy .NET connection strings, connecting through proxies that require the MARS prelogin flag, or when integrating with frameworks expecting MARS-enabled endpoints.\n\n---\n\n### Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD) Connection Examples\n\n`go-flow` provides native support for **Microsoft Entra ID** authentication (via `github.com/microsoft/go-mssqldb/azuread`). You can specify the driver as either `azuresql` or `sqlserver` (Flow automatically routes connection strings containing `fedauth=` to the Entra ID token provider).\n\n#### Example 9: Microsoft Entra Integrated Authentication (Single Sign-On / SSO)\n**Microsoft Entra Integrated** (`fedauth=ActiveDirectoryIntegrated`) is designed for machines that are domain-joined to on-premises Active Directory federated with Microsoft Entra ID, or joined directly via Microsoft Entra Hybrid Join. It uses the active Windows session ticket via Kerberos/WS-Trust to seamlessly negotiate an Entra access token **without requiring passwords or secrets in configuration files**.\n\n*URL format:*\n```text\nsqlserver://my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=sales_analytics\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryIntegrated\u0026encrypt=true\n```\n\n*ADO.NET semi-colon format:*\n```text\nserver=my-sql-srv.database.windows.net;database=sales_analytics;fedauth=ActiveDirectoryIntegrated;encrypt=true;\n```\n\n*Standard ODBC / ADO.NET alias syntax:*\n```text\nserver=my-sql-srv.database.windows.net;database=sales_analytics;Authentication=Active Directory Integrated;encrypt=true;\n```\n\n*XML pipeline definition (using either `driver=\"azuresql\"` or `driver=\"sqlserver\"`):*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"sales_dw\"\n          driver=\"azuresql\"\n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=sales_analytics\u0026amp;fedauth=ActiveDirectoryIntegrated\u0026amp;encrypt=true\"\n          max_open_conns=\"25\"\n          max_idle_conns=\"5\"\n          workload=\"analytics\" /\u003e\n```\n\n*CLI execution:*\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe -dsn \"sqlserver://my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=sales_analytics\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryIntegrated\u0026encrypt=true\"\n```\n\n#### Example 10: Microsoft Entra Default (`DefaultAzureCredential`)\nUses the Azure SDK `DefaultAzureCredential` provider. It dynamically resolves credentials from:\n1. Environment variables (`AZURE_CLIENT_ID`, `AZURE_TENANT_ID`, `AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET`)\n2. Workload Identity (AKS / Kubernetes pod service accounts)\n3. Managed Identity (Azure VMs, Azure Container Apps, Azure App Services)\n4. Azure Developer CLI (`az login` or `azd login`)\n5. Visual Studio Code credentials\n\n*URL format:*\n```text\nsqlserver://my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=enterprise_dw\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryDefault\u0026encrypt=true\n```\n\n*XML pipeline definition:*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"azure_dw\"\n          driver=\"azuresql\"\n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=enterprise_dw\u0026amp;fedauth=ActiveDirectoryDefault\u0026amp;encrypt=true\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 11: Microsoft Entra Managed Identity (System-Assigned \u0026 User-Assigned MSI)\nIdeal for automated workloads running on Azure Virtual Machines, Azure Functions, Azure Container Instances (ACI), or Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS).\n\n*System-Assigned Managed Identity:*\n```text\nsqlserver://my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=finance\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryMSI\u0026encrypt=true\n```\n\n*User-Assigned Managed Identity (specifying Client ID):*\n```text\nsqlserver://my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=finance\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryMSI\u0026user+id=11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555\u0026encrypt=true\n```\n\n*User-Assigned Managed Identity (specifying Resource ID):*\n```text\nserver=my-sql-srv.database.windows.net;database=finance;fedauth=ActiveDirectoryMSI;resource id=/subscriptions/sub-id/resourceGroups/rg-name/providers/Microsoft.ManagedIdentity/userAssignedIdentities/my-identity;encrypt=true;\n```\n\n#### Example 12: Microsoft Entra Service Principal (Headless CI/CD \u0026 Automated ETL)\nUsed when ETL pipelines run unattended outside Azure (e.g. on-premises data centers, AWS, GCP, or automated Jenkins/GitHub Actions runners).\n\n*With Client Secret:*\n```text\nserver=my-sql-srv.database.windows.net;database=warehouse;fedauth=ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal;user id=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000@tenant-uuid-1234;password=YourClientSecretKey!;encrypt=true;\n```\n*URL equivalent:*\n```text\nsqlserver://00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000@tenant-uuid-1234:YourClientSecretKey!@my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=warehouse\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal\u0026encrypt=true\n```\n\n*With Client Certificate (mTLS with Microsoft Entra):*\n```text\nserver=my-sql-srv.database.windows.net;database=warehouse;fedauth=ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal;user id=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000@tenant-uuid-1234;clientcertpath=/etc/ssl/certs/service_principal.pem;encrypt=true;\n```\n\n#### Example 13: Microsoft Entra Interactive (Browser MFA Prompt)\nPrompts the operator with an interactive browser or MFA authenticator prompt. Recommended for local developer workstations using Builder UI:\n```text\nsqlserver://admin@company.com@my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=dev_db\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryInteractive\u0026applicationclientid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\u0026encrypt=true\n```\n\n#### Example 14: Microsoft Entra Password (ROPC Flow)\nAuthenticates with an Entra username and password directly (non-MFA accounts only):\n```text\nsqlserver://user@company.com:MyEntraPass2026!@my-sql-srv.database.windows.net?database=staging\u0026fedauth=ActiveDirectoryPassword\u0026applicationclientid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\u0026encrypt=true\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. SQLite\n\n`go-flow` utilizes `modernc.org/sqlite`, a 100% pure Go SQLite engine that requires **no CGO and no external C compiler**. Connections are specified using standard filesystem paths or URI syntax.\n\n### Available Options \u0026 Pragmas Reference\n\n| Parameter / Pragma | Type | Allowed Values / Format | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| `mode` | string | `ro` (read-only)\u003cbr\u003e`rw` (read-write)\u003cbr\u003e`rwc` (read-write-create)\u003cbr\u003e`memory` (in-memory) | Access mode for opening database files. `rwc` creates the file if it does not already exist. |\n| `cache` | string | `shared`, `private` | Controls shared-cache mode among multiple connections within the same process. |\n| `immutable` | integer | `1`, `0` | Informs SQLite that the underlying file is on read-only media and will never be modified. Skips all locking and shared memory. |\n| `_pragma=journal_mode(...)` | pragma | `WAL`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`, `MEMORY`, `OFF` | `WAL` (Write-Ahead Logging) provides maximum concurrency, allowing readers and writers to operate simultaneously without locking. |\n| `_pragma=synchronous(...)` | pragma | `NORMAL`, `FULL`, `OFF` | In WAL mode, `NORMAL` ensures database durability across application crashes while dramatically speeding up write transactions. |\n| `_pragma=busy_timeout(...)` | pragma | Milliseconds (e.g. `5000`) | Sleep time before returning `SQLITE_BUSY` when waiting for another write lock to release. |\n| `_pragma=foreign_keys(...)` | pragma | `1` (on), `0` (off) | Enforces relational foreign key constraints. (SQLite defaults to off). |\n| `_pragma=temp_store(...)` | pragma | `MEMORY`, `FILE`, `DEFAULT` | Stores temporary tables and scratch query sorting indexes in RAM instead of disk files. |\n| `_pragma=cache_size(...)` | pragma | Negative integer (e.g. `-64000` = 64MB) | Sets the memory page cache allocated to the connection handle. |\n| `_pragma=locking_mode(...)` | pragma | `NORMAL`, `EXCLUSIVE` | `EXCLUSIVE` retains database file locks for the lifetime of the connection to maximize write throughput in single-process ETL. |\n| `_pragma=mmap_size(...)` | pragma | Bytes (e.g. `268435456` = 256MB) | Enables memory-mapped I/O up to the specified size for ultra-fast reading. |\n\n### SQLite Connection Examples\n\n#### Example 1: High-Performance Production File (WAL Mode)\nConfigures Write-Ahead Logging, 5-second lock timeout, RAM temp storage, and 64MB memory page cache:\n```text\nfile:./data/production.db?cache=shared\u0026mode=rwc\u0026_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)\u0026_pragma=synchronous(NORMAL)\u0026_pragma=busy_timeout(5000)\u0026_pragma=temp_store(MEMORY)\u0026_pragma=cache_size(-64000)\u0026_pragma=foreign_keys(1)\n```\n*XML pipeline syntax (note the escaped `\u0026amp;`):*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"local_warehouse\"\n          driver=\"sqlite\"\n          connection_string=\"file:./data/production.db?cache=shared\u0026amp;mode=rwc\u0026amp;_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)\u0026amp;_pragma=synchronous(NORMAL)\u0026amp;_pragma=busy_timeout(5000)\u0026amp;_pragma=temp_store(MEMORY)\u0026amp;_pragma=cache_size(-64000)\u0026amp;_pragma=foreign_keys(1)\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 2: Simple Local File Path\nCreates or connects to a local database file using default SQLite settings:\n```text\n./data/application.db\n```\n*On Windows:*\n```text\nC:\\etl\\databases\\application.db\n```\n\n#### Example 3: Shared In-Memory Database (Ephemeral)\nLives entirely in memory, persists across pipeline steps for the lifetime of the process, and is shared among all connection pool threads:\n```text\nfile::memory:?cache=shared\n```\n\n#### Example 4: Isolated In-Memory Database\n```text\n:memory:\n```\n\n#### Example 5: Read-Only Immutable Database\nOpens an existing reference database in strict read-only mode, bypassing all file lock overhead (ideal for Docker containers, read-only NFS volumes, or distribution bundles):\n```text\nfile:/opt/data/geo_reference.db?mode=ro\u0026immutable=1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Oracle Database\n\nThe Oracle driver (`github.com/sijms/go-ora/v2`) is a **pure Go Oracle client implementation**. It connects directly to Oracle Database instances over TCP/IP without requiring Oracle Instant Client, CGO, or `libclntsh.so`.\n\n### Available Options Reference\n\n| Parameter | Type | Allowed Values / Format | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| `CONNECTION TIMEOUT` / `timeout` | integer | Seconds (e.g. `10`) | TCP dial timeout before aborting connection attempts. |\n| `SSL` | string | `ENABLE`, `TRUE`, `FALSE` | Enables SSL/TLS transport encryption (TCPS protocol). |\n| `SSL VERIFY` | string | `TRUE`, `FALSE` | Validates server SSL certificate validity. Set to `FALSE` for self-signed certificates. |\n| `WALLET` | path | Path to Oracle Cloud Wallet (`cwallet.sso` or `.zip`) | Points to client credentials wallet for Oracle Cloud Autonomous Databases (ATP / ADW). |\n| `SERVER` | string | `DEDICATED`, `SHARED` | Instructs the Oracle TNS listener to assign a dedicated server process. |\n| `AUTH TYPE` | string | `DEFAULT`, `SYSDBA`, `SYSOPER` | Privileged administrative logon mode. |\n| `PREFETCH_ROWS` | integer | Number of rows (e.g. `1000`) | Number of rows prefetched per network round-trip. **Essential for large ETL SELECT queries.** |\n| `CLIENT IDENTIFIER` | string | Text identifier (e.g. `flow_etl`) | Client identity stored in `V$SESSION` for enterprise database auditing. |\n| `TRACE FILE` | path | File path (e.g. `./oracle_trace.log`) | Dumps internal TDS protocol packet traces for low-level debugging. |\n\n### Oracle Connection Examples\n\n#### Example 1: Standard Service Name Connection\nConnects to an Oracle Pluggable Database (PDB) via Service Name:\n```text\noracle://app_user:SecretPassword123@localhost:1521/XEPDB1\n```\n\n#### Example 2: Legacy SID (System Identifier) Connection\nConnects using an Oracle SID (e.g. `ORCL`):\n```text\noracle://system:ManagerPassword@db-host.internal:1521/ORCL\n```\n\n#### Example 3: High-Performance Bulk ETL with Prefetching\nConfigures dedicated server processes, 10-second timeout, and 2,500 row network prefetch buffers:\n```text\noracle://etl_reader:ComplexPass999!@oracle-cluster.internal:1521/PROD_SVC?SERVER=DEDICATED\u0026CONNECTION+TIMEOUT=10\u0026PREFETCH_ROWS=2500\u0026CLIENT+IDENTIFIER=Flow_ETL_Runner\n```\n*XML pipeline syntax (note the escaped `\u0026amp;`):*\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"oracle_dw\"\n          driver=\"oracle\"\n          connection_string=\"oracle://etl_reader:ComplexPass999!@oracle-cluster.internal:1521/PROD_SVC?SERVER=DEDICATED\u0026amp;CONNECTION+TIMEOUT=10\u0026amp;PREFETCH_ROWS=2500\u0026amp;CLIENT+IDENTIFIER=Flow_ETL_Runner\" /\u003e\n```\n\n#### Example 4: Oracle Autonomous Cloud Database (ATP / ADW via Wallet)\nConnects securely to Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OCI) using mutual TLS and the client wallet bundle:\n```text\noracle://admin:MyCloudPassword123@adb.us-ashburn-1.oraclecloud.com:1522/myatp_high?SSL=ENABLE\u0026WALLET=/etc/oracle/wallets/Wallet_myatp.zip\n```\n\n#### Example 5: EZCONNECT Format\n```text\nscott/tiger@localhost:1521/XEPDB1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Key-Value Storage Engines\n\nIn addition to relational SQL databases, `go-flow` includes first-class support for embedded and distributed Key-Value (KV) storage engines.\n\n### Redis \u0026 Valkey\n\nSupports single-node instances, managed Redis caches (e.g. AWS ElastiCache, Azure Redis, Google Memorystore), and TLS-encrypted clusters.\n\n#### Format:\n```text\nredis://[:password@]host[:port][/db_index][?param1=val1\u0026param2=val2]\n```\n*For TLS / SSL connections:*\n```text\nrediss://[:password@]host[:port][/db_index][?param1=val1\u0026param2=val2]\n```\n\n#### Examples:\n```text\n# Local unauthenticated Redis instance (Database 0)\nredis://localhost:6379/0\n\n# Authenticated Redis with password, custom database, and timeouts\nredis://:SecretRedisPass2026!@redis.internal:6379/2?dial_timeout=5s\u0026read_timeout=3s\u0026write_timeout=3s\n\n# Encrypted Redis over TLS (rediss://)\nrediss://:SecurePass@redis-cache.us-east-1.cache.amazonaws.com:6379/0?dial_timeout=10s\n```\n\n---\n\n### bbolt (Embedded KV)\n\n`bbolt` is an embedded, transactional key-value store stored in a single binary file.\n\n#### Format:\n```text\n/path/to/database.db[?timeout=duration\u0026mode=octal]\n```\n\n#### Examples:\n```text\n# Standard local cache database file\n./data/kv_cache.db\n\n# With explicit lock acquisition timeout\n./data/kv_cache.db?timeout=2s\n```\n\n---\n\n### BadgerDB (Embedded LSM-Tree KV)\n\n`BadgerDB` is an embedded Key-Value engine optimized for solid-state storage (SSDs) using Log-Structured Merge (LSM) trees.\n\n#### Format:\n```text\n/path/to/storage_directory[?sync_writes=bool]\n```\n*For in-memory ephemeral KV store:*\n```text\nbadger://:memory:\n```\n\n#### Examples:\n```text\n# Local directory on SSD with asynchronous write-flushing for maximum ingestion throughput\n./data/badger_store?sync_writes=false\n\n# In-memory ephemeral KV store\nbadger://:memory:\n```\n\n---\n\n### etcd (Distributed KV v3)\n\nSupports distributed configuration and consensus lookups across multi-node clusters.\n\n#### Format:\n```text\nhttp://endpoint1:port,http://endpoint2:port\n```\n*With client TLS certificates:*\n```text\nhttps://etcd1.internal:2379,https://etcd2.internal:2379?ca_cert=/path/to/ca.pem\u0026cert=/path/to/cert.pem\u0026key=/path/to/key.pem\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Flow Pipeline Configuration Integration\n\n### Defining Databases in `\u003cpipeline\u003e` XML\n\nDatabases are registered in the `\u003cdatabases\u003e` block of a pipeline definition. Connection strings support **variable interpolation** (e.g. `{{DB_PASSWORD}}` or `{{DB_HOST}}`), enabling credentials to be injected securely at runtime without hardcoding.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003c!-- 1. PostgreSQL Data Warehouse --\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"dw_postgres\"\n                  driver=\"postgres\"\n                  connection_string=\"postgresql://etl_app:{{PG_PASSWORD}}@postgres.internal:5432/warehouse?sslmode=require\u0026amp;connect_timeout=10\"\n                  max_open_conns=\"50\"\n                  max_idle_conns=\"10\"\n                  conn_max_lifetime_seconds=\"300\"\n                  workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- 2. SQL Server Source --\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"source_mssql\"\n                  driver=\"sqlserver\"\n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://sa:{{MSSQL_PASSWORD}}@sql-srv.internal:1433?database=Orders\u0026amp;encrypt=true\u0026amp;TrustServerCertificate=true\u0026amp;packet+size=32767\"\n                  max_open_conns=\"25\"\n                  max_idle_conns=\"5\"\n                  workload=\"oltp\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- 3. Local High-Throughput SQLite Staging --\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"staging_sqlite\"\n                  driver=\"sqlite\"\n                  connection_string=\"file:./scratch/staging.db?cache=shared\u0026amp;mode=rwc\u0026amp;_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)\u0026amp;_pragma=synchronous(NORMAL)\u0026amp;_pragma=busy_timeout(10000)\"\n                  max_open_conns=\"1\"\n                  workload=\"batch\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Steps referencing registered databases by name --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"extract_orders\" db=\"source_mssql\"\u003e\n            SELECT order_id, customer_id, total_amount FROM orders WHERE status = \u0027PENDING\u0027;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Telemetry \u0026 Execution Logging Database (`log_db`)\n\nWhen a database element is named `log_db`, `go-flow` automatically directs all pipeline execution telemetry, step events, row counts, and database SPID tracking to that database:\n\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"log_db\"\n          driver=\"sqlserver\"\n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://telemetry_user:{{LOG_PASSWORD}}@telemetry.internal:1433?database=pipeline_telemetry\u0026amp;encrypt=true\u0026amp;TrustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Loading Pipelines Directly from Databases (`sql://` / `db://`)\n\n`go-flow` can fetch pipeline XML definitions dynamically from a database repository using the `sql://` source prefix:\n\n```text\nsql://\u003cdriver\u003e@\u003cconnection_string\u003e#\u003cSQL_SELECT_QUERY\u003e\n```\n\n#### Examples:\n```powershell\n# From PostgreSQL\n.\\flow.exe -source \"sql://postgres@postgresql://app:SecretPass@localhost:5432/pipelines?sslmode=disable#SELECT xml_content FROM pipeline_catalog WHERE pipeline_name = \u0027daily_sales\u0027\"\n\n# From SQL Server\n.\\flow.exe -source \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:SecretPass!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026encrypt=disable#SELECT definition_xml FROM dbo.etl_scripts WHERE id = 42\"\n\n# From SQLite\n.\\flow.exe -source \"sql://sqlite@file:./repos/scripts.db?mode=ro#SELECT xml_body FROM registry WHERE name = \u0027extract_orders\u0027\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Security Best Practices for Connection Strings\n\n1. **Never Hardcode Plaintext Passwords in Version Control**:\n   - Use variable substitution: `connection_string=\"postgresql://app:{{SECRET_DB_PASS}}@host:5432/db\"`.\n   - Pass runtime values using `-var SECRET_DB_PASS=MyPassword` or environment variables `FLOW_VAR_SECRET_DB_PASS=MyPassword`.\n2. **Encrypt Options and Pipeline Files**:\n   - Use `crypto_tool` to encrypt options and pipeline files with AES-256-GCM.\n   - Supply decryption keys via `-key-file` to prevent credentials from being exposed in system process listings (`ps` or Process Hacker).\n3. **Always Escape Ampersands in XML**:\n   - Remember that XML parsers reject bare `\u0026` characters in attribute values. Always write `\u0026amp;` when joining query parameters inside `\u003cdatabase connection_string=\"...\" /\u003e`.\n4. **Enforce TLS Verification in Production**:\n   - For PostgreSQL: Use `sslmode=verify-full` or `sslmode=verify-ca`.\n   - For MySQL: Use `tls=true` or registered CA bundles.\n   - For SQL Server: Use `encrypt=true` and avoid `TrustServerCertificate=true` in untrusted networks.\n5. **Tune Connection Pool Sizes for ETL**:\n   - Avoid setting `max_open_conns` excessively high when executing parallel pipelines against shared databases.\n   - For SQLite, set `max_open_conns=\"1\"` or use WAL mode (`_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)`) to avoid database lock contention.\n","db_select_template_stdio.md":"# Database Query to Template and Standard I/O (`os.stdio`) Output\n\nThis guide explains how to query a database, format result rows using the `\u003ctemplate\u003e` engine, and stream the rendered output directly to standard output (`os.stdio` / `os.Stdout`) in Flow.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern Overview\n\nThe **Query $\\rightarrow$ Template $\\rightarrow$ Stdio** pattern decouples data extraction, presentation formatting, and stream writing:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    DB[(Database)] --\u003e|Streaming Cursor| Foreach[\"\u0026lt;foreach\u0026gt;\u003cbr/\u003eRow Iteration\"]\n    Foreach --\u003e|Exposes Row Columns as Variables| Template[\"\u0026lt;template\u0026gt;\u003cbr/\u003eGo text/template Engine\"]\n    Template --\u003e|Writes to output_var| Script[\"\u0026lt;script language=\u0027go\u0027\u0026gt;\u003cbr/\u003ehost/vars \u0026amp; os.Stdout\"]\n    Script --\u003e|Direct Stream| Stdio[\"Standard Output\u003cbr/\u003e(os.stdio / Console)\"]\n```\n\n1. **Database Cursor (`\u003cforeach\u003e`)**: Executes the SQL query and processes rows iteratively with constant $O(1)$ RAM usage.\n2. **Variable Binding**: In each loop iteration, all columns in the row (e.g. `id`, `name`, `salary`) are automatically exposed as in-scope pipeline variables.\n3. **Template Formatting (`\u003ctemplate\u003e`)**: The row variables are interpolated into custom text formats using Go\u0027s `text/template` engine and saved to a target variable (`output_var`).\n4. **Standard I/O Writing (`\u003cscript\u003e`)**: An embedded Go script imports `\"host/vars\"` and `\"os\"` to write the formatted string directly to `os.Stdout`.\n\n---\n\n## Complete Pipeline Example\n\nThe pipeline below is available in the repository at [`examples/db_select_template_stdio.xml`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/examples/db_select_template_stdio.xml).\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- 1. Pipeline Variables --\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DBPath\" type=\"string\" value=\"./demo_employees.db\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"MinSalary\" type=\"float\" value=\"100000.00\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- 2. Database Connection Definition\n         Uses SQLite for an immediately runnable, zero-dependency local example.\n         For other engines, see the connection string section below.\n    --\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"sample_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"{{DBPath}}\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- 3. Setup Sample Table and Data (Self-contained demonstration) --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"InitSampleData\" db=\"sample_db\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n                CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS employees (\n                    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n                    name TEXT NOT NULL,\n                    department TEXT NOT NULL,\n                    salary REAL NOT NULL\n                );\n                DELETE FROM employees;\n                INSERT INTO employees (id, name, department, salary) VALUES\n                    (101, \u0027Alice Smith\u0027, \u0027Engineering\u0027, 125000.50),\n                    (102, \u0027Bob Jones\u0027, \u0027Data Science\u0027, 118000.00),\n                    (103, \u0027Carol White\u0027, \u0027DevOps\u0027, 132500.75);\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- 4. Query Database and Iterate Over Rows in a Streaming Foreach Loop --\u003e\n        \u003cforeach id=\"SelectEmployees\" db=\"sample_db\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n                SELECT id, name, department, salary\n                FROM employees\n                WHERE salary \u003e= {{MinSalary}}\n                ORDER BY id;\n            ]]\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- 5. Render Row Data into a Variable Using Go text/template --\u003e\n            \u003ctemplate id=\"FormatEmployeeRow\" output_var=\"FormattedRow\"\u003e\n==================================================\nEmployee ID: {{.id}}\nName:        {{.name}}\nDepartment:  {{.department}}\nSalary:      ${{.salary}}\n==================================================\n            \u003c/template\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- 6. Print Formatted Output Directly to Standard Output (os.stdio / os.Stdout) --\u003e\n            \u003cscript id=\"PrintToStdio\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                \u003c![CDATA[\n                package main\n\n                import (\n                    \"fmt\"\n                    \"os\"\n                    \"host/vars\"\n                )\n\n                func main() {\n                    // Fetch the rendered template text from pipeline variables\n                    rowText := vars.GetString(\"FormattedRow\")\n\n                    // Output directly to os.Stdout (os.stdio)\n                    fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, rowText)\n                }\n                ]]\u003e\n            \u003c/script\u003e\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## Component Breakdown\n\n### 1. Database Configuration (`\u003cdatabases\u003e`)\n\nThe example uses SQLite for immediate, zero-dependency execution. You can target any supported database engine by updating the `driver` and `connection_string` attributes:\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- SQL Server --\u003e\n\u003cdatabase name=\"sample_db\" driver=\"sqlserver\" \n          connection_string=\"sqlserver://dbhost:1433?database=Production\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\u0026amp;trustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- PostgreSQL --\u003e\n\u003cdatabase name=\"sample_db\" driver=\"postgres\" \n          connection_string=\"postgres://app_user:secret@localhost:5432/production?sslmode=disable\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- MySQL --\u003e\n\u003cdatabase name=\"sample_db\" driver=\"mysql\" \n          connection_string=\"app_user:secret@tcp(localhost:3306)/production\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- Oracle --\u003e\n\u003cdatabase name=\"sample_db\" driver=\"oracle\" \n          connection_string=\"oracle://app_user:secret@localhost:1521/ORCLPDB1\" /\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. Streaming Query (`\u003cforeach\u003e`)\n\nThe `\u003cforeach\u003e` container executes the SQL query in streaming mode:\n\n* Query results are streamed row-by-row rather than loading the entire result set into memory.\n* Columns returned by the `SELECT` clause are automatically registered as current-row variables accessible inside the loop body.\n* Variable interpolation (`{{MinSalary}}`) allows parameterized filtering directly within the query.\n\n### 3. Template Engine (`\u003ctemplate\u003e`)\n\nThe `\u003ctemplate\u003e` node leverages Go\u0027s standard `text/template` library:\n\n* **Variable Access**: Row columns are accessed using dot notation (`{{.id}}`, `{{.name}}`, `{{.department}}`, `{{.salary}}`).\n* **Conditionals \u0026 Logic**: Native Go template directives such as `{{if .department}}...{{else}}...{{end}}` can be used.\n* **`output_var`**: Defines the pipeline variable where the rendered text string is stored.\n\n### 4. Printing to `os.stdio` (`\u003cscript language=\"go\"\u003e`)\n\nThe embedded Go script provides direct access to operating system file descriptors and standard streams:\n\n* **`\"host/vars\"`**: Built-in host integration package that exposes pipeline variables to the script runtime. Use `vars.GetString(\"FormattedRow\")` to retrieve the template output.\n* **`\"os\"` \u0026 `\"fmt\"`**: Standard Go packages. `fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, rowText)` or `os.Stdout.WriteString(rowText + \"\\n\")` flushes the rendered text to standard output (`os.stdio`).\n\n---\n\n## Alternative Script Languages\n\nWhile Go is fast and portable with zero external interpreter dependencies, the print step can also be written in other supported scripting languages:\n\n### PowerShell\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"PrintStdio\" language=\"powershell\"\u003e\n    Write-Host \"{{FormattedRow}}\"\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n### Bash / Linux Shell\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"PrintStdio\" language=\"bash\"\u003e\n    echo \"$FormattedRow\"\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n### Windows Command Prompt (`cmd`)\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"PrintStdio\" language=\"cmd\"\u003e\n    echo {{FormattedRow}}\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## Execution Instructions\n\n### Running from the Command Line\n\nRun the example using `flow.exe`:\n\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe -file .\\examples\\db_select_template_stdio.xml\n```\n\n### Overriding Parameters at Runtime\n\nOverride variables on the command line without modifying the XML file:\n\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe -file .\\examples\\db_select_template_stdio.xml -vars \"MinSalary=125000.00\"\n```\n\n### Console Output\n\n```text\n==================================================\nEmployee ID: 101\nName:        Alice Smith\nDepartment:  Engineering\nSalary:      $125000.5\n==================================================\n==================================================\nEmployee ID: 102\nName:        Bob Jones\nDepartment:  Data Science\nSalary:      $118000\n==================================================\n==================================================\nEmployee ID: 103\nName:        Carol White\nDepartment:  DevOps\nSalary:      $132500.75\n==================================================\n```\n\n---\n\n## Best Practices\n\n\u003e [!TIP]\n\u003e **Use CDATA Blocks for SQL \u0026 Scripts**: Wrapping queries and script code inside `\u003c![CDATA[ ... ]]\u003e` avoids XML entity escaping issues with characters like `\u003c`, `\u003e`, `\u0026`, or `\u0026\u0026`.\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e **Memory Efficiency**: The `\u003cforeach\u003e` node uses a streaming cursor by default. Even when iterating over millions of rows, memory consumption remains constant ($O(1)$).\n\n\u003e [!IMPORTANT]\n\u003e **Case Sensitivity**: Database column names exposed as variables within Go templates match the case returned by the database driver. For consistency across database engines (e.g. Oracle returning uppercase identifiers), use explicit SQL column aliases (e.g. `SELECT id AS id, name AS name ...`).\n","excel_read.md":"# Overview of the `\u003cexcel_read\u003e` in Flow\n\nThe `\u003cexcel_read\u003e` node in `flow` enables direct integration with `.xlsx` workbooks. You can extract data from Excel into JSON for APIs for downstream processing.\n\n## Key Attributes\n\n### `\u003cexcel_read\u003e`\n* **`file`**: Target path of the `.xlsx` file to read. Supports `{{VarName}}` interpolation.\n* **`sheet`**: (Optional) The name of the worksheet to read. Defaults to the first sheet if omitted.\n* **`header`**: (Optional) Boolean (`true` / `false`). If `true` (default), the first row is treated as keys for the resulting JSON.\n* **`output_var` / `var`**: The pipeline variable where the extracted JSON string will be stored.\n---\n\n## Examples\n\n### 1. Reading Excel Data into a JSON API Payload\nExtract configuration or bulk upload data from an Excel sheet and send it to an external API endpoint.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"FILE_PATH\" type=\"string\" value=\"./uploads/new_users.xlsx\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Reads the \"Users\" sheet into JSON (using row 1 as keys) --\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_read file=\"{{FILE_PATH}}\" sheet=\"Users\" output_var=\"USER_JSON_DATA\" /\u003e\n        \n        \u003csql_bulk \n            method=\"POST\" \n            uri=\"https://api.example.com/v1/users/bulk\" \n            content_type=\"application/json\" \n            data=\"{{USER_JSON_DATA}}\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. Reading Excel Data Without Headers\nSometimes data files don\u0027t have headers. You can set `header=\"false\"` to process the raw rows programmatically in a later script.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_read file=\"./raw_data/metrics.xlsx\" sheet=\"RawMetrics\" header=\"false\" output_var=\"RAW_DATA\" /\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- Process the array of arrays in a Go script --\u003e\n        \u003cscript lang=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"encoding/json\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                rawData := vars.GetString(\"RAW_DATA\")\n                fmt.Printf(\"Successfully loaded raw data: %d bytes\\n\", len(rawData))\n                // Add custom JSON unmarshaling and validation here\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","excel_write.md":"# Overview of the `\u003cexcel_write\u003e` Node in Flow\n\nThe `\u003cexcel_write\u003e` node in `flow` enables direct integration with `.xlsx` workbooks. You can extract data from Excel into JSON for APIs or downstream processing, and you can export database queries directly into styled, native Excel files.\n\n## Key Attributes\n\n### `\u003cexcel_write\u003e`\n* **`file`**: Target path on the local filesystem to save the `.xlsx` file. Supports interpolation.\n* **`sheet`**: (Optional) Name of the worksheet where data will be written. Defaults to `Sheet1`.\n* **`db`**: (Optional) Database connection name to execute the inline query against.\n* **Inline Content**: The SQL query used to pull data from the database and populate the Excel file.\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\n### 1. Simple Database Export to Excel\nExtract a report directly from a SQL database and save it as a native `.xlsx` file.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"erp_db\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://user:pass@localhost:1433\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_write file=\"./exports/customer_list.xlsx\" sheet=\"Customers\" db=\"erp_db\"\u003e\n            SELECT id, first_name, last_name, email, created_at \n            FROM dbo.customers \n            WHERE active = 1\n            ORDER BY created_at DESC;\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n    \n### 2. Dynamic Excel Generation with Variables\nUse pipeline variables to dynamically name the output file and filter the SQL query.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"REPORT_MONTH\" type=\"string\" value=\"August_2026\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DEPT_ID\" type=\"int\" value=\"42\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- The file path and query both use template variables --\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_write file=\"./reports/finance/Expenses_{{REPORT_MONTH}}.xlsx\" sheet=\"Expenses\" db=\"finance_db\"\u003e\n            SELECT expense_id, amount, category, date_filed \n            FROM dbo.expenses \n            WHERE department_id = {{DEPT_ID}} \n              AND FORMAT(date_filed, \u0027MMMM_yyyy\u0027) = \u0027{{REPORT_MONTH}}\u0027\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n    \n### 3. Multi-Sheet Export (Using Parallel execution)\nExtract multiple datasets simultaneously from the database into different Excel files using the `\u003cparallel\u003e` node.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cparallel max_threads=\"3\"\u003e\n            \u003cexcel_write file=\"./exports/dashboard/Sales.xlsx\" sheet=\"Sales_Data\" db=\"warehouse\"\u003e\n                SELECT * FROM mart.fct_sales WHERE year = 2026;\n            \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n            \n            \u003cexcel_write file=\"./exports/dashboard/Inventory.xlsx\" sheet=\"Stock\" db=\"warehouse\"\u003e\n                SELECT item_code, quantity, warehouse_loc FROM mart.dim_inventory;\n            \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n            \n            \u003cexcel_write file=\"./exports/dashboard/Employees.xlsx\" sheet=\"HR_Data\" db=\"warehouse\"\u003e\n                SELECT emp_id, department, status FROM mart.dim_employees;\n            \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 4. Adding different tabs to the same Excel file\nYou can create multiple sheets within a single Excel file by specifying different `sheet` attributes in multiple `\u003cexcel_write\u003e` nodes pointing to the same file.\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Monthly Sales and Inventory Report Pipeline\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"sales_db\" \n                  driver=\"postgres\" \n                  connection_string=\"host=localhost port=5432 user=postgres password=secret dbname=sales sslmode=disable\" \n                  description=\"Primary Analytics Database\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Tab 1: Executive Summary --\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"export_summary\" \n                     file=\"reports/Monthly_Executive_Report.xlsx\" \n                     sheet=\"Summary KPI\" \n                     db=\"sales_db\"\n                     description=\"Writes top-level monthly KPIs to the first tab\"\u003e\n            SELECT \n                DATE_TRUNC(\u0027month\u0027, order_date) AS month,\n                COUNT(order_id) AS total_orders,\n                SUM(total_amount) AS total_revenue\n            FROM orders\n            GROUP BY 1\n            ORDER BY 1 DESC;\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Tab 2: Customer Breakdown --\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"export_customers\" \n                     file=\"reports/Monthly_Executive_Report.xlsx\" \n                     sheet=\"Customer Details\" \n                     db=\"sales_db\"\n                     description=\"Appends customer breakdown to a second tab\"\u003e\n            SELECT \n                customer_id,\n                company_name,\n                country,\n                total_spent\n            FROM customer_summary\n            WHERE total_spent \u003e 10000;\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Tab 3: Regional Inventory --\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"export_inventory\" \n                     file=\"reports/Monthly_Executive_Report.xlsx\" \n                     sheet=\"Inventory Status\" \n                     db=\"sales_db\"\n                     description=\"Appends inventory levels to a third tab\"\u003e\n            SELECT \n                warehouse_id,\n                product_sku,\n                stock_on_hand,\n                reorder_level\n            FROM warehouse_inventory;\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","file_read.md":"# Overview of the `\u003cfile_read\u003e` Node in Flow\n\nThe `\u003cfile_read\u003e` in flow allow you to interact directly with the local filesystem. You can read configuration files or datasets into pipeline variables.\n\n## Key Attributes\n\n### `\u003cfile_read\u003e`\n* **`file` / `path` / `filename`**: Target path of the file to load. Supports `{{VarName}}` interpolation.\n* **`output_var` / `var`**: The pipeline variable where the file\u0027s contents will be stored as a string.\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\n### 1. Reading a JSON Config and Sending it via HTTP\nLoad a configuration file into memory and POST it to a remote endpoint.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cscripts\u003e\n        \u003cfile_read file=\"./data/payload.json\" output_var=\"JSON_DATA\" /\u003e\n        \n        \u003csql_bulk \n            method=\"POST\" \n            uri=\"https://api.example.com/sync\" \n            content_type=\"application/json\" \n            data=\"{{JSON_DATA}}\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/scripts\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. Reading a SQL Query from File and Executing It\nKeep your SQL scripts organized in files, load them dynamically, and execute them on your database.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"primary_db\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://user:pass@localhost:1433\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n    \u003cscripts\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Read SQL text into variable QUERY_TEXT --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_read file=\"./queries/nightly_cleanup.sql\" output_var=\"QUERY_TEXT\" /\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- Execute the SQL query --\u003e\n        \u003csql db=\"primary_db\" var=\"QUERY_TEXT\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/scripts\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 3. Modifying and Resaving a File using Scripts\nRead an existing file, process or modify it using a script (like Go, Bash, or PowerShell), and save the results to a new file.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cscripts\u003e\n        \u003c!-- 1. Read input CSV --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_read file=\"./data/raw_users.csv\" output_var=\"RAW_CSV\" /\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- 2. Process it via shell script, outputting to CLEAN_CSV --\u003e\n        \u003cscript lang=\"bash\" output_var=\"CLEAN_CSV\"\u003e\n            # Using grep to remove empty lines and invalid data\n            echo \"$RAW_CSV\" | grep -v \"^$\" | grep -i \"@example.com\"\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- 3. Save processed results to a new file --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_save file=\"./data/clean_users.csv\" var=\"CLEAN_CSV\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/scripts\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","file_save.md":"# Overview of the `\u003cfile_save\u003e` Node in Flow\n\nThe `\u003cfile_save\u003e` node in `flow` allows you to interact directly with the local filesystem. You can write generated templates, script outputs, or log data back to disk.\n\n## Key Attributes\n\n### `\u003cfile_save\u003e`\n* **`file` / `path` / `filename`**: Target path on the local filesystem. Supports `{{VarName}}` interpolation.\n* **`var` / `variable`**: (Optional) Source variable containing the text to write. If omitted, the inline text inside the element is used.\n* **`append`**: (Optional) Boolean (`true` / `false`). If `true`, text is appended; if `false` (default), the file is overwritten. \n\n*Note: `\u003cfile_save\u003e` automatically creates missing parent directories.*\n---\n\n## Examples\n    \n### 1. Rendering a Template and Saving the Output\nGenerate a dynamic HTML report using the `\u003ctemplate\u003e` node and save the resulting variable to disk.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"REPORT_DATE\" type=\"string\" value=\"2026-08-25\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cscripts\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Generate HTML content into REPORT_HTML --\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"gen_report\" output_var=\"REPORT_HTML\"\u003e\n            \u003chtml\u003e\n                \u003cbody\u003e\n                    \u003ch1\u003eDaily Report: {{.REPORT_DATE}}\u003c/h1\u003e\n                    \u003cp\u003eAll systems operational.\u003c/p\u003e\n                \u003c/body\u003e\n            \u003c/html\u003e\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- Save the HTML to an output directory --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_save file=\"./output/reports/daily_{{REPORT_DATE}}.html\" var=\"REPORT_HTML\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/scripts\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n    \n### 2. Modifying and Resaving a File using Scripts\nRead an existing file, process or modify it using a script (like Go, Bash, or PowerShell), and save the results to a new file.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cscripts\u003e\n        \u003c!-- 1. Read input CSV --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_read file=\"./data/raw_users.csv\" output_var=\"RAW_CSV\" /\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- 2. Process it via shell script, outputting to CLEAN_CSV --\u003e\n        \u003cscript lang=\"bash\" output_var=\"CLEAN_CSV\"\u003e\n            # Using grep to remove empty lines and invalid data\n            echo \"$RAW_CSV\" | grep -v \"^$\" | grep -i \"@example.com\"\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- 3. Save processed results to a new file --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_save file=\"./data/clean_users.csv\" var=\"CLEAN_CSV\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/scripts\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","foreach.md":"# `\u003cforeach\u003e` \u0026 `\u003cloop\u003e`\n\nThe `\u003cforeach\u003e` element (alias `\u003cloop\u003e`) executes nested pipeline steps iteratively for each record returned by a database driver query. It bridges relational query results with dynamic child task execution, automatically binding projected column values into the pipeline registry for use across subsequent SQL steps, scripts, templates, HTTP probes, file operations, and nested control groups.\n\nFlow\u0027s `\u003cforeach\u003e` engine is engineered for high-throughput enterprise pipelines, supporting both **constant-memory streaming** for processing tens of millions of records without memory spikes, and **in-memory buffering** to release database locks upfront.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture \u0026 Lifecycle\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A([Start Foreach Loop]) --\u003e B{Driver Query Source?}\n    B -- Inline SQL Body --\u003e C[Parse Inline SQL Text]\n    B -- var / variable Attribute --\u003e D[Resolve Variable Query Text]\n    \n    C --\u003e E[Execute Driver Query on Target DB]\n    D --\u003e E\n    \n    E --\u003e F{Mode?}\n    F -- stream=\u0027true\u0027 / mode=\u0027stream\u0027 --\u003e G[Open Constant-Memory Cursor]\n    F -- buffer=\u0027true\u0027 / mode=\u0027buffer\u0027 --\u003e H[Buffer Rows in Memory up to 100k \u0026 Release DB Cursor]\n    \n    G --\u003e I{Next Row Available?}\n    H --\u003e I\n    \n    I -- Yes --\u003e J[\"Bind Column Variables\u003cbr/\u003e{{col}}, {{COL}}, {{Col}}\u003cbr/\u003eSet {{LOOP_INDEX}}\"]\n    J --\u003e K[Execute Child Pipeline Nodes Sequentially]\n    \n    K --\u003e L{Step Failed?}\n    L -- Yes --\u003e M{on_error Policy?}\n    M -- stop (default) --\u003e N[Abort Pipeline \u0026 Report Failure]\n    M -- continue --\u003e O[Log Warning \u0026 Advance Loop]\n    M -- retry --\u003e P[Retry Step up to retry_count]\n    P --\u003e K\n    \n    L -- No --\u003e O\n    O --\u003e I\n    \n    I -- No (EOF) --\u003e Q[Record Iteration Metrics in LAST_OUTPUT]\n    Q --\u003e R([Exit Foreach Block])\n```\n\n---\n\n## XML Syntax \u0026 Attribute Reference\n\n```xml\n\u003cforeach id=\"ProcessBatch\"\n         db=\"source_db\"\n         language=\"sql\"\n         stream=\"true\"\n         buffer=\"false\"\n         mode=\"stream\"\n         on_error=\"stop\"\n         description=\"Iterate over pending customer orders\"\u003e\n    SELECT order_id, customer_email, total_amount FROM orders WHERE status = \u0027pending\u0027;\n\n    \u003cgroup id=\"ProcessSingleOrder\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"source_db\"\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Child nodes executed once per returned row --\u003e\n    \u003c/group\u003e\n\u003c/foreach\u003e\n```\n\n| Attribute | Mandatory | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `id` | Optional | `foreach_N` | Unique step identifier used in logs, execution metrics, and diagnostics. |\n| `db` / `database` | **Yes** | — | Name of the configured database connection used to execute the driver query. |\n| `language` / `lang` | Optional | `sql` | Driver query engine runtime. Defaults to `sql`. |\n| `var` / `variable` | Optional | — | Name of a pipeline variable containing the driver SQL query. If non-empty, takes precedence over inline XML body text. |\n| `stream` | Optional | `true` | Boolean enabling constant-memory streaming cursor mode. Consumes $O(1)$ RAM regardless of result set size. |\n| `buffer` | Optional | `false` | Boolean enabling in-memory row buffering (capped at 100,000 rows). Reads all records upfront to close the database cursor early. |\n| `mode` | Optional | `stream` | Explicit execution mode: `\u0027stream\u0027` ($O(1)$ streaming cursor) or `\u0027buffer\u0027` (in-memory buffer). |\n| `condition` / `cond` | Optional | — | Inline boolean expression evaluated before executing the loop. If false, the loop is skipped. |\n| `on_error` | Optional | `stop` | Failure policy when a child step fails: `stop` (halts pipeline), `continue` (logs warning and advances to next row), or `retry`. |\n| `retry_count` | Optional | `0` | Number of times to retry failed child steps before triggering `on_error`. |\n| `retry_interval` | Optional | — | Duration delay between retries (e.g., `2s`, `500ms`). |\n| `timeout` | Optional | — | Execution deadline duration for the loop (e.g., `30s`, `5m`). |\n| `description` | Optional | — | Human-readable explanation of the loop logic. |\n| *Inline Content* | Conditional | — | The driver SQL query placed between `\u003cforeach\u003e` and child tags. Required when `var` is not specified. |\n\n---\n\n## Streaming vs. Buffering Modes\n\nFlow provides two execution architectures for iterating over driver queries:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph Streaming [\"Mode: \u0027stream\u0027 (Default)\"]\n        S1[(Database Cursor)] --\u003e|Row by Row Stream| S2[Memory: O-1 Tiny Footprint]\n        S2 --\u003e|Direct Execution| S3[Child Steps]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Buffering [\"Mode: \u0027buffer\u0027 (buffer=\u0027true\u0027)\"]\n        B1[(Database Cursor)] --\u003e|Read All Rows Upfront| B2[Memory: Slices up to 100k]\n        B2 --\u003e|Cursor Closed Early| B3[Database Connection Freed]\n        B2 --\u003e|Iterate Buffered Slice| B4[Child Steps]\n    end\n```\n\n### 1. Constant-Memory Streaming (`mode=\"stream\"`, `stream=\"true\"`)\n* **Default behavior**: Ideal for massive tables (millions of rows).\n* **Memory footprint**: $O(1)$ constant overhead. Rows are fetched and processed on-the-fly via native database cursors.\n* **Connection hold**: Keeps the driver database connection open for the entire duration of the loop.\n\n### 2. In-Memory Buffering (`mode=\"buffer\"`, `buffer=\"true\"`)\n* **Use case**: Essential when child steps need to query or update the **same** database connection as the driver query without encountering connection pool starvation, cursor locks, or driver deadlocks.\n* **Safety limit**: Upfront row ingestion is capped at 100,000 rows to prevent process out-of-memory errors.\n* **Early release**: The database connection and cursor are released back to the pool before child steps start running.\n\n---\n\n## Variable Binding \u0026 Scoping\n\nFor every row processed, `\u003cforeach\u003e` automatically binds all projected columns into the pipeline registry:\n\n### 1. Case-Insensitive Column Variables\nColumns are registered in three naming conventions so child steps can reference them naturally:\n* **Original column name**: e.g., `{{order_id}}`\n* **Uppercase name**: e.g., `{{ORDER_ID}}`\n* **Lowercase name**: e.g., `{{order_id}}`\n\n### 2. Built-in Loop Index (`LOOP_INDEX`)\n* Flow sets the variable `LOOP_INDEX` to the zero-based iteration index (`0, 1, 2, ...`).\n* Access in SQL steps: `SELECT {{LOOP_INDEX}} AS current_iteration;`\n* Access in Go scripts: `idx := vars.GetInt(\"LOOP_INDEX\")`\n* Access in templates: `Iteration: {{ .LOOP_INDEX }}`\n\n### 3. Loop Result Diagnostics\nUpon completion, `\u003cforeach\u003e` writes summary statistics to the step output and `LAST_OUTPUT`, reporting:\n* `rows`: Total rows returned by driver query.\n* `iterations`: Total child iterations completed.\n* `duration`: Wall-clock processing time.\n\n---\n\n## Transaction Scoping with Foreach\n\nHow `\u003cforeach\u003e` interacts with database transactions depends on where `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` is placed:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph PatternA [\"Per-Iteration Transactions (Recommended)\"]\n        PA1[\"\u003cforeach\u003e\"] --\u003e PA2[\"\u003cgroup transaction=\u0027true\u0027\u003e\"]\n        PA2 --\u003e PA3[\"Commit / Rollback per Row\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph PatternB [\"Overarching Transaction (Atomic Batch)\"]\n        PB1[\"\u003cgroup transaction=\u0027true\u0027\u003e\"] --\u003e PB2[\"\u003cforeach\u003e\"]\n        PB2 --\u003e PB3[\"Commit / Rollback across Entire Loop\"]\n    end\n```\n\n1. **Per-Iteration Transaction (Recommended for large jobs)**:\n   Place `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` **inside** the `\u003cforeach\u003e`. Each iteration is an independent transaction. If row 5 fails with `on_error=\"continue\"`, rows 1–4 remain safely committed.\n2. **Overarching Transaction (Atomic Batch)**:\n   Place `\u003cforeach\u003e` **inside** `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`. The entire loop is wrapped in one single transaction. If any iteration fails, all changes across all previous iterations are rolled back. \n   \u003e [!WARNING]\n   \u003e Do not use overarching transactions for loops spanning thousands of rows, as long-held table locks will degrade database performance.\n\n---\n\n## Production Pipeline Examples\n\n### Example 1: Dynamic Table Replication Loop\nIterates through a database migration mapping table and bulk-streams each table using `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`:\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ReplicationPlan\" type=\"string\" \n                  value=\"SELECT source_table, destination_table, column_filter FROM etl_transfer_map WHERE is_active = 1\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"primary_oltp\" driver=\"sqlserver\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://prod-db:1433?database=AppDB\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"reporting_dw\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"host=dw-db port=5432 user=etl dbname=reports sslmode=require\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Loop over each active mapping row --\u003e\n        \u003cforeach id=\"ReplicateTables\" db=\"primary_oltp\" var=\"ReplicationPlan\" stream=\"true\"\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"TransferSingleTable\"\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Log progress with Go script --\u003e\n                \u003cscript id=\"LogIteration\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                    package main\n                    import (\n                        \"fmt\"\n                        \"host/vars\"\n                    )\n                    func main() {\n                        idx := vars.GetInt(\"LOOP_INDEX\")\n                        src := vars.GetString(\"source_table\")\n                        dst := vars.GetString(\"destination_table\")\n                        fmt.Printf(\"[Row #%d] Transferring table %s -\u003e %s\\n\", idx+1, src, dst)\n                    }\n                \u003c/script\u003e\n\n                \u003c!-- Truncate destination before copying --\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"TruncateStaging\" db=\"reporting_dw\"\u003e\n                    TRUNCATE TABLE {{destination_table}};\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n                \u003c!-- Bulk stream from source to destination --\u003e\n                \u003csql_bulk id=\"BulkCopy\" db=\"primary_oltp\" target_db=\"reporting_dw\"\n                          target_table=\"{{destination_table}}\" batch_size=\"50000\"\u003e\n                    SELECT {{column_filter}} FROM {{source_table}};\n                \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Multi-Tenant Invoicing with Per-Iteration Transactions\nGenerates monthly invoices for active accounts, isolating each customer transaction with automatic rollback on error:\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"billing_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"host=billing.internal user=postgres dbname=billing\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cforeach id=\"InvoiceGenerationLoop\" db=\"billing_db\" on_error=\"continue\"\u003e\n            SELECT account_id, account_tier, rate_per_unit \n            FROM accounts \n            WHERE status = \u0027ACTIVE\u0027 AND billing_cycle = \u0027MONTHLY\u0027;\n\n            \u003c!-- Independent atomic transaction for each account --\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"GenerateInvoiceTx\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"billing_db\" timeout=\"10s\"\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Calculate usage charges --\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"InsertInvoiceRecord\" db=\"billing_db\" output_var=\"NewInvoiceId\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO invoices (account_id, billing_date, status)\n                    VALUES (\u0027{{account_id}}\u0027, CURRENT_DATE, \u0027PENDING\u0027)\n                    RETURNING invoice_id;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n                \u003c!-- Create line items --\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"GenerateLineItems\" db=\"billing_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO invoice_items (invoice_id, description, amount)\n                    SELECT \u0027{{NewInvoiceId}}\u0027, service_name, quantity * {{rate_per_unit}}\n                    FROM unbilled_usage\n                    WHERE account_id = \u0027{{account_id}}\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n                \u003c!-- Mark usage as billed --\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"ClearUnbilledUsage\" db=\"billing_db\"\u003e\n                    UPDATE unbilled_usage \n                    SET billed = true \n                    WHERE account_id = \u0027{{account_id}}\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Dynamic File Export with In-Memory Buffering\nBuffers driver query results in memory to close the database connection immediately, then exports individual JSON files:\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"EXPORT_DIR\" type=\"string\" value=\"./exports/customers\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"crm_db\" driver=\"mysql\" connection_string=\"root:secret@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/crm\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- In-memory buffering releases crm_db connection upfront --\u003e\n        \u003cforeach id=\"ExportLoop\" db=\"crm_db\" buffer=\"true\" mode=\"buffer\"\u003e\n            SELECT customer_code, full_name, email, credit_limit \n            FROM customers \n            WHERE sync_required = 1;\n\n            \u003cgroup id=\"WriteCustomerFile\"\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Render customer JSON file --\u003e\n                \u003cfile_save id=\"SaveCustomerJson\" \n                           file=\"{{EXPORT_DIR}}/{{customer_code}}.json\" \n                           append=\"false\"\u003e\n                    {\n                      \"customer_code\": \"{{customer_code}}\",\n                      \"name\": \"{{full_name}}\",\n                      \"email\": \"{{email}}\",\n                      \"credit_limit\": {{credit_limit}},\n                      \"exported_at\": \"{{_TIMESTAMP}}\"\n                    }\n                \u003c/file_save\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: External Webhook Notification with Assertion Quality Gate\nDispatches payment alerts via HTTP POST and asserts HTTP 200/201 response status:\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"app_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"file:app.db?cache=shared\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cforeach id=\"WebhookLoop\" db=\"app_db\" on_error=\"continue\"\u003e\n            SELECT event_id, webhook_url, payload_json \n            FROM outbound_webhooks \n            WHERE delivered = 0;\n\n            \u003cgroup id=\"DispatchEvent\"\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Send HTTP Post --\u003e\n                \u003chttp_client id=\"SendWebhook\" \n                             url=\"{{webhook_url}}\" \n                             method=\"POST\" \n                             content_type=\"application/json\"\n                             status_into=\"HTTP_STATUS\" \n                             timeout=\"5s\"\u003e\n                    {{payload_json}}\n                \u003c/http_client\u003e\n\n                \u003c!-- Validate HTTP status --\u003e\n                \u003cassert id=\"VerifyDelivery\" var=\"HTTP_STATUS\" equals=\"200\" on_failure=\"warn\" /\u003e\n\n                \u003c!-- Mark as processed if successful --\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"MarkDelivered\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\n                    UPDATE outbound_webhooks SET delivered = 1 WHERE event_id = {{event_id}};\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: High-Performance Key-Value Store Migration\nReads user authentication credentials and streams them into an embedded Badger/Bolt Key-Value store:\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"auth_sql\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"host=localhost dbname=auth\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"cache_kv\" driver=\"badger\" connection_string=\"./data/cache.badger\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Streaming cursor for hundreds of thousands of sessions --\u003e\n        \u003cforeach id=\"MigrateSessions\" db=\"auth_sql\" stream=\"true\"\u003e\n            SELECT session_token, user_profile_json \n            FROM active_sessions;\n\n            \u003ckv id=\"PutSession\" db=\"cache_kv\" op=\"put\" bucket=\"sessions\" \n                key=\"{{session_token}}\" value=\"{{user_profile_json}}\" /\u003e\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","group.md":"# `\u003cgroup\u003e` Execution Group \u0026 Database Transactions Guide\n\nThe `\u003cgroup\u003e` element organizes child pipeline steps into a sequential, cohesive execution block. It serves three critical functions in Flow pipelines:\n1. **Logical Step Organization**: Cleanly groups related tasks under a shared identifier, descriptive label, and lifecycle scope.\n2. **Atomic Database Transactions \u0026 Dialect-Aware Savepoints**: Coordinates multiple `\u003csql\u003e` queries into an all-or-nothing transaction with automatic commit and rollback, plus dialect-aware savepoints for nested groups.\n3. **Conditional Execution \u0026 Error Resilience**: Evaluates conditions before running child steps and governs fault tolerance via granular retry policies (`on_error`, `retry_count`, `retry_interval`).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Overview\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Start([\"Enter \u0026lt;group\u0026gt;\"]) --\u003e CondCheck{\"Has Condition?\u003cbr/\u003e(condition / if_var)\"}\n    \n    CondCheck -- \"Condition Fails\" --\u003e Skipped([\"Skip Group \u0026amp; Children\"])\n    CondCheck -- \"Passes or None\" --\u003e TxCheck{\"transaction=\u0027true\u0027?\"}\n    \n    TxCheck -- \"No\" --\u003e ExecSteps[\"Execute Child Steps Sequentially\"]\n    TxCheck -- \"Yes\" --\u003e StackCheck{\"Active Outer Tx\u003cbr/\u003eon Same DB?\"}\n    \n    StackCheck -- \"No (Root Tx)\" --\u003e BeginTx[\"BeginTx(ctx)\u003cbr/\u003ePush to Transaction Stack\"]\n    StackCheck -- \"Yes (Nested)\" --\u003e CreateSP[\"Create Dialect Savepoint\u003cbr/\u003e• MSSQL: SAVE TRANSACTION sp_N\u003cbr/\u003e• ANSI: SAVEPOINT sp_N\"]\n    \n    BeginTx --\u003e ExecSteps\n    CreateSP --\u003e ExecSteps\n    \n    ExecSteps --\u003e StepResult{\"All Steps\u003cbr/\u003eSucceeded?\"}\n    \n    StepResult -- \"Yes\" --\u003e TxSuccess{\"transaction=\u0027true\u0027?\"}\n    TxSuccess -- \"No\" --\u003e Done([\"Group Completed\"])\n    TxSuccess -- \"Yes (Root)\" --\u003e CommitTx[\"Commit Transaction\u003cbr/\u003ePop Stack\"] --\u003e Done\n    TxSuccess -- \"Yes (Nested)\" --\u003e ReleaseSP[\"Release / Finalize Savepoint\u003cbr/\u003ePop Stack\"] --\u003e Done\n    \n    StepResult -- \"No (Error/Timeout)\" --\u003e ErrorPolicy{\"on_error Policy\"}\n    \n    ErrorPolicy -- \"retry (count \u003e 0)\" --\u003e RollbackAttempt[\"Rollback Tx or SP\u003cbr/\u003eWait retry_interval\"] --\u003e ExecSteps\n    ErrorPolicy -- \"continue\" --\u003e RollbackCont[\"Rollback Tx or SP\u003cbr/\u003eLog Warning \u0026amp; Continue\"] --\u003e Done\n    ErrorPolicy -- \"stop (Default)\" --\u003e RollbackFail[\"Rollback Tx or SP\u003cbr/\u003eAbort Pipeline\"] --\u003e Fail([\"Pipeline Error\"])\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete XML Syntax \u0026 Attribute Reference\n\n```xml\n\u003cgroup id=\"ProcessBatch\"\n       transaction=\"true\"\n       db=\"finance_db\"\n       timeout=\"45s\"\n       on_error=\"retry\"\n       retry_count=\"3\"\n       retry_interval=\"5s\"\n       condition=\"{{IsActive}} == true\"\n       description=\"Executes accounting batch with atomic commit and retry\"\u003e\n    \u003c!-- Sequential child steps: \u003csql\u003e, \u003cscript\u003e, \u003chttp_client\u003e, \u003ctemplate\u003e, etc. --\u003e\n\u003c/group\u003e\n```\n\n### Attribute Reference\n\n| Attribute | Type | Mandatory | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `id` | `string` | **Yes** (Recommended) | generated | Unique identifier for the group step, displayed in execution telemetry, logs, and flowcharts. |\n| `transaction` | `bool` | No | `false` | When `true`, wraps all child `\u003csql\u003e` steps inside an atomic database transaction. |\n| `tx` | `bool` | No | `false` | Alias for `transaction`. |\n| `db` | `string` | Conditional | — | The configured database connection name to open the transaction against. **Mandatory** when `transaction=\"true\"`. |\n| `database` | `string` | No | — | Alias for `db`. |\n| `timeout` | `string` | No | — | Transaction deadline duration (e.g. `30s`, `2m`, `1h`). Cancels the context and triggers an automatic rollback if exceeded. |\n| `on_error` | `string` | No | `stop` | Failure policy when a child step returns an error: `stop` (abort group and pipeline), `continue` (log and proceed to next sibling), or `retry` (reattempt group). |\n| `retry_count` | `int` | No | `0` | Number of retry attempts when `on_error=\"retry\"`. |\n| `retry_interval`| `string` | No | `1s` | Delay between retry attempts (e.g. `500ms`, `2s`, `10s`). |\n| `condition` | `string` | No | — | Inline boolean expression evaluated before execution (e.g. `{{Env}} == \u0027prod\u0027`, `{{TotalCount}} \u003e 0`). |\n| `cond` | `string` | No | — | Alias for `condition`. |\n| `if_var` | `string` | No | — | Name of a pipeline variable to test for execution (e.g. `RunBatch`). Alias `var`. |\n| `var` | `string` | No | — | Alias for `if_var`. |\n| `if_equals` | `string` | No | — | Expected variable value required for the group to execute. Aliases: `equals`, `if_val`, `val`, `value`. |\n| `equals` | `string` | No | — | Alias for `if_equals`. |\n| `if_val` | `string` | No | — | Alias for `if_equals`. |\n| `val` | `string` | No | — | Alias for `if_equals`. |\n| `value` | `string` | No | — | Alias for `if_equals`. |\n| `description` | `string` | No | — | Descriptive summary of the group\u0027s purpose. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Database Transactions \u0026 Dialect-Aware Savepoints\n\nFlow implements a thread-safe, stack-based transaction coordinator (`activeTxs`). It guarantees that all child database operations within the group succeed as a single atomic unit or roll back completely upon failure.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph OuterGroup [\"Outer \u0026lt;group id=\u0027financial_transfer\u0027 transaction=\u0027true\u0027 db=\u0027bank_db\u0027\u0026gt;\"]\n        T1[\"BeginTx() on bank_db\"] --\u003e S1[\"1. UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 500 WHERE id = 1\"]\n        \n        subgraph InnerGroup [\"Inner \u0026lt;group id=\u0027nested_audit\u0027 transaction=\u0027true\u0027 db=\u0027bank_db\u0027\u0026gt;\"]\n            SP[\"Create Savepoint (sp_1)\u003cbr/\u003e• MSSQL: SAVE TRANSACTION sp_1\u003cbr/\u003e• ANSI: SAVEPOINT sp_1\"] --\u003e S2[\"2. INSERT INTO transfer_audit_log VALUES (...)\"]\n            S2 --\u003e SPCheck{\"Audit Step Ok?\"}\n            SPCheck -- \"Success\" --\u003e SPRelease[\"Release / Retain Savepoint\"]\n            SPCheck -- \"Failure\" --\u003e SPRollback[\"ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp_1\u003cbr/\u003e(Outer Tx remains intact)\"]\n        end\n        \n        S1 --\u003e SP\n        SPRelease --\u003e S3[\"3. UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 500 WHERE id = 2\"]\n        SPRollback --\u003e S3\n        \n        S3 --\u003e FinalCheck{\"All Steps Ok?\"}\n        FinalCheck -- \"Success\" --\u003e CommitTx[\"Commit Outer Transaction\"]\n        FinalCheck -- \"Failure\" --\u003e RollbackTx[\"Rollback Full Transaction\"]\n    end\n```\n\n### Dialect-Aware Savepoints\nWhen an inner `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` targets the same database connection as an active outer transaction, Flow detects the nested state and automatically establishes a savepoint:\n\n| Database Driver | Savepoint Command | Rollback Command | Release Command |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **PostgreSQL** (`postgres`, `pgx`) | `SAVEPOINT sp_N` | `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp_N` | `RELEASE SAVEPOINT sp_N` |\n| **SQLite** (`sqlite3`) | `SAVEPOINT sp_N` | `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp_N` | `RELEASE SAVEPOINT sp_N` |\n| **MySQL / MariaDB** (`mysql`) | `SAVEPOINT sp_N` | `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp_N` | `RELEASE SAVEPOINT sp_N` |\n| **Oracle** (`godror`) | `SAVEPOINT sp_N` | `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp_N` | — *(Automatic on commit)* |\n| **Microsoft SQL Server** (`sqlserver`, `mssql`) | `SAVE TRANSACTION sp_N` | `ROLLBACK TRANSACTION sp_N` | — *(T-SQL omits release syntax)* |\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e If an inner group with a savepoint fails, Flow rolls back **only to that savepoint**, allowing the outer transaction to inspect the failure, execute alternative logic, or proceed to completion without corrupting the broader transaction.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Context Propagation \u0026 Execution Deadlines (`timeout`)\n\nDatabase locks held by uncommitted transactions can degrade database performance and cause deadlocks. Flow binds every transaction group to a Go `context.Context`:\n\n* **Granular Timeouts**: Specify `timeout=\"30s\"` or `timeout=\"5m\"` on the group.\n* **Automatic Rollback**: If the timeout duration expires or an external cancellation signal arrives, the context is cancelled immediately. Flow executes an atomic deferred rollback, closing the transaction and releasing all table locks.\n\n```xml\n\u003cgroup id=\"critical_update\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"crm_db\" timeout=\"15s\"\u003e\n    \u003csql id=\"apply_discount\" db=\"crm_db\"\u003e\n        UPDATE subscriptions SET discount_pct = 0.20 WHERE renew_date \u0026lt;= CURRENT_DATE;\n    \u003c/sql\u003e\n\u003c/group\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Error Handling \u0026 Retry Policies\n\nThe `\u003cgroup\u003e` element supports localized resilience and fault recovery policies:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Step[\"Child Step Fails\"] --\u003e Policy{\"on_error\"}\n    Policy -- \"stop\" --\u003e Stop[\"Rollback Tx \u0026amp; Halt Pipeline\"]\n    Policy -- \"continue\" --\u003e Cont[\"Rollback Tx \u0026amp; Log Warning\u003cbr/\u003eProceed to Next Node\"]\n    Policy -- \"retry\" --\u003e CountCheck{\"Retries Left?\"}\n    CountCheck -- \"Yes\" --\u003e Delay[\"Sleep retry_interval\"] --\u003e Reattempt[\"Rollback Tx \u0026amp; Re-run Group\"]\n    CountCheck -- \"No\" --\u003e Stop\n```\n\n* **`on_error=\"stop\"` (Default)**: Any child failure halts the group, initiates an immediate database rollback, and fails the pipeline.\n* **`on_error=\"continue\"`**: If a child step fails, the group\u0027s transaction is rolled back safely, but the pipeline execution continues with the subsequent sibling nodes.\n* **`on_error=\"retry\"`**: When paired with `retry_count=\"N\"` and `retry_interval=\"Xs\"`, transient failures (such as network drops or database deadlocks) trigger an immediate rollback followed by a clean re-execution of the group from the beginning.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Conditional Group Execution\n\nInstead of requiring an explicit `\u003cif\u003e\u003cthen\u003e...\u003c/then\u003e\u003c/if\u003e` construct, `\u003cgroup\u003e` supports direct conditional attributes:\n\n### 1. Simple Variable Equality Testing (`if_var` + `if_equals`)\n```xml\n\u003cgroup id=\"RunProductionMigrations\" if_var=\"Environment\" if_equals=\"production\"\u003e\n    \u003csql db=\"app_db\"\u003eALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS verified BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE;\u003c/sql\u003e\n\u003c/group\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. Boolean Expressions (`condition`)\n```xml\n\u003cgroup id=\"ProcessHighPriority\" condition=\"{{PendingRecords}} \u003e 1000 \u0026amp;\u0026amp; {{DryRun}} == false\"\u003e\n    \u003csql db=\"warehouse_db\"\u003eINSERT INTO processing_queue SELECT * FROM staging_records;\u003c/sql\u003e\n\u003c/group\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Interaction with Loops and Parallelism\n\n### Loops (`\u003cforeach\u003e`, `\u003cwhile\u003e`)\nWhen placed inside `\u003cforeach\u003e` or `\u003cwhile\u003e`, a `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` creates, commits, or rolls back an independent transaction **on every individual iteration**:\n* Failure in iteration `N` rolls back only iteration `N`.\n* Preceding iterations remain permanently committed.\n* Subsequent iterations continue according to the loop\u0027s error policy.\n\n### Parallel Execution (`\u003cparallel\u003e`)\nWhen running inside `\u003cparallel max_threads=\"...\"\u003e`:\n* Flow provisions a cloned, thread-isolated execution context for each worker branch.\n* If a worker executes `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`, its transaction is isolated to that worker\u0027s dedicated database connection.\n* Concurrent workers never share transaction states or collide on savepoint names.\n\n### High-Throughput Bulk Copy Caution (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`)\n\u003e [!WARNING]\n\u003e Do **NOT** wrap high-throughput streaming bulk copy (`\u003csql_bulk target_table=\"...\"\u003e` / `StreamETL`) inside a transaction-enabled `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`.\n\u003e \n\u003e SQL Server TDS bulk copy operates using its own internal batch-level commits (`batch_size=\"10000\"`). Wrapping native TDS fast-load streams inside an outer group transaction causes driver command overlap, connection pool exhaustion, and extensive table lock contention.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Comprehensive Production Examples\n\n### Example 1: Multi-Step Bank Transfer with Atomic Transaction \u0026 Timeout\nDemonstrates debiting one account, crediting another, and writing an audit trail with an enforced 30-second deadline.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"SenderID\" type=\"int\" value=\"101\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ReceiverID\" type=\"int\" value=\"202\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TransferAmount\" type=\"float\" value=\"150.00\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"core_banking\" driver=\"postgres\"\n                  connection_string=\"postgresql://app_user:Secret123@db.internal:5432/banking?sslmode=require\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Atomic transaction block with 30s timeout --\u003e\n        \u003cgroup id=\"transfer_funds_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"core_banking\" timeout=\"30s\"\n               description=\"Transfers funds atomically between accounts\"\u003e\n            \n            \u003c!-- Step 1: Deduct balance with balance constraint --\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"debit_sender\" db=\"core_banking\"\u003e\n                UPDATE accounts \n                SET balance = balance - {{TransferAmount}}, updated_at = NOW()\n                WHERE account_id = {{SenderID}} AND balance \u003e= {{TransferAmount}};\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Step 2: Ensure exactly 1 row was updated --\u003e\n            \u003cassert id=\"check_debit_applied\" var=\"debit_sender.rows_affected\" equals=\"1\"\n                    message=\"Insufficient funds or invalid sender account ID\" on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Step 3: Credit destination account --\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"credit_receiver\" db=\"core_banking\"\u003e\n                UPDATE accounts \n                SET balance = balance + {{TransferAmount}}, updated_at = NOW()\n                WHERE account_id = {{ReceiverID}};\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Step 4: Record immutable audit ledger --\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"record_ledger_entry\" db=\"core_banking\"\u003e\n                INSERT INTO transaction_ledger (from_acc, to_acc, amount, created_at)\n                VALUES ({{SenderID}}, {{ReceiverID}}, {{TransferAmount}}, NOW());\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/group\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Nested Savepoints (Outer Transaction with Inner Audit Group)\nDemonstrates dialect-aware savepoints. If the nested audit log fails, the inner savepoint rolls back without interrupting the outer order processing.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"store_db\" driver=\"sqlserver\"\n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://sql.prod.net:1433?database=Store\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Root transaction boundary --\u003e\n        \u003cgroup id=\"process_order_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"store_db\" timeout=\"1m\"\u003e\n            \n            \u003c!-- 1. Place order --\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"insert_order\" db=\"store_db\"\u003e\n                INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, total_amount) VALUES (4096, 299.95);\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- 2. Nested transaction: uses T-SQL \u0027SAVE TRANSACTION savepoint_1\u0027 --\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"nested_notification_log\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"store_db\" on_error=\"continue\"\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"log_notification\" db=\"store_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO notification_queue (order_id, channel) VALUES (4096, \u0027EMAIL\u0027);\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- 3. Finalize order status --\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"finalize_order\" db=\"store_db\"\u003e\n                UPDATE customer_profiles SET last_order_date = GETDATE() WHERE id = 4096;\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/group\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Conditional Execution with Error Retries\nDemonstrates verifying an external API health check before running a group that retries up to 3 times on transient database deadlocks.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ApiHealthy\" type=\"bool\" value=\"false\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"stage_db\" driver=\"postgres\"\n                  connection_string=\"postgresql://app:pass@localhost:5432/staging\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Probe upstream API --\u003e\n        \u003chttp_client id=\"check_upstream\" method=\"GET\" url=\"https://api.partner.com/health\" status_into=\"HttpStatus\" /\u003e\n        \n        \u003cscript id=\"eval_status\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n            import \"host/vars\"\n            func main() {\n                if vars.GetInt(\"HttpStatus\") == 200 {\n                    vars.SetBool(\"ApiHealthy\", true)\n                }\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Conditional group with retry policy --\u003e\n        \u003cgroup id=\"pull_partner_data\"\n               if_var=\"ApiHealthy\" if_equals=\"true\"\n               on_error=\"retry\" retry_count=\"3\" retry_interval=\"4s\"\n               transaction=\"true\" db=\"stage_db\"\u003e\n            \n            \u003chttp_client id=\"fetch_orders\" method=\"GET\" url=\"https://api.partner.com/v1/orders\" into=\"OrdersJson\" /\u003e\n            \n            \u003csql id=\"stage_raw_orders\" db=\"stage_db\"\u003e\n                INSERT INTO staging_raw_orders (payload, received_at)\n                VALUES (\u0027{{OrdersJson}}\u0027, NOW());\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/group\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Transactional Loop inside `\u003cforeach\u003e`\nDemonstrates streaming active user subscriptions and applying billing renewals, committing each user in its own independent transaction.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"billing_db\" driver=\"mysql\"\n                  connection_string=\"root:secret@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/billing?parseTime=true\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Loop driver: streams user IDs needing renewal --\u003e\n        \u003cforeach id=\"renew_subscribers\" db=\"billing_db\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            SELECT user_id, plan_cost, plan_tier \n            FROM subscriptions \n            WHERE status = \u0027active\u0027 AND next_billing_date \u003c= CURDATE();\n            ]]\u003e\n            \n            \u003c!-- Each row executes in its own isolated transaction --\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"apply_user_renewal\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"billing_db\" timeout=\"15s\" on_error=\"continue\"\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"deduct_user_credits\" db=\"billing_db\"\u003e\n                    UPDATE user_wallets \n                    SET balance = balance - {{plan_cost}} \n                    WHERE user_id = {{user_id}} AND balance \u003e= {{plan_cost}};\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n                \u003cassert id=\"check_wallet_deducted\" var=\"deduct_user_credits.rows_affected\" equals=\"1\"\n                        message=\"User {{user_id}} has insufficient credit balance\" /\u003e\n\n                \u003csql id=\"advance_billing_cycle\" db=\"billing_db\"\u003e\n                    UPDATE subscriptions \n                    SET next_billing_date = DATE_ADD(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 1 MONTH)\n                    WHERE user_id = {{user_id}};\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n                \u003csql id=\"log_billing_history\" db=\"billing_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO billing_events (user_id, amount, tier, billed_at)\n                    VALUES ({{user_id}}, {{plan_cost}}, \u0027{{plan_tier}}\u0027, NOW());\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: Concurrent Isolated Transactions in `\u003cparallel\u003e`\nDemonstrates running multi-regional partition archiving concurrently across 3 worker threads, each maintaining an isolated transaction.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"eu_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:pass@eu-db:5432/main\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"us_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:pass@us-db:5432/main\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"ap_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:pass@ap-db:5432/main\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cparallel max_threads=\"3\"\u003e\n            \u003c!-- Worker Branch 1: EU Partition Archive --\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"archive_eu_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"eu_db\" timeout=\"2m\"\u003e\n                \u003csql db=\"eu_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO orders_archive SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at \u0026lt; \u00272025-01-01\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n                \u003csql db=\"eu_db\"\u003e\n                    DELETE FROM orders WHERE created_at \u0026lt; \u00272025-01-01\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Worker Branch 2: US Partition Archive --\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"archive_us_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"us_db\" timeout=\"2m\"\u003e\n                \u003csql db=\"us_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO orders_archive SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at \u0026lt; \u00272025-01-01\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n                \u003csql db=\"us_db\"\u003e\n                    DELETE FROM orders WHERE created_at \u0026lt; \u00272025-01-01\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Worker Branch 3: APAC Partition Archive --\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"archive_ap_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"ap_db\" timeout=\"2m\"\u003e\n                \u003csql db=\"ap_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO orders_archive SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at \u0026lt; \u00272025-01-01\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n                \u003csql db=\"ap_db\"\u003e\n                    DELETE FROM orders WHERE created_at \u0026lt; \u00272025-01-01\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","http_client.md":"# HTTP_CLIENT Node Reference\r\n\r\n### Core Execution Flow\r\n1. **Variable Snapshot \u0026 Interpolation**: Reads current registry variables and replaces `{{VarName}}` placeholders in `uri`, `url`, `headers`, `content_type`, `data`, and inner element body text.\r\n2. **Transport \u0026 Client Assembly**: Constructs `http.Transport` with TLS/proxy settings and wraps it inside an `http.Client` with custom redirect and timeout policies via `BuildClientAndRequest`.\r\n3. **Request Execution**: Issues the HTTP request bound to the parent pipeline execution context (`context.Context`).\r\n4. **Response Handling \u0026 State Persistence**: Reads the response body and writes data to target pipeline variables (`output_var`, `status_code_var`), updating `LAST_OUTPUT`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Complete Attribute Specification\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Core Request \u0026 Variable Attributes\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Type | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `id` | `xs:string` | Auto (`http_N`) | Unique node identifier for logging and AST validation. |\r\n| `uri` / `url` | `xs:string` | **Required** | Target endpoint URL. Supports variable interpolation (`{{VarName}}`). |\r\n| `method` | `xs:string` | `GET` | HTTP verb (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`). |\r\n| `data` | `xs:string` | Optional | Direct payload data. Takes precedence over element body content. |\r\n| `headers` | `xs:string` | Optional | Custom request headers (`K1:V1,K2:V2` or newline-separated). |\r\n| `content_type` | `xs:string` | Optional | Shortcut header setter for `Content-Type`. |\r\n| `var` / `variable` / `output_var` / `output_variable` / `out_var` / `into` | `xs:string` | Optional | Registry variable key to store the response body. |\r\n| `status_code_var` / `status_code_variable` / `status_var` / `status_variable` / `status_into` | `xs:string` | Optional | Registry variable key to store the integer HTTP status code. |\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Go `http.Client` Settings\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Type | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `timeout` | `xs:string` | Unlimited | Total request timeout duration (e.g., `5s`, `30s`, `2m`). |\r\n| `follow_redirects` | `xs:boolean` | `true` | Follow HTTP 3xx redirect responses automatically. |\r\n| `max_redirects` | `xs:nonNegativeInteger` | Unlimited | Maximum redirect chain limit before erroring. |\r\n| `cookie_jar` | `xs:boolean` | `false` | Enable in-memory `cookiejar` session state. |\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Go `http.Transport` \u0026 TLS Tuning\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Type | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `proxy` | `xs:string` | Optional | Proxy URL (e.g., `http://proxy.internal:8080`). |\r\n| `tls_insecure_skip_verify` | `xs:boolean` | `false` | Disable SSL/TLS certificate validation checks. |\r\n| `tls_server_name` | `xs:string` | Optional | SNI host verification override. |\r\n| `tls_min_version` | `xs:string` | `1.2` | Minimum TLS version (`1.0`, `1.1`, `1.2`, `1.3`). |\r\n| `tls_max_version` | `xs:string` | `1.3` | Maximum TLS version restriction. |\r\n| `tls_handshake_timeout` | `xs:string` | Optional | Maximum wait duration for TLS handshake. |\r\n| `disable_keep_alives` | `xs:boolean` | `false` | Disable HTTP keep-alive connection reuse. |\r\n| `disable_compression` | `xs:boolean` | `false` | Disable `Accept-Encoding: gzip` headers. |\r\n| `max_idle_conns` | `xs:nonNegativeInteger` | Default | Global maximum idle connection pool capacity. |\r\n| `max_idle_conns_per_host` | `xs:nonNegativeInteger` | Default | Host-level maximum idle connection limit. |\r\n| `max_conns_per_host` | `xs:nonNegativeInteger` | Unlimited | Total connection pool limit per host. |\r\n| `idle_conn_timeout` | `xs:string` | Optional | Maximum idle duration before closing pooled connection. |\r\n| `response_header_timeout` | `xs:string` | Optional | Wait duration limit for server response headers. |\r\n| `expect_continue_timeout` | `xs:string` | Optional | Wait time limit for `100-continue` header responses. |\r\n| `max_response_header_bytes` | `xs:long` | Default | Maximum allowed size of response headers. |\r\n| `write_buffer_size` | `xs:nonNegativeInteger` | Default | Size of the write buffer in bytes. |\r\n| `read_buffer_size` | `xs:nonNegativeInteger` | Default | Size of the read buffer in bytes. |\r\n| `force_attempt_http2` | `xs:boolean` | Default | Force HTTP/2 protocol negotiation on TLS connections. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Payload Resolution \u0026 Variable Interpolation\r\n\r\nWhen preparing an outgoing payload (e.g., `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`), the engine applies a two-tier resolution strategy:\r\n\r\n1. **Attribute Data (`data=\"...\"`)**: If the `data` attribute is populated, it is selected as the payload body. `{{VarName}}` tokens inside the attribute are interpolated.\r\n2. **Inner Body Content**: If `data` is omitted, inner XML text is processed. Any `{{VarName}}` tags are replaced with corresponding values from the registry context.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Control Flow \u0026 Loop Integration\r\n\r\n### 4.1 SQL Driver Iteration (`\u003cforeach\u003e`)\r\nInside a `\u003cforeach\u003e` block, every returned database row populates registry variables with column values (bound in exact, lowercase, and uppercase forms) alongside `LOOP_INDEX`. `\u003chttp_client\u003e` consumes these variables per iteration:\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cforeach id=\"IterateUsers\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\r\n    SELECT user_id, email, account_tier FROM users WHERE sync_pending = 1;\r\n\r\n    \u003chttp_client \r\n        id=\"PostUserToApi\"\r\n        uri=\"[https://api.example.com/v1/sync](https://api.example.com/v1/sync)\"\r\n        method=\"POST\"\r\n        content_type=\"application/json\"\r\n        data=\u0027{\"id\": {{user_id}}, \"email\": \"{{email}}\", \"tier\": \"{{account_tier}}\"}\u0027\r\n        output_var=\"ApiResponseBody\"\r\n        status_code_var=\"ApiResponseCode\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003csql id=\"LogSync\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\r\n        UPDATE users \r\n        SET synced = 1, sync_code = {{ApiResponseCode}} \r\n        WHERE user_id = {{user_id}};\r\n    \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n\u003c/foreach\u003e\r\n```\r\n### 4.2 Polling Loops (`\u003cwhile\u003e`)\r\nInside a `\u003cwhile\u003e` loop, `\u003chttp_client\u003e` can poll remote job status endpoints until a termination condition is met:\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"JobState\" type=\"string\" value=\"RUNNING\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"JobID\" type=\"string\" value=\"JOB-99201\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003cwhile id=\"PollStatus\" var=\"JobState\" equals=\"RUNNING\" max_iterations=\"20\"\u003e\r\n            \u003chttp_client \r\n                id=\"CheckStatus\"\r\n                uri=\"[https://api.example.com/v1/jobs/](https://api.example.com/v1/jobs/){{JobID}}\"\r\n                method=\"GET\"\r\n                output_var=\"JobState\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n            \u003cscript id=\"Sleep\" language=\"go\"\u003e\r\n                package main\r\n                import \"time\"\r\n                func main() { time.Sleep(2 * time.Second) }\r\n            \u003c/script\u003e\r\n        \u003c/while\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 4.3 Concurrent Requests (`\u003cparallel\u003e`)\r\nInside `\u003cparallel\u003e` blocks, each child branch runs in an isolated worker thread with a cloned registry snapshot. Output variables updated by `\u003chttp_client\u003e` in thread branches are automatically merged or namespaced (`WORKER_\u003cID\u003e_\u003cVAR\u003e`) back to the main registry upon completion.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cparallel max_threads=\"2\"\u003e\r\n    \u003chttp_client \r\n        id=\"FetchServiceA\"\r\n        uri=\"[https://service-a.internal/health](https://service-a.internal/health)\"\r\n        output_var=\"HealthA\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003chttp_client \r\n        id=\"FetchServiceB\"\r\n        uri=\"[https://service-b.internal/health](https://service-b.internal/health)\"\r\n        output_var=\"HealthB\" /\u003e\r\n\u003c/parallel\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 5. Comprehensive Pipeline Examples\r\n\r\n#### Example A: SQL -\u003e HTTP POST -\u003e Go Response Parser\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- 1. Extract JSON payload from database --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"GetPayload\" db=\"analytics_db\" output_var=\"JsonPayload\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT JSON_OBJECT(\u0027sensor_id\u0027: id, \u0027reading\u0027: value) \r\n            FROM telemetry \r\n            WHERE processed = 0 \r\n            LIMIT 1;\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 2. Post JSON payload to cloud endpoint --\u003e\r\n        \u003chttp_client \r\n            id=\"SendTelemetry\"\r\n            uri=\"[https://cloud.example.com/v1/telemetry](https://cloud.example.com/v1/telemetry)\"\r\n            method=\"POST\"\r\n            content_type=\"application/json\"\r\n            timeout=\"10s\"\r\n            data=\"{{JsonPayload}}\"\r\n            output_var=\"CloudResponse\"\r\n            status_code_var=\"CloudStatus\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 3. Parse cloud response in Go --\u003e\r\n        \u003cscript id=\"ParseResponse\" language=\"go\"\u003e\r\n            package main\r\n            import (\r\n                \"fmt\"\r\n                \"host/vars\"\r\n            )\r\n            func main() {\r\n                status := vars.GetInt(\"CloudStatus\")\r\n                resp := vars.GetString(\"CloudResponse\")\r\n                fmt.Printf(\"Uploaded with status %d: %s\\n\", status, resp)\r\n            }\r\n        \u003c/script\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n\r\n```\r\n#### Example B: Secure Enterprise Connection (TLS \u0026 Proxy Setup)\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003chttp_client \r\n            id=\"SecureEnterprisePost\"\r\n            uri=\"[https://secure.partner.com/api/v2/ingest](https://secure.partner.com/api/v2/ingest)\"\r\n            method=\"POST\"\r\n            content_type=\"application/json\"\r\n            headers=\"Authorization: Bearer secret-token-key, X-Client-ID: Pipeline01\"\r\n            proxy=\"[http://proxy.corp.internal:8080](http://proxy.corp.internal:8080)\"\r\n            tls_min_version=\"1.3\"\r\n            tls_insecure_skip_verify=\"false\"\r\n            tls_server_name=\"secure.partner.com\"\r\n            timeout=\"30s\"\r\n            max_idle_conns=\"50\"\r\n            output_var=\"IngestResult\"\r\n            status_code_var=\"IngestCode\"\u003e\r\n            {\r\n                \"timestamp\": \"{{CURRENT_TIMESTAMP}}\",\r\n                \"environment\": \"production\"\r\n            }\r\n        \u003c/http_client\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n\r\n```\r\n","if_then_else.md":"# Conditional Branching (`\u003cif\u003e`, `\u003cthen\u003e`, `\u003celse\u003e`) in Flow 🔀\n\nFlow pipelines support conditional routing through native `\u003cif\u003e` blocks. Conditional branching enables dynamic ETL decision-making, such as executing data ingestion only when source timestamps diverge, routing failed API responses to fallback webhooks, bypassing destructive migrations outside maintenance windows, or running alternative database queries based on environment variables.\n\n---\n\n## 🏗️ Architecture \u0026 Branching Model\n\nFlow\u0027s conditional execution engine supports two primary patterns:\n1. **Explicit Branch Containers (`\u003cif\u003e`, `\u003cthen\u003e`, `\u003celse\u003e`)**: A parent `\u003cif\u003e` block encapsulates two distinct sub-branches. When the condition evaluates to true, child nodes inside `\u003cthen\u003e` are executed. When false, child nodes inside `\u003celse\u003e` are executed.\n2. **Inline Step Predicates (`if_var`, `if_equals`, `condition`)**: Many individual nodes (such as `\u003cgroup\u003e`, `\u003csql\u003e`, `\u003cscript\u003e`, `\u003cforeach\u003e`) support conditional attributes directly on the element, conditionally skipping the node if the predicate is not met without wrapping it in an `\u003cif\u003e` container.\n\n### Execution Lifecycle \u0026 Flowchart\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Start([\"Pipeline Execution Flow\"]) --\u003e Step1[\"Pre-Branch Execution\u003cbr/\u003e(SQL Query / Script / HTTP Check)\"]\n    Step1 --\u003e IfCond{\"❓ If Condition\u003cbr/\u003e(var == equals OR condition)\"}\n    IfCond -- \"Yes / True\" --\u003e ThenBranch[\"\u003cthen\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eExecute Truthy Steps\"]\n    IfCond -- \"No / False\" --\u003e ElseBranch[\"\u003celse\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eExecute Falsy Steps / Fallback\"]\n    ThenBranch --\u003e Rejoin((\"Rejoin Flow\"))\n    ElseBranch --\u003e Rejoin\n    Rejoin --\u003e NextStep[\"Subsequent Pipeline Steps\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ XML Syntax \u0026 Attribute Reference\n\n```xml\n\u003cif id=\"CheckStatus\"\n    var=\"JobStatus\"\n    equals=\"COMPLETED\"\n    condition=\"eq .JobStatus \u0027COMPLETED\u0027\"\n    description=\"Evaluate pipeline state before downstream processing\"\u003e\n    \u003cthen\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Child nodes executed when condition is truthy --\u003e\n    \u003c/then\u003e\n    \u003celse\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Optional child nodes executed when condition is falsy --\u003e\n    \u003c/else\u003e\n\u003c/if\u003e\n```\n\n### Complete Attributes Reference\n\n| Attribute | Type | Required | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`id`** | `string` | Optional | Unique identifier for this conditional step. Recommended for tracing and observability logging. |\n| **`var`** | `string` | Optional | Variable name to evaluate from the pipeline variable registry. Case-insensitive lookup. |\n| **`if_var`** | `string` | Optional | Canonical alias for `var`. |\n| **`equals`** | `string` | Optional | Expected value to compare `var` against. Case-insensitive string comparison. Supports template placeholders (e.g. `equals=\"{{TargetTimestamp}}\"`). |\n| **`val`** / **`value`** | `string` | Optional | Canonical aliases for `equals`. |\n| **`if_val`** / **`if_equals`** | `string` | Optional | Canonical aliases for `equals`. |\n| **`condition`** | `string` | Optional | Dynamic boolean expression or template condition evaluated against the variable context. |\n| **`cond`** | `string` | Optional | Canonical alias for `condition`. |\n| **`description`** | `string` | Optional | Human-readable explanation of why this branch condition is being tested. |\n\n### Child Elements\n\n*   **`\u003cthen\u003e`** *(Optional, max 1)*: Container holding child nodes (`\u003csql\u003e`, `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`, `\u003cscript\u003e`, `\u003cgroup\u003e`, `\u003chttp_client\u003e`, etc.) executed strictly when the condition evaluates to **true**.\n*   **`\u003celse\u003e`** *(Optional, max 1)*: Container holding child nodes executed strictly when the condition evaluates to **false**.\n*   **Inline Child Nodes**: If `\u003cthen\u003e` is omitted, direct child elements placed inside `\u003cif\u003e` default to the **truthy** branch.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Evaluation Mechanisms\n\nFlow provides three common ways to formulate conditional predicates:\n\n### 1. Direct Variable Equality (`var` + `equals`)\nThe simplest and most resilient mode. Evaluates whether the variable in `var` matches `equals`:\n```xml\n\u003cif id=\"CheckActive\" var=\"IsActive\" equals=\"true\"\u003e\n    \u003cthen\u003e\n        \u003csql db=\"prod_db\"\u003eUPDATE Users SET Verified = 1 WHERE Active = 1;\u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/then\u003e\n\u003c/if\u003e\n```\n\nDynamic placeholder comparisons are also supported:\n```xml\n\u003cif id=\"CompareTimestamps\" var=\"SOURCE_MAX_DATE\" equals=\"{{TARGET_MAX_DATE}}\"\u003e\n    \u003cthen\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Tables are already identical --\u003e\n    \u003c/then\u003e\n    \u003celse\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Tables differ: trigger synchronization --\u003e\n    \u003c/else\u003e\n\u003c/if\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. Upstream Flag Computation (SQL `CASE WHEN` or Go Script)\nFor complex date arithmetic, regex matching, or multi-variable logic, compute a boolean flag in an upstream step and evaluate it:\n```xml\n\u003c!-- Step 1: SQL computation --\u003e\n\u003csql id=\"CheckStaleData\" db=\"primary_db\" output_var=\"NEEDS_SYNC\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n    SELECT CASE WHEN DATEDIFF(hour, MAX(ExportTime), GETDATE()) \u003e 24 THEN \u0027true\u0027 ELSE \u0027false\u0027 END AS NEEDS_SYNC\n    FROM dbo.SyncLog;\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/sql\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- Step 2: Conditional execution --\u003e\n\u003cif id=\"SyncIfStale\" var=\"NEEDS_SYNC\" equals=\"true\"\u003e\n    \u003cthen\u003e\n        \u003cgroup id=\"RunETL\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"primary_db\"\u003e\n            \u003c!-- Sync steps --\u003e\n        \u003c/group\u003e\n    \u003c/then\u003e\n\u003c/if\u003e\n```\n\n### 3. Expression Conditions (`condition` / `cond`)\nExpression strings evaluate comparison rules against the current variable map:\n```xml\n\u003cif id=\"CheckThreshold\" condition=\"{{PendingRecords}} \u003e 1000\"\u003e\n    \u003cthen\u003e\n        \u003c!-- High-priority batch worker --\u003e\n    \u003c/then\u003e\n\u003c/if\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Production Pipeline Examples\n\n### Example 1: Database Health \u0026 System DB Verification (`if_than_else.xml`)\nChecks if system databases exist. If true, runs Group A steps; otherwise runs Group B fallback steps.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Database verification and conditional execution\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"PrimaryConn\" type=\"string\"\n            value=\"sqlserver://PROD_SERVER:1433?database=AppDB\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\u0026amp;trustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"primary_db\" connection_string=\"{{PrimaryConn}}\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Step 1: Query database system catalog and set ScriptAResult to \u0027true\u0027 or \u0027false\u0027 --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"ScriptA\" db=\"primary_db\" output_var=\"ScriptAResult\"\n            description=\"Query system databases to check if count is greater than zero\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            SELECT CASE WHEN COUNT(*) \u003e 0 THEN \u0027true\u0027 ELSE \u0027false\u0027 END FROM sys.databases;\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 2: Explicit \u003cif\u003e / \u003cthen\u003e / \u003celse\u003e branching --\u003e\n        \u003cif id=\"RouteExecution\" var=\"ScriptAResult\" equals=\"true\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003cgroup id=\"GroupA\"\u003e\n                    \u003csql id=\"GroupA_Step1\" db=\"primary_db\" description=\"Execute primary workflow\"\u003e\n                        \u003c![CDATA[ SELECT \u0027Executing Primary Production Group\u0027 AS status; ]]\u003e\n                    \u003c/sql\u003e\n                    \u003cscript id=\"GroupA_Step2\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                        \u003c![CDATA[\n                        package main\n                        import \"fmt\"\n                        func main() { fmt.Println(\"Primary branch completed successfully\") }\n                        ]]\u003e\n                    \u003c/script\u003e\n                \u003c/group\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n            \u003celse\u003e\n                \u003cgroup id=\"GroupB\"\u003e\n                    \u003csql id=\"GroupB_Step1\" db=\"primary_db\" description=\"Fallback when no databases exist\"\u003e\n                        \u003c![CDATA[ SELECT \u0027Executing Fallback Workflow\u0027 AS status; ]]\u003e\n                    \u003c/sql\u003e\n                \u003c/group\u003e\n            \u003c/else\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Concurrent Multi-Database Timestamp Check \u0026 Delta Sync\nExecutes queries across two databases in `\u003cparallel\u003e`, uses a lightweight comparison script to set `NEEDS_ACTION`, and conditionally performs bulk streaming replication.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Parallel timestamp check with conditional delta replication\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TABLE\" type=\"string\" value=\"dbo.BillingExport\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"SOURCE_MAX_DATE\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TARGET_MAX_DATE\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"NEEDS_ACTION\" type=\"string\" value=\"false\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"source_db\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://SOURCE_SRV:1433?database=Analytics\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\u0026amp;trustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"target_db\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://TARGET_SRV:1433?database=Warehouse\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\u0026amp;trustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Concurrent timestamp queries across both servers --\u003e\n        \u003cparallel id=\"fetch_max_timestamps\" max_threads=\"2\" description=\"Query source and target databases in parallel\"\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"get_source_max\" db=\"source_db\" output_var=\"SOURCE_MAX_DATE\"\u003e\n                \u003c![CDATA[ SELECT ISNULL(MAX(ExportTime), \u00271970-01-01\u0027) AS SOURCE_MAX_DATE FROM {{TABLE}}; ]]\u003e\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"get_target_max\" db=\"target_db\" output_var=\"TARGET_MAX_DATE\"\u003e\n                \u003c![CDATA[ SELECT ISNULL(MAX(ExportTime), \u00271970-01-01\u0027) AS TARGET_MAX_DATE FROM {{TABLE}}; ]]\u003e\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Fast Go comparison script evaluating delta condition --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"compare_timestamps\" language=\"go\" output_variable=\"NEEDS_ACTION\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                src := vars.GetString(\"SOURCE_MAX_DATE\")\n                tgt := vars.GetString(\"TARGET_MAX_DATE\")\n                if src != \"\" \u0026\u0026 tgt != \"\" \u0026\u0026 src != tgt {\n                    fmt.Println(\"true\")\n                } else {\n                    fmt.Println(\"false\")\n                }\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Branch: Replicate delta when dates diverge, or log up-to-date notice --\u003e\n        \u003cif id=\"check_sync_action\" var=\"NEEDS_ACTION\" equals=\"true\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003csql_bulk id=\"stream_delta_to_target\" db=\"source_db\" target_db=\"target_db\" target_table=\"{{TABLE}}\"\n                    batch_size=\"25000\" tablock=\"true\" description=\"Replicate delta rows into warehouse\"\u003e\n                    \u003c![CDATA[\n                    SELECT * FROM {{TABLE}} WHERE ExportTime \u003e \u0027{{TARGET_MAX_DATE}}\u0027;\n                    ]]\u003e\n                \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n            \u003celse\u003e\n                \u003cscript id=\"log_in_sync\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                    \u003c![CDATA[\n                    package main\n                    import \"fmt\"\n                    func main() { fmt.Println(\"Target warehouse is fully up to date. No sync required.\") }\n                    ]]\u003e\n                \u003c/script\u003e\n            \u003c/else\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: External API Response Status \u0026 Fallback Service Router\nCalls a primary external microservice; if the HTTP response is 200, parses the payload and persists to SQL; otherwise routes to a backup service.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"HTTP API failover router\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"app_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./app_data.db\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Ingest from Primary Service --\u003e\n        \u003chttp_client id=\"fetch_primary_rates\"\n            url=\"https://primary.api.example.com/v1/rates\"\n            method=\"GET\"\n            into=\"RatesPayload\"\n            status_into=\"PrimaryStatus\"\n            timeout=\"5s\"\n            on_error=\"continue\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003cif id=\"route_api_response\" var=\"PrimaryStatus\" equals=\"200\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003cjson_path id=\"extract_primary\" source=\"RatesPayload\" query=\"$.rates.USD\" into=\"USDRate\" /\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"save_primary\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO ExchangeRates (Currency, Rate, Source) VALUES (\u0027USD\u0027, {{USDRate}}, \u0027PrimaryAPI\u0027);\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n            \u003celse\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Fallback to Secondary Microservice --\u003e\n                \u003chttp_client id=\"fetch_backup_rates\"\n                    url=\"https://backup.api.example.com/rates/latest\"\n                    method=\"GET\"\n                    into=\"BackupPayload\"\n                    status_into=\"BackupStatus\"\n                    timeout=\"10s\" /\u003e\n                \u003cjson_path id=\"extract_backup\" source=\"BackupPayload\" query=\"$.rate\" into=\"USDRate\" /\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"save_backup\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\n                    INSERT INTO ExchangeRates (Currency, Rate, Source) VALUES (\u0027USD\u0027, {{USDRate}}, \u0027BackupAPI\u0027);\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/else\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Environment Deployment Gate \u0026 Maintenance Window\nChecks whether the active deployment is running in `production` and whether `MAINTENANCE_WINDOW` is set to `open`.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Gated production batch operation\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DEPLOY_ENV\" type=\"string\" value=\"production\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"MAINTENANCE_WINDOW\" type=\"string\" value=\"open\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"warehouse\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./warehouse.db\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Gated Execution --\u003e\n        \u003cif id=\"gate_maintenance\" var=\"MAINTENANCE_WINDOW\" equals=\"open\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003cgroup id=\"PerformHeavyMaintenance\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"warehouse\"\u003e\n                    \u003csql id=\"rebuild_indexes\" db=\"warehouse\"\u003e\n                        VACUUM;\n                    \u003c/sql\u003e\n                    \u003cscript id=\"notify_ops\" language=\"powershell\"\u003e\n                        Write-Host \"Production maintenance completed successfully.\"\n                    \u003c/script\u003e\n                \u003c/group\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n            \u003celse\u003e\n                \u003cscript id=\"skip_maintenance\" language=\"powershell\"\u003e\n                    Write-Host \"Maintenance window is CLOSED. Skipping heavy index rebuild.\"\n                \u003c/script\u003e\n            \u003c/else\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: Local File Cache Gate with Conditional Download\nChecks if an output file exists and is populated. If not cached, downloads the dataset from a remote URL and caches it to disk.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Local file cache with conditional remote download\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"CacheFile\" type=\"string\" value=\"cache/catalog.json\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"CacheValid\" type=\"string\" value=\"false\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Check cache file on disk --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"check_cache_exists\" language=\"go\" output_variable=\"CacheValid\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n                \"os\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                path := vars.GetString(\"CacheFile\")\n                if fi, err := os.Stat(path); err == nil \u0026\u0026 fi.Size() \u003e 0 {\n                    fmt.Println(\"true\")\n                } else {\n                    fmt.Println(\"false\")\n                }\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003cif id=\"evaluate_cache\" var=\"CacheValid\" equals=\"true\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003cfile_read id=\"read_cache\" file=\"{{CacheFile}}\" into=\"CatalogData\" /\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n            \u003celse\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Fetch and write cache --\u003e\n                \u003chttp_client id=\"download_catalog\" url=\"https://api.example.com/catalog.json\" into=\"CatalogData\" /\u003e\n                \u003cfile_save id=\"persist_cache\" file=\"{{CacheFile}}\" var=\"CatalogData\" mode=\"overwrite\" /\u003e\n            \u003c/else\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","index.md":"---\ntitle: Flow (go-flow) Documentation Hub\ndescription: Single-Binary, Zero-Dependency ETL \u0026 Workflow Orchestration Engine in Go with an Embedded HTMX Visual Studio\n---\n\n# Flow (`go-flow`) Documentation Hub\n\n\u003e **Slashing ETL Madness with Go, HTMX, and Declarative Pipelines**  \n\u003e **Current Version:** `1.1.24` | **Runtime:** Single-Binary Go (`flow.exe` / `flow`) | **Visual Studio:** `flow -builder`\n\n**Flow** (`go-flow`) is an enterprise-grade, single-binary ETL and workflow orchestration engine written in Go. It combines declarative, Git-friendly XML pipeline definitions with an embedded HTMX visual web builder (`-builder`), multi-engine relational and Key-Value database connectors, $O(1)$-memory bulk streaming replication, in-process Go scripting (via Yaegi), cross-platform AES-256-GCM encryption, and multi-standard digital signatures.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Navigation\n\n- [1. Executive Overview \u0026 Why Flow?](#1-executive-overview--why-flow)\n- [2. Architecture \u0026 Execution Lifecycle](#2-architecture--execution-lifecycle)\n- [3. Complete Documentation Directory](#3-complete-documentation-directory)\n  - [3.1 Executive Summaries \u0026 Visual Tour](#31-executive-summaries--visual-tour)\n  - [3.2 CLI, Configuration Hierarchy \u0026 Remote Sources](#32-cli-configuration-hierarchy--remote-sources)\n  - [3.3 Security: Encryption \u0026 Digital Signatures](#33-security-encryption--digital-signatures)\n  - [3.4 Databases, Bulk ETL, Key-Value \u0026 Transactions](#34-databases-bulk-etl-key-value--transactions)\n  - [3.5 Control Flow, Loops, Concurrency \u0026 Quality Gates](#35-control-flow-loops-concurrency--quality-gates)\n  - [3.6 Scripting, Templates, HTTP \u0026 Data Formats](#36-scripting-templates-http--data-formats)\n  - [3.7 Observability, Telemetry, Failure Handling \u0026 Debugging](#37-observability-telemetry-failure-handling--debugging)\n  - [3.8 Developer \u0026 Extension Guides](#38-developer--extension-guides)\n- [4. Visual Pipeline Builder Studio (`-builder`)](#4-visual-pipeline-builder-studio--builder)\n- [5. Quick Start \u0026 Sample Pipeline](#5-quick-start--sample-pipeline)\n- [6. Complete AST Component Matrix](#6-complete-ast-component-matrix)\n- [7. Ecosystem CLI Reference (`flow`, `crypto_tool`, `db_importer`)](#7-ecosystem-cli-reference-flow-crypto_tool-db_importer)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Overview \u0026 Why Flow?\n\nTraditional enterprise data orchestration forces teams to choose between heavyweight distributed clusters (Apache Airflow, Dagster, Prefect) that require dedicated Kubernetes/Celery infrastructure, or legacy proprietary GUI monoliths (SSIS, Informatica) that produce opaque, un-diffable binary files.\n\n**Flow** eliminates both extremes by compiling the entire orchestration runtime, database drivers, scripting interpreter, cryptographic verifier, and visual web studio into a **single zero-dependency binary**.\n\n| Capability | Traditional Python / Cluster (Airflow, Prefect) | Legacy Enterprise GUI (SSIS, Informatica) | **Flow (`go-flow`)** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Deployment Footprint** | Multi-container cluster, Redis/Celery, Python venvs | Heavy Windows server licenses \u0026 proprietary agents | **Single static binary (~30 MB), zero runtime dependencies** |\n| **Authoring Experience** | Imperative Python code only | Desktop-only drag-and-drop | **Bidirectional: Embedded Web UI (`-builder`) + Git-friendly XML** |\n| **High-Volume Bulk ETL** | Memory-heavy DataFrames (`pandas`) | Fast, but locked to specific vendor ecosystems | **$O(1)$ constant-memory streaming (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`, `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`)** |\n| **In-Process Scripting** | Native Python (dependency conflicts common) | C# / VB (requires compilation/GAC) | **Embedded Go (Yaegi) + PowerShell, Bash, Cmd, Zsh, CSX** |\n| **Cryptographic Security** | External vaults / custom plugins | OS-bound DPAPI encryption | **Cross-platform AES-256-GCM + OpenSSL/PKCS#7/OpenPGP signatures** |\n| **Audit \u0026 Observability** | Requires external Postgres + log forwarders | Proprietary logging tables | **Plug-and-play `log_db` sink, JSONL, \u0026 real-time stream events** |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architecture \u0026 Execution Lifecycle\n\nEvery pipeline run follows a deterministic, security-first execution pipeline: **Verify $\\rightarrow$ Decrypt $\\rightarrow$ Resolve Configuration Hierarchy $\\rightarrow$ Validate Schema/AST $\\rightarrow$ Execute Preflight $\\rightarrow$ Execute Flow $\\rightarrow$ Emit Audit Telemetry**.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Sources[\"1. Uniform Resource Loader\"]\n        S1[\"Local Filesystem\u003cbr/\u003e(scripts.xml / UTF-8 / UTF-16)\"]\n        S2[\"HTTP / HTTPS Endpoints\u003cbr/\u003e(https://...)\"]\n        S3[\"Database Repositories\u003cbr/\u003e(sql:// or db:// DSN Query)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Security[\"2. Decoupled Cryptographic Gate\"]\n        V[\"Digital Signature Verification\u003cbr/\u003e(OpenSSL X.509 / PKCS#7 .p7s / OpenPGP .asc / FLOWSIGNED:v1)\"]\n        D[\"AES-256-GCM Decryption\u003cbr/\u003e(PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 / FLOWENC:v1)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Config[\"3. Configuration \u0026 Validation\"]\n        H[\"Hierarchy Merge\u003cbr/\u003eBase (--file) \u0026lt; Options (--options) \u0026lt; Config (--config) \u0026lt; CLI (--vars)\"]\n        X[\"XSD \u0026amp; Semantic AST Validation\u003cbr/\u003e(--validate / pipeline.xsd)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Runtime[\"4. Execution Engine\"]\n        PRE[\"Preflight Quality Gates\u003cbr/\u003e(\u0026lt;preflight\u0026gt; / --preflight)\"]\n        FLOW[\"Main Workflow Execution\u003cbr/\u003e(\u0026lt;flow\u0026gt;: Sequential, \u0026lt;group transaction=\u0027true\u0027\u0026gt;, \u0026lt;parallel\u0026gt;, \u0026lt;foreach\u0026gt;, \u0026lt;while\u0026gt;)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Telemetry[\"5. Observability \u0026amp; Output\"]\n        OUT[\"Console Renderer\u003cbr/\u003e(csv, text, json, jsonpretty, markdown, stream)\"]\n        LOG[\"Database Audit Sink (log_db)\u003cbr/\u003e(pipeline_runs \u0026amp; pipeline_events)\"]\n        DOC[\"Auto-Documentation\u003cbr/\u003e(Mermaid.js Flowchart + XSLT 3.0)\"]\n    end\n\n    S1 \u0026 S2 \u0026 S3 --\u003e V\n    V --\u003e D\n    D --\u003e H\n    H --\u003e X\n    X --\u003e PRE\n    PRE --\u003e FLOW\n    FLOW --\u003e OUT \u0026 LOG \u0026 DOC\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complete Documentation Directory\n\nAll documentation in the `docs/` repository is organized below by topic.\n\n### 3.1 Executive Summaries \u0026 Visual Tour\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Business \u0026 Executive Summary**](BUSINESS_SUMMARY.md) | Architectural value proposition, TCO reduction, security posture, and enterprise comparison matrix. |\n| [**Automation \u0026 ETL Architecture Summary**](guides/AUTOMATION_ETL_SUMMARY.md) | Condensed technical overview of CLI flags, AST primitives, SQL dialect parameter binding, and telemetry schemas. |\n| [**Visual Builder Slideshow (Markdown)**](BUILDER_SLIDESHOW.md) | Step-by-step visual walkthrough of the 3-panel HTMX studio, modals, themes, and runner dashboard. |\n| [**Interactive Builder Slideshow (HTML)**](builder_slideshow.html) | Standalone interactive browser presentation showcasing the Flow Builder UI. |\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 CLI, Configuration Hierarchy \u0026 Remote Sources\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Complete CLI Reference (`flow`, `crypto_tool`, `db_importer`)**](guides/COMPLETE_CLI_OPTIONS_AND_ARGUMENTS.md) | Exhaustive flag reference, secret precedence rules, exit codes, and enterprise recipes across all three binaries. |\n| [**Summary of Available Pipeline XML Options**](guides/SUMMARY_OF_AVAILABLE_OPTIONS.md) | Complete XML element and attribute specification for `\u003cpipeline\u003e`, `\u003cdatabases\u003e`, `\u003cvariables\u003e`, `\u003cpreflight\u003e`, and `\u003cflow\u003e`. |\n| [**Configuration \u0026 Execution Hierarchy**](guides/CONFIG_HIERARCHY.md) | How Flow merges `--file`, `--options`, `--config`, and `--vars` across Dev, Staging, and Production environments. |\n| [**Variables Container (`\u003cvariables\u003e`)**](variables.md) | Declaring typed variable collections (`string`, `int`, `float`, `bool`, `date`, `datetime`) and recursive `{{var}}` expansion. |\n| [**Variable Element (`\u003cvariable\u003e`)**](variable.md) | Detailed reference for individual `\u003cvariable\u003e` definitions, type parsing, and runtime overrides. |\n| [**Remote File, HTTP \u0026 SQL DSN Sources**](guides/FLOW_WITH_REMOTE_FILE_SOURCE.md) | Loading pipelines, options, and configs from local paths, HTTP/HTTPS (with Basic Auth), and `sql://` or `db://` queries. |\n| [**Database-Backed XML Storage**](guides/README_DATABASE_XML.md) | Storing and executing `PipelineXML`, `OptionsXML`, and `ConfigXML` inside SQL Server (`dbo.flow_*_content`) via `db_importer`. |\n| [**UTF-8 \u0026 UTF-16 Encoding Guide**](guides/README_UTF.md) | Automatic BOM detection and transcoding for Windows PowerShell UTF-16LE files and SQL Server `NVARCHAR(MAX)` columns. |\n| [**Using Pipeline \u0026 Node Descriptions**](guides/USING_DESCRIPTIONS.md) | Best practices for self-documenting pipelines using `description` attributes rendered in logs and Mermaid flowcharts. |\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 Security: Encryption \u0026 Digital Signatures\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Security Order of Operations \u0026 Options**](guides/SECURITY_ORDER_AND_OPTIONS.md) | Deep dive into **Encrypt-then-Sign** vs. **Sign-then-Encrypt** vs. **Detached Signatures**, key precedence, and security flags. |\n| [**Single-File Cryptography (Combined Encryption \u0026 Signatures)**](guides/COMBINED_ENCRYPTION_AND_SIGNATURES.md) | End-to-end Windows (PowerShell/Cert Store) and Linux/macOS (OpenSSL/Bash) walkthroughs for unified `.sec` packages (`-wrap`). |\n| [**Cross-Platform Encryption Guide (`FLOWENC:v1:`)**](guides/ENCRYPTION.md) | AES-256-GCM architecture, PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 key derivation, memory zeroization, and at-rest database encryption. |\n| [**Cross-Platform Digital Signatures Guide (`FLOWSIG:v1:`)**](guides/SIGNATURES.md) | Pure-Go verification and signing with OpenSSL (RSA/ECDSA/Ed25519), PKCS#7/CMS (`.p7s`), and OpenPGP/GPG (`.asc`). |\n\n---\n\n### 3.4 Databases, Bulk ETL, Key-Value \u0026 Transactions\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Database Connections \u0026 Pooling (`\u003cdatabase\u003e`)**](database.md) | Configuring relational and KV drivers, connection pool tuning (`max_open_conns`, `max_idle_conns`, `workload`), and dialect rules. |\n| [**Database Connection Strings Reference**](database_connection_strings.md) | Copy-paste connection string templates for SQL Server (including Azure Entra ID `fedauth`), PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQLite, and KV stores. |\n| [**SQL Execution (`\u003csql\u003e`)**](sql.md) | Executing DDL, DML, and `SELECT` queries with intelligent statement classification (`isDMLQuery`), parameter binding, and `output_var`. |\n| [**High-Speed Bulk ETL (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`)**](sql_bulk.md) | $O(1)$ constant-memory cross-database streaming replication, native SQL Server TDS `CopyIn` (`tablock`), and batch tuning. |\n| [**Comparing `\u003csql\u003e` and `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`**](sql_and_sql_bulk.md) | Architectural comparison and decision guide for choosing between standard `\u003csql\u003e` execution and `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` streaming. |\n| [**Database Transactions \u0026 Savepoints**](guides/DATABASE_TRANSACTIONS.md) | Atomic `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` blocks, dialect-aware nested savepoints, parallel transaction isolation, and bulk copy caveats. |\n| [**Key-Value Store Operations (`\u003ckv\u003e` \u0026 `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`)**](kv.md) | Unified NoSQL interface for **bbolt**, **BadgerDB**, **Redis/Valkey**, and **etcd**, plus SQL-to-KV bulk streaming (`\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`). |\n| [**DB Select, Template \u0026 Stdio Pattern**](db_select_template_stdio.md) | End-to-end guide for querying relational data, formatting rows via `\u003ctemplate\u003e`, and streaming to standard output. |\n\n---\n\n### 3.5 Control Flow, Loops, Concurrency \u0026 Quality Gates\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Preflight Validation Gates (`\u003cpreflight\u003e`)**](preflight.md) | Pre-execution environment checks, connectivity verification, and schema readiness assertions before `\u003cflow\u003e` begins. |\n| [**Sequential \u0026 Transactional Grouping (`\u003cgroup\u003e`)**](group.md) | Organizing steps into logical units with optional conditional execution (`if_var`/`if_equals`) and atomic transactions. |\n| [**Conditional Branching (`\u003cif\u003e`, `\u003cthen\u003e`, `\u003celse\u003e`)**](if_then_else.md) | Dynamic branching using variable equality (`var`/`equals`) or expression evaluation (`condition`). |\n| [**Dataset Iteration (`\u003cforeach\u003e` / `\u003cloop\u003e`)**](foreach.md) | Row-by-row SQL cursor iteration in $O(1)$ streaming mode (`stream=\"true\"`) or in-memory buffered mode with automatic column binding. |\n| [**Conditional Loops (`\u003cwhile\u003e`)**](while.md) | State-driven polling and pagination loops with `WHILE_INDEX` tracking and `max_iterations` safety guards. |\n| [**Concurrent Worker Pools (`\u003cparallel\u003e`)**](parallel.md) | Multi-threaded execution (`max_threads`) with isolated variable cloning, cooperative cancellation, and collision namespacing. |\n| [**Declarative Assertions (`\u003cassert\u003e`)**](assert.md) | Data quality gates with `halt` vs. `warn` policies, failure flag variables (`fail_var`), and nested `\u003con_failure\u003e` recovery blocks. |\n\n---\n\n### 3.6 Scripting, Templates, HTTP \u0026 Data Formats\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Polyglot Scripting (`\u003cscript\u003e`)**](script.md) | In-process pure-Go **Yaegi** interpreter (`host/vars`, `host/db`) plus external shells (`pwsh`, `powershell`, `bash`, `sh`, `cmd`, `zsh`, `csx`). |\n| [**Text Templates (`\u003ctemplate\u003e`)**](template.md) | Dynamic SQL, JSON, and report generation using Go\u0027s `text/template` engine and helper functions. |\n| [**HTML Templates (`\u003ctemplate_html\u003e`)**](template_html.md) | Context-aware XSS-safe HTML email and dashboard rendering using Go\u0027s `html/template` engine. |\n| [**Comparing `\u003ctemplate\u003e` and `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e`**](template_and_template_html.md) | Side-by-side comparison of raw text interpolation vs. automatic HTML contextual escaping. |\n| [**HTTP / REST Client (`\u003chttp_client\u003e`)**](http_client.md) | Full-featured REST integration with custom headers, TLS/mTLS tuning, proxies, cookie jars, redirects, and status capture. |\n| [**JSONPath Extraction (`\u003cjson_path\u003e`)**](json_path.md) | Extracting scalars, objects, and arrays from API responses or files using JSONPath expressions. |\n| [**JSONPath Syntax \u0026 Examples Guide**](json_path_guide.md) | Comprehensive tutorial on JSONPath operators, recursive descent, array slicing, and filter expressions in Flow. |\n| [**XPath Extraction (`\u003cxml_xpath\u003e`)**](xml_path.md) | Querying XML documents and SOAP payloads with XPath expressions in `text`, `xml`, or `json_array` output modes. |\n| [**YAML Path Extraction (`\u003cyaml_path\u003e`)**](yaml_path.md) | Extracting configuration values and structured nodes from YAML files or variables. |\n| [**Excel Workbook Reader (`\u003cexcel_read\u003e`)**](excel_read.md) | Reading `.xlsx` worksheets into structured JSON arrays for downstream iteration and transformation. |\n| [**Excel Workbook Writer (`\u003cexcel_write\u003e`)**](excel_write.md) | Exporting SQL query results directly into multi-tab `.xlsx` spreadsheets with automatic header generation. |\n| [**File Reader (`\u003cfile_read\u003e`)**](file_read.md) | Loading local text, JSON, XML, or SQL files into pipeline variables. |\n| [**File Writer (`\u003cfile_save\u003e`)**](file_save.md) | Writing or appending pipeline variables and inline templates to disk with automatic directory creation. |\n\n---\n\n### 3.7 Observability, Telemetry, Failure Handling \u0026 Debugging\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Database Logging Configuration (`log_db`)**](guides/DATABASE_LOGGING_CONFIGURATION.md) | DDL schemas for PostgreSQL, SQL Server, MySQL, Oracle, and SQLite (`pipeline_runs` \u0026 `pipeline_events`), session auditing (`spid`, `db_user_name`), and `-node-output`. |\n| [**Failure Response \u0026 Error Propagation**](guides/FAILURE_RESPONSE.md) | Fail-fast semantics, cooperative `\u003cparallel\u003e` cancellation, cursor cleanup, transaction rollbacks, and `\u003cassert\u003e` recovery paths. |\n| [**Comprehensive Pipeline Debugging Guide**](guides/DEBUGGING_GUIDE.md) | 5 production debugging patterns (variable dumps, interactive operator prompts, loop tracing, `\u003cwhile\u003e` state mutation, and `\u003cassert\u003e` post-mortems) in Go and C# CSX. |\n| [**Observability \u0026 Event Sinks Usage Guide**](guides/development_observability_usage.md) | Programmatic guide to `ExecuteRun`, `JSONLineSink`, multi-sink fanout (`SetEventSinks`), row count metrics, and custom OpenTelemetry bridges. |\n\n---\n\n### 3.8 Developer, Build \u0026 Extension Guides\n\n| Document | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [**Compilation, Git Workflow \u0026 Build Options Guide**](guides/COMPILATION_AND_BUILD_GUIDE.md) | Complete guide to cloning via Git, checking out release tags, co-developing with `flow`, cross-compiling (`GOOS`/`GOARCH`), and all supported `go build` options. |\n| [**Executing Flow Pipelines (`Execute` vs `ExecuteRun`)**](guides/development_execution_guide.md) | Go SDK guide comparing flat `[]ScriptResult` execution with structured `RunResult` observability trees. |\n| [**Builder Modal Tooltips \u0026 Documentation Popups**](guides/development_builder_modal_tooltips_guide.md) | Step-by-step developer guide for adding interactive markdown documentation modals and `⚡ Use Example` autofill buttons to the Builder UI. |\n| [**Builder Theme Customization Guide**](guides/development_themes_guide.md) | Architecture of the 15 built-in UI themes (`slate`, `vscode-dark-plus`, `visual-studio-light`, `soft-white`, etc.) and how to add new themes. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Visual Pipeline Builder Studio (`-builder`)\n\nLaunching `flow -builder` starts an embedded HTMX web studio backed by a local SQLite project store (`flow_builder.db`).\n\n```bash\n# Launch the visual builder on an ephemeral port (or specify -builder-port 8080)\n./flow -builder -builder-port 8080\n```\n\n### Unified 3-Panel Studio (Default Slate Theme)\nThe interface provides a **Component Palette** (left), **Interactive 4-Phase Canvas** (center: *Variables*, *Databases*, *Preflight*, *Main Flow*), and **Live Bidirectional XML Preview** (right):\n\n![Flow Builder Default Slate Theme](screenshots/Builder_Default_Theme.png)\n\n### Live Pipeline Runner \u0026 Real-Time Telemetry\nSwitch to the **Runner** tab to execute preflight checks or full pipelines with live streaming event logs, execution duration tracking, and optional database output capture (`-node-output`):\n\n![Flow Builder Live Runner](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Execute_Options_File_Runner.png)\n\n### Built-In IDE Themes (15 Themes Included)\nCustomize the workspace appearance with 15 built-in themes persisted in `localStorage`, including **Visual Studio Light**, **Visual Studio Dark**, **VS Code Dark+**, **Nord Frost**, and **Soft White**:\n\n| Visual Studio Light Theme | Visual Studio Dark Theme |\n| :---: | :---: |\n| ![Visual Studio Light Theme](screenshots/Builder_VS_Light_Theme.png) | ![Visual Studio Dark Theme](screenshots/Builder_VS_Dark_Theme.png) |\n\n\u003eExplore the full visual tour in the [**Builder Slideshow Guide**](BUILDER_SLIDESHOW.md) or open the [**Interactive HTML Slideshow**](builder_slideshow.html).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Quick Start \u0026 Sample Pipeline\n\n### 1. Build or Run Flow\n```bash\n# Build the core engine\ngo build -o flow.exe .\n\n# Build the companion cryptographic utility (optional)\ngo build -o crypto_tool.exe ./crypto_tool\n```\n\n### 2. Author a Declarative Pipeline (`pipeline.xml`)\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Customer Analytics Sync \u0026amp; Executive Report\"\n          xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/go-flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"MinSpend\" type=\"float\" value=\"5000.00\" description=\"Minimum customer spend filter\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ReportPath\" type=\"string\" value=\"./reports/vip_customers.xlsx\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"source_pg\" driver=\"postgres\"\n                  connection_string=\"postgres://app:{{PG_PASS}}@localhost:5432/commerce?sslmode=disable\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"warehouse_mssql\" driver=\"sqlserver\"\n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://sa:{{MSSQL_PASS}}@localhost:1433?database=AnalyticsDW\u0026amp;trustServerCertificate=true\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"log_db\" driver=\"sqlite\"\n                  connection_string=\"file:./telemetry_audit.db?_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cpreflight\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"ping_source\" db=\"source_pg\" output_var=\"SourceReady\"\u003eSELECT \u0027OK\u0027;\u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"verify_source\" var=\"SourceReady\" equals=\"OK\" message=\"Source PostgreSQL database is unreachable!\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/preflight\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- O(1) Memory Bulk Streaming Replication from PostgreSQL to SQL Server --\u003e\n        \u003csql_bulk id=\"stream_vip_orders\"\n                  db=\"source_pg\"\n                  target_db=\"warehouse_mssql\"\n                  target_table=\"dbo.StagingVIPOrders\"\n                  batch_size=\"10000\"\n                  tablock=\"true\"\n                  output_var=\"RowsCopied\"\u003e\n            SELECT order_id, customer_id, order_date, total_amount\n            FROM orders\n            WHERE total_amount \u003e= {{MinSpend}};\n        \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Atomic Transaction Group in Warehouse --\u003e\n        \u003cgroup id=\"merge_warehouse_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"warehouse_mssql\"\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"upsert_vip_summary\" db=\"warehouse_mssql\"\u003e\n                MERGE INTO dbo.FactVIPOrders AS target\n                USING dbo.StagingVIPOrders AS src ON target.order_id = src.order_id\n                WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET total_amount = src.total_amount\n                WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (order_id, customer_id, order_date, total_amount)\n                VALUES (src.order_id, src.customer_id, src.order_date, src.total_amount);\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"truncate_staging\" db=\"warehouse_mssql\"\u003e\n                TRUNCATE TABLE dbo.StagingVIPOrders;\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/group\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Export Summary directly to Excel Workbook --\u003e\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"export_vip_excel\" file=\"{{ReportPath}}\" sheet=\"VIP Orders\" db=\"warehouse_mssql\"\u003e\n            SELECT customer_id, COUNT(order_id) AS order_count, SUM(total_amount) AS total_spent\n            FROM dbo.FactVIPOrders\n            GROUP BY customer_id\n            ORDER BY total_spent DESC;\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 3. Validate, Preflight, and Execute\n```bash\n# 1. Validate XML Schema and Semantic AST without executing\n./flow -file pipeline.xml -xsd ./xsd/pipeline.xsd -validate\n\n# 2. Run Preflight Checks only\n./flow -file pipeline.xml -preflight\n\n# 3. Execute Pipeline with Runtime Variable Overrides and Live Stream Output\n./flow -file pipeline.xml -vars \"MinSpend=10000.00\" -format stream\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Complete AST Component Matrix\n\n| Category | XML Element | Primary Purpose | Detailed Guide |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Setup \u0026 Config** | `\u003cvariables\u003e` / `\u003cvariable\u003e` | Typed variable definitions (`string`, `int`, `float`, `bool`, `date`, `datetime`) | [variables.md](variables.md) / [variable.md](variable.md) |\n| **Setup \u0026 Config** | `\u003cdatabases\u003e` / `\u003cdatabase\u003e` | Relational \u0026 Key-Value connection pools with dynamic `{{var}}` DSNs | [database.md](database.md) |\n| **Quality Gate** | `\u003cpreflight\u003e` | Pre-execution validation and environment readiness checks | [preflight.md](preflight.md) |\n| **Control Flow** | `\u003cgroup\u003e` | Sequential container with optional conditional check and atomic `transaction=\"true\"` | [group.md](group.md) |\n| **Control Flow** | `\u003cif\u003e` / `\u003cthen\u003e` / `\u003celse\u003e` | Dynamic conditional branching on variables or expressions | [if_then_else.md](if_then_else.md) |\n| **Control Flow** | `\u003cforeach\u003e` / `\u003cloop\u003e` | SQL dataset cursor iteration ($O(1)$ streaming or buffered) | [foreach.md](foreach.md) |\n| **Control Flow** | `\u003cwhile\u003e` | Conditional polling / pagination loop with `max_iterations` guard | [while.md](while.md) |\n| **Control Flow** | `\u003cparallel\u003e` | Concurrent worker pool (`max_threads`) with thread-isolated variables | [parallel.md](parallel.md) |\n| **Quality Gate** | `\u003cassert\u003e` | State invariant check (`halt` or `warn`) with `\u003con_failure\u003e` fallback block | [assert.md](assert.md) |\n| **Database \u0026 KV** | `\u003csql\u003e` | Single or multi-statement SQL query/DML execution | [sql.md](sql.md) |\n| **Database \u0026 KV** | `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` | High-speed $O(1)$ cross-database streaming bulk copy | [sql_bulk.md](sql_bulk.md) |\n| **Database \u0026 KV** | `\u003ckv\u003e` / `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e` | NoSQL Key-Value operations \u0026 SQL-to-KV bulk streaming (`bbolt`, `badger`, `redis`, `etcd`) | [kv.md](kv.md) |\n| **Code \u0026 Templates** | `\u003cscript\u003e` | Embedded Go (`Yaegi`) \u0026 shell scripts (`pwsh`, `bash`, `cmd`, `zsh`, `csx`) | [script.md](script.md) |\n| **Code \u0026 Templates** | `\u003ctemplate\u003e` | Go `text/template` dynamic text/SQL/JSON rendering | [template.md](template.md) |\n| **Code \u0026 Templates** | `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` | Go `html/template` XSS-safe HTML rendering | [template_html.md](template_html.md) |\n| **Web \u0026 Extraction** | `\u003chttp_client\u003e` | REST/HTTP client with TLS, proxy, timeout, and header controls | [http_client.md](http_client.md) |\n| **Web \u0026 Extraction** | `\u003cjson_path\u003e` | JSONPath query extraction (`value`, `json`, `json_array`) | [json_path.md](json_path.md) |\n| **Web \u0026 Extraction** | `\u003cxml_xpath\u003e` | XPath query extraction (`text`, `xml`, `json_array`) | [xml_path.md](xml_path.md) |\n| **Web \u0026 Extraction** | `\u003cyaml_path\u003e` | YAML path query extraction (`value`, `json`, `json_array`, `yaml`) | [yaml_path.md](yaml_path.md) |\n| **Files \u0026 Excel** | `\u003cexcel_read\u003e` | `.xlsx` worksheet reader to JSON array | [excel_read.md](excel_read.md) |\n| **Files \u0026 Excel** | `\u003cexcel_write\u003e` | SQL query exporter to multi-sheet `.xlsx` workbooks | [excel_write.md](excel_write.md) |\n| **Files \u0026 Excel** | `\u003cfile_read\u003e` | Local file reader into pipeline variable | [file_read.md](file_read.md) |\n| **Files \u0026 Excel** | `\u003cfile_save\u003e` | Local file writer / appender from variable or inline content | [file_save.md](file_save.md) |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Ecosystem CLI Reference (`flow`, `crypto_tool`, `db_importer`)\n\nFor full command-line documentation, see [**Complete CLI Options and Arguments**](guides/COMPLETE_CLI_OPTIONS_AND_ARGUMENTS.md).\n\n### Core Engine Flags (`flow.exe` / `flow`)\n\n| Flag | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `-file \u003cpath\\|uri\u003e` | `scripts.xml` | Pipeline XML path, HTTP(S) URL, or `sql://` / `db://` database query URI. |\n| `-options \u003cpath\\|uri\u003e` | `\"\"` | XML options profile defining default CLI flags (`\u003cflow_cli_options\u003e`). |\n| `-config \u003cpath\\|uri\u003e` | `\"\"` | Environment override XML (`CONFIG.xml`) for variables and databases. |\n| `-vars \"K=V,...\"` | `\"\"` | Runtime variable overrides (highest precedence; recursive expansion up to 3 levels). |\n| `-format \u003cfmt\u003e` | `csv` | Output renderer: `csv`, `text`, `json`, `jsonpretty`, `markdown`, or `stream`. |\n| `-validate` | `false` | Validates XML schema (`-xsd`) and AST semantics without executing nodes. |\n| `-preflight` | `false` | Executes `\u003cpreflight\u003e` nodes only and exits. |\n| `-builder` | `false` | Launches the interactive HTMX visual pipeline studio web server. |\n| `-builder-port \u003cint\u003e` | `0` | Port for the visual builder web server (`0` selects an ephemeral port). |\n| `-encrypted` | `false` | Decrypts AES-256-GCM input resources (auto-detected for `FLOWENC:v1:` payloads). |\n| `-key-file \u003cpath\u003e` | `\"\"` | Path to symmetric key/passphrase file (recommended over `-secure-key`). |\n| `-verify-signature` | `false` | Enforces digital signature verification before decryption or execution. |\n| `-public-key` / `-cert` | `\"\"` | Public key (`.pem`) or X.509 certificate (`.pem`/`.cer`) for signature verification. |\n| `-node-output` | `false` | Persists redacted node outputs into `pipeline_events.node_output` when `log_db` is active. |\n| `-xslt \u003cpath\u003e` / `-out` | `\"\"` | Transforms pipeline XML via XSLT 3.0 with an injected Mermaid.js diagram. |\n| `-debug` | `false` | Enables verbose diagnostic logging. |\n","json_path.md":"# Guide: JSONPath Extraction Node (`\u003cjson_path\u003e`) \r\n\r\nThe `\u003cjson_path\u003e` node provides lightweight, native querying and filtering over JSON documents in the Flow Pipeline Engine. It supports reading inputs from files or environment variables, evaluates expressions using JSONPath syntax, and structures outputs in multiple formats.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## ⚙️ Node Reference\r\n\r\n### Attributes\r\n- `id` (string, optional): Unique step identifier for logging and tracking.\r\n- `file` (string, optional): Path to the source JSON file on disk. Supports variable interpolation (e.g. `{{json_path}}`).\r\n- `var` (string, optional): Name of the pipeline variable containing the raw JSON string to query.\r\n- `jsonpath` or `path` (string, optional): The JSONPath query expression to execute.\r\n- `mode` (string, optional): Defines the formatting style of the output:\r\n  - `\"value\"` (default): Returns string values or stringified raw nodes. Multiple matched nodes are joined by newlines.\r\n  - `\"json\"`: Returns a single JSON-serialized element (if exactly one is matched).\r\n  - `\"json_array\"`: Returns all matching elements serialized as a single JSON array list.\r\n- `output_var` or `out_var` (string, required): Variable key to store the resulting output.\r\n\r\n\u003e [!NOTE]\r\n\u003e If neither `jsonpath` nor `path` attributes are defined, the engine automatically extracts the JSONPath expression from the inner tag text of the node.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 Examples\r\n\r\nThe following examples demonstrate common workflows using `\u003cjson_path\u003e`.\r\n\r\n### Example 1: Standard Extraction (Default `\"value\"` Mode)\r\nExtracts simple scalar values from an external file and joins the matched results into a newline-separated string.\r\n\r\n**Source JSON File (`books.json`):**\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"store\": {\r\n    \"books\": [\r\n      { \"title\": \"Sayings of the Century\", \"category\": \"reference\" },\r\n      { \"title\": \"Sword of Honour\", \"category\": \"fiction\" }\r\n    ]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariable name=\"src_file\" type=\"string\" value=\"books.json\" /\u003e\r\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n\u003cflow\u003e\r\n  \u003c!-- Query all titles in default text-join mode --\u003e\r\n  \u003cjson_path id=\"get_titles\" file=\"{{src_file}}\" jsonpath=\"$.store.books[*].title\" output_var=\"book_titles\" /\u003e\r\n\u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Resulting Output (`book_titles`):**\r\n```text\r\nSayings of the Century\r\nSword of Honour\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 2: Extracting Lists (`mode=\"json_array\"`)\r\nExtracts and serialized matched objects or numeric arrays as a structured JSON array suitable for passing to downstream APIs.\r\n\r\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n  \u003cflow\u003e\r\n  \u003c!-- Extract all book categories as a serialized JSON string array --\u003e\r\n  \u003cjson_path id=\"get_categories\" \r\n             file=\"books.json\" \r\n             jsonpath=\"$.store.books[*].category\" \r\n             mode=\"json_array\" \r\n             output_var=\"categories_json\" /\u003e\r\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Resulting Output (`categories_json`):**\r\n```json\r\n[\"reference\", \"fiction\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 3: Extracting Single Objects (`mode=\"json\"`)\r\nExtracts a specific nested object node and serializes it directly to a clean JSON string block.\r\n\r\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n  \u003cflow\u003e\r\n  \u003c!-- Extract only the first book object --\u003e\r\n  \u003cjson_path id=\"get_first_book\" \r\n             file=\"books.json\" \r\n             jsonpath=\"$.store.books[0]\" \r\n             mode=\"json\" \r\n             output_var=\"first_book\" /\u003e\r\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Resulting Output (`first_book`):**\r\n```json\r\n{\"title\": \"Sayings of the Century\", \"category\": \"reference\"}\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 4: Expression Filters \u0026 Variable Interpolation\r\nCombines expression-based filtering (such as price filters) with dynamic variable interpolation to query a document based on runtime pipeline values.\r\n\r\n**Source JSON File (`store.json`):**\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"store\": {\r\n    \"bicycle\": { \"color\": \"red\", \"price\": 19.95 },\r\n    \"unicycle\": { \"color\": \"blue\", \"price\": 45.00 }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariable name=\"max_price\" type=\"string\" value=\"25.00\" /\u003e\r\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n\r\n  \u003cflow\u003e\r\n    \u003c!-- Select only items with price less than the max_price variable value --\u003e\r\n    \u003cjson_path id=\"filter_by_price\" \r\n               file=\"store.json\" \r\n               jsonpath=\"$.store.*[?(@.price \u0026lt; {{max_price}})].color\" \r\n               output_var=\"cheap_colors\" /\u003e\r\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Resulting Output (`cheap_colors`):**\r\n```text\r\nred\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 5: Inline Expression Definitions\r\nFor complex JSONPath syntax that contains many special characters, specify the query expression inside the tag body content rather than as an attribute.\r\n\r\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariable name=\"raw_payload\" type=\"string\" value=\u0027{\"employees\": [{\"name\": \"Alice\", \"role\": \"developer\"}, {\"name\": \"Bob\", \"role\": \"manager\"}]}\u0027 /\u003e\r\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n  \u003cflow\u003e\r\n  \u003c!-- Uses chardata body text as the JSONPath expression and stores output variables --\u003e\r\n  \u003cjson_path id=\"get_developers\" var=\"raw_payload\" output_var=\"dev_names\"\u003e\r\n    $.employees[?(@.role == \"developer\")].name\r\n  \u003c/json_path\u003e\r\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Resulting Output (`dev_names`):**\r\n```text\r\nAlice\r\n```\r\n","json_path_guide.md":"# Guide: JSONPath Extraction Node (`\u003cjson_path\u003e`) \n\nThe `\u003cjson_path\u003e` node provides lightweight, native querying and filtering over JSON documents in the Flow Pipeline Engine. It supports reading inputs from files or environment variables, evaluates expressions using JSONPath syntax, and structures outputs in multiple formats.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Node Reference\n\n### Attributes\n- `id` (string, optional): Unique step identifier for logging and tracking.\n- `file` (string, optional): Path to the source JSON file on disk. Supports variable interpolation (e.g. `{{json_path}}`).\n- `var` (string, optional): Name of the pipeline variable containing the raw JSON string to query.\n- `jsonpath` or `path` (string, optional): The JSONPath query expression to execute.\n- `mode` (string, optional): Defines the formatting style of the output:\n  - `\"value\"` (default): Returns string values or stringified raw nodes. Multiple matched nodes are joined by newlines.\n  - `\"json\"`: Returns a single JSON-serialized element (if exactly one is matched).\n  - `\"json_array\"`: Returns all matching elements serialized as a single JSON array list.\n- `output_var` or `out_var` (string, required): Variable key to store the resulting output.\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e If neither `jsonpath` nor `path` attributes are defined, the engine automatically extracts the JSONPath expression from the inner tag text of the node.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Examples\n\nThe following examples demonstrate common workflows using `\u003cjson_path\u003e`.\n\n### Example 1: Standard Extraction (Default `\"value\"` Mode)\nExtracts simple scalar values from an external file and joins the matched results into a newline-separated string.\n\n**Source JSON File (`books.json`):**\n```json\n{\n  \"store\": {\n    \"books\": [\n      { \"title\": \"Sayings of the Century\", \"category\": \"reference\" },\n      { \"title\": \"Sword of Honour\", \"category\": \"fiction\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\n    \u003cvariable name=\"src_file\" type=\"string\" value=\"books.json\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n  \u003cflow\u003e\n    \u003c!-- Query all titles in default text-join mode --\u003e\n    \u003cjson_path id=\"get_titles\" file=\"{{src_file}}\" jsonpath=\"$.store.books[*].title\" output_var=\"book_titles\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`book_titles`):**\n```text\nSayings of the Century\nSword of Honour\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Extracting Lists (`mode=\"json_array\"`)\nExtracts and serialized matched objects or numeric arrays as a structured JSON array suitable for passing to downstream APIs.\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n\u003cflow\u003e\n  \u003c!-- Extract all book categories as a serialized JSON string array --\u003e\n  \u003cjson_path id=\"get_categories\" \n             file=\"books.json\" \n             jsonpath=\"$.store.books[*].category\" \n             mode=\"json_array\" \n             output_var=\"categories_json\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`categories_json`):**\n```json\n[\"reference\", \"fiction\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Extracting Single Objects (`mode=\"json\"`)\nExtracts a specific nested object node and serializes it directly to a clean JSON string block.\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n\u003cflow\u003e\n  \u003c!-- Extract only the first book object --\u003e\n  \u003cjson_path id=\"get_first_book\" \n             file=\"books.json\" \n             jsonpath=\"$.store.books[0]\" \n             mode=\"json\" \n             output_var=\"first_book\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`first_book`):**\n```json\n{\"title\": \"Sayings of the Century\", \"category\": \"reference\"}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Expression Filters \u0026 Variable Interpolation\nCombines expression-based filtering (such as price filters) with dynamic variable interpolation to query a document based on runtime pipeline values.\n\n**Source JSON File (`store.json`):**\n```json\n{\n  \"store\": {\n    \"bicycle\": { \"color\": \"red\", \"price\": 19.95 },\n    \"unicycle\": { \"color\": \"blue\", \"price\": 45.00 }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\n    \u003cvariable name=\"max_price\" type=\"string\" value=\"25.00\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n  \u003cflow\u003e\n    \u003c!-- Select only items with price less than the max_price variable value --\u003e\n    \u003cjson_path id=\"filter_by_price\" \n               file=\"store.json\" \n               jsonpath=\"$.store.*[?(@.price \u0026lt; {{max_price}})].color\" \n               output_var=\"cheap_colors\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n  \u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`cheap_colors`):**\n```text\nred\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: Inline Expression Definitions\nFor complex JSONPath syntax that contains many special characters, specify the query expression inside the tag body content rather than as an attribute.\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\n    \u003cvariable name=\"raw_payload\" type=\"string\" value=\u0027{\"employees\": [{\"name\": \"Alice\", \"role\": \"developer\"}, {\"name\": \"Bob\", \"role\": \"manager\"}]}\u0027 /\u003e\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\n  \u003cflow\u003e\n  \u003c!-- Uses chardata body text as the JSONPath expression and stores output variables --\u003e\n  \u003cjson_path id=\"get_developers\" var=\"raw_payload\" output_var=\"dev_names\"\u003e\n    $.employees[?(@.role == \"developer\")].name\n  \u003c/json_path\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`dev_names`):**\n```text\nAlice\n```\n","kv.md":"# Key-Value (KV) Store Integration\r\n\r\nThis guide covers how to configure and execute operations against Key-Value databases within the `flow` pipeline orchestration library. The engine supports both embedded key-value stores (`bbolt`, `BadgerDB`) and server-based instances (`Redis/Valkey`, `etcd`).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\nThe KV engine provides two primary execution nodes:\r\n* **`\u003ckv\u003e`**: Executes individual atomic operations (`get`, `put`/`set`, `delete`/`del`, `scan`/`list`). Returns logs and outputs compatible with standard `\u003csql\u003e` nodes.\r\n* **`\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`**: Performs high-throughput streaming and ETL between SQL databases and Key-Value stores. Reports progress compatible with `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` nodes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Supported Drivers\r\n\r\n| Driver Name | Type | Connection String Syntax | Primary Use Case |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **`bbolt`** / **`bolt`** | Embedded | `/path/to/database.db` | Lightweight local caching, transactional state persistence |\r\n| **`badger`** | Embedded | `/path/to/data_dir` | High-write throughput embedded storage |\r\n| **`redis`** / **`valkey`** | Server | `redis://:password@localhost:6379/0` | High-speed shared in-memory caching and messaging |\r\n| **`etcd`** | Server | `http://127.0.0.1:2379,http://127.0.0.1:22379` | Distributed consensus, dynamic configuration management |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Database Configuration\r\n\r\nDefine KV connections inside the `\u003cdatabases\u003e` XML container. Connection strings support standard variable interpolation (`{{VarName}}`).\r\n\r\n### Embedded Key-Value Database (`bbolt`)\r\nFor embedded stores like `bbolt`, pass the target file path in `connection_string`. The engine creates parent directories automatically if they do not exist.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n\r\n\u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabase \r\n        name=\"local_cache\" \r\n        driver=\"bbolt\" \r\n        connection_string=\"./data/app_cache.db\" /\u003e\r\n\u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n### Server-Based Key-Value Database (Redis)\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n\u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabase \r\n        name=\"redis_store\" \r\n        driver=\"redis\" \r\n        connection_string=\"redis://:secret_pass@127.0.0.1:6379/0\" /\u003e\r\n\u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n## XML Reference \u0026 Execution Nodes\r\n### 1.Single Operations (\u003ckv\u003e)\r\nExecutes point reads, writes, deletions, or range scans against a target bucket/namespace.\r\n\r\n|Attribute|Required|Default|Description|\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n|id|No|Auto|Unique execution step identifier.|\r\n|db|Yes|—|Target database connection handle.|\r\n|bucket|No|default|Bucket name (bbolt/BadgerDB) or key prefix namespace (Redis/etcd).|\r\n|op|No|*|Operation type: get, put/set, delete/del, scan/list.|\r\n|key|No|*|Target record key.|\r\n|value|No|*|Record value payload for write operations.|\r\n|var|No|—|Environment variable containing a dynamic DSL string.|\r\n|output_var|No|LAST_OUTPUT|Variable that receives the command output or value.|\r\n* Note: Operations, keys, and values can be defined via XML attributes or inline body DSL text.\r\n  \r\n### Result Formatting\r\n\r\n`\u003ckv\u003e` returns standard result logs compatible with `\u003csql\u003e` execution observability:\r\n- **Write Ops (put, delete)**: Logs `(1 row(s) affected)`.\r\n- **Read Ops (get, scan)**: Formats tabular outputs (`KEY\\tVALUE\\n...`) and logs `(N row(s) returned)`.\r\n\r\n### 2. Bulk Operations (\u003ckv_bulk\u003e)\r\nStreams data sets directly between relational SQL queries and Key-Value buckets.\r\n|Attribute|Required|Default|Description|\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n|id|No|Auto|Unique execution step identifier.|\r\n|db|Yes|—|Source database connection handle.|\r\n|target_db|No|db|Destination database connection handle (KV or SQL).|\r\n|target_bucket|No|—|Target KV bucket or namespace receiving streamed records.|\r\n|batch_size|No|10000|Number of key-value pairs written per commit chunk.|\r\n|output_var|No|—|Variable receiving the total count of transferred records.|\r\n\r\n* Note: Bulk operations are optimized for high-throughput data transfer between SQL and KV stores.\r\n* Note: The `target_db` and `target_bucket` attributes must be correctly specified to ensure successful data transfer.\r\n\r\n## Usage Examples\r\n\r\n### 1. Embedded Write \u0026 Read (bbolt)\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Embedded KV Operations Example\"\r\n          xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"CACHE_FILE\" type=\"string\" value=\"./data/pipeline.db\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"USER_ID\" type=\"string\" value=\"usr_9901\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"USER_DATA\" type=\"string\" value=\u0027{\"status\":\"active\",\"tier\":\"gold\"}\u0027 /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"embedded_store\" driver=\"bbolt\" connection_string=\"{{CACHE_FILE}}\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Write record to \"user_sessions\" bucket --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"write_session\" \r\n            db=\"embedded_store\" \r\n            bucket=\"user_sessions\" \r\n            op=\"put\" \r\n            key=\"{{USER_ID}}\" \r\n            value=\"{{USER_DATA}}\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Read record from \"user_sessions\" bucket into variable \"RETRIEVED_SESSION\" --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"read_session\" \r\n            db=\"embedded_store\" \r\n            bucket=\"user_sessions\" \r\n            op=\"get\" \r\n            key=\"{{USER_ID}}\" \r\n            output_var=\"RETRIEVED_SESSION\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Output assertion --\u003e\r\n        \u003cassert id=\"check_session\" var=\"RETRIEVED_SESSION\" operator=\"ne\" value=\"\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 2. Inline Command DSL Usage\r\nYou can supply key-value commands using inline text body instead of XML attributes:\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n    \u003c!-- Inline PUT command --\u003e\r\n    \u003ckv id=\"inline_write\" db=\"embedded_store\" bucket=\"config\"\u003e\r\n        PUT system_status operational\r\n    \u003c/kv\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003c!-- Inline GET command --\u003e\r\n    \u003ckv id=\"inline_read\" db=\"embedded_store\" bucket=\"config\" output_var=\"SYS_STATUS\"\u003e\r\n        GET system_status\r\n    \u003c/kv\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3. Key Prefix Scanning\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Scan all keys starting with \u0027usr_\u0027 in the \u0027accounts\u0027 bucket --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv id=\"scan_users\" \r\n            db=\"embedded_store\" \r\n            bucket=\"accounts\" \r\n            op=\"scan\" \r\n            key=\"usr_\" \r\n            output_var=\"USER_LIST\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 4. High-Performance Bulk ETL (PostgreSQL to bbolt)\r\nStream 100,000+ user records directly from PostgreSQL into a local bbolt embedded database chunked in batches of 5,000 records.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline description=\"PostgreSQL to bbolt Streaming ETL\"\r\n          xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"postgres_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/app?sslmode=disable\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"cache_db\" driver=\"bbolt\" connection_string=\"./cache/lookup.db\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- The SQL query MUST return at least 2 columns: Column 1 = Key, Column 2 = Value --\u003e\r\n        \u003ckv_bulk id=\"cache_user_tokens\" \r\n                 db=\"postgres_db\" \r\n                 target_db=\"cache_db\" \r\n                 target_bucket=\"tokens\" \r\n                 batch_size=\"5000\" \r\n                 output_var=\"COPIED_COUNT\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT \r\n                user_id AS key, \r\n                auth_token AS value \r\n            FROM active_user_tokens \r\n            WHERE is_valid = true\r\n        \u003c/kv_bulk\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003cassert id=\"verify_bulk_transfer\" var=\"COPIED_COUNT\" operator=\"ne\" value=\"0\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Observability \u0026 Error Handling\r\n\r\nAll \u003ckv\u003e and \u003ckv_bulk\u003e operations emit lifecycle telemetry events captured by standard observers and event sinks:  \r\n* Attempt Tracking: Execution start/finish duration, row count metrics (read, written, affected), and attempt status (succeeded, failed, canceled).\r\n* Error Classification: File lock errors, invalid buckets, missing connection handles, or network disconnects are mapped to standard ErrorClass definitions (e.g., ErrorClassFileSystem, ErrorClassDatabase).\r\n* Redaction: Sensitive keys or value strings matching security patterns are masked in error outputs.\r\n","parallel.md":"# Concurrent Step Execution: `\u003cparallel\u003e`\n\nThe `\u003cparallel\u003e` component executes multiple child tasks or branches concurrently across an asynchronous worker thread pool. Rather than executing child tasks sequentially from top to bottom, `\u003cparallel\u003e` dispatches each child step into a separate goroutine and establishes a synchronized join barrier, ensuring all concurrent operations finish before the pipeline advances to the subsequent step.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture \u0026 Lifecycle\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Start([Enter \u003cparallel\u003e]) --\u003e PoolConfig[\"Initialize Worker Pool\u003cbr/\u003e(max_threads=N, default: 4)\"]\n    PoolConfig --\u003e Dispatch[\"Spawn Concurrent Worker Threads\"]\n\n    subgraph Concurrent Execution\n        direction TB\n        Dispatch --\u003e BranchA[\"Branch 1: SQL Pre-check\"]\n        Dispatch --\u003e BranchB[\"Branch 2: API HTTP Call\"]\n        Dispatch --\u003e BranchC[\"Branch 3: Bulk ETL Extract\"]\n        Dispatch --\u003e BranchD[\"Branch 4: File Processing\"]\n    end\n\n    BranchA --\u003e Barrier{{\"Join Barrier / Sync Barrier\u003cbr/\u003e(sync.WaitGroup)\"}}\n    BranchB --\u003e Barrier\n    BranchC --\u003e Barrier\n    BranchD --\u003e Barrier\n\n    Barrier --\u003e ErrorCheck{\"Any Branch Failed?\"}\n    ErrorCheck -- Yes --\u003e ErrorPolicy[\"Apply on_error Policy\u003cbr/\u003e(stop / continue / retry)\"]\n    ErrorCheck -- No --\u003e Advance([\"Advance to Next Pipeline Step\"])\n    ErrorPolicy --\u003e Advance\n```\n\n---\n\n## Attribute Reference\n\n| Attribute | Type | Default | Mandatory | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `id` | `string` | *(generated)* | No | Unique identifier for the parallel step. Used for logging, metrics, and tracing. |\n| `max_threads` | `positiveInteger` | `4` | No | Maximum number of concurrent worker threads/goroutines allowed to execute simultaneously. |\n| `threads` | `positiveInteger` | `4` | No | Alias for `max_threads`. Defines concurrency limit. |\n| `concurrency` | `positiveInteger` | `4` | No | Alias for `max_threads`. Defines concurrency limit. |\n| `timeout` | `string` | *(none)* | No | Maximum duration for all parallel tasks to complete (e.g. `30s`, `5m`, `1h`). |\n| `on_error` | `string` | `stop` | No | Action taken if any child branch returns an error: `stop` (fail pipeline), `continue` (log and proceed), or `retry`. |\n| `description` | `string` | `\"\"` | No | Human-readable explanation of the parallel operations. |\n\n---\n\n## Core Concurrency Guarantees\n\n### 1. Synchronized Join Barrier\nWhen `\u003cparallel\u003e` begins, child tasks are queued and assigned to the worker pool. The orchestrator halts forward progress on the main flow until **all** child branches have either completed successfully or terminated with an error.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Seq1[\"Sequential Step 1\"] --\u003e ParStart[\"\u003cparallel\u003e Entry\"]\n    ParStart --\u003e Worker1[\"Worker Goroutine 1\"]\n    ParStart --\u003e Worker2[\"Worker Goroutine 2\"]\n    ParStart --\u003e Worker3[\"Worker Goroutine 3\"]\n    Worker1 --\u003e Join((\"Join Barrier\"))\n    Worker2 --\u003e Join\n    Worker3 --\u003e Join\n    Join --\u003e Seq2[\"Sequential Step 2\"]\n```\n\n### 2. Database Connection Pooling Considerations\nWhen running multiple `\u003csql\u003e` or `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` nodes concurrently within `\u003cparallel\u003e`, ensure that the underlying `\u003cdatabase\u003e` connection pool has enough capacity:\n- Increase `max_open_conns` on your `\u003cdatabase\u003e` definition to match or exceed `max_threads`.\n- If `max_threads=\"8\"` and `max_open_conns=\"2\"`, worker tasks will wait in a database queue, bottlenecking concurrency.\n\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"analytics_db\" \n          connection_string=\"{{AnalyticsConnStr}}\" \n          max_open_conns=\"10\" \n          max_idle_conns=\"5\" /\u003e\n```\n\n### 3. Variable Thread-Safety \u0026 Scoping\n- **Input Variables**: All child steps can safely read pipeline variables (`{{VarName}}` or `vars.Get*`).\n- **Output Variables**: When concurrent steps write to variables (`output_var`), ensure each child step writes to an independent variable name (e.g. `BRANCH_A_STATUS`, `BRANCH_B_STATUS`) to avoid race conditions.\n\n---\n\n## Production Pipeline Examples\n\n### Example 1: Parallel Health Check Across Multiple Systems\nQuery multiple databases concurrently to determine synchronization state before taking action.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"SourceConn\" type=\"string\" value=\"sqlserver://SRV01:1433?database=AppDB\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TargetConn\" type=\"string\" value=\"sqlserver://SRV02:1433?database=ReportingDB\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"SOURCE_MAX_DATE\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TARGET_MAX_DATE\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"src_db\" connection_string=\"{{SourceConn}}\" max_open_conns=\"4\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"tgt_db\" connection_string=\"{{TargetConn}}\" max_open_conns=\"4\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Execute both queries at the same time --\u003e\n        \u003cparallel id=\"fetch_max_timestamps\" max_threads=\"2\" description=\"Query source and target databases in parallel\"\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"get_source_max\" db=\"src_db\" output_var=\"SOURCE_MAX_DATE\"\u003e\n                SELECT ISNULL(MAX(UpdatedAt), \u00271970-01-01\u0027) AS SOURCE_MAX_DATE FROM dbo.Orders;\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"get_target_max\" db=\"tgt_db\" output_var=\"TARGET_MAX_DATE\"\u003e\n                SELECT ISNULL(MAX(UpdatedAt), \u00271970-01-01\u0027) AS TARGET_MAX_DATE FROM dbo.OrdersArchive;\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Fast comparison script evaluating delta condition --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"compare_timestamps\" language=\"go\" output_variable=\"NEEDS_ACTION\"\n            description=\"Evaluate whether source date exceeds target date\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                src := vars.GetString(\"SOURCE_MAX_DATE\")\n                tgt := vars.GetString(\"TARGET_MAX_DATE\")\n                if src != \"\" \u0026\u0026 tgt != \"\" \u0026\u0026 src != tgt {\n                    fmt.Println(\"true\")\n                } else {\n                    fmt.Println(\"false\")\n                }\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Process findings: sync delta if dates diverge --\u003e\n        \u003cif id=\"check_sync_action\" var=\"NEEDS_ACTION\" equals=\"true\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003csql_bulk id=\"sync_delta\" db=\"src_db\" target_db=\"tgt_db\" target_table=\"dbo.OrdersArchive\" batch_size=\"10000\"\n                    description=\"Bulk stream modified delta rows\"\u003e\n                    \u003c![CDATA[\n                    SELECT * FROM dbo.Orders WHERE UpdatedAt \u003e \u0027{{TARGET_MAX_DATE}}\u0027;\n                    ]]\u003e\n                \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n            \u003celse\u003e\n                \u003cscript id=\"log_in_sync\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                    \u003c![CDATA[\n                    package main\n                    import \"fmt\"\n                    func main() { fmt.Println(\"Target archive is fully up to date. No sync needed.\") }\n                    ]]\u003e\n                \u003c/script\u003e\n            \u003c/else\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Concurrent API Ingestion and Staging\nFetch disparate datasets from external REST endpoints in parallel, save them to local storage, and extract JSON payloads.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Fetch multiple external data feeds concurrently\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ApiKey\" type=\"string\" value=\"secret_live_token\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"RatesJson\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"UsersJson\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cparallel id=\"parallel_ingestion\" max_threads=\"4\" description=\"Download currency and user data simultaneously\"\u003e\n            \u003c!-- Branch 1: Exchange Rates --\u003e\n            \u003chttp_client id=\"fetch_rates\" url=\"https://api.example.com/v1/rates\" method=\"GET\" into=\"RatesJson\"\u003e\n                \u003cheaders\u003e\n                    \u003cheader name=\"Authorization\"\u003eBearer {{ApiKey}}\u003c/header\u003e\n                \u003c/headers\u003e\n            \u003c/http_client\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Branch 2: Active User Profiles --\u003e\n            \u003chttp_client id=\"fetch_users\" url=\"https://api.example.com/v1/users/active\" method=\"GET\" into=\"UsersJson\"\u003e\n                \u003cheaders\u003e\n                    \u003cheader name=\"Authorization\"\u003eBearer {{ApiKey}}\u003c/header\u003e\n                \u003c/headers\u003e\n            \u003c/http_client\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Parse responses after both downloads finish --\u003e\n        \u003cjson_path id=\"extract_usd_rate\" source=\"var\" var=\"RatesJson\" query=\"$.rates.USD\" into=\"USD_RATE\" /\u003e\n        \u003cjson_path id=\"extract_user_count\" source=\"var\" var=\"UsersJson\" query=\"$.total_count\" into=\"TOTAL_USERS\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Multi-Table Archive \u0026 Cleanup Operations\nExecute independent maintenance statements concurrently across large reporting tables.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Parallel table maintenance\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"warehouse_db\" \n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://WH01:1433?database=Warehouse\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\" \n                  max_open_conns=\"10\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cparallel id=\"nightly_maintenance\" max_threads=\"3\" timeout=\"15m\" description=\"Run archive purges concurrently\"\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"purge_audit_logs\" db=\"warehouse_db\"\u003e\n                DELETE FROM dbo.AuditLog WHERE CreatedAt \u0026lt; DATEADD(DAY, -90, GETUTCDATE());\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"purge_session_cache\" db=\"warehouse_db\"\u003e\n                DELETE FROM dbo.SessionCache WHERE ExpiresAt \u0026lt; GETUTCDATE();\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"rebuild_stage_indexes\" db=\"warehouse_db\"\u003e\n                ALTER INDEX ALL ON dbo.StagingEvents REBUILD;\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Concurrent File Transformations\nRead multiple local data files simultaneously, run transformation scripts, and export formatted reports.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Parallel report extraction\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cparallel id=\"generate_department_reports\" max_threads=\"3\"\u003e\n            \u003cexcel_read id=\"read_finance\" file=\"./data/finance_q3.xlsx\" sheet=\"Summary\" output_var=\"FINANCE_DATA\" /\u003e\n            \u003cexcel_read id=\"read_sales\" file=\"./data/sales_q3.xlsx\" sheet=\"Summary\" output_var=\"SALES_DATA\" /\u003e\n            \u003cfile_read id=\"read_hr_csv\" path=\"./data/hr_headcount.csv\" into=\"HR_DATA\" /\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n\n        \u003cassert id=\"validate_finance_loaded\" var=\"FINANCE_DATA\" condition=\"ne .FINANCE_DATA \\\"\\\"\" message=\"Finance data missing\" /\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"validate_sales_loaded\" var=\"SALES_DATA\" condition=\"ne .SALES_DATA \\\"\\\"\" message=\"Sales data missing\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: High-Throughput Bulk Replication Across Multiple Target Tables\nStream extracts concurrently from a core database into multiple target tables on an analytics server.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Concurrent multi-table replication\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"oltp_db\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://OLTP:1433?database=Core\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\" max_open_conns=\"8\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"olap_db\" connection_string=\"sqlserver://OLAP:1433?database=Analytics\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\" max_open_conns=\"8\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cparallel id=\"bulk_replicate_all\" max_threads=\"3\" timeout=\"30m\"\u003e\n            \u003csql_bulk id=\"copy_customers\" db=\"oltp_db\" target_db=\"olap_db\" target_table=\"dbo.DimCustomers\" batch_size=\"25000\" tablock=\"true\"\u003e\n                SELECT CustomerID, AccountNumber, Name, TerritoryID, ModifiedDate FROM Sales.Customer;\n            \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n            \u003csql_bulk id=\"copy_products\" db=\"oltp_db\" target_db=\"olap_db\" target_table=\"dbo.DimProducts\" batch_size=\"25000\" tablock=\"true\"\u003e\n                SELECT ProductID, Name, ProductNumber, StandardCost, ListPrice FROM Production.Product;\n            \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n            \u003csql_bulk id=\"copy_territories\" db=\"oltp_db\" target_db=\"olap_db\" target_table=\"dbo.DimTerritory\" batch_size=\"5000\" tablock=\"true\"\u003e\n                SELECT TerritoryID, Name, CountryRegionCode, [Group] FROM Sales.SalesTerritory;\n            \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## Best Practices \u0026 Troubleshooting\n\n\u003e [!TIP]\n\u003e **Right-size `max_threads`**: Default is 4. Avoid setting `max_threads` higher than the number of available CPU cores or database connection limits, as excess goroutines cause thread thrashing and connection pool exhaustion.\n\n\u003e [!IMPORTANT]\n\u003e **Independent Output Variables**: Never configure two parallel branches to output into the same variable name. Each concurrent branch should write to a uniquely named variable.\n\n\u003e [!WARNING]\n\u003e **Deadlock Prevention**: If multiple concurrent branches interact with the same database, ensure they modify disjoint sets of rows or tables to prevent database transaction deadlocks.\n","preflight.md":"# Preflight Nodes: Setup and Validation\n\nPreflight nodes are designed to run environment checks, initializations, and structural assertions **before** the main data-streaming pipelines (`\u003cflow\u003e`) are executed. This ensures that resources are available, configurations are correct, and the pipeline fails fast before performing heavy ETL operations.\n\n\u003e [!IMPORTANT]\n\u003e **No Flow Execution during Preflight**: Running a `\u003cpreflight\u003e` block does **not** execute the main `\u003cflow\u003e` pipeline nodes. It is intended as a non-destructive runtime validation/test run to guarantee that running in a specific environment with those precise variables and configuration will execute exactly as intended. Once validation is complete, the pipeline can later be run in production without the preflight overhead.\n\n---\n\n## How It Works\n\n1. **Separation of Concerns**: Preflight nodes and flow nodes are separated during XML parsing into `PreflightNodes` and `FlowNodes` respectively.\n2. **Preflight-Only Execution**: Preflight checks are executed by passing `cfg.PreflightNodes` to the pipeline `Executor`. The `\u003cflow\u003e` block is ignored during this execution step.\n3. **Fail-Fast Design**: If any preflight node fails or returns an error, the execution can be halted immediately, preventing the main `\u003cflow\u003e` from running with incorrect or missing parameters.\n\n---\n\n## Is the Flow Executed During Preflight?\n\n\u003e [!CAUTION]\n\u003e **NO. The main `\u003cflow\u003e` block is never executed when running a preflight check.**\n\nPreflight is explicitly intended as a **runtime validation and test run**. Its core purposes are:\n* To ensure that the application can run in the target environment.\n* To verify that all required environment variables are set and reachable.\n* To confirm that the specific configuration file compiles and executes safely.\n\nOnce the preflight validation passes successfully, you can be highly confident that running the same configuration file later with the preflight block omitted (or skipped) will execute exactly as intended.\n\n---\n\n## Is Preflight Executed When Running the Flow?\n\n\u003e [!IMPORTANT]\n\u003e **NO. The `\u003cpreflight\u003e` block is never executed when running the flow normally.**\n\nThe executor only executes the slice of nodes explicitly passed to `Execute(ctx, nodes)`. If you wish to run the pipeline, you must choose whether to run preflight validation, flow execution, or both:\n\n```go\n// OPTION A: Run flow only (Skipping preflight checks entirely)\nresults, err := executor.Execute(ctx, cfg.FlowNodes)\n\n// OPTION B: Safe Run (Preflight checks first, then Flow)\n_, err := executor.Execute(ctx, cfg.PreflightNodes)\nif err == nil {\n    results, err := executor.Execute(ctx, cfg.FlowNodes)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## XML Schema Syntax\n\nA preflight block is defined using the `\u003cpreflight\u003e` element. Within this block, you can place standard pipeline AST nodes such as `\u003cscript\u003e`, `\u003cfile_save\u003e`, `\u003cfile_read\u003e`, and `\u003cassert\u003e`:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cpreflight\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Preflight tasks here --\u003e\n    \u003c/preflight\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Main ETL flow here --\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Environment Verification\nThis example checks if the correct database connectivity is established and that critical environment variables are set before proceeding.\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ENV\" type=\"string\" value=\"staging\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cpreflight\u003e\n        \u003c!-- 1. Assert that we are not in production if running tests --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"check_env\" var=\"ENV\" equals=\"production\" operator=\"!=\" message=\"Warning: Running in production!\" on_failure=\"warn\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- 2. Verify we can fetch the system status --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"ping_service\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"net/http\"\n                \"os\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                resp, err := http.Head(\"https://api.yourdomain.com/health\")\n                if err != nil || resp.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {\n                    println(\"API service is offline\")\n                    os.Exit(1)\n                }\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/preflight\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Flow starts only if preflight check passes --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"load_data\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            func main() {\n                println(\"Streaming data...\")\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### Example 2: Directory Preparation\nThis example ensures that the destination folder structure exists and logs a marker file before downloading bulk records.\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"EXPORT_DIR\" type=\"string\" value=\"./exports\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cpreflight\u003e\n        \u003c!-- 1. Run shell script to create directories --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"make_dir\" language=\"bash\"\u003e\n            mkdir -p {{EXPORT_DIR}}\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- 2. Write a marker indicating preflight run started --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_save id=\"preflight_marker\" file=\"{{EXPORT_DIR}}/preflight.log\" append=\"false\"\u003e\n            Preflight started. Ready to receive files.\n        \u003c/file_save\u003e\n    \u003c/preflight\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cfile_save id=\"data_payload\" file=\"{{EXPORT_DIR}}/records.json\"\u003e\n            {\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"ETL Payload\"}\n        \u003c/file_save\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### Example 3: Schema \u0026 Version Assertion\nThis example reads a remote configuration file or local manifest to assert that the pipeline version is supported.\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cpreflight\u003e\n        \u003c!-- 1. Read local version manifest --\u003e\n        \u003cfile_read id=\"read_manifest\" file=\"manifest.json\" output_var=\"MANIFEST_DATA\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- 2. Use JSONPath to extract the required version --\u003e\n        \u003cjson_path id=\"get_version\" var=\"MANIFEST_DATA\" jsonpath=\"$.version\" output_var=\"CURRENT_VERSION\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- 3. Assert that the version meets minimum compatibility --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"check_version\" var=\"CURRENT_VERSION\" equals=\"2.0.0\" operator=\"==\" message=\"Unsupported pipeline version\" on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/preflight\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"execute_migration\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            func main() {\n                println(\"Running migration flow...\")\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## Split Example: Pipeline Structure vs. Environment Configuration\n\nIt is common to separate your static pipeline execution structure (`pipeline.xml`) from dynamic, environment-specific databases and variables (`config.xml`). Below is an example demonstrating this setup.\n\n### 1. `config.xml` (Environment Setup \u0026 Env-Specific Preflights)\nThis file defines environment-specific databases, parameters, and **preflight validation checks** tailored strictly to this staging environment (e.g., verifying that the staging API is online).\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ENV\" type=\"string\" value=\"staging\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"API_ENDPOINT\" type=\"string\" value=\"https://staging.api.service.com\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"primary_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"host=localhost port=5432 user=db_user dbname=staging_db sslmode=disable\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- Staging environment preflight verification --\u003e\n    \u003cpreflight\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"verify_staging_api\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"net/http\"\n                \"os\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                resp, err := http.Head(\"https://staging.api.service.com/health\")\n                if err != nil || resp.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {\n                    println(\"Staging API is unreachable!\")\n                    os.Exit(1)\n                }\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/preflight\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. `pipeline.xml` (Pipeline Logic \u0026 General Preflight)\nThis file defines the general validation logic (within `\u003cpreflight\u003e`) and the heavy data movement tasks (within `\u003cflow\u003e`), completely independent of hardcoded credentials.\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cpreflight\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Ensure we are running against correct environment configuration --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"assert_staging\" var=\"ENV\" equals=\"staging\" operator=\"==\" message=\"Incorrect environment detected!\" on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Verify database connection from config.xml before flowing --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"verify_db\" db=\"primary_db\"\u003e\n            SELECT 1;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/preflight\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Execute ETL query against primary_db defined in config.xml --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"stream_records\" db=\"primary_db\"\u003e\n            SELECT * FROM users_staging;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 3. Orchestrating Split Files in Go\n\nTo parse and execute both files together, load them sequentially into your `ParseXMLConfig` parser, merge the `PreflightNodes` from both `PipelineConfig` outputs, and run them sequentially:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"io/ioutil\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"github.com/etl-madness/flow\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\t// Read and parse environmental config\n\tconfigData, _ := ioutil.ReadFile(\"config.xml\")\n\tenvCfg, _ := flow.ParseXMLConfig(configData)\n\n\t// Read and parse structural pipeline\n\tpipelineData, _ := ioutil.ReadFile(\"pipeline.xml\")\n\tpipeCfg, _ := flow.ParseXMLConfig(pipelineData)\n\n\t// Initialize Registry with both databases and variables\n\tregistry := flow.NewRegistry()\n\tregistry.InitVariables(envCfg.Variables)\n\tregistry.InitDatabases(envCfg.Databases)\n\n\texecutor := flow.NewExecutor(registry)\n\n\t// Merge env-specific and pipeline-specific preflights\n\tallPreflights := append(envCfg.PreflightNodes, pipeCfg.PreflightNodes...)\n\n\t// Safe Test Run / Preflight\n\tlog.Println(\"Running combined preflight validation...\")\n\t_, err := executor.Execute(context.Background(), allPreflights)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Fatalf(\"Preflight failed! Flow aborted: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\t// Execution of flow (only reaches here if all preflight checks pass)\n\tlog.Println(\"Preflight passed successfully. Executing pipeline flow...\")\n\t_, err = executor.Execute(context.Background(), pipeCfg.FlowNodes)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Fatalf(\"Flow execution failed: %v\", err)\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n","script.md":"# `\u003cscript\u003e` Node Reference \u0026 Cross-Platform Guide\n\nThe `\u003cscript\u003e` node executes dynamic code within an ETL Madness Flow pipeline. It bridges declarative XML workflow orchestration with programmatic logic, enabling custom transformations, system checks, file manipulations, API integrations, and database operations.\n\nFlow supports multiple scripting engines, ranging from its **built-in zero-dependency Go interpreter** (which runs identically on Windows, Linux, and macOS without requiring any compiler or runtime installation) to platform shells (PowerShell, Bash, POSIX sh, Zsh, Cmd) and .NET Script (CSX).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Complete Attribute Specification\n\nThe `\u003cscript\u003e` node accepts the following attributes:\n\n| Attribute | XML Schema Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `id` | `xs:string` | Auto (`script_N`) | Unique step identifier used for logging, telemetry, and execution flow tracking. |\n| `language` / `lang` | `xs:string` | `powershell` (in Builder) | The script engine or interpreter runtime (e.g., `go`, `powershell`, `pwsh`, `bash`, `sh`, `cmd`, `git-bash`, `zsh`, `csx`, `sql`). |\n| `output_var` / `output_variable` / `out_var` | `xs:string` | *None* | Pipeline variable name that receives the captured standard output (`stdout`) of the script. |\n| `var` / `variable` | `xs:string` | *None* | Name of an existing pipeline variable containing the script code to execute. When specified, overrides the XML body content. |\n| `timeout` | `xs:string` | *None* | Maximum execution duration before terminating the script (e.g., `10s`, `45s`, `5m`, `1h`). |\n| `description` | `xs:string` | *None* | Human-readable explanation displayed in logs, Mermaid flowcharts, and HTML execution summaries. |\n| `on_error` | `xs:string` | `stop` | Failure policy when the script exits with a non-zero code (`stop`, `continue`, `retry`). |\n| `retry_count` | `xs:int` | `0` | Number of retry attempts when `on_error=\"retry\"`. |\n| `retry_interval` | `xs:string` | `1s` | Delay duration between retry attempts (e.g., `2s`, `10s`). |\n| `db` / `database` | `xs:string` | *None* | Database connection alias for `language=\"sql\"` or database-bound operations. |\n| `target_db` | `xs:string` | *None* | Destination database connection alias for cross-database streaming. |\n| `target_table` | `xs:string` | *None* | Target table name for streaming operations. |\n| `batch_size` | `xs:int` | `500` | Chunk batch size for bulk data operations. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Supported Runtimes \u0026 Cross-Platform Compatibility Matrix\n\n| Runtime / Engine | `language` Value(s) | Windows | Linux | macOS | Dependencies | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **Interpreted Go** | `go` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | **None** (Built into Flow binary via Yaegi) | **Recommended for multi-platform pipelines.** Native access to Go standard library, `host/vars`, and `host/db`. |\n| **PowerShell Core** | `pwsh` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | `pwsh` in `PATH` | Modern cross-platform PowerShell (v7+). Runs identically across OS platforms. |\n| **Windows PowerShell** | `powershell` | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | Pre-installed on Windows (`powershell.exe`) | Windows PowerShell 5.1. Executes with `-NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass`. |\n| **GNU Bash** | `bash` | ⚠️ | ✅ | ✅ | `/bin/bash` or `bash` in `PATH` | Native on Linux/macOS. Requires Git Bash, MSYS2, or WSL on Windows. |\n| **POSIX Shell** | `shell`, `sh` | ⚠️ | ✅ | ✅ | `/bin/sh` or `sh` in `PATH` | Lightweight POSIX script. On Windows falls back to available `sh.exe` or `cmd.exe`. |\n| **Windows Command** | `cmd` | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | Windows native (`cmd.exe /c`) | Legacy batch scripts (`.bat` / `.cmd`). Windows only. |\n| **Git Bash** | `git-bash`, `gitbash` | ✅ | ⚠️ | ⚠️ | Git for Windows installed | Specifically targets Git\u0027s Bash binary on Windows (`C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe`). |\n| **Z Shell** | `zsh` | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | `/bin/zsh` | Default login shell on modern macOS; standard on Linux. |\n| **C# Script (.NET)** | `csx`, `dotnet-script` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | `dotnet-script` CLI tool | Executes C# code with Roslyn compiler. Requires .NET SDK and `dotnet-script`. |\n| **SQL Script** | `sql` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Configured `\u003cdatabase\u003e` in pipeline | Executes native SQL against target database connection. |\n\n\u003e [!TIP]\n\u003e **Cross-Platform Recommendation**: For pipelines intended to run seamlessly across Windows, Linux containers, and macOS runners, prefer **`language=\"go\"`**. Because the Go interpreter is embedded directly within the Flow executable, your pipeline requires **zero external software or interpreter installations** on target machines.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Variable Passing \u0026 Output Capture Mechanics\n\nThere are two primary mechanisms for sharing data between the Flow pipeline and scripts:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph FlowEngine [\"Flow Engine Context\"]\n        Registry[\"Pipeline Variable Registry\u003cbr/\u003e{{VarName}}\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph ScriptExecution [\"Script Step Execution\"]\n        Direction1[\"1. Pre-Execution Interpolation\u003cbr/\u003e{{VarName}} replaced in source text\"]\n        Direction2[\"2. Host API Access\u003cbr/\u003ehost/vars.GetString(\u0027VarName\u0027)\"]\n        Direction3[\"3. Output Variable Capture\u003cbr/\u003estdout captured into output_var\"]\n    end\n\n    Registry --\u003e Direction1 --\u003e ScriptExecution\n    Registry \u003c--\u003e Direction2 \u003c--\u003e ScriptExecution\n    ScriptExecution --\u003e Direction3 --\u003e Registry\n```\n\n### 1. Template Interpolation (`{{VarName}}`)\nBefore any script runs, Flow scans the script content and replaces all `{{VarName}}` placeholders with their current runtime values.\n- Works across **all** script languages (`go`, `bash`, `powershell`, `cmd`, `csx`).\n- Evaluated prior to passing code to the interpreter.\n\n### 2. Built-in Host APIs (Go Scripts Only)\nGo scripts can import the built-in host package `\"host/vars\"` for dynamic, typed interaction with the pipeline variable registry:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"host/vars\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    // Read typed variables\n    server := vars.GetString(\"ServerHost\")\n    port   := vars.GetInt(\"ServerPort\")\n    active := vars.GetBool(\"IsProduction\")\n    rate   := vars.GetFloat(\"ExchangeRate\")\n\n    // Write back directly to the pipeline registry\n    vars.Set(\"ProcessedCount\", 42)\n    vars.SetString(\"StatusMessage\", \"All records validated\")\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Capturing Output via `output_var`\nAny content written to standard output (`stdout` / `fmt.Print` / `Write-Host` / `echo` / `Console.Write`) is captured by the engine and stored in the variable named by `output_var`:\n- Subsequent pipeline steps can reference this variable via `{{VariableName}}` or `vars.GetString(\"VariableName\")`.\n- Trailing newlines (`\\r\\n` or `\\n`) are trimmed automatically to prevent formatting discrepancies.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Cross-Platform Scripting Best Practices\n\nWhen writing scripts designed to run on multiple operating systems:\n\n1. **Path Separators**: Avoid hardcoding `\\` or `/`.\n   - In Go: Use `filepath.Join(\"folder\", \"file.csv\")` or `filepath.FromSlash(path)`.\n   - In PowerShell: Use `Join-Path $folder $file`.\n   - In Bash: Always use forward slashes `/`.\n2. **Line Endings (CRLF vs. LF)**:\n   - Windows outputs `\\r\\n`; Unix outputs `\\n`.\n   - When checking string equality in conditions (`\u003cif\u003e`), trim whitespace or normalize returns.\n3. **Quoting and CDATA**:\n   - Always wrap script source inside an XML `\u003c![CDATA[ ... ]]\u003e` block to avoid XML entity conflicts (`\u003c`, `\u003e`, `\u0026`, `\"`).\n4. **Environment Variables**:\n   - Windows environment variable names are case-insensitive; Linux and macOS are strictly case-sensitive.\n   - Use pipeline variables (`{{MyVar}}` or `vars.GetString(\"MyVar\")`) instead of system environment variables whenever possible.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Comprehensive Cross-Platform Examples\n\n### Example 1: Universal Cross-Platform File Inspection (`language=\"go\"`)\n\nThis script inspects a directory, calculates file sizes and MD5 hashes, and outputs a JSON summary. It runs identically on Windows, Linux, and macOS with **zero external dependencies**.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TargetDir\" type=\"string\" value=\"./data_inbound\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"FileManifest\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"ScanDirectory\" language=\"go\" output_var=\"FileManifest\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n\n            import (\n                \"crypto/md5\"\n                \"encoding/hex\"\n                \"encoding/json\"\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"io\"\n                \"os\"\n                \"path/filepath\"\n                \"time\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Name      string    `json:\"name\"`\n                Path      string    `json:\"path\"`\n                SizeBytes int64     `json:\"size_bytes\"`\n                MD5       string    `json:\"md5\"`\n                Modified  time.Time `json:\"modified\"`\n            }\n\n            func getFileMD5(filePath string) (string, error) {\n                f, err := os.Open(filePath)\n                if err != nil {\n                    return \"\", err\n                }\n                defer f.Close()\n\n                h := md5.New()\n                if _, err := io.Copy(h, f); err != nil {\n                    return \"\", err\n                }\n                return hex.EncodeToString(h.Sum(nil)), nil\n            }\n\n            func main() {\n                dir := vars.GetString(\"TargetDir\")\n                if dir == \"\" {\n                    dir = \".\"\n                }\n\n                var files []FileInfo\n                entries, err := os.ReadDir(dir)\n                if err != nil {\n                    fmt.Printf(\"[]\")\n                    return\n                }\n\n                for _, entry := range entries {\n                    if entry.IsDir() {\n                        continue\n                    }\n                    fullPath := filepath.Join(dir, entry.Name())\n                    info, err := entry.Info()\n                    if err != nil {\n                        continue\n                    }\n\n                    hash, _ := getFileMD5(fullPath)\n                    files = append(files, FileInfo{\n                        Name:      entry.Name(),\n                        Path:      filepath.ToSlash(fullPath),\n                        SizeBytes: info.Size(),\n                        MD5:       hash,\n                        Modified:  info.ModTime().UTC(),\n                    })\n                }\n\n                jsonBytes, _ := json.MarshalIndent(files, \"\", \"  \")\n                fmt.Print(string(jsonBytes))\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: OS Detection and Dynamic Platform Routing\n\nDetermines the host operating system at runtime using Go\u0027s built-in `runtime.GOOS`, registers the OS name into a pipeline variable, and conditionally executes platform-specific actions.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"CurrentOS\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"HostCores\" type=\"int\" value=\"1\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Step 1: Detect Operating System and Core Count --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"DetectHostEnvironment\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"runtime\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n\n            func main() {\n                osName := runtime.GOOS       // \"windows\", \"linux\", \"darwin\"\n                arch   := runtime.GOARCH     // \"amd64\", \"arm64\"\n                cpus   := runtime.NumCPU()\n\n                vars.SetString(\"CurrentOS\", osName)\n                vars.Set(\"HostCores\", cpus)\n\n                fmt.Printf(\"Detected Host OS: %s (%s) with %d CPUs\\n\", osName, arch, cpus)\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 2: Windows Branch --\u003e\n        \u003cif id=\"CheckWindows\" var=\"CurrentOS\" equals=\"windows\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003cscript id=\"WindowsLog\" language=\"powershell\"\u003e\n                    \u003c![CDATA[\n                    Write-Host \"Running on Windows. System Drive: $env:SystemDrive\"\n                    ]]\u003e\n                \u003c/script\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 3: Linux / macOS Branch --\u003e\n        \u003cif id=\"CheckUnix\" var=\"CurrentOS\" equals=\"linux\"\u003e\n            \u003cthen\u003e\n                \u003cscript id=\"LinuxLog\" language=\"bash\"\u003e\n                    \u003c![CDATA[\n                    echo \"Running on Linux. Kernel: $(uname -r), Uptime: $(uptime -p 2\u003e/dev/null || uptime)\"\n                    ]]\u003e\n                \u003c/script\u003e\n            \u003c/then\u003e\n        \u003c/if\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Cross-Platform Zip Archive Generator (`language=\"go\"`)\n\nCompresses ETL export files into a `.zip` archive across all platforms without calling external tools like `zip`, `tar`, or 7-Zip.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"SourceFolder\" type=\"string\" value=\"./exports\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ZipFile\" type=\"string\" value=\"./exports_archive.zip\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ArchivedFilesCount\" type=\"int\" value=\"0\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"CreateZipArchive\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n\n            import (\n                \"archive/zip\"\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"io\"\n                \"os\"\n                \"path/filepath\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n\n            func main() {\n                srcDir  := vars.GetString(\"SourceFolder\")\n                zipPath := vars.GetString(\"ZipFile\")\n\n                zipFile, err := os.Create(zipPath)\n                if err != nil {\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Failed to create zip file: %v\\n\", err)\n                    return\n                }\n                defer zipFile.Close()\n\n                archive := zip.NewWriter(zipFile)\n                defer archive.Close()\n\n                count := 0\n                err = filepath.Walk(srcDir, func(path string, info os.FileInfo, err error) error {\n                    if err != nil || info.IsDir() {\n                        return err\n                    }\n\n                    relPath, err := filepath.Rel(srcDir, path)\n                    if err != nil {\n                        return err\n                    }\n\n                    header, err := zip.FileInfoHeader(info)\n                    if err != nil {\n                        return err\n                    }\n                    header.Name = filepath.ToSlash(relPath)\n                    header.Method = zip.Deflate\n\n                    writer, err := archive.CreateHeader(header)\n                    if err != nil {\n                        return err\n                    }\n\n                    file, err := os.Open(path)\n                    if err != nil {\n                        return err\n                    }\n                    defer file.Close()\n\n                    _, err = io.Copy(writer, file)\n                    if err == nil {\n                        count++\n                    }\n                    return err\n                })\n\n                vars.Set(\"ArchivedFilesCount\", count)\n                fmt.Printf(\"Successfully packaged %d files into %s\\n\", count, zipPath)\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: REST API Integration with Bearer Token (`language=\"go\"`)\n\nIssues an HTTP POST request to an external token authority, parses the response JSON, and saves the bearer token into the pipeline registry for downstream `\u003chttp_client\u003e` steps.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"AuthEndpoint\" type=\"string\" value=\"https://auth.internal.example.com/oauth/token\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ClientId\" type=\"string\" value=\"flow-etl-worker\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ClientSecret\" type=\"string\" value=\"{{SECURE_CLIENT_SECRET}}\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"AccessToken\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"FetchAuthToken\" language=\"go\" timeout=\"30s\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n\n            import (\n                \"bytes\"\n                \"encoding/json\"\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"io\"\n                \"net/http\"\n                \"time\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n\n            type TokenResponse struct {\n                AccessToken string `json:\"access_token\"`\n                TokenType   string `json:\"token_type\"`\n                ExpiresIn   int    `json:\"expires_in\"`\n            }\n\n            func main() {\n                url    := vars.GetString(\"AuthEndpoint\")\n                id     := vars.GetString(\"ClientId\")\n                secret := vars.GetString(\"ClientSecret\")\n\n                payload := map[string]string{\n                    \"client_id\":     id,\n                    \"client_secret\": secret,\n                    \"grant_type\":    \"client_credentials\",\n                }\n                bodyBytes, _ := json.Marshal(payload)\n\n                client := \u0026http.Client{Timeout: 15 * time.Second}\n                resp, err := client.Post(url, \"application/json\", bytes.NewBuffer(bodyBytes))\n                if err != nil {\n                    fmt.Printf(\"HTTP Request failed: %v\\n\", err)\n                    return\n                }\n                defer resp.Body.Close()\n\n                respData, _ := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)\n                if resp.StatusCode \u003c 200 || resp.StatusCode \u003e= 300 {\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Auth server returned status %d: %s\\n\", resp.StatusCode, string(respData))\n                    return\n                }\n\n                var tokenResp TokenResponse\n                if err := json.Unmarshal(respData, \u0026tokenResp); err != nil {\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Failed to decode token JSON: %v\\n\", err)\n                    return\n                }\n\n                vars.SetString(\"AccessToken\", tokenResp.AccessToken)\n                fmt.Printf(\"Successfully acquired token (expires in %d sec)\\n\", tokenResp.ExpiresIn)\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Downstream HTTP step consumes the generated token --\u003e\n        \u003chttp_client id=\"FetchProtectedData\"\n                     url=\"https://api.internal.example.com/v1/customers\"\n                     method=\"GET\"\n                     headers=\"Authorization:Bearer {{AccessToken}},Accept:application/json\"\n                     output_var=\"CustomersJSON\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: Cross-Platform PowerShell Core (`language=\"pwsh\"`)\n\nDemonstrates modern PowerShell Core (`pwsh`) syntax that runs identically on Linux, Windows, and macOS to calculate disk storage metrics.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DiskSpaceReport\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"CheckFreeDiskSpace\" language=\"pwsh\" output_var=\"DiskSpaceReport\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            # Cross-platform .NET DriveInfo works on Windows, Linux, and macOS\n            $drives = [System.IO.DriveInfo]::GetDrives() | Where-Object { $_.IsReady -and $_.DriveType -eq \u0027Fixed\u0027 }\n            \n            $report = @()\n            foreach ($d in $drives) {\n                $totalGB = [math]::Round($d.TotalSize / 1GB, 2)\n                $freeGB  = [math]::Round($d.AvailableFreeSpace / 1GB, 2)\n                $pctFree = [math]::Round(($freeGB / $totalGB) * 100, 1)\n\n                $report += [PSCustomObject]@{\n                    RootDirectory = $d.RootDirectory.FullName\n                    TotalGB       = $totalGB\n                    FreeGB        = $freeGB\n                    PercentFree   = $pctFree\n                }\n            }\n\n            $report | ConvertTo-Json -Compress\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 6: Cross-Platform POSIX Shell Script (`language=\"sh\"`)\n\nA strictly POSIX-compliant shell script that runs under any Bourne-compatible shell (`/bin/sh`, `bash`, `dash`, `zsh`) across Unix environments and Windows Git Bash.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"StagingDir\" type=\"string\" value=\"./staging_data\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"CleanResult\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"CleanupOldStagingFiles\" language=\"sh\" output_var=\"CleanResult\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            # POSIX-compliant file purging: removes .tmp files older than 7 days\n            TARGET_DIR=\"{{StagingDir}}\"\n            \n            if [ -d \"$TARGET_DIR\" ]; then\n                DELETED_COUNT=$(find \"$TARGET_DIR\" -type f -name \"*.tmp\" -mtime +7 -exec rm -f {} + -print | wc -l)\n                echo \"Cleaned up $DELETED_COUNT stale staging files in $TARGET_DIR\"\n            else\n                mkdir -p \"$TARGET_DIR\"\n                echo \"Created missing staging directory: $TARGET_DIR\"\n            fi\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 7: C# Scripting via .NET Script (`language=\"csx\"`)\n\nExecutes C# code using `dotnet-script`, utilizing LINQ and `System.Text.Json` to process a variable payload.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"RawScores\" type=\"string\" value=\"95,82,78,99,88,91,65,74\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ScoreStats\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"ComputeScoreStats\" language=\"csx\" output_var=\"ScoreStats\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            using System;\n            using System.Linq;\n            using System.Text.Json;\n\n            string raw = \"{{RawScores}}\";\n            var scores = raw.Split(\u0027,\u0027, StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries)\n                            .Select(s =\u003e int.Parse(s.Trim()))\n                            .ToList();\n\n            var stats = new\n            {\n                Count = scores.Count,\n                Min = scores.Min(),\n                Max = scores.Max(),\n                Average = Math.Round(scores.Average(), 2),\n                Median = scores.OrderBy(x =\u003e x).Skip(scores.Count / 2).First()\n            };\n\n            Console.Write(JsonSerializer.Serialize(stats));\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 8: Database Connection Health \u0026 Latency Probe (`language=\"go\"` with `\"host/db\"`)\n\nUses the built-in `\"host/db\"` integration package to inspect an active database connection pool, measure network round-trip ping latency in milliseconds, and record health status.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"production_db\"\n                  driver=\"postgres\"\n                  connection_string=\"host=db.example.internal port=5432 user=flow_user password=SecretPassword dbname=analytics sslmode=require\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DbPingLatencyMs\" type=\"int\" value=\"0\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DbStatus\" type=\"string\" value=\"UNKNOWN\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"ProbeDatabaseHealth\" language=\"go\" timeout=\"10s\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n\n            import (\n                \"context\"\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"time\"\n                \"host/db\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n\n            func main() {\n                conn, err := db.Get(\"production_db\")\n                if err != nil {\n                    vars.SetString(\"DbStatus\", \"CONNECTION_POOL_NOT_FOUND\")\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Database pool error: %v\\n\", err)\n                    return\n                }\n\n                ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 5*time.Second)\n                defer cancel()\n\n                start := time.Now()\n                err = conn.PingContext(ctx)\n                durationMs := int(time.Since(start).Milliseconds())\n\n                vars.Set(\"DbPingLatencyMs\", durationMs)\n\n                if err != nil {\n                    vars.SetString(\"DbStatus\", \"PING_FAILED\")\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Database ping failed after %d ms: %v\\n\", durationMs, err)\n                } else {\n                    vars.SetString(\"DbStatus\", \"HEALTHY\")\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Database connection healthy (ping latency: %d ms)\\n\", durationMs)\n                }\n            }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003cassert id=\"EnsureDatabaseHealthy\"\n                var=\"DbStatus\"\n                equals=\"HEALTHY\"\n                message=\"Production database connection failed latency health probe!\"\n                on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 9: Dynamic Script Execution from a Variable (`var=\"...\"`)\n\nExecutes code stored inside a pipeline variable rather than an inline XML body. This pattern enables dynamic scripts generated by earlier pipeline steps or loaded from an external repository or encrypted payload.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DynamicGoCode\" type=\"string\"\u003e\n            \u003cvalue\u003e\n                package main\n                import (\n                    \"fmt\"\n                    \"time\"\n                )\n                func main() {\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Dynamic execution at UTC: %s\\n\", time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339))\n                }\n            \u003c/value\u003e\n        \u003c/variable\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DynamicOutput\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- The var attribute directs Flow to execute the Go code stored in DynamicGoCode --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"RunDynamicCode\"\n                language=\"go\"\n                var=\"DynamicGoCode\"\n                output_var=\"DynamicOutput\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003cscript id=\"LogDynamicOutput\" language=\"powershell\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            Write-Host \"Result of dynamic execution: {{DynamicOutput}}\"\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Error Handling \u0026 Retry Policies\n\nWhen a script fails (e.g. non-zero exit code, unhandled Go panic, or timeout expiration), the `\u003cscript\u003e` node responds according to `on_error`:\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- Stop on failure (Default) --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"CriticalStep\" language=\"go\" on_error=\"stop\"\u003e\n    ...\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- Continue execution on failure --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"OptionalStep\" language=\"bash\" on_error=\"continue\"\u003e\n    ...\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- Automatic retry with exponential or fixed backoff --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"FlakyNetworkCall\"\n        language=\"go\"\n        on_error=\"retry\"\n        retry_count=\"3\"\n        retry_interval=\"5s\"\n        timeout=\"15s\"\u003e\n    ...\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Summary \u0026 Quick Reference\n\n| Goal | Recommended Approach |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Max portability (Windows, Linux, macOS)** | Use `\u003cscript language=\"go\"\u003e` with `\"host/vars\"`. Requires zero host dependencies. |\n| **Pass data between steps** | Use `output_var=\"VarName\"` to capture `stdout`, or `vars.SetString(\"VarName\", val)` in Go. |\n| **Access pipeline variables in script** | Use `{{VarName}}` anywhere in the body, or `vars.GetString(\"VarName\")` in Go. |\n| **Prevent XML parsing errors** | Always enclose script bodies inside `\u003c![CDATA[ ... ]]\u003e`. |\n| **Prevent runaway executions** | Set `timeout=\"30s\"` or `timeout=\"5m\"`. |\n| **Enforce database prerequisites** | Use `language=\"go\"` with `\"host/db\"` to probe connections before running large bulk ETL jobs. |\n","sql.md":"# SQL Query \u0026 Command Node (`\u003csql\u003e`) Guide 🚀\n\nThe `\u003csql\u003e` node executes native SQL scripts, queries, and multi-statement database commands directly on any registered database connection. It is engineered for relational database workloads ranging from simple single-statement lookups to massive multi-million-row in-database transformations and data warehouse consolidations.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Attribute Specifications\n\n| Attribute | Required | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `id` | **Yes** | — | Unique step identifier across the pipeline. |\n| `db` / `database` | **Yes** | — | Name of the database configured in the `\u003cdatabases\u003e` section. |\n| `output_var` / `into` / `var` | No | — | Pipeline variable name to capture returned query result sets (as JSON arrays) or affected row counts. |\n| `timeout` | No | — | Execution deadline duration (e.g. `30s`, `5m`, `45m`). |\n| `condition` / `cond` | No | — | Condition expression required to execute this step. |\n| `on_error` | No | `stop` | Failure handling strategy: `stop`, `continue`, or `retry`. |\n| `retry_count` | No | `0` | Number of retries on transient connection or query errors. |\n| `retry_interval` | No | `1s` | Duration between retries (e.g. `2s`, `500ms`). |\n| `description` | No | — | Human-readable explanation of this SQL operation. |\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Intelligent Query vs. DML Classification (`isDMLQuery`)\n\nFlow features a built-in token-aware SQL analyzer that automatically distinguishes between data retrieval (`SELECT`, `SHOW`, `WITH`) and data mutation (`INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `MERGE`, `CREATE`, `DROP`):\n\n1. **Comments \u0026 CDATA Stripping**: Leading/trailing single-line comments (`-- ...`), multi-line comments (`/* ... */`), and XML `\u003c![CDATA[ ... ]]\u003e` tags are cleanly stripped before token evaluation.\n2. **Column Name Collision Safety**: Queries selecting columns named `deleted_at`, `is_deleted`, `insert_ts`, or `update_count` are correctly classified as queries and are **not** misclassified as DML mutations.\n3. **Row-Returning Mutations**: Statements utilizing clauses like `RETURNING` (PostgreSQL, SQLite, Oracle) or `OUTPUT` (SQL Server) are executed as queries so returned rows are preserved in `output_var`.\n4. **Summary Selects \u0026 Rowcounts**: Complex multi-statement batches that conclude with a scalar summary (such as `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT;` or `SELECT count(*);`) return that scalar value directly into `output_var`.\n\n---\n\n## 🔒 Atomic Transactions with `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`\n\nTo coordinate multiple `\u003csql\u003e` steps inside an atomic database transaction with automatic commit and rollback:\n\n```xml\n\u003cgroup id=\"financial_batch\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"main_db\" timeout=\"2m\"\u003e\n    \u003csql id=\"debit_account\" db=\"main_db\"\u003e\n        UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 250 WHERE id = 101;\n    \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003csql id=\"credit_account\" db=\"main_db\"\u003e\n        UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 250 WHERE id = 202;\n    \u003c/sql\u003e\n\u003c/group\u003e\n```\n\n- **Dialect-Aware Savepoints**: Nested groups on the same database automatically use savepoints (`SAVE TRANSACTION` on MSSQL, ANSI `SAVEPOINT` on PostgreSQL/SQLite/MySQL).\n- **Deferred Rollback**: If any child step errors, the entire transaction (or nested savepoint) is rolled back before propagation.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Practical Examples\n\n### Example 1: Schema Initialization \u0026 Table Setup (DDL)\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"reporting_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./reporting.db\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"init_schema\" db=\"reporting_db\" description=\"Initialize reporting tables\"\u003e\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customer_daily_summary (\n                customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n                total_spend REAL NOT NULL,\n                last_order_date TEXT NOT NULL,\n                status TEXT DEFAULT \u0027ACTIVE\u0027\n            );\n            CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_cust_status ON customer_daily_summary(status);\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Data Extraction into Pipeline Variable\n\nExtracting rows into a pipeline variable formats the result as a structured JSON array suitable for downstream loops, assertions, or templates:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"oltp_pg\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/appdb\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"get_top_spenders\" \n             db=\"oltp_pg\" \n             output_var=\"VIP_CUSTOMERS\"\n             timeout=\"30s\"\u003e\n            SELECT \n                customer_id, \n                full_name, \n                lifetime_value, \n                country_code\n            FROM customers\n            WHERE lifetime_value \u003e= 10000.00\n            ORDER BY lifetime_value DESC\n            LIMIT 50;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Assert that high spenders were retrieved --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"check_vip_count\" \n                var=\"VIP_CUSTOMERS\" \n                condition=\"count(VIP_CUSTOMERS) \u003e 0\" \n                message=\"Expected at least one VIP customer.\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Multi-Stage Enterprise ETL Batch Returning `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT`\n\nThis production pattern runs in-database transformations across millions of rows in SQL Server without transferring heavy intermediate datasets across the network:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"dw_sqlserver\" \n                  driver=\"sqlserver\" \n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://etl:P@ssword1@dw.internal:1433?database=EnterpriseDW\"\n                  workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"nightly_consolidation\" \n             db=\"dw_sqlserver\" \n             timeout=\"45m\"\n             output_var=\"ROWS_PROCESSED\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            SET NOCOUNT ON;\n            SET XACT_ABORT ON;\n\n            BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n\n            -- 1. Deduplicate raw incoming events into staging temp table\n            IF OBJECT_ID(\u0027tempdb..#StagedEvents\u0027) IS NOT NULL DROP TABLE #StagedEvents;\n\n            CREATE TABLE #StagedEvents (\n                EventKey BIGINT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,\n                AccountID INT NOT NULL,\n                Amount DECIMAL(18,4) NOT NULL,\n                EventTimestamp DATETIME2(3) NOT NULL\n            );\n\n            INSERT INTO #StagedEvents WITH (TABLOCK)\n            SELECT EventKey, AccountID, Amount, EventTimestamp\n            FROM (\n                SELECT EventKey, AccountID, Amount, EventTimestamp,\n                       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY EventKey ORDER BY IngestTimestamp DESC) AS rn\n                FROM Ingest.RawEvents\n                WHERE ProcessingStatus = \u0027PENDING\u0027\n            ) t WHERE t.rn = 1;\n\n            -- 2. Upsert into production Fact table\n            MERGE INTO Fact.AccountEvents WITH (TABLOCK) AS tgt\n            USING #StagedEvents AS src\n            ON (tgt.EventKey = src.EventKey)\n            WHEN MATCHED THEN\n                UPDATE SET tgt.Amount = src.Amount, tgt.LastModifiedUTC = GETUTCDATE()\n            WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN\n                INSERT (EventKey, AccountID, Amount, EventTimestamp, CreatedUTC)\n                VALUES (src.EventKey, src.AccountID, src.Amount, src.EventTimestamp, GETUTCDATE());\n\n            -- 3. Mark processed in raw ledger\n            UPDATE raw\n            SET raw.ProcessingStatus = \u0027PROCESSED\u0027\n            FROM Ingest.RawEvents raw\n            INNER JOIN #StagedEvents stg ON raw.EventKey = stg.EventKey;\n\n            DROP TABLE #StagedEvents;\n\n            COMMIT TRANSACTION;\n\n            -- 4. Return strictly scalar rowcount to Flow output variable\n            SELECT @@ROWCOUNT AS affected_rows;\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: PostgreSQL `RETURNING` Clause Capturing Inserted Rows\n\n```xml\n\u003csql id=\"insert_and_capture_ids\" db=\"oltp_pg\" output_var=\"INSERTED_RECORDS\"\u003e\n    INSERT INTO order_audit (order_id, action, performed_by)\n    VALUES \n        (1001, \u0027APPROVED\u0027, \u0027system\u0027),\n        (1002, \u0027APPROVED\u0027, \u0027system\u0027)\n    RETURNING id, order_id, created_at;\n\u003c/sql\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Best Practices\n\n1. **Keep Heavy Data in the Engine**: When transforming existing database records, use `\u003csql\u003e` with in-database SQL (`MERGE`, `INSERT INTO ... SELECT`, `UPDATE ... JOIN`) rather than pulling millions of records into Go memory.\n2. **End Multi-Statement Batches with a Scalar Query**: When executing DDL/DML batches where you need telemetry metrics, conclude with `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT;` or `SELECT count(*);`.\n3. **Use CDATA for Complex Queries**: Enclose SQL containing comparison operators (`\u003c`, `\u003e`, `\u003c=`, `\u003e=`) or `\u0026` characters within `\u003c![CDATA[ ... ]]\u003e` to prevent XML parsing syntax errors.\n4. **Tune Timeouts**: Always set explicit `timeout=\"5m\"` or `timeout=\"30m\"` for long-running batches to prevent indefinite pipeline hangs on locked resources.\n","sql_and_sql_bulk.md":"# SQL and SQL Bulk Operations Guide 🚀\r\n\r\nThe Flow execution engine provides first-class support for relational database workloads ranging from simple single-statement lookups to massive multi-million-row streaming replications and multi-stage enterprise data warehouse transforms.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Workload Recommendation Matrix: What Node \u0026 Options Should You Use?\r\n\r\nChoosing the right node and tuning configuration is critical for achieving optimal throughput, minimizing connection contention, and avoiding memory or transaction bottlenecks.\r\n\r\n| Workload Scenario | Recommended Node | Key Options \u0026 Attributes | Rationale \u0026 Performance Impact |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Schema Initialization (DDL) \u0026 Basic Queries** | `\u003csql\u003e` | `db=\"db_name\"`\u003cbr\u003e`output_var=\"MY_VAR\"` | Direct execution. Statements returning rows (`SELECT`, `SHOW`, `WITH`) format outputs into `output_var`. |\r\n| **Complex In-Database ETL / Multi-Stage Transformations** | `\u003csql\u003e` | `db=\"db_name\"`\u003cbr\u003e`output_var=\"ROW_COUNT\"` | Ideal for heavy in-database processing (CTEs, `MERGE`, temp tables, indexing). Keeps data inside the database engine; ends with `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT` or summary scalar. |\r\n| **Cross-Database Table Replication (e.g. Postgres → SQL Server)** | `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` | `db=\"src_db\"`\u003cbr\u003e`target_db=\"dst_db\"`\u003cbr\u003e`target_table=\"fact_table\"`\u003cbr\u003e`batch_size=\"10000\"` | Streams rows across disparate engines with constant $O(1)$ memory. Eliminates intermediate CSV/JSON files and memory bloat. |\r\n| **High-Throughput SQL Server Ingestion** | `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` | `tablock=\"true\"`\u003cbr\u003e`check_constraints=\"false\"`\u003cbr\u003e`batch_size=\"25000\"` | Activates SQL Server minimal logging (`TABLOCK`) and disables constraint checking during bulk insertion for maximum MB/sec throughput. |\r\n| **Preserving Identity \u0026 Explicit NULLs during Bulk Copies** | `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` | `keep_nulls=\"true\"`\u003cbr\u003e`fire_triggers=\"false\"` | Prevents default column constraints from replacing explicit `NULL` values during bulk ingestion. |\r\n| **Multi-Step Atomic Transactions with Rollback** | `\u003cgroup\u003e` + `\u003csql\u003e` | `transaction=\"true\"`\u003cbr\u003e`db=\"db_name\"`\u003cbr\u003e`timeout=\"60s\"` | Wraps multiple child `\u003csql\u003e` steps in a single atomic transaction. Automatically leverages dialect-aware savepoints (`SAVE TRANSACTION` for MSSQL, ANSI `SAVEPOINT` for PostgreSQL/SQLite/MySQL) for nested groups on the same database. |\r\n| **Row-by-Row Dynamic Operations (e.g. Webhook / API per record)** | `\u003cforeach\u003e` + `\u003csql\u003e` | `db=\"db_name\"`\u003cbr\u003e`buffer=\"false\"` (default) | Streams rows one-by-one into child tasks with constant memory, safely handling 60M+ rows. Use `buffer=\"true\"` only for small control sets (\u003c100k rows) to release DB locks early. |\r\n\r\n### Decision Flowchart\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    Start[\"Incoming Database Task\"] --\u003e Q1{\"Are you moving data across tables or different databases?\"}\r\n    \r\n    Q1 -- \"Yes (Replication / Ingestion)\" --\u003e Q2{\"Are you transforming rows in Go or calling external APIs per row?\"}\r\n    Q2 -- \"No (Direct Copy)\" --\u003e Bulk[\"Use \u003csql_bulk\u003e\u003cbr\u003e• Set batch_size=10000-50000\u003cbr\u003e• Enable tablock for MSSQL\u003cbr\u003e• Zero RAM footprint\"]\r\n    Q2 -- \"Yes (Row-by-Row APIs/Scripts)\" --\u003e ForEach[\"Use \u003cforeach\u003e\u003cbr\u003e• Default streaming mode: O(1) RAM\u003cbr\u003e• Sub-millisecond cursor fetch\"]\r\n\r\n    Q1 -- \"No (In-Database Processing)\" --\u003e Q3{\"Do multiple steps require atomic commit / rollback?\"}\r\n    Q3 -- \"Yes\" --\u003e GroupTx[\"Wrap in \u003cgroup transaction=\u0027true\u0027 db=\u0027...\u0027\u003e\u003cbr\u003e• Automatic SAVEPOINT nesting\u003cbr\u003e• Safe deferred rollbacks\"]\r\n    Q3 -- \"No\" --\u003e SqlNode[\"Use standard \u003csql\u003e\u003cbr\u003e• Supports DDL, DML, CTEs, MERGE\u003cbr\u003e• Intelligent query vs DML detection\u003cbr\u003e• Captures SELECT @@ROWCOUNT\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Node Reference \u0026 Attribute Specifications\r\n\r\n### 1. The `\u003csql\u003e` Node\r\n\r\nThe `\u003csql\u003e` node executes native SQL scripts directly on a registered database handle.\r\n\r\n#### Attributes\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Required | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| `id` | **Yes** | — | Unique step identifier across the pipeline. |\r\n| `db` / `database` | **Yes** | — | Name of the database configured in `\u003cdatabases\u003e`. |\r\n| `output_var` / `var` | No | — | Name of pipeline variable to capture the returned query result set or affected row count. |\r\n| `timeout` | No | — | Execution deadline duration (e.g., `30s`, `5m`). |\r\n\r\n#### Intelligent Query vs. DML Classification (`isDMLQuery`)\r\nFlow features a built-in token-aware SQL analyzer:\r\n- **Comments \u0026 CDATA Stripping**: Comments (`-- ...`, `/* ... */`) and XML CDATA sections are cleanly stripped prior to token inspection.\r\n- **Column Name Safety**: Queries selecting columns named `deleted_at`, `is_deleted`, `insert_ts`, or `update_count` are recognized as standard queries and are **not** misclassified as DML statements.\r\n- **Row-Returning Mutations**: Statements utilizing clauses like `RETURNING` (Postgres, SQLite, Oracle) or `OUTPUT` (SQL Server) are executed as queries so returned rows are preserved in `output_var`.\r\n- **Summary Selects**: Complex multi-statement DML batches that conclude with a scalar summary (such as `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT;`) return that scalar result directly to `output_var`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2. The `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` Node\r\n\r\nThe `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` node is an optimized streaming pipeline primitive that pipes rows from a source SQL query directly into a destination table.\r\n\r\n#### Attributes\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Required | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| `id` | **Yes** | — | Unique step identifier. |\r\n| `db` / `database` | **Yes** | — | Source database connection handle. |\r\n| `target_db` | No | Source `db` | Destination database connection handle. |\r\n| `target_table` | **Yes** | — | Destination table name receiving bulk inserts. |\r\n| `batch_size` | No | `10000` | Number of rows committed per network chunk. |\r\n| `tablock` | No | `true` | *(SQL Server)* Acquires table-level lock for minimal transaction logging. |\r\n| `check_constraints` | No | `false` | *(SQL Server)* Validates foreign keys/check constraints during bulk load. |\r\n| `fire_triggers` | No | `false` | *(SQL Server)* Fires insert triggers on the target table during bulk load. |\r\n| `keep_nulls` | No | `false` | *(SQL Server)* Preserves incoming `NULL` values instead of replacing with column defaults. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | — | Stores total number of rows streamed to destination. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 💡 Practical Examples\r\n\r\n### Example 1: Simple SQL Query and Bulk Copy\r\n\r\nA clean, minimal example demonstrating table setup, standard `\u003csql\u003e` querying into a pipeline variable, and cross-database `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` replication.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"oltp_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/orders_app\" /\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"reporting_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./reporting.db\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- 1. Simple SQL: Initialize target reporting table --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"init_reporting_schema\" db=\"reporting_db\"\u003e\r\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customer_daily_spend (\r\n                customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\r\n                total_amount REAL,\r\n                order_date TEXT\r\n            );\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 2. Simple SQL Bulk: Stream aggregated results from Postgres to SQLite --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql_bulk id=\"sync_daily_spend\"\r\n                  db=\"oltp_db\"\r\n                  target_db=\"reporting_db\"\r\n                  target_table=\"customer_daily_spend\"\r\n                  batch_size=\"5000\"\r\n                  output_var=\"RECORDS_SYNCED\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT \r\n                customer_id, \r\n                SUM(amount) AS total_amount, \r\n                CAST(order_date AS TEXT) AS order_date\r\n            FROM orders\r\n            WHERE order_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL \u00271 day\u0027\r\n            GROUP BY customer_id, order_date;\r\n        \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- 3. Simple SQL: Query the row count from the destination table --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"verify_sync\" db=\"reporting_db\" output_var=\"VERIFY_COUNT\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_synced_customers FROM customer_daily_spend;\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 2: Complex Enterprise SQL Transform on Millions of Rows Returning `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT`\r\n\r\nThis production-grade script executes a high-volume data warehouse consolidation across tens of millions of records on Microsoft SQL Server. It performs:\r\n1. Temporary table creation with clustered columnstore and primary key hints.\r\n2. Deduplication using window functions (`ROW_NUMBER()`).\r\n3. High-volume `MERGE` into a target fact table (handling inserts and updates in a single pass).\r\n4. Cascading updates to audit ledger status records.\r\n5. Index reorganization on modified partitions.\r\n6. Returns exclusively `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT` so the pipeline captures the total affected rows without transmitting massive intermediate datasets over the wire.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"dw_sqlserver\" \r\n                  driver=\"sqlserver\" \r\n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://etl_user:P@ssw0rd123@sql-dw.corp.internal:1433?database=EnterpriseDW\u0026amp;encrypt=true\"\r\n                  max_open_conns=\"50\"\r\n                  max_idle_conns=\"10\"\r\n                  workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Complex Enterprise Batch: Executes multi-stage transforms on millions of rows, returning only @@ROWCOUNT --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql id=\"execute_nightly_warehouse_consolidation\" \r\n             db=\"dw_sqlserver\" \r\n             timeout=\"45m\"\r\n             output_var=\"TOTAL_ROWS_AFFECTED\"\u003e\r\n            \u003c![CDATA[\r\n            SET NOCOUNT ON;\r\n            SET XACT_ABORT ON;\r\n\r\n            BEGIN TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\n            -- 1. Create optimized staging structure in tempdb\r\n            IF OBJECT_ID(\u0027tempdb..#StagedEvents\u0027) IS NOT NULL \r\n                DROP TABLE #StagedEvents;\r\n\r\n            CREATE TABLE #StagedEvents (\r\n                EventKey BIGINT NOT NULL,\r\n                AccountID INT NOT NULL,\r\n                EventCode VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\r\n                PayloadAmount DECIMAL(18,4) NOT NULL,\r\n                EventTimestamp DATETIME2(3) NOT NULL,\r\n                RowHash VARBINARY(32) NOT NULL,\r\n                DedupRank INT NOT NULL,\r\n                INDEX IX_StagedEvents_Key CLUSTERED (EventKey, AccountID)\r\n            );\r\n\r\n            -- 2. Populate staging and filter duplicates across 15M+ raw ingest records\r\n            INSERT INTO #StagedEvents WITH (TABLOCK)\r\n            SELECT \r\n                r.EventKey,\r\n                r.AccountID,\r\n                r.EventCode,\r\n                r.PayloadAmount,\r\n                r.EventTimestamp,\r\n                HASHBYTES(\u0027SHA2_256\u0027, CONCAT(r.EventKey, \u0027|\u0027, r.AccountID, \u0027|\u0027, r.PayloadAmount, \u0027|\u0027, r.EventTimestamp)) AS RowHash,\r\n                ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\r\n                    PARTITION BY r.EventKey, r.AccountID \r\n                    ORDER BY r.IngestTimestamp DESC, r.SequenceID DESC\r\n                ) AS DedupRank\r\n            FROM RawIngest.IncomingEvents r WITH (NOLOCK)\r\n            WHERE r.IngestDate \u003e= CAST(DATEADD(DAY, -1, GETUTCDATE()) AS DATE)\r\n              AND r.ProcessingStatus = \u0027PENDING\u0027;\r\n\r\n            -- 3. Perform High-Volume MERGE into Production Fact Table\r\n            MERGE INTO Fact.AccountTransactions WITH (TABLOCK) AS tgt\r\n            USING (\r\n                SELECT \r\n                    EventKey,\r\n                    AccountID,\r\n                    EventCode,\r\n                    PayloadAmount,\r\n                    EventTimestamp,\r\n                    RowHash\r\n                FROM #StagedEvents\r\n                WHERE DedupRank = 1\r\n            ) AS src\r\n            ON (tgt.EventKey = src.EventKey AND tgt.AccountID = src.AccountID)\r\n            \r\n            -- Update existing records if payload or hash has evolved\r\n            WHEN MATCHED AND tgt.RowHash \u003c\u003e src.RowHash THEN\r\n                UPDATE SET \r\n                    tgt.EventCode = src.EventCode,\r\n                    tgt.PayloadAmount = src.PayloadAmount,\r\n                    tgt.EventTimestamp = src.EventTimestamp,\r\n                    tgt.RowHash = src.RowHash,\r\n                    tgt.LastModifiedUTC = GETUTCDATE(),\r\n                    tgt.VersionNumber = tgt.VersionNumber + 1\r\n\r\n            -- Insert newly recognized events\r\n            WHEN NOT MATCHED BY TARGET THEN\r\n                INSERT (\r\n                    EventKey,\r\n                    AccountID,\r\n                    EventCode,\r\n                    PayloadAmount,\r\n                    EventTimestamp,\r\n                    RowHash,\r\n                    CreatedUTC,\r\n                    LastModifiedUTC,\r\n                    VersionNumber\r\n                )\r\n                VALUES (\r\n                    src.EventKey,\r\n                    src.AccountID,\r\n                    src.EventCode,\r\n                    src.PayloadAmount,\r\n                    src.EventTimestamp,\r\n                    src.RowHash,\r\n                    GETUTCDATE(),\r\n                    GETUTCDATE(),\r\n                    1\r\n                );\r\n\r\n            -- 4. Mark staging source rows as PROCESSED in the raw ingest ledger\r\n            UPDATE raw\r\n            SET \r\n                raw.ProcessingStatus = \u0027PROCESSED\u0027,\r\n                raw.ProcessedUTC = GETUTCDATE()\r\n            FROM RawIngest.IncomingEvents raw\r\n            INNER JOIN #StagedEvents stg \r\n                ON raw.EventKey = stg.EventKey \r\n               AND raw.AccountID = stg.AccountID\r\n            WHERE stg.DedupRank = 1;\r\n\r\n            -- 5. Drop temp table to reclaim tempdb storage immediately\r\n            DROP TABLE #StagedEvents;\r\n\r\n            COMMIT TRANSACTION;\r\n\r\n            -- 6. Return strictly @@ROWCOUNT to the Flow pipeline variable\r\n            SELECT @@ROWCOUNT AS affected_rows;\r\n            ]]\u003e\r\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Verify execution metric in subsequent step --\u003e\r\n        \u003cscript id=\"log_affected_rows\" language=\"go\"\u003e\r\n            package main\r\n            import (\r\n                \"fmt\"\r\n                \"host/vars\"\r\n            )\r\n            func main() {\r\n                count := vars.GetString(\"TOTAL_ROWS_AFFECTED\")\r\n                fmt.Printf(\"Warehouse consolidation complete. Affected rows reported: %s\\n\", count)\r\n            }\r\n        \u003c/script\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### Why This Pattern Works in Flow\r\n1. **Intelligent Query Detection**: Flow strips the leading T-SQL commands (`SET NOCOUNT ON`, `BEGIN TRANSACTION`, `MERGE`, etc.) and detects that the script concludes with a row-returning statement (`SELECT @@ROWCOUNT`).\r\n2. **Zero In-Memory Payload**: Instead of returning 15 million rows across the network into Go\u0027s memory, the database handles all merges and updates locally using its storage engine. Only the single scalar row count is returned and stored in `TOTAL_ROWS_AFFECTED`.\r\n3. **Safe Timeouts**: The `timeout=\"45m\"` attribute ensures that long-running operations are granted appropriate time to complete, while guaranteeing cleanup if a threshold is exceeded.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Example 3: High-Performance Cross-Database Bulk Replication (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`)\r\n\r\nThis example replicates 20 million rows from a PostgreSQL transaction log into a Microsoft SQL Server data lake staging table with minimal transaction logging and optimized batch sizes.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"source_pg\" \r\n                  driver=\"postgres\" \r\n                  connection_string=\"postgresql://pg_read:Secret2026@pg-cluster.internal:5432/tx_db?sslmode=require\"\r\n                  max_open_conns=\"30\"\r\n                  workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"dest_mssql\" \r\n                  driver=\"sqlserver\" \r\n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://sa:Secret2026@mssql-dw.internal:1433?database=StagingLake\"\r\n                  max_open_conns=\"30\"\r\n                  workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Stream 20M+ rows directly between engines with zero RAM buffering --\u003e\r\n        \u003csql_bulk id=\"replicate_tx_log\"\r\n                  db=\"source_pg\"\r\n                  target_db=\"dest_mssql\"\r\n                  target_table=\"stg_transaction_log\"\r\n                  batch_size=\"25000\"\r\n                  tablock=\"true\"\r\n                  check_constraints=\"false\"\r\n                  fire_triggers=\"false\"\r\n                  keep_nulls=\"true\"\r\n                  output_var=\"REPLICATED_ROW_COUNT\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT \r\n                tx_id,\r\n                account_id,\r\n                merchant_id,\r\n                amount,\r\n                currency,\r\n                created_at,\r\n                metadata_json\r\n            FROM transactions.ledger\r\n            WHERE created_at \u003e= \u00272026-01-01 00:00:00Z\u0027\r\n            ORDER BY tx_id ASC;\r\n        \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### Key Performance Features in this Configuration:\r\n- `batch_size=\"25000\"`: Minimizes network round-trip overhead while remaining within server memory buffer limits.\r\n- `tablock=\"true\"`: Enables table-level locking in SQL Server, switching ingestion from row-by-row logging to fast minimal bulk logging.\r\n- `check_constraints=\"false\"`: Bypasses constraint validation during the copy to maximize insertion throughput (constraints can be validated in bulk afterward).\r\n- `keep_nulls=\"true\"`: Ensures explicit `NULL` values from PostgreSQL are retained and not overwritten by SQL Server column default definitions.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🛠️ Summary Best Practices\r\n\r\n1. **Keep Transformations in the Database Engine**: When transforming existing database records, use `\u003csql\u003e` with in-database DML (`MERGE`, `INSERT INTO ... SELECT`, `UPDATE ... JOIN`) rather than pulling rows into Go to modify them. End your script with `SELECT @@ROWCOUNT` or `SELECT count(*)`.\r\n2. **Use `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` for Table-to-Table Transfers**: Whenever data must move between different tables or databases, always prefer `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` over custom loops or scripts.\r\n3. **Use Default Streaming in `\u003cforeach\u003e` for Large Data**: If you must loop over query results, rely on `\u003cforeach\u003e` default streaming mode ($O(1)$ memory). Only add `buffer=\"true\"` if your loop contains \u003c100,000 rows and you need to release the database connection immediately.\r\n4. **Wrap Multi-Step Sequences in `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`**: Ensure atomicity and eliminate deadlocks using Flow\u0027s automatic dialect-aware savepoint nesting (`SAVE TRANSACTION` on MSSQL, `SAVEPOINT` on PostgreSQL/SQLite/MySQL).\r\n","sql_bulk.md":"# SQL Bulk Streaming Replication Node (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`) Guide 🚀\n\nThe `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` node is an optimized pipeline streaming primitive that extracts rows from a source SQL query and streams them directly into a target destination table with constant $O(1)$ memory usage. It bypasses intermediate disk files (CSV, Parquet, JSON) and eliminates memory bloat, safely copying tens of millions of records across heterogeneous database engines.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Attribute Specifications\n\n| Attribute | Required | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `id` | **Yes** | — | Unique step identifier across the pipeline. |\n| `db` / `database` | **Yes** | — | Source database connection declared in `\u003cdatabases\u003e`. |\n| `target_table` / `table` | **Yes** | — | Destination table name receiving bulk inserts. |\n| `target_db` / `target_database` | No | Source `db` | Destination database connection handle (defaults to source database for intra-database table copies). |\n| `batch_size` | No | `10000` | Number of rows committed per network chunk (recommended: `10000`–`50000`). |\n| `tablock` | No | `true` | *(SQL Server)* Acquires table-level lock for minimal transaction logging and maximum insertion speed. |\n| `check_constraints` | No | `false` | *(SQL Server)* Validates foreign keys and check constraints during bulk load. Set `false` for fastest ingestion. |\n| `fire_triggers` | No | `false` | *(SQL Server)* Executes insert triggers on the target table during bulk load. Set `false` for high-throughput ETL. |\n| `keep_nulls` | No | `false` | *(SQL Server)* Preserves incoming `NULL` values instead of replacing them with target column default definitions. |\n| `timeout` | No | — | Bulk copy timeout duration (e.g. `15m`, `2h`). |\n| `output_var` | No | — | Captures total number of rows streamed to destination into a pipeline variable. |\n| `condition` / `cond` | No | — | Condition expression required to execute this step. |\n| `on_error` | No | `stop` | Failure handling strategy: `stop`, `continue`, or `retry`. |\n| `retry_count` | No | `0` | Number of retries on transient connection errors. |\n| `description` | No | — | Human-readable explanation of this bulk replication step. |\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 High-Performance Tuning Matrix\n\n| Option / Parameter | High-Throughput ETL Setting | Safe Audit / Strict Setting | Impact \u0026 Mechanism |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `batch_size` | `25000` to `50000` | `5000` | Larger batch sizes amortize network roundtrips. Keep $\\le 50000$ to avoid excessive memory buffers on the target server. |\n| `tablock` | `true` | `false` | **SQL Server**: Enables table-level exclusive lock, qualifying the operation for minimal transaction log writes ($10\\times$ faster). |\n| `check_constraints` | `false` | `true` | When `false`, SQL Server bypasses checking foreign keys per row during bulk load. Validate constraints in bulk afterward. |\n| `fire_triggers` | `false` | `true` | Disabling triggers prevents row-by-row trigger firing overhead during multi-million-row ingestion. |\n| `keep_nulls` | `true` | `false` | When `true`, explicit `NULL` values from source columns are retained instead of being overwritten by column default constraints. |\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Practical Examples\n\n### Example 1: Cross-Database Aggregation Streaming (PostgreSQL → SQLite)\n\nStreams aggregated daily orders directly from a production PostgreSQL instance into a local reporting SQLite database:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"oltp_pg\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@pg-primary:5432/orders\" /\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"reporting_sqlite\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./reporting_daily.db\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Initialize destination schema --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"init_dst_schema\" db=\"reporting_sqlite\"\u003e\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS daily_sales_summary (\n                customer_id INTEGER NOT NULL,\n                total_amount REAL NOT NULL,\n                order_count INTEGER NOT NULL,\n                summary_date TEXT NOT NULL\n            );\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Stream aggregated rows directly into SQLite --\u003e\n        \u003csql_bulk id=\"sync_daily_sales\"\n                  db=\"oltp_pg\"\n                  target_db=\"reporting_sqlite\"\n                  target_table=\"daily_sales_summary\"\n                  batch_size=\"5000\"\n                  output_var=\"ROWS_COPIED\"\u003e\n            SELECT \n                customer_id,\n                SUM(amount) AS total_amount,\n                COUNT(*) AS order_count,\n                CAST(order_date AS TEXT) AS summary_date\n            FROM orders\n            WHERE order_date \u003e= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL \u00271 day\u0027\n            GROUP BY customer_id, order_date;\n        \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Validate with assertion --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"verify_replication\"\n                var=\"ROWS_COPIED\"\n                condition=\"ROWS_COPIED \u003e 0\"\n                message=\"Expected at least one daily summary record to be copied.\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Massive 20M+ Row Ingestion into SQL Server with TABLOCK\n\nTransfers millions of transaction records from a transactional PostgreSQL log into a Microsoft SQL Server enterprise staging table:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"source_pg\" \n                  driver=\"postgres\" \n                  connection_string=\"postgresql://etl:Secret2026@pg-cluster.corp:5432/tx_db?sslmode=require\"\n                  max_open_conns=\"30\"\n                  workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\n\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"dest_mssql\" \n                  driver=\"sqlserver\" \n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://sa:Secret2026@mssql-dw.corp:1433?database=StagingLake\"\n                  max_open_conns=\"30\"\n                  workload=\"bulk\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003csql_bulk id=\"replicate_tx_ledger\"\n                  db=\"source_pg\"\n                  target_db=\"dest_mssql\"\n                  target_table=\"stg_transaction_ledger\"\n                  batch_size=\"25000\"\n                  tablock=\"true\"\n                  check_constraints=\"false\"\n                  fire_triggers=\"false\"\n                  keep_nulls=\"true\"\n                  timeout=\"2h\"\n                  output_var=\"TOTAL_REPLICATED\"\u003e\n            SELECT \n                tx_id,\n                account_id,\n                merchant_id,\n                amount,\n                currency,\n                created_at,\n                metadata_json\n            FROM transactions.ledger\n            WHERE created_at \u003e= \u00272026-01-01 00:00:00Z\u0027\n            ORDER BY tx_id ASC;\n        \u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Intra-Database Table-to-Table Streaming (Archival / Staging)\n\nWhen `target_db` is omitted, `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` automatically reuses the source `db` connection:\n\n```xml\n\u003csql_bulk id=\"archive_closed_tickets\"\n          db=\"crm_database\"\n          target_table=\"tickets_archive_2025\"\n          batch_size=\"10000\"\n          output_var=\"ARCHIVED_COUNT\"\u003e\n    SELECT * \n    FROM tickets \n    WHERE status = \u0027CLOSED\u0027 \n      AND closed_date \u003c \u00272026-01-01\u0027;\n\u003c/sql_bulk\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Workload Comparison: `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` vs. `\u003csql\u003e` vs. `\u003cforeach\u003e`\n\n| Workload Requirement | Recommended Node | Reason |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **Move rows between different database engines** | `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` | Native streaming pipe; zero intermediate files; $O(1)$ RAM usage. |\n| **Transform data within the same database engine** | `\u003csql\u003e` | Keeps data in the database engine (`INSERT INTO ... SELECT`, `MERGE`); fastest performance. |\n| **Call an external REST API or webhook per row** | `\u003cforeach\u003e` | Iterates row-by-row with streaming cursor; allows dynamic child tasks. |\n| **Intra-database table replication** | `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` | Chunks inserts across batches without transaction log exhaustion. |\n","template.md":"# Text Template Generator Node (`\u003ctemplate\u003e`) Guide 📄\n\nThe `\u003ctemplate\u003e` node renders plaintext, configuration files, SQL queries, dynamic emails, or JSON/YAML payloads by leveraging Go\u0027s standard `text/template` engine. It evaluates your active pipeline variables and custom functions, storing the resulting text into a pipeline variable or writing it directly to a disk file.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Attribute Specifications\n\n| Attribute | Required | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `id` | **Yes** | — | Unique step identifier across the pipeline. |\n| `into` / `output_var` / `var` | No | — | Name of pipeline variable to store the rendered text output. |\n| `file` | No | — | File path to write the rendered text directly to disk (or path to external template if used as source). |\n| `name` | No | Step `id` | Template name assigned during template parsing. |\n| `engine` | No | `text` | Templating engine: `text` (Go `text/template`). |\n| `mode` | No | — | Optional output mode or file permission specification. |\n| `timeout` | No | — | Template rendering deadline duration (e.g. `10s`, `1m`). |\n| `condition` / `cond` | No | — | Condition expression required to execute this step. |\n| `description` | No | — | Human-readable explanation of this template step. |\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e If you are generating HTML content (such as HTML emails or dashboard reports), use [`\u003ctemplate_html\u003e`](template_html.md) instead, which provides automatic contextual escaping to prevent security vulnerabilities.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Go Standard Library Reference\n\nThe `\u003ctemplate\u003e` node is powered directly by Go\u0027s `text/template` package. The following official documentation covers the full syntax, actions, variables, pipelines, and built-in functions available inside your templates:\n\n| Resource | URL |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **`text/template` package docs** | [pkg.go.dev/text/template](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template) |\n| **Built-in template functions** | [pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Functions](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Functions) |\n| **Template actions reference** | [pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Actions](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Actions) |\n\n\u003e [!TIP]\n\u003e The full list of built-in functions (`and`, `call`, `html`, `index`, `slice`, `js`, `len`, `not`, `or`, `print`, `printf`, `println`, `urlquery`, comparison operators) is documented at [pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Functions](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Functions).\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Template Syntax \u0026 Expressions\n\nThe `\u003ctemplate\u003e` node exposes Go\u0027s standard `text/template` engine syntax:\n\n### Accessing Pipeline Variables\n- Single variable: `{{.MY_VAR}}` or `{{ .MY_VAR }}`\n- Nested structures / JSON objects: `{{.CONFIG.Database.Host}}`\n- Missing variable safety: Empty strings are rendered if a variable does not exist.\n\n### Conditional Logic\n```go\n{{if .IS_PRODUCTION}}\nEnvironment: PRODUCTION\nLog Level: ERROR\n{{else if .IS_STAGING}}\nEnvironment: STAGING\nLog Level: DEBUG\n{{else}}\nEnvironment: DEVELOPMENT\nLog Level: TRACE\n{{end}}\n```\n\n### Looping Over Collections (`range`)\n```go\n{{range .USERS}}\nUser: {{.name}} | Email: {{.email}} | Role: {{.role}}\n{{else}}\nNo users found in active pipeline batch.\n{{end}}\n```\n\n### Built-In Comparison Functions\n- `eq arg1 arg2`: Returns `true` if `arg1 == arg2`\n- `ne arg1 arg2`: Returns `true` if `arg1 != arg2`\n- `lt arg1 arg2`: Returns `true` if `arg1 \u003c arg2`\n- `le arg1 arg2`: Returns `true` if `arg1 \u003c= arg2`\n- `gt arg1 arg2`: Returns `true` if `arg1 \u003e arg2`\n- `ge arg1 arg2`: Returns `true` if `arg1 \u003e= arg2`\n- `and`, `or`, `not`: Standard boolean operators\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Practical Examples\n\n### Example 1: Inline Template into Pipeline Variable\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"USER_NAME\" type=\"string\" value=\"Alice\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"BATCH_ID\" type=\"string\" value=\"B-2026-101\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"STATUS\" type=\"string\" value=\"SUCCESS\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"render_notification\" into=\"SLACK_MESSAGE\"\u003e\n            [ETL Notification]\n            Batch: {{.BATCH_ID}}\n            Triggered By: {{.USER_NAME}}\n            Status: {{.STATUS}}\n            Timestamp: 2026-10-11T18:00:00Z\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n\n        \u003chttp_client id=\"send_slack\"\n                     url=\"https://hooks.slack.com/services/T00/B00/X00\"\n                     method=\"POST\"\n                     content_type=\"application/json\"\u003e\n            {\"text\": \"{{SLACK_MESSAGE}}\"}\n        \u003c/http_client\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Generating a JSON Payload for Webhook Calls\n\nUse `\u003ctemplate\u003e` to build JSON payloads with dynamic values:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TRANSACTION_ID\" type=\"string\" value=\"TX-99482\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"AMOUNT\" type=\"float\" value=\"1250.75\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"CURRENCY\" type=\"string\" value=\"USD\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"build_webhook_payload\" into=\"POST_BODY\"\u003e\n            {\n                \"event\": \"transaction_processed\",\n                \"transaction_id\": \"{{.TRANSACTION_ID}}\",\n                \"amount\": {{.AMOUNT}},\n                \"currency\": \"{{.CURRENCY}}\",\n                \"reconciled\": true\n            }\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n\n        \u003chttp_client id=\"post_webhook\"\n                     url=\"https://api.partner.com/v1/events\"\n                     method=\"POST\"\n                     content_type=\"application/json\"\u003e\n            {{POST_BODY}}\n        \u003c/http_client\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Dynamic SQL Query Generation\n\nGenerate dynamic SQL statements based on runtime parameters and execute them with a downstream `\u003csql\u003e` node:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TARGET_SCHEMA\" type=\"string\" value=\"staging\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"MIN_BALANCE\" type=\"int\" value=\"5000\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"app_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/appdb\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"build_archive_query\" into=\"ARCHIVE_SQL\"\u003e\n            INSERT INTO {{.TARGET_SCHEMA}}.high_value_accounts (account_id, balance, tier)\n            SELECT id, balance, \u0027GOLD\u0027\n            FROM public.accounts\n            WHERE balance \u003e= {{.MIN_BALANCE}};\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n\n        \u003csql id=\"execute_archive\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\n            {{ARCHIVE_SQL}}\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Writing Formatted Configuration Files Directly to Disk\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"APP_PORT\" type=\"int\" value=\"8080\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"LOG_LEVEL\" type=\"string\" value=\"INFO\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"generate_env_file\" file=\"./dist/.env\"\u003e\n            PORT={{.APP_PORT}}\n            LOG_LEVEL={{.LOG_LEVEL}}\n            BUILD_DATE=2026-10-11\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Summary Best Practices\n\n1. **Use `\u003ctemplate\u003e` for Plaintext \u0026 Structured Non-HTML Formats**: Ideal for SQL, Markdown, CSV, JSON, YAML, and server configuration files.\n2. **Switch to `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` for HTML Documents**: When rendering HTML emails, webpages, or browser dashboards, use `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` for automatic contextual XSS protection.\n3. **Validate Output with Assertions**: Use `\u003cassert\u003e` on variables populated by `\u003ctemplate\u003e` to ensure required tokens or valid JSON formatting were produced before proceeding.\n4. **Full Syntax Reference**: Consult the official Go docs at [pkg.go.dev/text/template](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template) for the complete action syntax, pipelines, variables, and built-in function list.\n","template_and_template_html.md":"# Overview of the `\u003ctemplate\u003e` and `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` Nodes in Flow\n\nThe `\u003ctemplate\u003e` and `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` nodes in `flow` allow you to dynamically generate text, configuration files, emails, or payloads by leveraging Go\u0027s powerful `text/template` engine. The `\u003ctemplate\u003e` node uses the `text/template` engine, while the `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` node uses the `html/template` engine, which provides automatic HTML escaping to help prevent XSS vulnerabilities. They evaluate your current pipeline variables and render them into the desired output format, which can then be saved to a variable for use in subsequent pipeline steps.\n\n## Key Attributes\n* **`id`**: Unique identifier for the template node.\n* **`name`**: (Optional) Name of the template being parsed.\n* **`file`**: (Optional) Path to an external template file. If omitted, the node uses inline text.\n* **`output_var`** (or **`var`**): The variable where the rendered text will be stored.\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\n### 1. Simple Inline Template\nUse the template element to quickly generate text using pipeline variables.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"USER_NAME\" type=\"string\" value=\"Alice\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ROLE\" type=\"string\" value=\"Admin\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"welcome_msg\" output_var=\"EMAIL_BODY\"\u003e\n            Hello {{.USER_NAME}}, \n            Welcome back! Your current role is: {{.ROLE}}.\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n        \u003cscript lang=\"bash\"\u003e\n            echo \"$EMAIL_BODY\"\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. Loading an External Template File\nFor larger templates (like HTML emails or complex configurations), you can point the node to an external file.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"REPORT_DATE\" type=\"string\" value=\"2026-08-25\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TOTAL_SALES\" type=\"float\" value=\"15430.50\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"gen_report\" file=\"templates/monthly_report.tmpl\" output_var=\"HTML_REPORT\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 3. Conditional Rendering\nBecause it uses Go\u0027s `text/template`, you can use native template logic like `if/else` directly inside the template.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"IS_PREMIUM\" type=\"bool\" value=\"true\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"USER\" type=\"string\" value=\"Bob\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"promo_msg\" output_var=\"PROMO_TEXT\"\u003e\n            Hi {{.USER}},\n            {{if .IS_PREMIUM}}\n            Thank you for being a premium subscriber! Here is your 20% discount code.\n            {{else}}\n            Upgrade to premium today to get exclusive discounts!\n            {{end}}\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 4. Generating a JSON Payload for HTTP POST\nA great use case for templates is cleanly generating JSON bodies for subsequent `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` calls.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ORDER_ID\" type=\"int\" value=\"9924\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"STATUS\" type=\"string\" value=\"SHIPPED\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"build_json\" output_var=\"REQ_BODY\"\u003e\n            {\n                \"order_id\": {{.ORDER_ID}},\n                \"status\": \"{{.STATUS}}\",\n                \"timestamp\": \"2026-08-25T18:00:00Z\"\n            }\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n        \n        \u003csql_bulk method=\"POST\" uri=\"https://api.example.com/webhook\" content_type=\"application/json\" data=\"{{REQ_BODY}}\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 5. Dynamic SQL Query Generation\nYou can render complex SQL statements and pass them into SQL scripts or stream ETL steps.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TARGET_TABLE\" type=\"string\" value=\"sales_q3\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"MIN_AMOUNT\" type=\"int\" value=\"1000\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate id=\"build_sql\" output_var=\"DYNAMIC_SQL\"\u003e\n            SELECT id, amount, customer_id \n            FROM dbo.raw_sales \n            WHERE amount \u003e {{.MIN_AMOUNT}}\n            INTO {{.TARGET_TABLE}};\n        \u003c/template\u003e\n        \n        \u003c!-- Execute SQL from variable --\u003e\n        \u003csql db=\"analytics\" var=\"DYNAMIC_SQL\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### 6. Streaming Database Rows to Template and Standard Output (`os.stdio`)\nFor a comprehensive guide and runnable example demonstrating database streaming row iteration, Go template formatting, and direct printing to `os.Stdout` (`os.stdio`), see:\n* Guide: [`docs/db_select_template_stdio.md`](db_select_template_stdio.md)\n* Example Pipeline: [`examples/db_select_template_stdio.xml`](../examples/db_select_template_stdio.xml)\n\n","template_html.md":"# Context-Aware HTML Template Node (`\u003ctemplate_html\u003e`) Guide 🌐\n\nThe `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` node (alias: `\u003chtml_template\u003e`) renders dynamic HTML reports, emails, dashboard components, and status pages using Go\u0027s standard `html/template` package. Unlike standard plaintext templating, `html/template` is context-aware: it automatically inspects the surrounding HTML syntax (tags, attributes, JavaScript, CSS, URIs) and applies the precise escaping required to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and code injection vulnerabilities.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Attribute Specifications\n\n| Attribute | Required | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `id` | **Yes** | — | Unique step identifier across the pipeline. |\n| `into` / `output_var` / `var` | No | — | Pipeline variable name to store the rendered HTML document or fragment. |\n| `file` | No | — | File path to write the rendered HTML directly to disk (e.g. `reports/summary.html`). |\n| `name` | No | Step `id` | Template name assigned during template parsing. |\n| `engine` | No | `html` | Templating engine: `html` (Go `html/template`). |\n| `mode` | No | — | Optional file permission or output formatting specification. |\n| `timeout` | No | — | Template rendering deadline duration (e.g. `10s`, `1m`). |\n| `condition` / `cond` | No | — | Condition expression required to execute this step. |\n| `description` | No | — | Human-readable explanation of this HTML template step. |\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Go Standard Library Reference\n\nThe `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` node is powered directly by Go\u0027s `html/template` package, which shares the same syntax as `text/template` but adds context-aware HTML escaping. Official documentation:\n\n| Resource | URL |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **`html/template` package docs** | [pkg.go.dev/html/template](https://pkg.go.dev/html/template) |\n| **`text/template` syntax reference** | [pkg.go.dev/text/template](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template) |\n| **Built-in template functions** | [pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Functions](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Functions) |\n| **Template actions reference** | [pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Actions](https://pkg.go.dev/text/template#hdr-Actions) |\n| **Security model** | [pkg.go.dev/html/template#hdr-Security_Model](https://pkg.go.dev/html/template#hdr-Security_Model) |\n\n\u003e [!TIP]\n\u003e `html/template` is a drop-in superset of `text/template`. Any valid `text/template` syntax also works in `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` — the only difference is the automatic context-sensitive escaping applied at render time.\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ Context-Aware Escaping Explained\n\nGo\u0027s `html/template` parses the full HTML document grammar. When variables are interpolated, it applies different sanitizers depending on the exact context:\n\n| Injection Context | Template Snippet | Automatic Sanitization Applied |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **HTML Body** | `\u003cp\u003e{{.MESSAGE}}\u003c/p\u003e` | Converts `\u003c`, `\u003e`, `\u0026`, `\"`, and `\u0027` to HTML character entities (`\u0026lt;`, `\u0026gt;`, `\u0026amp;`). |\n| **HTML Attribute** | `\u003cinput value=\"{{.NAME}}\"\u003e` | Quotes and escapes special characters so malicious input cannot break out of attributes. |\n| **Hyperlink / URL** | `\u003ca href=\"{{.LINK}}\"\u003e` | Validates URI schemes; filters dangerous prefixes like `javascript:`. |\n| **Inline JavaScript** | `\u003cscript\u003evar id = {{.USER_ID}};\u003c/script\u003e` | Encodes values into valid JSON/JavaScript literals, preventing script breakout attacks. |\n| **CSS Style Block** | `\u003cdiv style=\"color: {{.COLOR}}\"\u003e` | Strips invalid CSS properties and prevents style-based script injection. |\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Practical Examples\n\n### Example 1: Executive ETL Execution Summary Report (HTML File)\n\nGenerates a standalone, styled HTML execution dashboard and writes it directly to disk:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"PIPELINE_NAME\" type=\"string\" value=\"Nightly Customer Ledger Sync\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"RECORDS_PROCESSED\" type=\"int\" value=\"142580\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"EXECUTION_STATUS\" type=\"string\" value=\"SUCCESS\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"DURATION\" type=\"string\" value=\"1m 42s\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate_html id=\"render_dashboard\" file=\"./reports/latest_execution.html\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            \u003c!DOCTYPE html\u003e\n            \u003chtml lang=\"en\"\u003e\n            \u003chead\u003e\n                \u003cmeta charset=\"UTF-8\"\u003e\n                \u003ctitle\u003eETL Execution Report - {{.PIPELINE_NAME}}\u003c/title\u003e\n                \u003cstyle\u003e\n                    body { font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif; background: #0f172a; color: #f8fafc; padding: 2rem; }\n                    .card { background: #1e293b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px; max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid #334155; }\n                    .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 9999px; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; }\n                    .badge-success { background: #065f46; color: #34d399; }\n                    .stat { margin: 12px 0; font-size: 14px; color: #94a3b8; }\n                    .stat strong { color: #f8fafc; }\n                \u003c/style\u003e\n            \u003c/head\u003e\n            \u003cbody\u003e\n                \u003cdiv class=\"card\"\u003e\n                    \u003ch2\u003e{{.PIPELINE_NAME}}\u003c/h2\u003e\n                    \u003cdiv class=\"stat\"\u003eStatus: \u003cspan class=\"badge badge-success\"\u003e{{.EXECUTION_STATUS}}\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n                    \u003cdiv class=\"stat\"\u003eRecords Processed: \u003cstrong\u003e{{.RECORDS_PROCESSED}}\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n                    \u003cdiv class=\"stat\"\u003eDuration: \u003cstrong\u003e{{.DURATION}}\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n                    \u003cdiv class=\"stat\"\u003eGenerated: \u003cstrong\u003e2026-10-11T18:00:00Z\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n                \u003c/div\u003e\n            \u003c/body\u003e\n            \u003c/html\u003e\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/template_html\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Tabular Report Iterating Over Extracted Database Records\n\nCombines `\u003csql\u003e` extraction with `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` collection iteration (`{{range ...}}`):\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"sales_db\" driver=\"sqlite\" connection_string=\"./sales.db\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Extract top sales representatives into JSON array variable --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"fetch_top_sales\" db=\"sales_db\" output_var=\"TOP_REPS\"\u003e\n            SELECT rep_name, region, quarterly_sales\n            FROM representatives\n            WHERE quarterly_sales \u003e= 100000\n            ORDER BY quarterly_sales DESC;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Render safe HTML email fragment --\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate_html id=\"format_email_table\" into=\"EMAIL_TABLE_HTML\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n            \u003ctable border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\"\u003e\n                \u003ctr style=\"background-color: #2563eb; color: white;\"\u003e\n                    \u003cth\u003eRepresentative\u003c/th\u003e\n                    \u003cth\u003eRegion\u003c/th\u003e\n                    \u003cth\u003eQuarterly Sales\u003c/th\u003e\n                \u003c/tr\u003e\n                {{range .TOP_REPS}}\n                \u003ctr\u003e\n                    \u003ctd\u003e{{.rep_name}}\u003c/td\u003e\n                    \u003ctd\u003e{{.region}}\u003c/td\u003e\n                    \u003ctd align=\"right\"\u003e${{.quarterly_sales}}\u003c/td\u003e\n                \u003c/tr\u003e\n                {{else}}\n                \u003ctr\u003e\n                    \u003ctd colspan=\"3\" align=\"center\"\u003eNo qualifying sales records found.\u003c/td\u003e\n                \u003c/tr\u003e\n                {{end}}\n            \u003c/table\u003e\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/template_html\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Contextual XSS Protection Demonstration\n\nDemonstrating that malicious inputs like `\u003cscript\u003ealert(\u0027xss\u0027)\u003c/script\u003e` are safely escaped into harmless entities without manual developer sanitization:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Untrusted user input containing malicious markup --\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"USER_INPUT\" type=\"string\" value=\"\u0026lt;script\u0026gt;alert(\u0027pwnd\u0027)\u0026lt;/script\u0026gt;\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"PROFILE_URL\" type=\"string\" value=\"javascript:stealTokens()\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003ctemplate_html id=\"safe_rendering\" into=\"SAFE_HTML\"\u003e\n            \u003cdiv\u003e\n                \u003c!-- Automatically escaped to \u0026lt;script\u0026gt;alert(\u0027pwnd\u0027)\u0026lt;/script\u0026gt; --\u003e\n                \u003cp\u003eWelcome, {{.USER_INPUT}}!\u003c/p\u003e\n                \n                \u003c!-- Automatically neutralized because scheme is javascript: --\u003e\n                \u003ca href=\"{{.PROFILE_URL}}\"\u003eView Profile\u003c/a\u003e\n            \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003c/template_html\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Summary Best Practices\n\n1. **Always Use `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` for Web / Email Output**: Never use plaintext `\u003ctemplate\u003e` for content rendered in web browsers or email clients.\n2. **Enclose Markup in CDATA**: Wrap HTML template blocks containing `\u003c`, `\u003e`, or `\u0026` within `\u003c![CDATA[ ... ]]\u003e` to prevent XML parser syntax errors.\n3. **Use `into` for Variables, `file` for Disk Output**: Specify `into=\"VAR_NAME\"` if passing HTML to `\u003chttp_client\u003e` (e.g. SendGrid or SES API), or `file=\"path/to/file.html\"` to save directly to disk.\n4. **Full Syntax \u0026 Security Reference**: See the official Go docs at [pkg.go.dev/html/template](https://pkg.go.dev/html/template) for the complete escaping model, supported contexts, and security guarantees.\n","variable.md":"# Pipeline Variables (`\u003cvariable\u003e`) \u0026 Registry Guide\n\nThe `\u003cvariable\u003e` element initializes a named pipeline variable within the top-level `\u003cvariables\u003e` container. Flow provides a high-performance, thread-safe variable registry (`*flow.Registry`) that enables dynamic parameterization of SQL queries, file paths, connection strings, HTTP endpoints, loop drivers, and cross-runtime script execution.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Syntax \u0026 Attribute Reference\n\nPipeline variables are declared inside the `\u003cvariables\u003e` collection at the beginning of the pipeline:\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables description=\"Global pipeline configuration and state variables\"\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"var-batch-id\" name=\"BatchID\" type=\"int\" value=\"1001\" description=\"Current batch identifier\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"var-target-table\" name=\"TargetTable\" type=\"string\" value=\"daily_sales\" description=\"Destination table\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"var-is-prod\" name=\"IsProduction\" type=\"bool\" value=\"true\" description=\"Environment switch\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003c!-- flow nodes --\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### `\u003cvariables\u003e` Container Attributes\n\n| Attribute | Mandatory | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `description` | No | `\"\"` | Optional human-readable description of the variables collection. |\n\n### `\u003cvariable\u003e` Element Attributes\n\n| Attribute | Mandatory | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | **Yes** | — | Unique variable name. Used for interpolation (e.g., `{{VariableName}}`), registry lookups, and OS environment variable exports. |\n| `value` | **Yes** | — | Initial scalar string value assigned to the variable. Evaluated and converted according to `type`. |\n| `type` | No | `string` | Data type for parsing and internal representation. Supported types: `string`, `int`, `integer`, `bool`, `boolean`, `float`, `double`, `float64`, `datetime`, `date`. |\n| `id` | No | `\"\"` | Optional unique identifier for the variable definition element. |\n| `description` | No | `\"\"` | Optional human-readable description of the variable\u0027s purpose, origin, or usage. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Supported Data Types \u0026 Value Parsing\n\nWhen initializing variables via XML, Flow parses string values into typed native representations:\n\n| Type Name | Aliases | Description | Parsing Rules \u0026 Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `string` | — *(Default)* | Scalar text / string | Preserves literal text as entered: `value=\"production\"`, `value=\"C:\\Data\\Reports\"`. |\n| `int` | `integer` | 64-bit signed integer (`int64`) | Parses base-10 integer numbers: `value=\"5000\"`, `value=\"-42\"`. |\n| `bool` | `boolean` | Boolean flag | Case-insensitive boolean parsing: `true`, `false`, `1`, `0`, `t`, `f`, `TRUE`, `FALSE`. |\n| `float` | `double`, `float64` | 64-bit floating point (`float64`) | Parses IEEE 754 floating point numbers: `value=\"99.95\"`, `value=\"-0.0042\"`. |\n| `datetime` | — | ISO 8601 / RFC 3339 Timestamp | Parses standard timestamp formats (e.g. `2026-01-15T08:30:00Z`, `2026-01-15 08:30:00`). |\n| `date` | — | Calendar Date | Parses calendar dates formatted as `YYYY-MM-DD` (e.g. `2026-01-15`). |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Variable Scopes \u0026 Concurrency Model\n\nVariables are managed by a centralized, thread-safe registry (`*flow.Registry`). Their scoping behavior adapts automatically based on the execution context:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Root[\"Root Pipeline Registry\u003cbr/\u003e\u003ccode\u003e[BatchID: 1001] [TargetTable: \u0027sales\u0027] [Env: \u0027prod\u0027]\u003c/code\u003e\"]\n\n    Root --\u003e|Sequential Blocks: \u0026lt;flow\u0026gt;, \u0026lt;group\u0026gt;| Shared[\"Shared Registry Mutex\u003cbr/\u003e• Immediate read/write visibility\u003cbr/\u003e• Step B immediately sees Step A\u0027s updates\"]\n\n    Root --\u003e|Parallel Blocks: \u0026lt;parallel\u0026gt;| Workers[\"Thread Snapshots / Clones\u003cbr/\u003e• Worker 0: Local copy + \u003ccode\u003e_THREAD_ID=0\u003c/code\u003e\u003cbr/\u003e• Worker 1: Local copy + \u003ccode\u003e_THREAD_ID=1\u003c/code\u003e\"]\n\n    Workers --\u003e|Worker Completion| Merge[\"Dirty Merge \u0026 Conflict Resolution\u003cbr/\u003e• Clean variables merged back to parent\u003cbr/\u003e• Colliding edits namespaced to \u003ccode\u003eWORKER_\u0026lt;id\u0026gt;_\u0026lt;key\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e\"]\n```\n\n### Sequential Execution (Default)\nIn root `\u003cflow\u003e`, `\u003cpreflight\u003e`, and `\u003cgroup\u003e` blocks:\n- All steps share a single synchronized registry.\n- Variable reads and mutations (`output_var`, `into`) are immediately visible to all subsequent steps.\n\n### Parallel Execution (`\u003cparallel\u003e`)\nWhen running concurrent branches inside `\u003cparallel\u003e` blocks:\n- Flow **snapshots (deep-clones)** the variable registry for each concurrent worker thread.\n- Automatically injects an integer `_THREAD_ID` variable (`0`, `1`, `2`, ...) into each worker\u0027s local scope.\n- Workers mutate their private copy without blocking or corrupting other threads.\n- Upon worker completion, only **mutated (\"dirty\") variables** are merged back into the parent registry.\n- If two or more parallel workers mutate the same key with differing values, Flow applies automatic **conflict resolution**, namespacing colliding entries to `WORKER_\u003cid\u003e_\u003ckey\u003e` to prevent race conditions and stale overwrites.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Variable Interpolation Syntax\n\nFlow supports mustache-style double curly brace interpolation: `{{VariableName}}`. Placeholders are resolved case-insensitively at runtime across pipeline components:\n\n### 1. SQL Statements \u0026 Queries (`\u003csql\u003e`, `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`)\n```xml\n\u003csql id=\"QueryWithLimit\" db=\"reporting_db\"\u003e\n    SELECT order_id, customer_name, total_amount\n    FROM {{TargetTable}}\n    WHERE order_date \u003e= \u0027{{StartDate}}\u0027\n    LIMIT {{BatchLimit}};\n\u003c/sql\u003e\n```\n\n### 2. Database Connection Strings (`\u003cdatabase\u003e`)\n```xml\n\u003cdatabase name=\"dynamic_db\" driver=\"postgres\"\n          connection_string=\"host={{DB_HOST}} port={{DB_PORT}} user={{DB_USER}} password={{DB_PASS}} dbname={{DB_NAME}}\" /\u003e\n```\n\n### 3. File \u0026 Directory Paths (`\u003cfile_read\u003e`, `\u003cfile_save\u003e`, `\u003cexcel_read\u003e`, `\u003cexcel_write\u003e`)\n```xml\n\u003cfile_read id=\"LoadBatchFile\" path=\"{{StorageDir}}/incoming/{{BatchID}}_{{BatchDate}}.csv\" into=\"RawCsvData\" /\u003e\n```\n\n### 4. HTTP Client URLs \u0026 Headers (`\u003chttp_client\u003e`)\n```xml\n\u003chttp_client id=\"SyncWebhook\" method=\"POST\"\n             url=\"https://api.internal.net/v1/orders/{{OrderID}}\"\n             headers=\"Authorization: Bearer {{API_TOKEN}}; Content-Type: application/json\"\n             body=\"{{PayloadJson}}\" /\u003e\n```\n\n### 5. Conditional Expressions (`\u003cif\u003e`, `\u003cgroup\u003e`, `\u003cwhile\u003e`, `\u003cassert\u003e`)\n```xml\n\u003cassert id=\"VerifyTotal\" var=\"RecordCount\" equals=\"{{ExpectedCount}}\" message=\"Row count mismatch!\" /\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Setting \u0026 Mutating Variables at Runtime\n\nVariables can be dynamically updated by any pipeline node supporting output capture:\n\n| Pipeline Node | Output Attribute | Capture Behavior |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `\u003csql\u003e` | `output_var` / `into` | Captures single scalar result (single row/col) or tab-delimited text. |\n| `\u003cscript\u003e` | `output_var` | Captures trimmed standard output (`stdout`) produced by script execution. |\n| `\u003chttp_client\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Captures HTTP response body. `status_into` captures status code. |\n| `\u003ctemplate\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Captures rendered Go `text/template` output. |\n| `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Captures rendered Go `html/template` (context-aware escaped) output. |\n| `\u003cfile_read\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Reads entire file content into string variable. |\n| `\u003cexcel_read\u003e` | `output_var` / `into` | Captures parsed Excel table rows formatted as delimited or JSON data. |\n| `\u003cjson_path\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Extracts specific value, object, or array matching JSONPath expression. |\n| `\u003cyaml_path\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Evaluates path query against YAML data and stores extracted value. |\n| `\u003cxml_xpath\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Evaluates XPath expression on XML document and stores result. |\n| `\u003ckv\u003e` | `into` / `output_var` | Retrieves stored value for given key from Key-Value store. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Automatic Loop Injections\n\nLooping constructs automatically bind contextual iteration variables:\n\n### `\u003cforeach\u003e`\nIterates over rows returned by a SQL driver query:\n- `LOOP_INDEX`: 0-indexed loop iteration counter (`0`, `1`, `2`, ...).\n- **Column Bindings**: Every column returned by the driver query is automatically injected into the iteration registry under three casing formats:\n  1. **Exact Case**: as defined in SQL `SELECT` (e.g. `CustomerId`).\n  2. **Lowercase**: `customerid`.\n  3. **Uppercase**: `CUSTOMERID`.\n\n```xml\n\u003cforeach id=\"ProcessAccounts\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\n    SELECT account_id, org_name, status FROM accounts WHERE active = 1;\n\n    \u003c!-- Automatically available: {{account_id}}, {{org_name}}, {{status}}, {{LOOP_INDEX}} --\u003e\n    \u003csql id=\"LogAccount\" db=\"audit_db\"\u003e\n        INSERT INTO audit_log (account_id, message, iteration)\n        VALUES ({{account_id}}, \u0027Processed {{org_name}}\u0027, {{LOOP_INDEX}});\n    \u003c/sql\u003e\n\u003c/foreach\u003e\n```\n\n### `\u003cwhile\u003e`\nIterates while condition remains true:\n- `WHILE_INDEX`: 0-indexed iteration counter (`0`, `1`, `2`, ...).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Consuming Variables in Script Nodes\n\nFlow passes all registry variables into script runtimes:\n\n### 1. Embedded Go Interpreter (`yaegi`)\nImport `\"host/vars\"` to access strongly-typed getter functions:\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"host/vars\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    batchId := vars.GetInt(\"BatchID\")\n    table   := vars.GetString(\"TargetTable\")\n    isProd  := vars.GetBool(\"IsProduction\")\n    fmt.Printf(\"Batch %d targeting %s (Prod=%t)\\n\", batchId, table, isProd)\n}\n```\n\nAvailable `\"host/vars\"` Methods:\n- `vars.Get(name string) interface{}`\n- `vars.GetString(name string) string`\n- `vars.GetInt(name string) int`\n- `vars.GetBool(name string) bool`\n- `vars.GetFloat(name string) float64`\n\n### 2. C# (`dotnet-script` / `csx`)\nVariables are exposed as OS environment variables:\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\nstring targetTable = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"TargetTable\");\nstring rawBatchId  = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"BatchID\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Running for {targetTable} (Batch {rawBatchId})\");\n```\n\n### 3. PowerShell (`pwsh` / `powershell`)\nVariables are accessible via the `$env:` drive:\n```powershell\nWrite-Host \"Processing table: $env:TargetTable\"\nWrite-Host \"Batch ID: $env:BatchID\"\n```\n\n### 4. Linux Bash \u0026 POSIX Shell (`bash` / `shell`)\nVariables are exported into the child process environment:\n```bash\necho \"Processing table: ${TargetTable}\"\necho \"Current environment: ${IsProduction}\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Variable Precedence \u0026 Overrides\n\nWhen a variable name is defined in multiple places, Flow evaluates them in strict order of precedence (highest wins):\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart BT\n    subgraph Precedence [\"Variable Precedence Hierarchy (Highest to Lowest)\"]\n        Base[\"4. Base Pipeline Declarations (\u0026lt;variables\u0026gt;)\u003cbr/\u003e\u003ci\u003eLowest Precedence\u003c/i\u003e\"]\n        Config[\"3. External Config Overlays (-config override.xml)\"]\n        CLI[\"2. Command-Line Overrides (-vars \u0027Key=Val\u0027)\"]\n        Runtime[\"1. Dynamic Step Mutations (output_var, into, foreach)\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cb\u003eHighest Precedence\u003c/b\u003e\"]\n\n        Base --\u003e Config\n        Config --\u003e CLI\n        CLI --\u003e Runtime\n    end\n```\n\n1. **Base Pipeline XML**: Initial values declared in `\u003cvariables\u003e`.\n2. **External Config File (`-config override.xml`)**: Overrides matching variable keys before execution starts.\n3. **CLI Arguments (`-vars \"Key=Value,Count=50\"`)**: Overrides matching variable keys at launch.\n4. **Runtime Node Mutations (`output_var`, `into`)**: Overwrites registry entries during pipeline execution.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Comprehensive Examples\n\n### Example 1: Multi-Type Variable Initialization \u0026 Validation\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables description=\"ETL batch parameters and thresholds\"\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"v1\" name=\"BatchID\" type=\"int\" value=\"42\" description=\"Current execution batch ID\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"v2\" name=\"BatchDate\" type=\"date\" value=\"2026-10-11\" description=\"Processing calendar date\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"v3\" name=\"ThresholdRate\" type=\"float\" value=\"0.95\" description=\"Quality score threshold\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"v4\" name=\"DryRun\" type=\"bool\" value=\"false\" description=\"Toggle commit vs rollback\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable id=\"v5\" name=\"ReportTitle\" type=\"string\" value=\"Monthly Aggregates\" description=\"Report heading\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"CheckBatchID\" var=\"BatchID\" equals=\"42\" message=\"BatchID was not initialized properly\" /\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"CheckDryRun\" var=\"DryRun\" equals=\"false\" message=\"DryRun flag must be false\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### Example 2: Dynamic SQL Scalar Capture \u0026 Conditional Branching\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"PendingCount\" type=\"int\" value=\"0\" description=\"Number of pending records\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Capture scalar count into PendingCount --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"CountPending\" db=\"warehouse_db\" output_var=\"PendingCount\"\u003e\n            SELECT COUNT(*) FROM staging_events WHERE status = \u0027PENDING\u0027;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Execute group only when pending rows exist --\u003e\n        \u003cgroup id=\"ProcessBatchGroup\" if_var=\"PendingCount\" if_val=\"0\" equals=\"false\"\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"ProcessPending\" db=\"warehouse_db\"\u003e\n                UPDATE staging_events \n                SET status = \u0027PROCESSED\u0027, processed_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n                WHERE status = \u0027PENDING\u0027;\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n        \u003c/group\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### Example 3: Cross-Language Variable Passing (Go to PowerShell to SQL)\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"SourceDir\" type=\"string\" value=\"./data/incoming\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"FileCount\" type=\"int\" value=\"0\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Step 1: PowerShell counts files and writes to stdout --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"CountIncomingFiles\" language=\"powershell\" output_var=\"FileCount\"\u003e\n            $files = Get-ChildItem -Path $env:SourceDir -Filter *.csv\n            Write-Host -NoNewline $files.Count\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 2: Go script evaluates threshold --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"EvaluateThreshold\" language=\"go\" output_var=\"EvaluationStatus\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                count := vars.GetInt(\"FileCount\")\n                if count \u003e 0 {\n                    fmt.Print(\"READY\")\n                } else {\n                    fmt.Print(\"EMPTY\")\n                }\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 3: SQL records the audit log using interpolated variables --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"RecordAudit\" db=\"audit_db\"\u003e\n            INSERT INTO etl_audit (source_directory, files_found, status)\n            VALUES (\u0027{{SourceDir}}\u0027, {{FileCount}}, \u0027{{EvaluationStatus}}\u0027);\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### Example 4: Thread-Isolated Variables in `\u003cparallel\u003e` Workers\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"BaseOutputDir\" type=\"string\" value=\"./output\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cparallel id=\"ConcurrentShardProcess\"\u003e\n            \u003c!-- Worker 0: _THREAD_ID is 0 --\u003e\n            \u003cscript id=\"ProcessShard0\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                package main\n                import (\n                    \"fmt\"\n                    \"host/vars\"\n                )\n                func main() {\n                    thread := vars.GetInt(\"_THREAD_ID\")\n                    dir := vars.GetString(\"BaseOutputDir\")\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Worker %d writing to %s/shard_%d\\n\", thread, dir, thread)\n                }\n            \u003c/script\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Worker 1: _THREAD_ID is 1 --\u003e\n            \u003cscript id=\"ProcessShard1\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                package main\n                import (\n                    \"fmt\"\n                    \"host/vars\"\n                )\n                func main() {\n                    thread := vars.GetInt(\"_THREAD_ID\")\n                    dir := vars.GetString(\"BaseOutputDir\")\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Worker %d writing to %s/shard_%d\\n\", thread, dir, thread)\n                }\n            \u003c/script\u003e\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n","variables.md":"# Flow Variable Management \u0026 Usage Guide\n\nFlow supports dynamic environment variables stored in a thread-safe registry (`*flow.Registry`). These variables enable you to parameterize your SQL queries, loop drivers, streaming configurations, and dynamic Go scripts.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Variable Hierarchy \u0026 Scope\n\nVariables are kept in a single unified registry, but their behavior and scope change depending on the pipeline execution tree block:\n\n| Scope | Context | Behavior |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Global / Registry** | Sequential execution | Read and write actions are immediate and shared. Scripts executing in a sequence (inside a standard group or root block) can read variables written by preceding scripts. |\n| **Thread-Isolated** | `\u003cparallel\u003e` blocks | When running concurrent branches, Flow **snapshots (clones)** the registry\u0027s variables for each worker thread, injecting a unique thread-specific `_THREAD_ID` variable (starting from `0`). Only variables mutated by a worker (`dirty` variables) are merged back to the parent. If multiple parallel workers mutate the same key, a conflict-resolution routine namespaces them into `WORKER_\u003cid\u003e_\u003ckey\u003e` to prevent race conditions or overwriting concurrent edits with stale values. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Using Variables\n\n### SQL Queries (Variable Interpolation)\nFor SQL nodes, variables are dynamically interpolated before execution using double curly brace placeholders: `{{VarName}}`.\n\n```xml\n\u003csql id=\"QueryWithLimit\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\n    SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = \u0027PENDING\u0027 LIMIT {{MaxLimit}};\n\u003c/sql\u003e\n```\n\n### Go Scripts (Yaegi `vars` Exports)\nInside dynamic Go scripts, variables are retrieved using the built-in host package `\"host/vars\"`. The package exposes typed getters:\n\n*   `vars.Get(name string) interface{}`\n*   `vars.GetString(name string) string`\n*   `vars.GetInt(name string) int`\n*   `vars.GetBool(name string) bool`\n*   `vars.GetFloat(name string) float64`\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"host/vars\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    threshold := vars.GetInt(\"Threshold\")\n    table := vars.GetString(\"TargetTable\")\n    fmt.Printf(\"Processing %s with threshold: %d\\n\", table, threshold)\n}\n```\n\n### C# Scripts (`dotnet-script` / `csx` Environment Variables)\nInside dynamic C# scripts, all registry variables are loaded directly into the OS process environment. You can retrieve them using:\n\n*   `Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"VariableName\")`\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nstring targetTable = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"TargetTable\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Writing to {targetTable}\");\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Setting Variables in Scripts\n\n### SQL Queries (`output_var`)\nTo capture a value returned from a SQL query, use the `output_var` attribute.\n*   If the SQL query returns a single row with a single column, `output_var` stores that value.\n*   Otherwise, it captures the entire tab-separated results block.\n\n```xml\n\u003csql id=\"GetMaxID\" db=\"app_db\" output_var=\"LastProcessedID\"\u003e\n    SELECT COALESCE(MAX(id), 0) FROM logs;\n\u003c/sql\u003e\n```\n\n### Go Scripts (`output_var` / Stdout Capture)\nBecause the Yaegi host bindings do not expose a variable mutation method, you write values to variables by printing them to **stdout**. The `output_var` attribute will capture the printed console output and store it as a string variable.\n\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"CalculateStats\" language=\"go\" output_var=\"ResultScore\"\u003e\n    package main\n    import \"fmt\"\n    func main() {\n        score := 98.4\n        // Captured directly by \"ResultScore\"\n        fmt.Printf(\"%.1f\", score)\n    }\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n### C# Scripts (`output_var` / Stdout Capture)\nSimilar to Go and shell scripts, `dotnet-script` outputs to **stdout** are captured by the `output_var` attribute and stored back into the pipeline variable registry.\n\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"CsharpCalc\" language=\"dotnet-script\" output_var=\"ResultSum\"\u003e\n    using System;\n    int a = 10;\n    int b = 20;\n    Console.Write(a + b); // Captured directly by \"ResultSum\"\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Loops (`\u003cforeach\u003e`)\n\nWhen iterating over records using a `\u003cforeach\u003e` block, the loop driver query binds the following variables automatically for each iteration:\n\n*   `LOOP_INDEX`: The current 0-indexed loop iteration index (integer).\n*   **Columns**: The values of the current row are bound to variables matching the column names. To prevent casing issues, the engine binds them in three casings:\n    1.  **Exact Case** (e.g. `UserId`)\n    2.  **Lowercase** (e.g. `userid`)\n    3.  **Uppercase** (e.g. `USERID`)\n\n```xml\n\u003cforeach id=\"IterateUsers\" db=\"app_db\" var=\"UserId\"\u003e\n    SELECT id, username, email FROM users WHERE active = 1;\n    \n    \u003c!-- Each iteration binds \"id\", \"username\", \"email\", and \"LOOP_INDEX\" --\u003e\n    \u003csql id=\"ProcessUser\" db=\"app_db\"\u003e\n        INSERT INTO user_audit (user_id, action) \n        VALUES ({{id}}, \u0027Processed iteration {{LOOP_INDEX}}\u0027);\n    \u003c/sql\u003e\n\u003c/foreach\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Advanced Examples\n\n### Example A: Exporting Multiple Parameters (Comma-Separated Output)\nWhen you need to output multiple distinct values from a script to be consumed as separate parameters in a subsequent step, you can format the output as a delimited string and parse it inside the next Go script.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Step 1: Export a delimited config from Go --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"GenerateParams\" language=\"go\" output_var=\"MultiParams\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import \"fmt\"\n            func main() {\n                env := \"production\"\n                retries := 5\n                timeout := 30\n                fmt.Printf(\"%s,%d,%d\", env, retries, timeout)\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 2: Consume and split parameters inside another Go script --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"UseParams\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"strings\"\n                \"strconv\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                raw := vars.GetString(\"MultiParams\")\n                parts := strings.Split(raw, \",\")\n                if len(parts) == 3 {\n                    env := parts[0]\n                    retries, _ := strconv.Atoi(parts[1])\n                    timeout, _ := strconv.Atoi(parts[2])\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Env: %s, Retries: %d, Timeout: %d\\n\", env, retries, timeout)\n                }\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### Example B: Passing JSON Output Between Scripts\nFor complex, structured data, you can output a JSON string, capture it, and parse it back into typed structs in subsequent dynamic Go scripts.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Step 1: Query database config details, formatting output as JSON --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"FetchServiceConfig\" language=\"go\" output_var=\"ServiceJSON\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"encoding/json\"\n                \"fmt\"\n            )\n            type ConnectionDetails struct {\n                Host string `json:\"host\"`\n                Port int    `json:\"port\"`\n                SSL  bool   `json:\"ssl\"`\n            }\n            func main() {\n                cfg := ConnectionDetails{\n                    Host: \"db-replica.internal\",\n                    Port: 5432,\n                    SSL:  true,\n                }\n                bytes, _ := json.Marshal(cfg)\n                fmt.Println(string(bytes))\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 2: Unmarshal and utilize the JSON payload in a subsequent step --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"ConnectToService\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"encoding/json\"\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            type ConnectionDetails struct {\n                Host string `json:\"host\"`\n                Port int    `json:\"port\"`\n                SSL  bool   `json:\"ssl\"`\n            }\n            func main() {\n                jsonStr := vars.GetString(\"ServiceJSON\")\n                var cfg ConnectionDetails\n                if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(jsonStr), \u0026cfg); err != nil {\n                    fmt.Printf(\"Failed to unmarshal config: %v\\n\", err)\n                    return\n                }\n                fmt.Printf(\"Successfully established connection to %s:%d (SSL: %t)\\n\", cfg.Host, cfg.Port, cfg.SSL)\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n### Example C: Inter-operating Go and C# Scripts\nYou can easily pass state between dynamic Go interpreter scripts and C# process-executed scripts using variables.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"Threshold\" type=\"int\" value=\"42\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Step 1: Read Threshold variable in C#, run calculations, and store output --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"CsharpStep\" language=\"dotnet-script\" output_var=\"CS_Result\"\u003e\n            using System;\n            string rawThreshold = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"Threshold\");\n            if (int.TryParse(rawThreshold, out int threshold)) {\n                Console.Write($\"Threshold was {threshold}, calculated result is {threshold * 2}\");\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Step 2: Use the C# result inside a Go script --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"GoStep\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            package main\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n            func main() {\n                csVal := vars.GetString(\"CS_Result\")\n                fmt.Printf(\"Go received from C#: %s\\n\", csVal)\n            }\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n","while.md":"# Conditional Iteration: `\u003cwhile\u003e`\n\nThe `\u003cwhile\u003e` component repeats execution of its inner child steps as long as an evaluation condition or variable comparison remains true. It is designed for polling asynchronous database jobs, paginated REST API extractions, wait-and-retry workflows, and multi-pass batching loops.\n\n`\u003cwhile\u003e` includes a built-in safety ceiling (`max_iterations`, default 1000) to protect against runaway infinite loops and automatically injects a zero-based iteration tracker `{{WHILE_INDEX}}`.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture \u0026 Lifecycle\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Start([Enter \u003cwhile\u003e Loop]) --\u003e InitIndex[\"Initialize WHILE_INDEX = 0\"]\n    InitIndex --\u003e CheckLimit{\"WHILE_INDEX \u003e= max_iterations?\u003cbr/\u003e(default: 1000)\"}\n\n    CheckLimit -- \"Yes (Safety Guard)\" --\u003e Tripwire[/\"Error / Terminate Loop\u003cbr/\u003e(Loop limit exceeded)\"/]\n    CheckLimit -- \"No\" --\u003e EvalCond{\"Evaluate Loop Condition\u003cbr/\u003econdition or var == equals\"}\n\n    EvalCond -- \"False / Not Equal\" --\u003e Exit([Loop Terminates Successfully])\n    EvalCond -- \"True / Equal\" --\u003e ExecChildren[\"Execute Child Steps Sequentially\"]\n\n    ExecChildren --\u003e Incr[\"Increment WHILE_INDEX = WHILE_INDEX + 1\"]\n    Incr --\u003e CheckLimit\n```\n\n---\n\n## Attribute Reference\n\n| Attribute | Type | Default | Mandatory | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `id` | `string` | *(generated)* | No | Unique identifier for the while loop step. |\n| `var` | `string` | `\"\"` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable monitored for equality (e.g. `JobStatus`). |\n| `if_var` | `string` | `\"\"` | No | Alias for `var`. |\n| `equals` | `string` | `\"\"` | Conditional | Loop continues while `var` equals this string or value (e.g. `RUNNING` or `true`). |\n| `val` / `value` | `string` | `\"\"` | No | Aliases for `equals`. |\n| `if_val` / `if_equals` | `string` | `\"\"` | No | Aliases for `equals`. |\n| `condition` | `string` | `\"\"` | Conditional | Expression evaluated before each iteration (e.g. `ne .Status \"DONE\"` or `lt .PAGE_COUNT 10`). |\n| `cond` | `string` | `\"\"` | No | Alias for `condition`. |\n| `max_iterations` | `positiveInteger` | `1000` | No | Infinite loop guard ceiling. Pipeline halts if iterations reach this threshold. |\n| `max_loops` | `positiveInteger` | `1000` | No | Alias for `max_iterations`. |\n| `timeout` | `string` | *(none)* | No | Optional timeout duration for the total while block (e.g. `5m`, `30s`). |\n| `on_error` | `string` | `stop` | No | Error handling policy for inner child failures: `stop`, `continue`, or `retry`. |\n| `retry_count` | `integer` | `0` | No | Number of retries permitted per child failure before applying `on_error`. |\n| `description` | `string` | `\"\"` | No | Human-readable explanation of the loop\u0027s purpose. |\n\n---\n\n## Key Capabilities \u0026 Runtime Guarantees\n\n### 1. Loop Control Modes\n`\u003cwhile\u003e` supports two distinct condition styles:\n\n- **Variable Equality Mode (`var` + `equals`)**:\n  Monitors a specific variable and repeats as long as its value matches `equals`.\n  ```xml\n  \u003cwhile id=\"poll_sync\" var=\"SYNC_STATUS\" equals=\"IN_PROGRESS\" max_iterations=\"30\"\u003e\n      \u003c!-- Child steps query status and update SYNC_STATUS --\u003e\n  \u003c/while\u003e\n  ```\n\n- **Expression Condition Mode (`condition`)**:\n  Evaluates boolean or comparison expressions dynamically before each pass.\n  ```xml\n  \u003cwhile id=\"page_iterator\" condition=\"and (eq .HAS_MORE \\\"true\\\") (lt .CURRENT_PAGE 50)\" max_iterations=\"50\"\u003e\n      \u003c!-- Child steps fetch next page and update HAS_MORE --\u003e\n  \u003c/while\u003e\n  ```\n\n### 2. Built-in `WHILE_INDEX` Variable\nOn every pass through the loop, the engine injects a zero-based iteration index named `WHILE_INDEX`:\n- **Iteration 1**: `{{WHILE_INDEX}}` = `0`\n- **Iteration 2**: `{{WHILE_INDEX}}` = `1`\n- **Iteration 3**: `{{WHILE_INDEX}}` = `2`\n\nChild steps, Go scripts (`vars.GetInt(\"WHILE_INDEX\")`), SQL queries, and log statements can access `{{WHILE_INDEX}}` directly.\n\n### 3. Infinite Loop Protection (`max_iterations`)\nTo prevent unbounded resource consumption or runaway API costs, `\u003cwhile\u003e` defaults to a maximum ceiling of **1,000 iterations**. If the loop condition remains true after reaching `max_iterations`, execution halts immediately with an explanatory error.\n\n### 4. Pacing \u0026 Delay\nMost polling workflows require pauses between iterations. You can introduce delays using an embedded `\u003cscript language=\"go\"\u003e` with `time.Sleep(...)`:\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"poll_delay\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n    package main\n    import \"time\"\n    func main() {\n        time.Sleep(3 * time.Second)\n    }\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## Production Pipeline Examples\n\n### Example 1: Asynchronous Database Job Polling (MSSQL)\nPoll the status of a long-running SQL Server Agent or stored procedure task until completion.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Poll MSSQL background job status\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"JobStatus\" type=\"string\" value=\"RUNNING\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"TargetJobId\" type=\"string\" value=\"JOB_2026_0981\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"primary_db\" \n                  connection_string=\"sqlserver://SRV01:1433?database=AppDB\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Loop while JobStatus is RUNNING, max 30 attempts (approx 60s total) --\u003e\n        \u003cwhile id=\"poll_mssql_job\" var=\"JobStatus\" equals=\"RUNNING\" max_iterations=\"30\"\n               description=\"Wait for background batch procedure to complete\"\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Step 1: Wait 2 seconds between polling checks --\u003e\n            \u003cscript id=\"sleep_step\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                package main\n                import \"time\"\n                func main() {\n                    time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)\n                }\n            \u003c/script\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Step 2: Query database and update JobStatus output variable --\u003e\n            \u003csql id=\"fetch_status\" db=\"primary_db\" output_var=\"JobStatus\"\u003e\n                SELECT TOP 1 [status]\n                FROM dbo.async_tasks WITH (NOLOCK)\n                WHERE task_id = \u0027{{TargetJobId}}\u0027;\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Step 3: Log progress using WHILE_INDEX --\u003e\n            \u003cscript id=\"log_attempt\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                package main\n                import (\n                    \"fmt\"\n                    \"host/vars\"\n                )\n                func main() {\n                    idx := vars.GetInt(\"WHILE_INDEX\")\n                    status := vars.GetString(\"JobStatus\")\n                    fmt.Printf(\"[Attempt #%d] Current Job Status: %s\\n\", idx+1, status)\n                }\n            \u003c/script\u003e\n        \u003c/while\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Final verification gate --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"verify_success\" var=\"JobStatus\" equals=\"SUCCESS\" message=\"Background job failed or timed out\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Paginated REST API Extraction\nRetrieve records page by page from an external REST API until `has_more` returns false or the max page limit is reached.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Paginated REST API Data Extraction\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"PAGE_NUM\" type=\"int\" value=\"1\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"HAS_MORE\" type=\"string\" value=\"true\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"API_RESPONSE\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cwhile id=\"paginate_api\" var=\"HAS_MORE\" equals=\"true\" max_iterations=\"100\"\n               description=\"Fetch user pages until has_more is false\"\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Request current page --\u003e\n            \u003chttp_client id=\"fetch_page\" \n                         url=\"https://api.example.com/v2/events?page={{PAGE_NUM}}\u0026amp;limit=100\" \n                         method=\"GET\" \n                         into=\"API_RESPONSE\" /\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Extract has_more boolean flag --\u003e\n            \u003cjson_path id=\"check_has_more\" source=\"var\" var=\"API_RESPONSE\" query=\"$.pagination.has_more\" into=\"HAS_MORE\" /\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Append page payload to raw log file --\u003e\n            \u003cfile_save id=\"append_page\" file=\"./data/events_feed.jsonl\" source=\"var\" var=\"API_RESPONSE\" append=\"true\" /\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- Increment PAGE_NUM for the next pass --\u003e\n            \u003cscript id=\"incr_page\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                package main\n                import \"host/vars\"\n                func main() {\n                    current := vars.GetInt(\"PAGE_NUM\")\n                    vars.Set(\"PAGE_NUM\", current+1)\n                }\n            \u003c/script\u003e\n        \u003c/while\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: External Service Readiness \u0026 Health Check\nWait for a remote container or database service to become healthy with exponential backoff.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Wait for remote service readiness\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"IS_READY\" type=\"string\" value=\"false\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"HTTP_STATUS\" type=\"int\" value=\"0\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003cwhile id=\"wait_for_health\" var=\"IS_READY\" equals=\"false\" max_iterations=\"20\" timeout=\"5m\"\u003e\n            \u003c!-- Ping health endpoint --\u003e\n            \u003chttp_client id=\"check_health\" url=\"http://microservice.internal/healthz\" method=\"GET\" status_into=\"HTTP_STATUS\" on_error=\"continue\" /\u003e\n\n            \u003c!-- If HTTP 200, mark IS_READY to true to exit loop --\u003e\n            \u003cif id=\"eval_health\" condition=\"eq .HTTP_STATUS 200\"\u003e\n                \u003cthen\u003e\n                    \u003cvariable id=\"set_ready\" name=\"IS_READY\" value=\"true\" /\u003e\n                \u003c/then\u003e\n                \u003celse\u003e\n                    \u003c!-- Backoff pause before re-checking --\u003e\n                    \u003cscript id=\"backoff_sleep\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                        package main\n                        import \"time\"\n                        func main() {\n                            time.Sleep(3 * time.Second)\n                        }\n                    \u003c/script\u003e\n                \u003c/else\u003e\n            \u003c/if\u003e\n        \u003c/while\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Chunked Queue Consumer \u0026 Staging Purge\nProcess a work queue in batches until no pending records remain in the queue table.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Drain work queue table in batches\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"PENDING_COUNT\" type=\"int\" value=\"1\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"queue_db\" connection_string=\"postgres://app:secret@localhost:5432/appdb?sslmode=disable\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Loop while there are pending tasks (PENDING_COUNT \u003e 0) --\u003e\n        \u003cwhile id=\"drain_queue\" condition=\"gt .PENDING_COUNT 0\" max_iterations=\"500\"\u003e\n            \u003c!-- Process 1000 items in a transaction --\u003e\n            \u003cgroup id=\"batch_worker\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"queue_db\"\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"archive_batch\" db=\"queue_db\"\u003e\n                    WITH batch AS (\n                        SELECT id FROM processing_queue \n                        WHERE status = \u0027PENDING\u0027 \n                        LIMIT 1000 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\n                    )\n                    UPDATE processing_queue q\n                    SET status = \u0027PROCESSED\u0027, processed_at = NOW()\n                    FROM batch\n                    WHERE q.id = batch.id;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n\n                \u003c!-- Check remaining count --\u003e\n                \u003csql id=\"count_remaining\" db=\"queue_db\" output_var=\"PENDING_COUNT\"\u003e\n                    SELECT COUNT(*) AS PENDING_COUNT FROM processing_queue WHERE status = \u0027PENDING\u0027;\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\n            \u003c/group\u003e\n        \u003c/while\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: Multi-Stage Pipeline State Polling with `\u003cassert\u003e`\nPoll a cloud export or machine learning job until status reaches either `COMPLETED` or `FAILED`, using assertions to catch fatal errors.\n\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Cloud export job status monitor\"\n    xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"JOB_ID\" type=\"string\" value=\"EXP_8829\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"STATUS\" type=\"string\" value=\"PENDING\" /\u003e\n        \u003cvariable name=\"RESPONSE\" type=\"string\" value=\"\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n    \u003cflow\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Keep polling while STATUS is PENDING or PROCESSING --\u003e\n        \u003cwhile id=\"monitor_cloud_job\" condition=\"or (eq .STATUS \\\"PENDING\\\") (eq .STATUS \\\"PROCESSING\\\")\" max_iterations=\"40\"\u003e\n            \u003cscript id=\"wait_5s\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n                package main\n                import \"time\"\n                func main() {\n                    time.Sleep(5 * time.Second)\n                }\n            \u003c/script\u003e\n\n            \u003chttp_client id=\"get_status\" url=\"https://cloud.example.com/api/jobs/{{JOB_ID}}\" method=\"GET\" into=\"RESPONSE\" /\u003e\n            \u003cjson_path id=\"parse_status\" source=\"var\" var=\"RESPONSE\" query=\"$.job.status\" into=\"STATUS\" /\u003e\n        \u003c/while\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Guarantee final status was COMPLETED --\u003e\n        \u003cassert id=\"assert_job_finished_ok\" var=\"STATUS\" equals=\"COMPLETED\" \n                message=\"Export job did not complete successfully\" on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## Best Practices \u0026 Troubleshooting\n\n\u003e [!TIP]\n\u003e **Always Include a Delay**: In polling loops, always add a `\u003cscript\u003e` sleep step between iterations to prevent aggressive CPU spinning or API rate-limit penalties (`HTTP 429 Too Many Requests`).\n\n\u003e [!IMPORTANT]\n\u003e **Update Monitored Variables**: Ensure that at least one child step within the `\u003cwhile\u003e` block alters the monitored variable (`output_var=\"...\"`, `\u003cvariable\u003e`, or `vars.Set(...)`). If the variable is never updated, the loop will run until `max_iterations` is hit.\n\n\u003e [!WARNING]\n\u003e **Set Realistic `max_iterations`**: Tailor `max_iterations` to your operational expectations (e.g. `max_iterations=\"30\"` with a 2-second sleep gives a 60-second window). Relying on the 1000 default for a 5-second polling loop could keep a blocked pipeline open for over an hour.\n","xml_path.md":"# Guide: XML XPath Extraction Node (`\u003cxml_xpath\u003e`)\n\nThe `\u003cxml_xpath\u003e` node provides advanced XML querying and parsing functionality within the Flow Pipeline Engine. Powered by `xmlquery`, it allows users to execute standard XPath expressions over XML files or environment variables and extract matched elements into plaintext, raw XML, or JSON formats.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Node Reference\n\n### Attributes\n- `id` (string, optional): Unique step identifier.\n- `file` (string, optional): Path to the source XML file on disk. Supports dynamic path interpolation (e.g. `{{xml_path}}`).\n- `var` (string, optional): Variable key containing the raw XML string payload to parse.\n- `xpath` (string, optional): The XPath query expression to execute.\n- `mode` (string, optional): Output serialization format:\n  - `\"text\"` (default): Returns the inner text of matched elements, joined by newlines.\n  - `\"xml\"`: Returns raw XML strings (including element tags and nested content), joined by newlines.\n  - `\"json_array\"`: Returns matched elements or text contents serialized into a single JSON array list of strings.\n- `output_var` (string, required): Variable key to store the resulting output.\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e If the `xpath` attribute is omitted, the engine automatically extracts the XPath query from the inner body text of the element.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Practical Examples\n\nThe following examples demonstrate common workflows using `\u003cxml_xpath\u003e`.\n\n### Example 1: Extracting Inner Text (Default `\"text\"` Mode)\nRetrieves the text inside targeted tags and joins the results together with newlines.\n\n**Source XML File (`inventory.xml`):**\n```xml\n\u003cinventory\u003e\n  \u003citem id=\"101\"\u003e\n    \u003cname\u003eWireless Mouse\u003c/name\u003e\n    \u003ccategory\u003ePeripherals\u003c/category\u003e\n  \u003c/item\u003e\n  \u003citem id=\"102\"\u003e\n    \u003cname\u003eMechanical Keyboard\u003c/name\u003e\n    \u003ccategory\u003ePeripherals\u003c/category\u003e\n  \u003c/item\u003e\n\u003c/inventory\u003e\n```\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\n    \u003cvariable name=\"xml_file\" type=\"string\" value=\"inventory.xml\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\n\u003cflow\u003e\n  \u003c!-- Query names in default plaintext join mode --\u003e\n  \u003cxml_xpath id=\"get_names\" file=\"{{xml_file}}\" xpath=\"//item/name\" output_var=\"item_names\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`item_names`):**\n```text\nWireless Mouse\nMechanical Keyboard\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Extracting Outer XML Elements (`mode=\"xml\"`)\nSelects whole node trees and outputs them with their XML tags preserved.\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cflow\u003e\n    \u003c!-- Select the full raw XML structures for all items --\u003e\n    \u003cxml_xpath id=\"get_raw_items\" \n               file=\"inventory.xml\" \n               xpath=\"//item\" \n               mode=\"xml\" \n               output_var=\"raw_items\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`raw_items`):**\n```xml\n\u003citem id=\"101\"\u003e\u003cname\u003eWireless Mouse\u003c/name\u003e\u003ccategory\u003ePeripherals\u003c/category\u003e\u003c/item\u003e\n\u003citem id=\"102\"\u003e\u003cname\u003eMechanical Keyboard\u003c/name\u003e\u003ccategory\u003ePeripherals\u003c/category\u003e\u003c/item\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Exporting to JSON Lists (`mode=\"json_array\"`)\nExtracts attribute values or text nodes and packs them into a marshalled JSON string array.\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cflow\u003e\n    \u003c!-- Query the \u0027id\u0027 attribute values of all items and output as JSON list --\u003e\n    \u003cxml_xpath id=\"get_ids\" \n               file=\"inventory.xml\" \n               xpath=\"//item/@id\" \n               mode=\"json_array\" \n               output_var=\"item_ids_json\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`item_ids_json`):**\n```json\n[\"101\", \"102\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: XPath Expressions with Variable Interpolation\nLeverages runtime pipeline variables inside the XPath query itself for dynamic filtering.\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\n    \u003cvariable name=\"target_id\" type=\"string\" value=\"102\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\n  \u003cflow\u003e\n    \u003c!-- Query item name dynamically using target_id variable --\u003e\n    \u003cxml_xpath id=\"get_target_name\" \n               file=\"inventory.xml\" \n               xpath=\"//item[@id=\u0027{{target_id}}\u0027]/name\" \n               output_var=\"target_name\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`target_name`):**\n```text\nMechanical Keyboard\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: Inline Query Specifications (Inner Text)\nAllows writing long, complex, or formatted XPath expressions cleanly inside the node body instead of cluttering attributes.\n\n**XML Pipeline Definition:**\n```xml\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\n  \u003cvariables\u003e\n    \u003cvariable name=\"raw_xml\" type=\"string\" value=\"\u0026lt;root\u0026gt;\u0026lt;user role=\u0027admin\u0027\u0026gt;Alice\u0026lt;/user\u0026gt;\u0026lt;user role=\u0027user\u0027\u0026gt;Bob\u0026lt;/user\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/root\u0026gt;\" /\u003e\n  \u003c/variables\u003e\n\n  \u003c!-- Executes query written directly within body text --\u003e\n  \u003cxml_xpath id=\"query_admins\" var=\"raw_xml\" output_var=\"admin_users\"\u003e\n    //user[@role=\u0027admin\u0027]\n  \u003c/xml_xpath\u003e\n  \u003c/flow\u003e\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\n```\n\n**Resulting Output (`admin_users`):**\n```text\nAlice\n```\n","yaml_path.md":"# Overview of the `\u003cyaml_path\u003e` Node in Flow\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cyaml_path\u003e` node in `flow` enables evaluating path queries against raw YAML content loaded from disk files or stored inside pipeline variables. It converts YAML structures into normalized JSON in memory and evaluates path expressions, writing the output back to downstream pipeline variables.\r\n\r\n## Key Attributes\r\n\r\n* **`file`**: (Optional) Path to a `.yaml` or `.yml` file on disk. Supports `{{VarName}}` variable interpolation.\r\n* **`var`**: (Optional) Pipeline environment variable containing raw YAML text.\r\n* **`path` / `yamlpath`**: (Optional) The path query expression to evaluate. Alternatively, the path expression can be written inside the element body.\r\n* **`mode`**: (Optional) Format of the extracted output:\r\n  * `value` (Default): Returns scalar text or line-separated value matches.\r\n  * `json`: Returns extracted sub-objects as a formatted JSON string.\r\n  * `json_array`: Serializes all matched nodes into a JSON array string.\r\n  * `yaml`: Serializes matched nodes back into clean YAML block format.\r\n* **`output_var` / `out_var`**: Target pipeline environment variable where extracted results will be saved.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Examples\r\n\r\n### 1. Extracting a Database Host from a Configuration File\r\nLoad a service configuration file and extract a scalar database connection string.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"CONFIG_FILE\" type=\"string\" value=\"./config/app.yaml\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Extract the database host string into variable DB_HOST --\u003e\r\n        \u003cyaml_path \r\n            file=\"{{CONFIG_FILE}}\" \r\n            path=\"$.database.connection.host\" \r\n            output_var=\"DB_HOST\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003cscript lang=\"bash\"\u003e\r\n            echo \"Connecting to database at: $DB_HOST\"\r\n        \u003c/script\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 2. Extracting Nested Objects as JSON (mode=\"json\")\r\nExtract a sub-map or configuration block from a YAML file directly into a JSON string to pass into an HTTP client node.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Extract the entire telemetry configuration block as JSON --\u003e\r\n        \u003cyaml_path \r\n            file=\"./config/services.yaml\" \r\n            path=\"$.telemetry.metrics\" \r\n            mode=\"json\" \r\n            output_var=\"METRICS_CONFIG_JSON\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003csql_bulk \r\n            method=\"POST\" \r\n            uri=\"[https://api.example.com/v1/config](https://api.example.com/v1/config)\" \r\n            content_type=\"application/json\" \r\n            data=\"{{METRICS_CONFIG_JSON}}\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3. Multi-Line Body Expression with Variable Interpolation\r\nPlace dynamic or complex path queries directly inside the element body using template variable substitution.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cvariables\u003e\r\n        \u003cvariable name=\"ENVIRONMENT\" type=\"string\" value=\"production\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/variables\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003cfile_read file=\"./config/deployments.yaml\" output_var=\"MANIFEST_YAML\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Filter deployment settings based on pipeline environment --\u003e\r\n        \u003cyaml_path var=\"MANIFEST_YAML\" output_var=\"ACTIVE_REPLICAS\"\u003e\r\n            $.environments[?(@.name == \u0027{{ENVIRONMENT}}\u0027)].replicaCount\r\n        \u003c/yaml_path\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 4. Extracting Kubernetes Container Images (mode=\"json_array\")\r\nExtract a list of container images from a Kubernetes manifest file as a clean JSON array string.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Query all container images across all specs --\u003e\r\n        \u003cyaml_path \r\n            file=\"./k8s/deployment.yaml\" \r\n            mode=\"json_array\" \r\n            output_var=\"CONTAINER_IMAGES\"\u003e\r\n            $.spec.template.spec.containers[*].image\r\n        \u003c/yaml_path\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003cscript lang=\"bash\"\u003e\r\n            echo \"Deployed Images Array: $CONTAINER_IMAGES\"\r\n        \u003c/script\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 5. Re-serializing Matched Nodes to YAML (mode=\"yaml\")\r\nExtract a sub-section of a larger YAML document and output it formatted as valid YAML for writing to disk.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003cfile_read file=\"./config/cluster.yaml\" output_var=\"FULL_CLUSTER_YAML\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Extract storage spec back out as pure YAML --\u003e\r\n        \u003cyaml_path \r\n            var=\"FULL_CLUSTER_YAML\" \r\n            path=\"$.cluster.storage\" \r\n            mode=\"yaml\" \r\n            output_var=\"STORAGE_YAML\" /\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Write the isolated storage configuration to a new file --\u003e\r\n        \u003cfile_save file=\"./output/storage_spec.yaml\" var=\"STORAGE_YAML\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```","guides/AUTOMATION_ETL_SUMMARY.md":"**Flow** is a Go-based, enterprise-grade ETL orchestration engine and pipeline executor driven by XML pipeline definitions and an optional HTMX visual web UI. It bridges declarative pipeline design with execution, supporting multi-dialect relational and key-value database operations, parallel processing, embedded scripting (via the Yaegi Go interpreter), multi-sink event telemetry, and automated diagram generation.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Core System Architecture \u0026 CLI Interface\r\n\r\nThe application functions as a zero-dependency binary CLI runner and interactive server. Pipeline behavior, inputs, and environments are managed through CLI flags, XML configurations, and variable injection mechanisms.\r\n\r\n| Flag / Parameter | Type | Default | Automation \u0026 ETL Purpose |\r\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `-builder` | `bool` | `false` | Launches the interactive HTMX web server backed by `flow_builder.db` for visual pipeline design. |\r\n| `-builder-port` | `int` | `0` | Specifies the listening port for the visual builder web server (default `0` for dynamic ephemeral port). |\r\n| `-file` | `string` | `scripts.xml` | Specifies the primary XML configuration file containing pipeline nodes, variables, and DB definitions. |\r\n| `-config` | `string` | `\"\"` | Secondary XML configuration file path for environment-specific overrides (DBs, variables, preflight nodes). |\r\n| `-vars` | `string` | `\"\"` | Comma-separated `key=value` runtime variable overrides (e.g., `-vars \"TargetTable=foo,Limit=100\"`). |\r\n| `-options` | `string` | `\"\"` | XML file containing pre-configured default CLI flag options (applies options only if not explicitly set on CLI). |\r\n| `-validate` | `bool` | `false` | Runs non-executing validation passes (XSD schema validation and semantic AST check). |\r\n| `-preflight` | `bool` | `false` | Executes preflight validation nodes only (e.g., source file checks, staging table verification) without running main flow. |\r\n| `-format` | `string` | `csv` | Execution result output renderer (`json`, `jsonpretty`, `text`, `markdown`, `csv`, `stream`). |\r\n| `-xslt` / `-out` | `string` | `\"\"` | Path to XSLT stylesheet and output file to transform pipeline XML and inject generated Mermaid diagrams. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Orchestration \u0026 Control Flow Primitives\r\n\r\nFlow builds an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) from XML definitions, supporting complex execution topologies, branching logic, and streaming data movement.\r\n\r\n| AST Element | Execution Semantics \u0026 Capabilities |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| `\u003cscript\u003e` | Executes script logic in specified languages (e.g., Go via Yaegi, SQL) with optional target database row-streaming (`target_db`). |\r\n| `\u003csql\u003e` / `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` | Executes single or bulk SQL operations against registered relational or K/V database connections. |\r\n| `\u003cgroup\u003e` | Groups related execution steps into a logical sequential unit. |\r\n| `\u003cparallel\u003e` | Executes child pipeline nodes concurrently across threads and joins them at completion. |\r\n| `\u003cif\u003e` | Evaluates dynamic conditions (`condition` string or `var == equals`) to branch into `\u003cthen\u003e` or `\u003celse\u003e` blocks. |\r\n| `\u003cforeach\u003e` / `\u003cloop\u003e` / `\u003cwhile\u003e` | Iterates over dataset records or variable criteria, executing child nodes per row until completion. |\r\n| `\u003cpreflight\u003e` | Dedicated execution phase intended for schema sanity checks, target table truncations, and prerequisite validation. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Multi-Dialect Database Support \u0026 Telemetry Sinks\r\n\r\nFlow provides abstract database handling across both traditional relational engines and Key-Value stores. Connection strings support dynamic variable template resolution (e.g., `{{log_db_cs}}`).\r\n\r\n| Storage Engine / Dialect | Parameter Placeholder | Special SQL Dialect Handling |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| **PostgreSQL** | `$1, $2, $3` | Auto-detected from `postgres://` or `dbname=` connection strings. |\r\n| **Microsoft SQL Server** | `@p1, @p2, @p3` | Auto-detects driver; prepends `dbo.` to table names if schema is omitted. |\r\n| **Oracle** | `:1, :2, :3` | Auto-detected via `oracle://` or driver reflection (`godror`, `oracle`). |\r\n| **MySQL / SQLite / K/V** | `?, ?, ?` | Standard positional binding; handles K/V lookup state and SQL dialects. |\r\n\r\n#### Operational Logging \u0026 Audit Schemas\r\n\r\nWhen a `log_db` database connection is configured, Flow automatically logs telemetry via `DatabaseSink` into two standardized tables:\r\n\r\n1. **`pipeline_runs` (Pipeline-Level Summary Log):**\r\n* Fields: `run_id`, `file_path`, `config_path`, `status`, `started_at`, `finished_at`, `duration_ms`, `task_count`, `error_class`, `error_message`.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n2. **`pipeline_events` (Step-Level Event Audit):**\r\n* Fields: `run_id`, `execution_id`, `sequence_num`, `occurred_at`, `event_type`, `node_kind`, `node_id`, `status`, `error_message`, `rows_read`, `rows_written`, `rows_affected`.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Automation Lifecycle, Validation, and Documentation\r\n\r\n```\r\n[XML Script + Option Profile] ➔ [XSD \u0026 AST Validation] ➔ [Preflight Nodes] ➔ [Parallel / Sequential Execution] ➔ [Multi-Sink Audit Logging]\r\n                                                                                          │\r\n                                                                                          ├──\u003e Console Output (JSON/CSV/Markdown)\r\n                                                                                          └──\u003e Mermaid Diagram \u0026 XSLT Report\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n* **Configuration Inheritance:** Variables and database configurations are merged hierarchically: Base XML (`scripts.xml`) \u003c Override XML (`-config`) \u003c CLI parameters (`-vars`). Connection string templates (e.g., `{{db_password}}`) are iteratively expanded up to 3 depth levels.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Pre-Execution Safety:** `-validate` verifies both XSD structural compliance (using `xmllint`) and semantic AST validity (checking node linkages and database references) without touching data stores.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Live Observability:** Using `-format stream`, execution progress is piped in real-time to stdout as RFC3339-timestamped CSV event streams alongside standard `DatabaseSink` persistence.\r\n\r\n\r\n* **Visual Documentation Generation:** Flow parses pipeline XML directly into Mermaid.js TD flowcharts, capturing variable tables, database connections, conditional branches, loops, and parallel join nodes. The XSLT 3.0 transformation engine (`helium`/`xslt3`) can merge this flowchart directly into transformed reports or automated documentation packages.","guides/COMBINED_ENCRYPTION_AND_SIGNATURES.md":"# Single-File Cryptography: Combining Encryption \u0026 Digital Signatures\n\nThis guide explains how to combine **digital signatures** and **AES-256-GCM encryption** into a **single, unified file** for execution with `flow.exe` / `flow`. It details the cryptographic envelope formats, explains how to avoid common configuration pitfalls (such as detached signature confusion), and provides end-to-end walkthroughs for **Windows (PowerShell / Windows Cert Store)** and **Linux / macOS (OpenSSL / Bash / zsh)**, including generating self-signed certificates with both OpenSSL and `crypto_tool`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Unified Envelopes vs. Detached Signatures\n\n`go-flow` and its companion tool `crypto_tool` support two distinct distribution models for signed and encrypted pipelines:\n\n| Model | Files on Disk | File Reference in `options.xml` | How Verification Operates |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **Detached Signature** | **2 Files**\u003cbr\u003e`pipeline.xml.enc`\u003cbr\u003e`pipeline.xml.enc.sig` | Points to the **payload** (`pipeline.xml.enc`).\u003cbr\u003e*Never point to the `.sig` file!* | `flow.exe` auto-discovers `\u003cfile\u003e.sig`, verifies the detached signature against the payload, and then decrypts the payload. |\n| **Unified Single File**\u003cbr\u003e*(Recommended)* | **1 File**\u003cbr\u003e`pipeline.xml.sec` | Points directly to the single unified package (`pipeline.xml.sec`). | Both the cryptographic signature and the encrypted payload are packaged together in a single self-contained envelope. |\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Detached[\"Detached Signature (2 Files on Disk)\"]\n        D1[\"pipeline.xml.enc (Payload)\"]\n        D2[\"pipeline.xml.enc.sig (Detached Signature)\"]\n        D1 -.-\u003e|\"flow auto-detects companion\"| D2\n    end\n\n    subgraph Unified[\"Unified Package (1 Single File on Disk)\"]\n        U1[\"pipeline.xml.sec\u003cbr/\u003eFLOWSIGNED:v1:FORMAT:\u0026lt;Signature\u0026gt;:\u0026lt;FLOWENC Ciphertext\u0026gt;\"]\n    end\n```\n\n### The Common Pitfall: Pointing to `.sig` Files Directly\n\nA common mistake when using detached signatures is configuring `options.xml` or `-file` to point directly to the `.sig` file:\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- INCORRECT: Pointing directly to a detached signature file --\u003e\n\u003cfile\u003e.\\deploy\\pipeline.xml.enc.sig\u003c/file\u003e\n\u003cencrypted\u003etrue\u003c/encrypted\u003e\n\u003csecure-key\u003eSecretKey\u003c/secure-key\u003e\n```\n\nWhen this happens:\n1. `pipeline.xml.enc.sig` contains only an ASCII signature header (e.g. `FLOWSIG:v1:OPENSSL-RSA-SHA256:...`).\n2. Because `\u003cencrypted\u003etrue\u003c/encrypted\u003e` is set, `flow` attempts to decrypt the ASCII signature bytes as if they were AES-256 ciphertext.\n3. AES-GCM authentication tag validation fails immediately with:\n   ```\n   failed to decrypt resource: decryption failed: invalid key or tampered ciphertext\n   ```\n4. **Resolution**: Point `\u003cfile\u003e` to `pipeline.xml.enc` (which lets `flow` auto-detect `pipeline.xml.enc.sig`), or use a **unified single-file envelope** as described below.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Two Unified Architectures\n\n`go-flow` supports two single-file patterns:\n\n### Architecture A: Encrypt-then-Sign (`FLOWSIGNED:v1:` with `-wrap`) \u0026mdash; Recommended\n\nIn this pattern, the plaintext pipeline XML is encrypted first, and the resulting ciphertext is signed and packaged into a self-contained envelope using `crypto_tool -wrap`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Plaintext XML\"] --\u003e|\"1. AES-256-GCM\u003cbr/\u003e(crypto_tool -action encrypt)\"| B[\"Ciphertext\u003cbr/\u003e(FLOWENC:v1:...)\"]\n    B --\u003e|\"2. Digital Sign \u0026 Wrap\u003cbr/\u003e(crypto_tool -action sign -wrap)\"| C[\"Unified Package\u003cbr/\u003e(FLOWSIGNED:v1:...)\"]\n```\n\n#### Envelope Anatomy:\n```text\nFLOWSIGNED:v1:\u003cFORMAT\u003e:\u003cbase64(DigitalSignature)\u003e:\u003cbase64(FLOWENC:v1:Salt:Nonce:Ciphertext:Tag)\u003e\n```\n\n#### Why This Is Recommended:\n* **Zero Attack Surface:** `flow` validates the digital signature over the ciphertext **first**. Untrusted or tampered payloads are rejected before decryption keys are touched or decrypted data is loaded into memory.\n* **Transparent Decryption:** Once the outer signature passes verification, the engine automatically extracts the inner `FLOWENC:v1:` payload and decrypts it using the provided `-key-file`, `-secure-key`, or `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`.\n\n---\n\n### Architecture B: Sign-then-Encrypt (`FLOWENC:v1:` containing `FLOWSIGNED:v1:`)\n\nIn this pattern, the plaintext XML is signed into a `FLOWSIGNED:v1:` envelope first, and then the entire signed package is encrypted into an outer `FLOWENC:v1:` payload.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Plaintext XML\"] --\u003e|\"1. Digital Sign \u0026 Wrap\u003cbr/\u003e(crypto_tool -action sign -wrap)\"| B[\"Signed Envelope\u003cbr/\u003e(FLOWSIGNED:v1:...)\"]\n    B --\u003e|\"2. AES-256-GCM\u003cbr/\u003e(crypto_tool -action encrypt)\"| C[\"Encrypted Package\u003cbr/\u003e(FLOWENC:v1:...)\"]\n```\n\n#### Envelope Anatomy:\n```text\nFLOWENC:v1:\u003cbase64(AES-256-GCM-Ciphertext-Enclosing-FLOWSIGNED-Envelope)\u003e\n```\n\n#### When to Use:\n* **Signer Privacy:** In regulated or multi-tenant environments where the identity of the signer, certificate metadata, and signature algorithms must remain strictly confidential from external observers.\n* **Lifecycle:** `flow` decrypts the outer ciphertext first, detects that the inner content is a `FLOWSIGNED:v1:` envelope, and immediately verifies the digital signature before executing the pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## 3. End-to-End Walkthrough: Windows (PowerShell \u0026 Certificates)\n\nThis walkthrough demonstrates creating and running a **unified single-file pipeline** on Windows using standard PowerShell and an X.509 code signing certificate.\n\n### Prerequisites \u0026 Example Setup\n\nAssume the following directory structure:\n* `C:\\etl\\pipelines\\` \u0026mdash; Source directory containing raw pipeline definitions.\n* `C:\\etl\\deploy\\` \u0026mdash; Production deployment directory.\n* `C:\\certs\\` \u0026mdash; Secure certificate and key storage.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Exporting or Generating Certificate \u0026 Private Key on Windows\n\n#### Option 1: Export Public Certificate from Windows Certificate Store\n```powershell\n# Locate your code-signing certificate thumbprint\nGet-ChildItem -Path Cert:\\CurrentUser\\My -CodeSigningCert\n\n# Export the public certificate in X.509 format (.cer)\n$cert = Get-Item Cert:\\CurrentUser\\My\\3F4B8C1E0A2D3E4F5A6B7C8D9E0F1A2B3C4D5E6F\nExport-Certificate -Cert $cert -FilePath C:\\certs\\enterprise_code_signer.cer\n```\n\n#### Option 2: Export Private Key via PFX \u0026 Convert to PEM\nIf the private key is marked exportable, export the PFX and extract the PEM private key:\n```powershell\n# Export PFX with a temporary export passphrase\n$pwd = ConvertTo-SecureString -String \"ExportTempPass123!\" -AsPlainText -Force\nExport-PfxCertificate -Cert $cert -FilePath C:\\certs\\enterprise_signer.pfx -Password $pwd\n\n# Extract the private key as standard PEM using OpenSSL\nopenssl pkcs12 -in C:\\certs\\enterprise_signer.pfx -nocerts -out C:\\certs\\enterprise_signer_key.pem -nodes\nopenssl pkcs12 -in C:\\certs\\enterprise_signer.pfx -clcerts -nokeys -out C:\\certs\\enterprise_code_signer.pem\n```\n\n#### Option 3: Generate New Keypair \u0026 Self-Signed Certificate Directly with `crypto_tool`\nIf you do not have an existing enterprise certificate authority, generate an RSA keypair and self-signed X.509 certificate directly (no external OpenSSL required):\n```powershell\n.\\crypto_tool.exe -action gen-keypair `\n    -keypair-type rsa `\n    -bits 4096 `\n    -out-priv C:\\certs\\enterprise_signer_key.pem `\n    -out-pub C:\\certs\\enterprise_signer_pub.pem `\n    -out-cert C:\\certs\\enterprise_code_signer.pem `\n    -cn \"Enterprise ETL Pipeline Signer\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Encrypt the Plaintext Pipeline\n\nGiven a plaintext pipeline `C:\\etl\\pipelines\\daily_sales_etl.xml`, encrypt it using AES-256-GCM. \n\n\u003e [!TIP]\n\u003e To prevent secrets from appearing in process listings, supply the encryption passphrase via `-key-file` or the `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` environment variable.\n\n```powershell\n# Store key securely in a key file\nSet-Content -Path C:\\certs\\encryption.key -Value \"EnterpriseSecretPassphrase2026!\" -NoNewline\n\n# Encrypt pipeline to text-armored ciphertext\n.\\crypto_tool.exe -action encrypt `\n    -in C:\\etl\\pipelines\\daily_sales_etl.xml `\n    -out C:\\etl\\deploy\\daily_sales_etl.enc `\n    -key-file C:\\certs\\encryption.key\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Sign and Wrap into a Single Unified File\n\nSign the encrypted file with your code signing certificate and include the `-wrap` flag:\n\n```powershell\n.\\crypto_tool.exe -action sign `\n    -in C:\\etl\\deploy\\daily_sales_etl.enc `\n    -out C:\\etl\\deploy\\daily_sales_etl_unified.sec `\n    -private-key C:\\certs\\enterprise_signer_key.pem `\n    -cert C:\\certs\\enterprise_code_signer.pem `\n    -sig-type openssl `\n    -wrap\n```\n\n*Output:*\n```text\nSuccessfully signed C:\\etl\\deploy\\daily_sales_etl.enc -\u003e C:\\etl\\deploy\\daily_sales_etl_unified.sec (format: OPENSSL-RSA-SHA256)\n```\n\nThe resulting file `daily_sales_etl_unified.sec` is a **single text-armored file** containing both the digital signature and the encrypted payload. No separate `.sig` file is generated or needed.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Configure `options.xml` for Unified Single-File Execution\n\nCreate your execution options file (`C:\\etl\\options\\daily_sales_options.xml`):\n\n```xml\n\u003cflow_cli_options xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/go-flow/main/xsd/options.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003coptions\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Single unified file containing signature + encrypted payload --\u003e\n        \u003cfile\u003eC:\\etl\\deploy\\daily_sales_etl_unified.sec\u003c/file\u003e\n        \u003cformat\u003estream\u003c/format\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Security flags --\u003e\n        \u003cverify-signature\u003etrue\u003c/verify-signature\u003e\n        \u003cencrypted\u003etrue\u003c/encrypted\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Verification certificate (X.509 .cer or .pem) --\u003e\n        \u003ccert\u003eC:\\certs\\enterprise_code_signer.pem\u003c/cert\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Decryption key file (avoids storing passwords in XML) --\u003e\n        \u003ckey-file\u003eC:\\certs\\encryption.key\u003c/key-file\u003e\n    \u003c/options\u003e\n\u003c/flow_cli_options\u003e\n```\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e `\u003ccert\u003e` supports both PEM certificates (`.pem`, `.crt`) and raw Windows binary DER certificates (`.cer`).\n\nIf your signing certificate was issued by an internal Enterprise Root CA, you can also supply `\u003cca-cert\u003e` to validate the complete chain of trust:\n\n```xml\n\u003cca-cert\u003eC:\\certs\\enterprise_root_ca.cer\u003c/ca-cert\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Execute the Pipeline on Windows with `flow.exe`\n\nExecute the pipeline using either the options file or direct CLI flags:\n\n#### Method A: Using `options.xml`\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe -options C:\\etl\\options\\daily_sales_options.xml\n```\n\n#### Method B: Using Direct Command Line Flags\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe `\n    -file C:\\etl\\deploy\\daily_sales_etl_unified.sec `\n    -cert C:\\certs\\enterprise_code_signer.pem `\n    -key-file C:\\certs\\encryption.key `\n    -verify-signature `\n    -encrypted `\n    -format stream\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. End-to-End Walkthrough: Linux \u0026 macOS (Bash / zsh)\n\nThis walkthrough demonstrates creating self-signed certificates, signing, encrypting, and running a **unified single-file pipeline** on **Linux** (Ubuntu, Debian, RHEL, Alpine) and **macOS**.\n\n### Prerequisites \u0026 Example Setup\n\nAssume standard POSIX production paths:\n* `/opt/etl/pipelines/` \u0026mdash; Source directory containing raw pipeline definitions.\n* `/opt/etl/deploy/` \u0026mdash; Production deployment directory.\n* `/etc/ssl/flow/` (or `~/.certs/`) \u0026mdash; Restricted certificate and key storage.\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p /opt/etl/pipelines /opt/etl/deploy /opt/etl/options /etc/ssl/flow\nchmod 700 /etc/ssl/flow\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Create a Self-Signed Certificate \u0026 Keypair\n\nYou can generate your self-signed code-signing certificate using standard OpenSSL or directly with `crypto_tool`.\n\n#### Option A: Using OpenSSL (Standard on Linux \u0026 macOS)\n\nGenerate a 4096-bit RSA private key and self-signed X.509 certificate in a single command:\n\n```bash\n# Generate private key and self-signed certificate (valid for 365 days)\nopenssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 \\\n    -keyout /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_key.pem \\\n    -out /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem \\\n    -days 365 \\\n    -nodes \\\n    -subj \"/C=US/ST=California/L=San Francisco/O=Enterprise ETL/CN=Flow Pipeline Signer\"\n\n# Restrict private key permissions (owner read-only)\nchmod 600 /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_key.pem\nchmod 644 /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem\n```\n\n*For modern Elliptic Curve (ECDSA P-256) instead of RSA:*\n```bash\nopenssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 \\\n    -keyout /etc/ssl/flow/flow_ecdsa_key.pem \\\n    -out /etc/ssl/flow/flow_ecdsa_cert.pem \\\n    -days 365 \\\n    -nodes \\\n    -subj \"/CN=Flow ECDSA Pipeline Signer\"\n\nchmod 600 /etc/ssl/flow/flow_ecdsa_key.pem\n```\n\n#### Option B: Using `crypto_tool` Directly (Pure Go \u0026mdash; Zero External Dependencies)\n\nIf OpenSSL is not installed (e.g. minimal Docker containers, Alpine Linux, or lightweight VMs), use `crypto_tool`\u0027s built-in `gen-keypair` action:\n\n```bash\n# Generate 4096-bit RSA keypair and self-signed X.509 certificate\n./crypto_tool -action gen-keypair \\\n    -keypair-type rsa \\\n    -bits 4096 \\\n    -out-priv /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_key.pem \\\n    -out-pub /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_pub.pem \\\n    -out-cert /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem \\\n    -cn \"Flow Pipeline Signer\"\n\n# Secure permissions\nchmod 600 /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_key.pem\nchmod 644 /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_pub.pem\n```\n\n*Output:*\n```text\nSuccessfully generated rsa keypair:\n  Private key: /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_key.pem\n  Public key:  /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_pub.pem\n  Certificate: /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Create a Secure Symmetric Encryption Key File\n\nStore your AES-256 encryption passphrase in a dedicated key file with restricted POSIX permissions (`0600`) so it cannot be read by other system users:\n\n```bash\n# Create key file with restricted permissions\necho -n \"EnterpriseSecretPassphrase2026!\" \u003e /etc/ssl/flow/encryption.key\nchmod 600 /etc/ssl/flow/encryption.key\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Encrypt the Pipeline (AES-256-GCM)\n\nEncrypt the raw pipeline definition `/opt/etl/pipelines/daily_sales_etl.xml`:\n\n```bash\n./crypto_tool -action encrypt \\\n    -in /opt/etl/pipelines/daily_sales_etl.xml \\\n    -out /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl.enc \\\n    -key-file /etc/ssl/flow/encryption.key\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Sign and Wrap into a Unified File (`.sec`)\n\nDigitally sign the encrypted ciphertext using your private key and wrap it into a single `FLOWSIGNED:v1:...` envelope:\n\n```bash\n./crypto_tool -action sign \\\n    -in /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl.enc \\\n    -out /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl_unified.sec \\\n    -priv /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_key.pem \\\n    -cert /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem \\\n    -sig-type openssl \\\n    -wrap\n```\n\n*Output:*\n```text\nSuccessfully signed /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl.enc -\u003e /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl_unified.sec (format: OPENSSL-RSA-SHA256)\n```\n\n#### Streamlined Alternative: Single-Line Unix Pipe (Zero Intermediate Files)\nBecause `crypto_tool` supports standard input/output (`-in -` and `-out -`), you can encrypt and sign in a single command pipeline without writing `daily_sales_etl.enc` to disk:\n\n```bash\n./crypto_tool -action encrypt -in /opt/etl/pipelines/daily_sales_etl.xml -key-file /etc/ssl/flow/encryption.key -out - | \\\n./crypto_tool -action sign -in - -priv /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_key.pem -cert /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem -wrap -out /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl_unified.sec\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Verify the Unified File Manually (Optional)\n\nVerify that the unified `.sec` file passes cryptographic validation against the self-signed certificate:\n\n```bash\n./crypto_tool -action verify \\\n    -in /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl_unified.sec \\\n    -cert /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem\n```\n\n*Output:*\n```text\nSignature VALID (OPENSSL via RSA-SHA256, Signer: Flow Pipeline Signer)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Execute on Linux / macOS with `flow`\n\n#### Method A: Using Command Line Flags\n```bash\n./flow \\\n    -file /opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl_unified.sec \\\n    -cert /etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem \\\n    -key-file /etc/ssl/flow/encryption.key \\\n    -verify-signature \\\n    -encrypted \\\n    -format stream\n```\n\n#### Method B: Using Options File (`/opt/etl/options/daily_sales_options.xml`)\n```xml\n\u003cflow_cli_options xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/go-flow/main/xsd/options.xsd\"\u003e\n    \u003coptions\u003e\n        \u003cfile\u003e/opt/etl/deploy/daily_sales_etl_unified.sec\u003c/file\u003e\n        \u003cformat\u003estream\u003c/format\u003e\n        \u003cverify-signature\u003etrue\u003c/verify-signature\u003e\n        \u003cencrypted\u003etrue\u003c/encrypted\u003e\n        \u003ccert\u003e/etc/ssl/flow/flow_signer_cert.pem\u003c/cert\u003e\n        \u003ckey-file\u003e/etc/ssl/flow/encryption.key\u003c/key-file\u003e\n    \u003c/options\u003e\n\u003c/flow_cli_options\u003e\n```\n\n```bash\n./flow -options /opt/etl/options/daily_sales_options.xml\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Execution Lifecycle \u0026 Verification Output\n\nWhen `flow` executes a unified package, it performs verification and decryption in sequential order:\n\n```text\n[FLOW] Verifying digital signature on resource \"daily_sales_etl_unified.sec\"...\n[FLOW] Signature verified successfully. Signer: \"Flow Pipeline Signer\" (Algorithm: OPENSSL-RSA-SHA256).\n[FLOW] Decrypting AES-256-GCM payload using provided security key...\n[FLOW] Decryption successful.\n[FLOW] Parsing pipeline XML schema and abstract syntax tree...\n[FLOW] Pipeline initialized: \"daily_sales_pipeline\". Executing nodes...\n```\n\nIf any tampering occurs:\n* **Altered Ciphertext or Signature:** \n  ```text\n  Error reading script file: digital signature verification failed for resource \"...daily_sales_etl_unified.sec\": RSA signature verification failed\n  ```\n* **Tampered Certificate or Untrusted Signer:**\n  ```text\n  Error reading script file: certificate verification failed: certificate signed by unknown authority\n  ```\n* **Tampered Payload inside Decryption Stage:**\n  ```text\n  Error reading script file: decryption failed: invalid key or tampered ciphertext\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Summary Cheat Sheet (Windows, Linux, macOS)\n\n| Task | Windows (PowerShell) | Linux / macOS (Bash / zsh) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Create Self-Signed Certificate (OpenSSL)** | `openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -days 365 -nodes -subj \"/CN=Signer\"` | `openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -days 365 -nodes -subj \"/CN=Signer\"` |\n| **Create Self-Signed Certificate (`crypto_tool`)** | `.\\crypto_tool.exe -action gen-keypair -bits 4096 -out-priv key.pem -out-cert cert.pem -cn \"Signer\"` | `./crypto_tool -action gen-keypair -bits 4096 -out-priv key.pem -out-cert cert.pem -cn \"Signer\"` |\n| **Create Secure Key File** | `Set-Content -Path secret.key -Value \"Passphrase\" -NoNewline` | `echo -n \"Passphrase\" \u003e secret.key \u0026\u0026 chmod 600 secret.key` |\n| **1. Encrypt XML** | `.\\crypto_tool.exe -action encrypt -in job.xml -out job.enc -key-file secret.key` | `./crypto_tool -action encrypt -in job.xml -out job.enc -key-file secret.key` |\n| **2. Sign \u0026 Wrap (Single File)** | `.\\crypto_tool.exe -action sign -in job.enc -out job.sec -priv key.pem -cert cert.pem -wrap` | `./crypto_tool -action sign -in job.enc -out job.sec -priv key.pem -cert cert.pem -wrap` |\n| **3. Streamlined Piped Single-Step** | `.\\crypto_tool.exe -action encrypt -in job.xml -key-file secret.key -out - \\| .\\crypto_tool.exe -action sign -in - -priv key.pem -cert cert.pem -wrap -out job.sec` | `./crypto_tool -action encrypt -in job.xml -key-file secret.key -out - \\| ./crypto_tool -action sign -in - -priv key.pem -cert cert.pem -wrap -out job.sec` |\n| **4. Verify Manually** | `.\\crypto_tool.exe -action verify -in job.sec -cert cert.pem` | `./crypto_tool -action verify -in job.sec -cert cert.pem` |\n| **5. Run Single File with Flow** | `.\\flow.exe -file job.sec -cert cert.pem -key-file secret.key -verify-signature -encrypted` | `./flow -file job.sec -cert cert.pem -key-file secret.key -verify-signature -encrypted` |\n| **6. Run via Options File** | `.\\flow.exe -options options.xml` | `./flow -options options.xml` |\n","guides/COMPILATION_AND_BUILD_GUIDE.md":"# Compilation, Git Workflow \u0026 Build Options Guide\n\n\u003e **Complete Reference for Cloning, Building, Cross-Compiling, and Packaging the `go-flow` Ecosystem**  \n\u003e **Binaries Covered:** `flow` (`flow.exe`), `crypto_tool` (`crypto_tool.exe`), `db_importer` (`db_importer.exe`)  \n\u003e **Go Toolchain:** Go `1.26.5+` | **Pure-Go Static Compilation:** Supported (`CGO_ENABLED=0`)\n\nThis guide provides step-by-step instructions for cloning the **Flow (`go-flow`)** repository with Git, managing dependencies (including co-development with the core `github.com/etl-madness/flow` library), and compiling all three ecosystem binaries across Windows, Linux, and macOS using every supported Go compiler option.\n\n---\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Architecture of the Build Ecosystem](#1-architecture-of-the-build-ecosystem)\n2. [Prerequisites \u0026 Toolchain Verification](#2-prerequisites--toolchain-verification)\n3. [Git Instructions (Clone, Tags, Branches \u0026 Co-Development)](#3-git-instructions-clone-tags-branches--co-development)\n4. [Quick Build: All Three Ecosystem Binaries](#4-quick-build-all-three-ecosystem-binaries)\n5. [Complete Reference of Supported `go build` Options](#5-complete-reference-of-supported-go-build-options)\n6. [Cross-Compilation Guide (Windows, Linux, macOS, ARM64)](#6-cross-compilation-guide-windows-linux-macos-arm64)\n7. [Air-Gapped \u0026 Offline Vendor Builds](#7-air-gapped--offline-vendor-builds)\n8. [Automated Multi-Platform Release Scripts](#8-automated-multi-platform-release-scripts)\n9. [Running the Test Suite \u0026 Post-Build Verification](#9-running-the-test-suite--post-build-verification)\n10. [Optional External Script Runtimes (`dotnet-script` \u0026 PowerShell `pwsh`)](#10-optional-external-script-runtimes-dotnet-script--powershell-pwsh)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architecture of the Build Ecosystem\n\nThe `go-flow` repository contains **three executable `main` packages** and relies on **100% pure-Go database and cryptographic drivers**, allowing you to compile zero-dependency static binaries without a C compiler (`gcc`/`clang`):\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph Repo[\"Git Repository: github.com/etl-madness/go-flow\"]\n        M1[\"Root Package (.)\u003cbr/\u003emain.go, builder/, pkg/flowcrypto/\"]\n        M2[\"Crypto Utility (./crypto_tool)\u003cbr/\u003ecrypto_tool/main.go\"]\n        M3[\"SQL Importer (./db_importer)\u003cbr/\u003edb_importer/mssql_importer.go\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Deps[\"Pure-Go Embedded Drivers \u0026 Libraries\"]\n        D1[\"Core Engine: github.com/etl-madness/flow\"]\n        D2[\"Relational: go-mssqldb, lib/pq, mysql, go-ora/v2, modernc.org/sqlite\"]\n        D3[\"NoSQL \u0026 KV: bbolt, badger/v4, go-redis/v9, etcd/v3\"]\n        D4[\"Scripting \u0026 Crypto: Yaegi (Go), gopenpgp/v2, x/crypto\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Binaries[\"Compiled Standalone Executables\"]\n        B1[\"flow / flow.exe\u003cbr/\u003e(CLI Engine + HTMX Visual Studio)\"]\n        B2[\"crypto_tool / crypto_tool.exe\u003cbr/\u003e(Keygen, Encrypt, Sign, Verify, Inspect)\"]\n        B3[\"db_importer / db_importer.exe\u003cbr/\u003e(Database XML Repository Loader)\"]\n    end\n\n    Deps --\u003e Repo\n    M1 --\u003e B1\n    M2 --\u003e B2\n    M3 --\u003e B3\n```\n\n| Target Binary | Source Package Path | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`flow`** / **`flow.exe`** | `.` (Repository Root) | Main ETL \u0026 workflow orchestration engine, schema validator, XSLT/Mermaid doc generator, and embedded HTMX Visual Pipeline Studio (`-builder`). |\n| **`crypto_tool`** / **`crypto_tool.exe`** | `./crypto_tool` | Standalone CLI for RSA/Ed25519 key generation, AES-256-GCM encryption (`FLOWENC:v1:`), digital signing (`FLOWSIGNED:v1:`, `.sig`, `.p7s`), and verification. |\n| **`db_importer`** / **`db_importer.exe`** | `./db_importer` | Bulk imports `PipelineXML`, `OptionsXML`, and `ConfigXML` files (UTF-8 or UTF-16) into SQL Server tables (`dbo.flow_*_content`). |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Prerequisites \u0026 Toolchain Verification\n\n1. **Go SDK**: Version `1.26.5` or newer (as specified in `go.mod`).\n2. **Git**: Version `2.30+` for cloning, tag checkout, and module resolution.\n3. **C Compiler *(Optional)***: **Not required** for standard or production builds (`CGO_ENABLED=0` works natively). Only required if compiling with the `-race` data race detector enabled.\n\nVerify your environment before building:\n\n```bash\n# Verify Go and Git versions\ngo version\ngit --version\n\n# Inspect active Go environment variables (GOOS, GOARCH, CGO_ENABLED, GOMODCACHE)\ngo env GOOS GOARCH CGO_ENABLED GOPROXY\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Git Instructions (Clone, Tags, Branches \u0026 Co-Development)\n\n### 3.1 Clone the Repository (HTTPS or SSH)\n\n```bash\n# Option A: Clone via HTTPS\ngit clone https://github.com/etl-madness/go-flow.git\ncd go-flow\n\n# Option B: Clone via SSH\ngit clone git@github.com:etl-madness/go-flow.git\ncd go-flow\n```\n\n### 3.2 Checkout a Specific Release Tag (Recommended for Production)\n\nTo build a deterministic release version (such as `v1.1.24`, `v1.1.25`, or `v1.1.26`):\n\n```bash\n# Fetch all remote tags\ngit fetch --all --tags\n\n# List the 10 most recent semantic version tags\ngit tag --sort=-v:refname | head -n 10\n\n# Checkout a specific release tag into a detached HEAD or release branch\ngit checkout tags/v1.1.26 -b release-v1.1.26\n\n# Confirm exact commit hash and tag\ngit status\ngit rev-parse --short HEAD\n```\n\n### 3.3 Shallow Clone for Fast CI/CD Pipelines\n\nIn automated CI/CD runners (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Azure DevOps) where full commit history is unnecessary:\n\n```bash\n# Clone only the single commit for tag v1.1.26\ngit clone --depth 1 --branch v1.1.26 https://github.com/etl-madness/go-flow.git\ncd go-flow\n```\n\n### 3.4 Updating an Existing Local Clone\n\n```bash\n# Pull latest changes on main and prune deleted remote branches\ngit checkout main\ngit pull --ff-only origin main\ngit fetch --prune --prune-tags\n\n# Synchronize and verify Go module dependencies against go.sum\ngo mod download\ngo mod verify\n```\n\n### 3.5 Co-Developing `go-flow` and `flow` Locally (Go Workspaces)\n\nIf you are modifying both `github.com/etl-madness/go-flow` (CLI \u0026 Visual Builder) and `github.com/etl-madness/flow` (Core Execution Engine) side-by-side:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Clone both repositories into the same parent directory (e.g., repos/etl-madness/)\ncd ..\ngit clone https://github.com/etl-madness/flow.git\ncd go-flow\n\n# Option A: Use Go Workspace mode (Recommended — does not dirty go.mod)\ngo work init . ../flow\ngo work sync\n\n# Option B: Temporary go.mod replace directive\ngo mod edit -replace github.com/etl-madness/flow=../flow\ngo mod tidy\n\n# Revert the replace directive before committing to git:\ngo mod edit -dropreplace github.com/etl-madness/flow\ngo mod tidy\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Quick Build: All Three Ecosystem Binaries\n\n### Windows (PowerShell)\n\n```powershell\n# 1. Build the core Flow engine \u0026 HTMX Visual Builder\ngo build -o flow.exe .\n\n# 2. Build the companion cryptographic utility\ngo build -o crypto_tool.exe ./crypto_tool\n\n# 3. Build the SQL Server XML repository importer\ngo build -o db_importer.exe ./db_importer\n```\n\n### Linux \u0026 macOS (Bash / Zsh)\n\n```bash\n# 1. Build the core Flow engine \u0026 HTMX Visual Builder\ngo build -o flow .\n\n# 2. Build the companion cryptographic utility\ngo build -o crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\n\n# 3. Build the SQL Server XML repository importer\ngo build -o db_importer ./db_importer\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Complete Reference of Supported `go build` Options\n\nBelow is the comprehensive matrix of Go compiler, linker, and environment options supported when building `flow`, `crypto_tool`, and `db_importer`, along with copy-paste examples for each option.\n\n### Summary Matrix of Build Options\n\n| Option / Env Var | Category | Effect on `go-flow` Binaries | Recommended For |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`-o \u003cpath\u003e`** | Output | Sets output executable path and filename (`flow.exe`, `bin/flow`). | All builds |\n| **`CGO_ENABLED=0`** | Linker / Portability | Disables `cgo`, producing a **100% static binary** with zero libc/C dependencies. | Production, Docker `scratch` / `distroless` / Alpine |\n| **`-trimpath`** | Security / Reproducibility | Strips all local filesystem paths (e.g., `C:\\Users\\...`) from the compiled binary and stack traces. | Production \u0026 Security-Hardened Releases |\n| **`-ldflags=\"-s -w\"`** | Size Optimization | Omits the symbol table (`-s`) and DWARF debug tables (`-w`), shrinking binary size by ~25–30%. | Production \u0026 Edge Deployments |\n| **`-buildvcs=true\\|false`** | Provenance | Embeds (or omits) Git commit SHA, timestamp, and dirty-tree status in Go build info (`go version -m`). | CI/CD Auditing (`true`) or Reproducible (`false`) |\n| **`-mod=readonly`** | Dependency Lock | Fails the build if `go.mod` or `go.sum` needs updating, preventing accidental dependency drift. | CI/CD Pipelines |\n| **`-mod=vendor`** | Air-Gapped / Offline | Compiles strictly using the local `./vendor` directory without any network access. | Air-Gapped / Restricted Enterprise Networks |\n| **`-race`** | Concurrency Analysis | Instruments memory access to detect data races in `\u003cparallel\u003e` workflows (requires `CGO_ENABLED=1`). | QA, Integration Testing \u0026 Parallel Debugging |\n| **`-gcflags=\"all=-N -l\"`** | Interactive Debugging | Disables compiler optimizations (`-N`) and function inlining (`-l`) for accurate breakpoints in Delve (`dlv`). | Local Developer Debugging |\n| **`-pgo=auto`** | Performance | Applies Profile-Guided Optimization from `default.pgo` CPU profiles (~5–10% throughput boost). | High-Volume Bulk ETL (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`, `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`) |\n| **`-a`** | Cache Control | Forces recompilation of all packages, ignoring cached build artifacts in `GOCACHE`. | Clean Release Builds |\n| **`-v` / `-x` / `-work`** | Build Diagnostics | Prints compiled package names (`-v`), exact compiler commands (`-x`), and preserves temp workdir (`-work`). | Troubleshooting Toolchain Issues |\n| **`GOAMD64=v1..v4`** | CPU Microarchitecture | Targets specific x86-64 instruction sets (`v1` baseline SSE2, `v3` AVX2/BMI2, `v4` AVX-512). | Hardware-Tuned Data Warehouse Servers |\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Examples for Every Supported Option\n\n#### 5.1 Production Hardened \u0026 Size-Optimized Build (`CGO_ENABLED=0`, `-trimpath`, `-ldflags=\"-s -w\"`)\nProduces a compact, reproducible, statically linked binary with no local filesystem paths leaked in panic traces:\n\n```powershell\n# Windows (PowerShell)\n$env:CGO_ENABLED = \"0\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/flow.exe .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/crypto_tool.exe ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/db_importer.exe ./db_importer\n```\n\n```bash\n# Linux / macOS (Bash)\nCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/flow .\nCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\nCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/db_importer ./db_importer\n```\n\n#### 5.2 Embedding \u0026 Inspecting Git VCS Provenance (`-buildvcs=true`)\nBy default, Go embeds the exact Git commit hash, commit timestamp, and whether uncommitted modifications existed at build time. You can enforce this with `-buildvcs=true` and inspect any compiled `flow` binary using `go version -m`:\n\n```bash\n# Build with explicit VCS metadata stamping\ngo build -buildvcs=true -trimpath -o flow.exe .\n\n# Inspect embedded Git commit, Go version, compiler flags, and module hashes\ngo version -m flow.exe\n```\n\n*Sample `go version -m` output:*\n```text\nflow.exe: go1.26.5\n        path    github.com/etl-madness/go-flow\n        mod     github.com/etl-madness/go-flow  (devel)\n        dep     github.com/etl-madness/flow     v1.3.4\n        build   -buildmode=exe\n        build   -compiler=gc\n        build   -trimpath=true\n        build   vcs=git\n        build   vcs.revision=a1b2c3d4e5f6...\n        build   vcs.time=2026-10-31T12:00:00Z\n        build   vcs.modified=false\n```\n\n#### 5.3 Interactive Debugger Build (`-gcflags=\"all=-N -l\"`)\nWhen stepping through `flow` or `builder/` in **VS Code**, **GoLand**, or **Delve (`dlv`)**, disable inlining and register optimizations so all local variables remain inspectable:\n\n```bash\n# Build with full DWARF symbols and optimizations disabled\ngo build -gcflags=\"all=-N -l\" -o flow_debug.exe .\n\n# Launch under Delve debugger\ndlv exec ./flow_debug.exe -- -file pipeline.xml -debug\n```\n\n#### 5.4 Data Race Detector Build (`-race`)\nWhen testing complex `\u003cparallel\u003e` pipelines or custom Go extensions, compile with `-race` (requires `CGO_ENABLED=1` and a C compiler such as MinGW-w64 `gcc` on Windows or `build-essential` on Linux):\n\n```powershell\n# Windows (PowerShell with MinGW-w64 gcc in PATH)\n$env:CGO_ENABLED = \"1\"\ngo build -race -o flow_race.exe .\n./flow_race.exe -file parallel_pipeline.xml\n```\n\n```bash\n# Linux / macOS\nCGO_ENABLED=1 go build -race -o flow_race .\n./flow_race -file parallel_pipeline.xml\n```\n\n#### 5.5 CPU Microarchitecture Tuning (`GOAMD64` \u0026 `GOARM64`)\nFor high-throughput `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`, `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`, and AES-256-GCM encryption on modern server hardware, target modern SIMD instruction sets:\n\n```bash\n# Target x86-64-v3 (AVX2, BMI2, FMA — Intel Haswell+ / AMD Excavator+ / modern cloud VMs)\nGOAMD64=v3 CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o flow_avx2.exe .\n\n# Target x86-64-v4 (AVX-512 — Intel Skylake-X / Ice Lake+ / AMD Zen 4+)\nGOAMD64=v4 CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o flow_avx512.exe .\n\n# Target ARM64 v9.0 with LSE atomics and crypto extensions (AWS Graviton3/4, Apple M-series)\nGOOS=linux GOARCH=arm64 GOARM64=v9.0,lse,crypto CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o flow_graviton .\n```\n\n#### 5.6 Diagnostic \u0026 Force-Rebuild Options (`-a`, `-v`, `-x`, `-work`)\nWhen diagnosing build cache issues or auditing compiler invocations:\n\n```bash\n# Force full rebuild (-a) and print each package as it compiles (-v)\ngo build -a -v -o flow.exe .\n\n# Print every underlying compiler/linker command (-x) and preserve temporary work directory (-work)\ngo build -x -work -o flow.exe .\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cross-Compilation Guide (Windows, Linux, macOS, ARM64)\n\nBecause `go-flow` uses pure-Go drivers (`modernc.org/sqlite`, `go-mssqldb`, `lib/pq`, `go-sql-driver/mysql`, `go-ora/v2`, `bbolt`, `badger`), you can cross-compile for **any operating system and CPU architecture from a single workstation** by setting `GOOS`, `GOARCH`, and `CGO_ENABLED=0`.\n\n### 6.1 Cross-Compiling from Windows (PowerShell)\n\n```powershell\n# Ensure pure-Go static linking is enabled for all targets\n$env:CGO_ENABLED = \"0\"\n\n# 1. Windows x64 (amd64)\n$env:GOOS = \"windows\"; $env:GOARCH = \"amd64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-amd64/flow.exe .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-amd64/crypto_tool.exe ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-amd64/db_importer.exe ./db_importer\n\n# 2. Windows ARM64 (Surface / Windows on ARM)\n$env:GOOS = \"windows\"; $env:GOARCH = \"arm64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-arm64/flow.exe .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-arm64/crypto_tool.exe ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-arm64/db_importer.exe ./db_importer\n\n# 3. Linux x64 (amd64 — Ubuntu, RHEL, Debian, Alpine, Docker scratch)\n$env:GOOS = \"linux\"; $env:GOARCH = \"amd64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-amd64/flow .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-amd64/crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-amd64/db_importer ./db_importer\n\n# 4. Linux ARM64 (AWS Graviton, Raspberry Pi 4/5, Kubernetes ARM nodes)\n$env:GOOS = \"linux\"; $env:GOARCH = \"arm64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-arm64/flow .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-arm64/crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-arm64/db_importer ./db_importer\n\n# 5. macOS Apple Silicon (darwin/arm64 — M1/M2/M3/M4)\n$env:GOOS = \"darwin\"; $env:GOARCH = \"arm64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-arm64/flow .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-arm64/crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-arm64/db_importer ./db_importer\n\n# 6. macOS Intel (darwin/amd64)\n$env:GOOS = \"darwin\"; $env:GOARCH = \"amd64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-amd64/flow .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-amd64/crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-amd64/db_importer ./db_importer\n\n# 7. FreeBSD x64 \u0026 ARM64 (freebsd/amd64 \u0026 freebsd/arm64)\n$env:GOOS = \"freebsd\"; $env:GOARCH = \"amd64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-amd64/flow .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-amd64/crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-amd64/db_importer ./db_importer\n\n$env:GOOS = \"freebsd\"; $env:GOARCH = \"arm64\"\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-arm64/flow .\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-arm64/crypto_tool ./crypto_tool\ngo build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-arm64/db_importer ./db_importer\n\n# Reset environment variables to host defaults\nRemove-Item Env:GOOS, Env:GOARCH, Env:CGO_ENABLED -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n```\n\n### 6.2 Cross-Compiling from Linux / macOS / FreeBSD (Bash / Sh)\n\n```bash\n# 1. Linux amd64 \u0026 arm64\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-amd64/flow .\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux GOARCH=arm64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/linux-arm64/flow .\n\n# 2. Windows amd64 \u0026 arm64\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-amd64/flow.exe .\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=windows GOARCH=arm64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/windows-arm64/flow.exe .\n\n# 3. macOS Apple Silicon (arm64) \u0026 Intel (amd64)\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-arm64/flow .\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=darwin GOARCH=amd64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/darwin-amd64/flow .\n\n# 4. FreeBSD amd64 \u0026 arm64\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=freebsd GOARCH=amd64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-amd64/flow .\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=freebsd GOARCH=arm64 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/freebsd-arm64/flow .\n```\n\n\u003e **Note on Platform-Specific PKCS#7 Signing (`crypto_tool -format p7s`)**:\n\u003e - **Verification** of `.p7s` (PKCS#7/CMS), `.sig` (OpenSSL), and `.asc` (OpenPGP) signatures is implemented in **100% pure Go** (`pkg/flowcrypto/pkcs7.go`) and works identically across Windows, Linux, and macOS.\n\u003e - **Signing** in `.p7s` format using the Windows Certificate Store (`-cert-thumbprint` / `-cert-store`) invokes PowerShell\u0027s `System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs.SignedCms` when run on Windows, whereas `-format sig` (OpenSSL RSA/ECDSA/Ed25519) is pure Go on all platforms.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Air-Gapped \u0026 Offline Vendor Builds\n\nFor enterprise environments where build servers have no outbound internet access:\n\n```bash\n# Step 1 (On a connected machine): Vendor all module dependencies into ./vendor\ngit clone https://github.com/etl-madness/go-flow.git\ncd go-flow\ngo mod tidy\ngo mod verify\ngo mod vendor\n\n# Optional: Commit or archive the repository including ./vendor\ntar -czf go-flow-vendored.tar.gz .\n\n# Step 2 (On the air-gapped build server): Compile strictly from ./vendor\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOPROXY=off go build -mod=vendor -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o flow.exe .\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOPROXY=off go build -mod=vendor -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o crypto_tool.exe ./crypto_tool\nCGO_ENABLED=0 GOPROXY=off go build -mod=vendor -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o db_importer.exe ./db_importer\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Automated Multi-Platform Release Scripts\n\n### PowerShell Release Builder (`build-all.ps1`)\nBuilds all 3 binaries across all 6 target platforms and generates a `SHA256SUMS.txt` manifest:\n\n```powershell\n$ErrorActionPreference = \"Stop\"\n$targets = @(\n    @{ GOOS = \"windows\"; GOARCH = \"amd64\"; Ext = \".exe\" },\n    @{ GOOS = \"windows\"; GOARCH = \"arm64\"; Ext = \".exe\" },\n    @{ GOOS = \"linux\";   GOARCH = \"amd64\"; Ext = \"\"     },\n    @{ GOOS = \"linux\";   GOARCH = \"arm64\"; Ext = \"\"     },\n    @{ GOOS = \"darwin\";  GOARCH = \"amd64\"; Ext = \"\"     },\n    @{ GOOS = \"darwin\";  GOARCH = \"arm64\"; Ext = \"\"     },\n    @{ GOOS = \"freebsd\"; GOARCH = \"amd64\"; Ext = \"\"     },\n    @{ GOOS = \"freebsd\"; GOARCH = \"arm64\"; Ext = \"\"     }\n)\n\n$env:CGO_ENABLED = \"0\"\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path \"dist\" | Out-Null\n$hashes = @()\n\nforeach ($t in $targets) {\n    $env:GOOS = $t.GOOS\n    $env:GOARCH = $t.GOARCH\n    $outDir = \"dist/$($t.GOOS)-$($t.GOARCH)\"\n    New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path $outDir | Out-Null\n\n    Write-Host \"Building $($t.GOOS)/$($t.GOARCH)...\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\n    go build -mod=readonly -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o \"$outDir/flow$($t.Ext)\" .\n    go build -mod=readonly -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o \"$outDir/crypto_tool$($t.Ext)\" ./crypto_tool\n    go build -mod=readonly -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o \"$outDir/db_importer$($t.Ext)\" ./db_importer\n\n    Get-ChildItem -Path $outDir -File | ForEach-Object {\n        $h = (Get-FileHash $_.FullName -Algorithm SHA256).Hash.ToLower()\n        $rel = \"$($t.GOOS)-$($t.GOARCH)/$($_.Name)\"\n        $hashes += \"$h  $rel\"\n    }\n}\n\n$hashes | Set-Content -Path \"dist/SHA256SUMS.txt\" -Encoding UTF8\nRemove-Item Env:GOOS, Env:GOARCH, Env:CGO_ENABLED -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\nWrite-Host \"Release build complete! Checksums written to dist/SHA256SUMS.txt\" -ForegroundColor Green\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Running the Test Suite \u0026 Post-Build Verification\n\nAfter cloning or modifying the repository, run the automated test suite and smoke-test the compiled binaries:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Run all unit \u0026 integration tests across the entire repository\ngo test ./...\n\n# 2. Run specific package tests with verbose output\ngo test -v .\ngo test -v ./builder\ngo test -v ./pkg/flowcrypto\ngo test -v ./crypto_tool\ngo test -v ./db_importer\n\n# 3. Verify the compiled binaries respond to CLI flags\n./flow -h\n./crypto_tool -h\n./db_importer -h\n\n# 4. Validate a sample pipeline against the XSD schema without executing\n./flow -file scripts.xml -xsd ./xsd/pipeline.xsd -validate\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Optional External Script Runtimes (`dotnet-script` \u0026 PowerShell `pwsh`)\n\nBy default, `\u003cscript language=\"go\"\u003e` runs **in-process** via the embedded pure-Go **Yaegi** interpreter and requires zero external tools. However, if your pipelines use C# scripts (`\u003cscript language=\"csx\"\u003e`) or cross-platform PowerShell 7+ (`\u003cscript language=\"pwsh\"\u003e`), you can install those optional runtimes from the command line as shown below.\n\n| `\u003cscript\u003e` Language Attribute | Runtime Binary Invoked | Bundled in `flow`? | When Installation Is Needed |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `language=\"go\"` | Embedded **Yaegi** engine | **Yes (Built-in)** | Never — compiled directly into `flow`. |\n| `language=\"sh\"` / `\"bash\"` / `\"zsh\"` / `\"cmd\"` | Host OS shell | **OS Built-in** | Included on standard POSIX / Windows hosts. |\n| `language=\"csx\"` | `dotnet-script` | Optional external | Required only if pipelines execute C# `.csx` `\u003cscript\u003e` nodes. |\n| `language=\"pwsh\"` | `pwsh` (PowerShell 7+) | Optional external | Required on Linux, macOS, FreeBSD (or Windows for PS7+) when using `language=\"pwsh\"`. |\n\n---\n\n### 10.1 Installing `dotnet-script` via Command Line (for `\u003cscript language=\"csx\"\u003e`)\n\n`dotnet-script` is distributed as a cross-platform **.NET Global Tool** and works on Windows, Linux, macOS, and FreeBSD wherever the .NET SDK (`dotnet` 10.0+) is installed.\n\n#### Step 1: Install the .NET SDK (if not already installed)\n\n```powershell\n# Windows (via winget)\nwinget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.10\n```\n\n```bash\n# Ubuntu / Debian Linux\nsudo apt-get update \u0026\u0026 sudo apt-get install -y dotnet-sdk-10.0\n\n# RHEL / Rocky / AlmaLinux / Fedora\nsudo dnf install -y dotnet-sdk-10.0\n\n# macOS (via Homebrew)\nbrew install --cask dotnet-sdk\n\n# FreeBSD (via pkg)\nsudo pkg install -y dotnet\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Install `dotnet-script` Globally via `dotnet tool`\n\n```bash\n# Install the latest stable dotnet-script global tool\ndotnet tool install -g dotnet-script\n\n# (Optional) Update an existing installation to the latest version\ndotnet tool update -g dotnet-script\n\n# (Optional) Install a specific pinned version for reproducible CI environments\ndotnet tool install -g dotnet-script --version 1.6.0\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Ensure `~/.dotnet/tools` Is in `PATH` and Verify\n\n```powershell\n# Windows (PowerShell) — automatically added by the SDK installer, or add for current session:\n$env:PATH += \";$HOME\\.dotnet\\tools\"\ndotnet-script --version\ndotnet-script eval \"Console.WriteLine(21 * 2);\"\n```\n\n```bash\n# Linux / macOS / FreeBSD (Bash / Zsh / Sh)\nexport PATH=\"$PATH:$HOME/.dotnet/tools\"\n# Persist in ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, or ~/.profile:\necho \u0027export PATH=\"$PATH:$HOME/.dotnet/tools\"\u0027 \u003e\u003e ~/.profile\n\n# Verify dotnet-script execution\ndotnet-script --version\ndotnet-script eval \u0027Console.WriteLine(\"dotnet-script ready on \" + Environment.OSVersion);\u0027\n```\n\n---\n\n### 10.2 Installing PowerShell (`pwsh`) on Linux, macOS \u0026 FreeBSD (for `\u003cscript language=\"pwsh\"\u003e`)\n\nWhen a pipeline step uses `\u003cscript language=\"pwsh\"\u003e`, Flow invokes the cross-platform `pwsh` executable.\n\n#### A. Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, RHEL, Alpine)\n\n```bash\n# Ubuntu / Debian (via Microsoft Package Repository)\nsudo apt-get update \u0026\u0026 sudo apt-get install -y wget apt-transport-https software-properties-common\nsource /etc/os-release\nwget -q \"https://packages.microsoft.com/config/${ID}/${VERSION_ID}/packages-microsoft-prod.deb\"\nsudo dpkg -i packages-microsoft-prod.deb \u0026\u0026 rm packages-microsoft-prod.deb\nsudo apt-get update \u0026\u0026 sudo apt-get install -y powershell\n\n# RHEL 8/9, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, Fedora (via dnf)\nsudo dnf install -y https://packages.microsoft.com/config/rhel/9/packages-microsoft-prod.rpm\nsudo dnf install -y powershell\n\n# Universal Linux Alternative (via Snap)\nsudo snap install powershell --classic\n```\n\n#### B. macOS (Intel \u0026 Apple Silicon)\n\n```bash\n# Install PowerShell 7+ via Homebrew Cask\nbrew install --cask powershell\n\n# Verify installation\npwsh -Version\n```\n\n#### C. FreeBSD (and Any OS with the .NET SDK Installed)\n\nOn FreeBSD (as well as minimal Linux containers or non-root environments), PowerShell can be installed directly as a **.NET Global Tool** using the `dotnet` CLI:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Install the .NET SDK on FreeBSD (if not already present)\nsudo pkg install -y dotnet\n\n# 2. Install PowerShell (pwsh) as a .NET Global Tool\ndotnet tool install --global PowerShell\n\n# 3. Ensure ~/.dotnet/tools is on PATH\nexport PATH=\"$PATH:$HOME/.dotnet/tools\"\n\n# 4. Verify pwsh in non-interactive mode\npwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command \u0027$PSVersionTable.PSVersion\u0027\n```\n\n---\n\n## Related Documentation\n- [**Polyglot Scripting (`\u003cscript\u003e`)**](../script.md)\n- [**Complete CLI Reference (`flow`, `crypto_tool`, `db_importer`)**](COMPLETE_CLI_OPTIONS_AND_ARGUMENTS.md)\n- [**Configuration \u0026 Execution Hierarchy**](CONFIG_HIERARCHY.md)\n- [**Security Order of Operations \u0026 Options**](SECURITY_ORDER_AND_OPTIONS.md)\n- [**Database-Backed XML Storage (`db_importer`)**](README_DATABASE_XML.md)\n","guides/COMPLETE_CLI_OPTIONS_AND_ARGUMENTS.md":"# Command-Line Arguments Reference: `flow.exe`, `crypto_tool`, and `db_importer`\n\nThis document provides a comprehensive command-line reference for all three executable utilities in the **ETL Madness Flow** ecosystem:\n\n1. [`flow.exe`](#1-flowexe-core-pipeline-engine--builder-server): The primary pipeline execution engine, preflight validator, XML/AST schema verifier, visual builder server, and database import/export manager.\n2. [`crypto_tool`](#2-crypto_tool-cryptographic-utility): Standalone utility for AES-256-GCM encryption/decryption, digital signature signing/verification (OpenSSL, OpenPGP, PKCS#7/CMS), and cryptographic keypair generation.\n3. [`db_importer`](#3-db_importer-mssql-database-xml-importer--exporter): Dedicated CLI utility to import XML pipelines, options, and configs into SQL Server repositories (or export to file/stdout), featuring automatic UTF-8/UTF-16 encoding handling and at-rest encryption.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture \u0026 Shared Standards\n\n### Ecosystem Overview\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph SecurityLayer[\"1. Cryptography \u0026 Packaging\"]\n        CT[\"crypto_tool\"]\n        CT --\u003e|\"Encrypt (AES-256-GCM)\"| ENC[\"Encrypted XML\u003cbr\u003e(FLOWENC:v1:...)\"]\n        CT --\u003e|\"Sign (OpenSSL / PGP / PKCS#7)\"| SIG[\"Signed Package\u003cbr\u003e(FLOWSIGNED:v1:... or .sig)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph StorageLayer[\"2. Repository Storage\"]\n        DBI[\"db_importer\"]\n        DB[\"SQL Server / Database\u003cbr\u003e(dbo.flow_*_content)\"]\n        ENC --\u003e DBI\n        SIG --\u003e DBI\n        DBI --\u003e|\"MERGE / Upsert\"| DB\n    end\n\n    subgraph RuntimeLayer[\"3. Execution Engine\"]\n        FLOW[\"flow.exe\"]\n        DB --\u003e|\"sql:// or db:// URI\"| FLOW\n        ENC --\u003e|\"Local file / HTTP\"| FLOW\n        SIG --\u003e|\"Local file / HTTP\"| FLOW\n        FLOW --\u003e|\"Telemetry \u0026 Events\"| SINK[\"Audit Tables / DB Sink\"]\n    end\n```\n\n### Shared Secret Resolution Precedence\n\nWhen encryption or decryption operations are performed, all three utilities resolve passphrases and cryptographic keys using the identical precedence hierarchy:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    P1[\"1. Key File (-key-file \u0026lt;path\u0026gt;)\u003cbr\u003eSafest: reads key directly from protected file\"] --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P2[\"2. Command-Line Flag (-secure-key or -key)\u003cbr\u003eEmits warning: exposed in system process listings\"]\n    P2 --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P3[\"3. XML Options Profile (\u0026lt;secure-key\u0026gt;)\u003cbr\u003e(flow.exe only via -options profile)\"]\n    P3 --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P4[\"4. FLOW_SECURE_KEY\u003cbr\u003eEnvironment variable\"]\n    P4 --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P5[\"5. SECURE_KEY\u003cbr\u003eFallback generic environment variable\"]\n\n    classDef safe fill:#e6fffa,stroke:#047857,stroke-width:2px;\n    classDef warn fill:#fffbeb,stroke:#b45309,stroke-width:2px;\n    classDef env fill:#f0f9ff,stroke:#0284c7,stroke-width:2px;\n    class P1 safe;\n    class P2 warn;\n    class P3,P4,P5 env;\n```\n\n\u003e [!WARNING]\n\u003e **Process Listing Security Warning**: Passing encryption keys or passphrases directly on the command line via `-secure-key` exposes secrets to other users and processes on the system (e.g., via `ps aux`, `Get-Process`, or Windows Task Manager). In production, automated schedulers, and CI/CD pipelines, always pass keys using `-key-file` or the `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` environment variable.\n\n### Supported Resource URIs\n\nArguments in `flow.exe` that load resources (`-file`, `-options`, `-config`, `-import-file`) support uniform resource resolution:\n\n| Resource Type | Format | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Local File** | Standard relative or absolute path | `-file \".\\pipelines\\finance.xml\"` |\n| **HTTP / HTTPS** | `http://...` or `https://...` | `-file \"https://internal.repo/pipelines/etl.xml\"` |\n| **HTTP Basic Auth** | `https://Basic%20\u003cbase64\u003e@host/...` | `-file \"https://Basic%20dXNlcjpwYXNz@internal.repo/etl.xml\"` |\n| **SQL Server Query** | `sql://sqlserver@\u003cdsn\u003e#\u003cSELECT_QUERY\u003e` | `-options \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=ETL#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name=\u0027PROD\u0027\"` |\n| **Generic DB Alias** | `db://\u003cdriver\u003e@\u003cdsn\u003e#\u003cSELECT_QUERY\u003e` | `-file \"db://postgres@app_user:pass@db:5432/app#SELECT pipeline_xml FROM flow_pipelines WHERE name=\u0027BILLING\u0027\"` |\n\n### Common Exit Codes\n\nAll three tools conform to standard operating system exit codes:\n\n* **`0`**: Success (pipeline ran successfully, schema/AST valid, verification passed, or import/export completed).\n* **`1`**: Failure (execution error, validation error, signature verification failed, decryption key missing/invalid, or database connection error).\n\n---\n\n## 1. `flow.exe`: Core Pipeline Engine \u0026 Builder Server\n\n`flow.exe` is the central executable. It executes ETL pipelines, runs preflight validations, starts an interactive HTMX web server for visual pipeline authoring, transforms pipeline definitions via XSLT into diagrams, and manages local or external repository persistence.\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe [options...]\n```\n\n### Full Flag Reference Table\n\n| Flag | Type | Default | Category | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `-file` | `string` | `scripts.xml` | Execution | Path or URI to XML file containing scripts, databases, and workflow nodes. Supports local files, HTTP/HTTPS URLs, and `sql://` / `db://` database queries. |\n| `-options` | `string` | `\"\"` | Execution | Path or URI to an XML options defaults file (`\u003cflow_cli_options\u003e\u003coptions\u003e...`). Values in this file set flag defaults without overriding flags explicitly passed on the CLI. |\n| `-config` | `string` | `\"\"` | Execution | Optional path or URI to a `CONFIG.xml` file containing variable and database connection overrides. Merged hierarchically with the base file. |\n| `-format` | `string` | `csv` | Execution | Output formatting applied to execution results. Supported: `csv`, `text`, `json`, `jsonpretty` (or `prettyjson`), `markdown` (or `md`, `table`), and `stream`. |\n| `-vars` | `string` | `\"\"` | Execution | Comma-separated `key=value` runtime variable overrides (e.g., `-vars \"TargetTable=tbl_prod,Threshold=500\"`). Recursively expands `{{var}}` placeholders up to 3 levels deep. |\n| `-validate` | `bool` | `false` | Validation | Validates XML schema (XSD if specified) and AST semantic structure without executing any pipeline nodes. Exits with code `0` on success. |\n| `-preflight` | `bool` | `false` | Validation | Executes preflight validation nodes only (`\u003cpreflight\u003e` section), skipping the main pipeline `\u003cflow\u003e` nodes. |\n| `-xsd` | `string` | `\"\"` | Validation | Path to an XML Schema Definition (`.xsd`) file used to validate the pipeline XML via `xmllint` prior to parsing. |\n| `-node-output` | `bool` | `false` | Diagnostic | When logging to a database (`log_db`), persists each node\u0027s execution output (`ResultsString` with credential redaction) to the `node_output` column on `node.finished` rows in `pipeline_events`. Off by default. |\n| `-debug` | `bool` | `false` | Diagnostic | Enables verbose console logging, including connection string resolution, driver dialect detection, digital signature verification metadata, and sink telemetry. |\n| `-gopath` | `string` | `$GOPATH` | Runtime | GOPATH directory for dynamic package imports used by the embedded Yaegi Go interpreter engine. Defaults to the host `GOPATH` environment variable. |\n| `-xslt` | `string` | `\"\"` | Visualization | Path to custom XSLT stylesheet. Transforms pipeline XML while injecting an auto-generated Mermaid workflow diagram. Exits immediately after transformation. |\n| `-out` | `string` | `\"\"` | Visualization | Output file path for XSLT-transformed XML produced by `-xslt`. |\n| `-encrypted` | `bool` | `false` | Security | Indicates that input files/database resources are AES-256-GCM encrypted and must be decrypted prior to parsing, or instructs `-export-file` / `-import-file` to encrypt. |\n| `-secure-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Symmetric encryption or decryption key/passphrase. Emits a security warning to `stderr`; falls back to `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` or `SECURE_KEY`. |\n| `-key-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Path to file containing symmetric key/passphrase. Preferred over `-secure-key` to prevent exposing secrets in system process tables. |\n| `-verify-signature`| `bool` | `false` | Security | Enforces digital signature verification before decrypting or executing the pipeline. |\n| `-public-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Path to public key file (PEM format; RSA, ECDSA, Ed25519) for digital signature verification. |\n| `-cert` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Path to X.509 certificate file (PEM or DER format) for signature verification (PKCS#7/CMS or X.509 RSA/ECDSA). |\n| `-ca-cert` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Path to root CA certificate file for signature verification certificate chain validation. |\n| `-keyring` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Path to OpenPGP armored public keyring file for GPG signature verification. |\n| `-signature` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Path to detached digital signature file (`.sig`, `.asc`, `.p7s`). When omitted, companion files with matching basenames are automatically checked. |\n| `-builder` | `bool` | `false` | Builder UI | Starts the interactive local HTMX visual pipeline builder web server and opens the UI. |\n| `-builder-port` | `int` | `0` | Builder UI | Port for the visual builder web server. Default `0` chooses an ephemeral dynamic port. Can be set via `FLOW_BUILDER_PORT` or `PORT` environment variables. |\n| `-builder-db` | `string` | `flow_builder.db` | Builder UI | SQLite database file path used for visual builder local project storage. |\n| `-purge-db` | `bool` | `false` | Builder UI | Purges all pipeline data from the SQLite visual builder database, re-initializes defaults, and exits. |\n| `-import-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Repository | Path to a pipeline, options, or config XML file to import into the SQLite builder database or an external database specified by `-dsn`. |\n| `-import-name` | `string` | `\"\"` | Repository | Custom record name for item imported with `-import-file`. Defaults to the source file basename without extension. |\n| `-import-type` | `string` | `pipeline` | Repository | Item type for `-import-file`: `pipeline`, `options`, or `config`. |\n| `-export-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Repository | Destination file path to export a pipeline, options, or config file (or `-` for standard output `stdout`). |\n| `-export-name` | `string` | `\"\"` | Repository | Name of the pipeline, options, or config to export from the database (required when `-export-file` is specified). |\n| `-export-type` | `string` | `pipeline` | Repository | Item type for `-export-file`: `pipeline`, `options`, or `config`. |\n| `-dsn` | `string` | `\"\"` | Repository | External database DSN for direct database-to-database repository import and export (supports SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQLite). |\n\n### Output Formats (`-format`)\n\n| Format | Output Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `csv` *(Default)* | Standard comma-separated table displaying `ScriptID`, `ReturnCode`, `Duration`, and `ResultsString`. |\n| `text` | Human-readable plain text tabular format. |\n| `json` | Compact JSON array containing all `ScriptResult` objects. |\n| `jsonpretty` / `prettyjson` | Formatted multi-line JSON with 4-space indentation. |\n| `markdown` / `md` / `table` | GitHub Flavored Markdown table. |\n| `stream` | Real-time console event streaming mode (`ExecuteRun` engine) with live node transitions, parent/child execution relationships, and audit sink telemetry. |\n\n### Environment Variables Recognized by `flow.exe`\n\n* `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`: Primary symmetric passphrase used to decrypt encrypted scripts, options, and configs.\n* `SECURE_KEY`: Secondary fallback passphrase if `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` is unset.\n* `FLOW_BUILDER_PORT`: Default port for the HTMX builder server when `-builder-port` is not explicitly passed.\n* `PORT`: Generic fallback port for web services (e.g., in cloud environments like Cloud Run or Heroku).\n* `GOPATH`: Go workspace directory used by Yaegi to import third-party packages in interpreted script blocks.\n* `USER` / `USERNAME`: Captured for execution audit logs in `dbo.pipeline_runs`.\n* `HOSTNAME` / `COMPUTERNAME`: Captured for execution audit logs in `dbo.pipeline_runs`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `crypto_tool`: Cryptographic Utility\n\n`crypto_tool` is a self-contained, zero-dependency command-line utility for encryption, decryption, digital signing, signature verification, and keypair generation.\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```bash\ncrypto_tool [options...]\n```\n\n### Full Flag Reference Table\n\n| Flag | Alias | Type | Default | Category | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `-action` | — | `string` | `encrypt` | Operation | Cryptographic operation to perform: `encrypt`, `decrypt`, `gen-key`, `sign`, `verify`, or `gen-keypair`. |\n| `-gen-key` | — | `bool` | `false` | Operation | Shortcut flag for `-action gen-key`. Generates a 256-bit cryptographically secure symmetric key. |\n| `-in` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | I/O | Input file path (use `\"-\"` or leave empty for standard input `stdin`). |\n| `-out` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | I/O | Output file path (use `\"-\"` or leave empty for standard output `stdout`). Output files containing secret keys are written with `0600` permissions. |\n| `-secure-key` | `-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Symmetric | Passphrase or raw key for AES-256-GCM encryption or decryption. Emits a security warning to `stderr`; falls back to `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` or `SECURE_KEY`. |\n| `-key-file` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | Symmetric | Path to file containing encryption/decryption key/passphrase. Prevents exposing secrets in operating system process listings. |\n| `-binary` | — | `bool` | `false` | Symmetric / Sig | Output raw binary ciphertext or ASN.1 signature bytes instead of the default text-safe armored envelope (`FLOWENC:v1:...` or `FLOWSIG:v1:...`). |\n| `-sig-type` | — | `string` | `openssl` | Signing | Digital signature format for `-action sign`: `openssl` (RSA/ECDSA/Ed25519), `pgp` (OpenPGP), or `pkcs7` (CMS / PKCS#7 / Windows Authenticode). |\n| `-private-key` | `-priv` | `string` | `\"\"` | Signing | Private key file path (PEM or OpenPGP format) used to generate digital signatures. |\n| `-passphrase` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | Signing | Passphrase for decrypting password-protected private keys (PKCS#1, PKCS#8, or OpenPGP). |\n| `-cert` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | Sign / Verify | X.509 certificate file path (PEM or DER format). Required when creating PKCS#7 signatures (`-sig-type pkcs7`); used for cert-based verification. |\n| `-wrap` | — | `bool` | `false` | Signing | Wraps both the digital signature and input content into a single unified `FLOWSIGNED:v1:...` envelope. Eliminates the need for detached `.sig` files. |\n| `-signature` | `-sig` | `string` | `\"\"` | Verification | Detached signature file path for `-action verify`. If omitted, automatically checks for companion files `\u003cin\u003e.sig`, `\u003cin\u003e.asc`, or `\u003cin\u003e.p7s`. |\n| `-public-key` | `-pub` | `string` | `\"\"` | Verification | Public key file path (PEM or OpenPGP format) for signature verification. |\n| `-ca-cert` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | Verification | Root or intermediate CA certificate file path used to validate certificate chains during PKCS#7 or X.509 verification. |\n| `-keyring` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | Verification | OpenPGP armored public keyring file path for OpenPGP/GPG signature verification. |\n| `-keypair-type` | — | `string` | `rsa` | Keygen | Asymmetric keypair type for `-action gen-keypair`: `rsa`, `ecdsa` (P-256), `ed25519`, or `pgp` (aliases: `gpg`, `openpgp`). |\n| `-bits` | — | `int` | `2048` | Keygen | Bit length for RSA keypair generation (e.g., `2048`, `3072`, `4096`). |\n| `-out-priv` | — | `string` | `private_key.pem` | Keygen | Output file path for generated private key. File permissions are automatically restricted to `0600` (owner read/write only). |\n| `-out-pub` | — | `string` | `public_key.pem` | Keygen | Output file path for generated public key (written with `0644` permissions). |\n| `-out-cert` | — | `string` | `\"\"` | Keygen | Optional output path to generate a self-signed X.509 certificate during keypair generation (valid for 365 days). |\n| `-cn` | — | `string` | `Flow Code Signer` | Keygen | Common Name (`CN`) embedded in the generated X.509 certificate or OpenPGP user identity. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. `db_importer`: MSSQL Database XML Importer \u0026 Exporter\n\n`db_importer` is a dedicated utility for migrating pipelines, options profiles, and variable configs into and out of relational database storage (SQL Server `dbo.flow_*_content` tables). It includes automatic UTF-8 and UTF-16 character encoding translation and supports transparent at-rest encryption.\n\n### Command Syntax\n\n```powershell\n.\\db_importer.exe [options...]\n```\n\n### Full Flag Reference Table\n\n| Flag | Type | Default | Category | Description |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `-action` | `string` | `import` | Operation | Database operation to execute: `import` (upserts record into table) or `export` (extracts record from table). |\n| `-export` | `bool` | `false` | Operation | Shortcut boolean flag to perform an export operation (equivalent to `-action export`). |\n| `-dsn` | `string` | `sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true` | Connection | Connection string for SQL Server instance (URL format or ADO/ODBC connection string). |\n| `-file` | `string` | `github_ai_credit_usage.xml` | I/O | Path to input XML file to import, or destination path for exported XML. Use `\"-\"` or leave empty to read from `stdin` (import) or write to `stdout` (export). |\n| `-name` | `string` | `github_ai_credit_usage` | Target | Unique logical name key of the record in the database (`Name` column in target table). Used for upsert matching and queries. |\n| `-table` | `string` | `pipeline` | Target | Target table and column mapping: `pipeline`, `options`, or `config`. See [Database Mapping](#target-table-mappings) below. |\n| `-desc` | `string` | `Imported via Go importer` | Metadata | Descriptive text stored in the `Description` column of the table (import only). |\n| `-encrypted` | `bool` | `false` | Security | When importing: encrypts content into an armored `FLOWENC:v1:...` envelope before inserting into the database.\u003cbr\u003eWhen exporting: decrypts encrypted database content before writing to file or stdout. |\n| `-secure-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Passphrase or key for AES-256-GCM encryption/decryption. Emits a security warning to `stderr`; falls back to `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` or `SECURE_KEY`. |\n| `-key-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Security | Path to file containing the encryption key/passphrase. Preferred over `-secure-key` to avoid exposing secrets in process tables. |\n\n### Target Table Mappings\n\n| `-table` Value | Database Table Target | XML Payload Column | Record Identifier Column |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `pipeline` *(Default)* | `dbo.flow_pipeline_content` | `PipelineXML` (`NVARCHAR(MAX)`) | `Name` (`VARCHAR(120)`) |\n| `options` | `dbo.flow_options_content` | `OptionsXML` (`NVARCHAR(MAX)`) | `Name` (`VARCHAR(120)`) |\n| `config` | `dbo.flow_config_content` | `ConfigXML` (`NVARCHAR(MAX)`) | `Name` (`VARCHAR(120)`) |\n\n### Encoding \u0026 Security Features\n\n* **BOM \u0026 UTF-16 Auto-Detection**: Automatically detects and decodes UTF-8 BOM (`0xEF 0xBB 0xBF`), UTF-16 Little Endian BOM (`0xFF 0xFE`), and UTF-16 Big Endian BOM (`0xFE 0xFF`). Files generated by PowerShell or Windows editors are cleanly ingested without manual conversion.\n* **Idempotent Upsert (MERGE)**: Imports use an atomic `MERGE INTO` statement on SQL Server. If the record name already exists, its `Description` and XML payload are updated in place; otherwise, a new record is inserted.\n* **Hardened File Permissions**: Exported decrypted files written to disk are automatically locked down with `0600` permissions (owner read/write only).\n\n---\n\n## Practical Usage Examples\n\n### 1. `flow.exe` Workflows\n\n#### Basic Pipeline Execution with Markdown Output\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe -file \".\\pipelines\\nightly_billing.xml\" -format markdown\n```\n\n#### Passing Runtime Overrides (`-vars` and `-config`)\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe `\n  -file \".\\pipelines\\etl.xml\" `\n  -config \".\\configs\\production.xml\" `\n  -vars \"BatchDate=2026-10-10,MaxRetries=5,TargetSchema=stage\" `\n  -debug\n```\n\n#### Preflight Checks \u0026 Schema Validation Only\n```powershell\n# Validate XSD schema and AST without executing\n.\\flow.exe -file \".\\pipelines\\etl.xml\" -xsd \".\\xsd\\pipeline.xsd\" -validate\n\n# Execute only the preflight nodes (smoke tests, DB pings)\n.\\flow.exe -file \".\\pipelines\\etl.xml\" -preflight\n```\n\n#### Running Directly from a SQL Database Query\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe `\n  -file \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=ETL\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name=\u0027BILLING_JOB\u0027\" `\n  -options \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=ETL\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name=\u0027PROD_OPTIONS\u0027\" `\n  -key-file \"C:\\secrets\\flow_master.key\"\n```\n\n#### Starting the HTMX Visual Builder\n```powershell\n# Launch visual builder on a specific port with custom SQLite storage\n.\\flow.exe -builder -builder-port 8080 -builder-db \"C:\\flow\\builder_workspace.db\"\n\n# Reset / purge the builder SQLite database\n.\\flow.exe -purge-db -builder-db \"C:\\flow\\builder_workspace.db\"\n```\n\n#### Generating a Mermaid Diagram via XSLT\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe -file \".\\pipelines\\order_processing.xml\" -xslt \".\\transforms\\mermaid.xslt\" -out \".\\docs\\diagram.xml\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. `crypto_tool` Workflows\n\n#### Symmetric Encryption \u0026 Key Generation\n```bash\n# 1. Generate a cryptographically secure 256-bit AES key\ncrypto_tool -gen-key -out master.key\n\n# 2. Encrypt an XML pipeline (outputs text-armored FLOWENC:v1:... file)\ncrypto_tool -action encrypt -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.enc -key-file master.key\n\n# 3. Decrypt the file\ncrypto_tool -action decrypt -in pipeline.xml.enc -out pipeline.xml -key-file master.key\n```\n\n#### Generating Asymmetric Keypairs \u0026 Certificates\n```bash\n# RSA Keypair + Self-Signed X.509 Certificate\ncrypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type rsa -bits 2048 \\\n  -out-priv release_priv.pem -out-pub release_pub.pem -out-cert release_cert.pem \\\n  -cn \"Production Code Signer\"\n\n# ECDSA (P-256) Keypair\ncrypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type ecdsa -out-priv ec_priv.pem -out-pub ec_pub.pem\n\n# OpenPGP / GPG Armored Keypair\ncrypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type pgp -out-priv gpg_priv.asc -out-pub gpg_pub.asc -cn \"ETL Admin\"\n```\n\n#### Signing Files\n```bash\n# OpenSSL Detached Signature (.sig)\ncrypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.sig -private-key release_priv.pem -sig-type openssl\n\n# PKCS#7 / CMS Detached Signature (.p7s)\ncrypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.p7s -private-key release_priv.pem -cert release_cert.pem -sig-type pkcs7\n\n# Self-Contained Signed Package (FLOWSIGNED:v1:... envelope)\ncrypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.signed -private-key release_priv.pem -wrap\n```\n\n#### Verifying Signatures\n```bash\n# Verify detached signature\ncrypto_tool -action verify -in pipeline.xml -signature pipeline.xml.sig -public-key release_pub.pem\n\n# Verify self-contained unified envelope and extract payload\ncrypto_tool -action verify -in pipeline.xml.signed -public-key release_pub.pem -out verified_pipeline.xml\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. `db_importer` Workflows\n\n#### Importing XML Pipelines into SQL Server\n```powershell\n# Plain XML import\n.\\db_importer.exe `\n  -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=ETL\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\" `\n  -table pipeline `\n  -name \"github_billing\" `\n  -file \".\\pipelines\\github_billing.xml\" `\n  -desc \"Production GitHub Billing Workflow\"\n\n# Encrypted import (stored encrypted at rest in SQL Server)\n.\\db_importer.exe `\n  -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=ETL\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\" `\n  -table options `\n  -name \"OPTIONS_FINANCE\" `\n  -file \".\\options\\finance_options.xml\" `\n  -encrypted `\n  -key-file \"C:\\secrets\\flow_master.key\"\n```\n\n#### Exporting from SQL Server to File or Stdout\n```powershell\n# Export and automatically decrypt to local file with 0600 permissions\n.\\db_importer.exe `\n  -action export `\n  -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=ETL\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\" `\n  -table pipeline `\n  -name \"github_billing\" `\n  -file \".\\exported_pipeline.xml\" `\n  -encrypted `\n  -key-file \"C:\\secrets\\flow_master.key\"\n\n# Pipe exported XML directly to stdout\n.\\db_importer.exe -export -table pipeline -name \"github_billing\" -file - | xmllint --format -\n```\n\n---\n\n## End-to-End Enterprise Recipes\n\n### Recipe 1: Production CI/CD Release (Encrypt-then-Sign)\n\nSecure pipeline distribution workflow where untrusted files are cryptographically verified before decryption keys are ever accessed:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Developer as Build / CI Pipeline\n    participant Crypto as crypto_tool\n    participant Importer as db_importer\n    participant SQL as SQL Server Repo\n    participant Engine as flow.exe (Prod Host)\n\n    Developer-\u003e\u003eCrypto: crypto_tool -action encrypt -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.enc -key-file secret.key\n    Developer-\u003e\u003eCrypto: crypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml.enc -out pipeline.xml.signed -private-key priv.pem -wrap\n    Developer-\u003e\u003eImporter: db_importer -table pipeline -name \"DAILY_JOB\" -file pipeline.xml.signed\n    Importer-\u003e\u003eSQL: MERGE INTO dbo.flow_pipeline_content\n    Engine-\u003e\u003eSQL: Query via sql:// URI\n    Engine-\u003e\u003eEngine: 1. Verify Digital Signature (release_pub.pem)\n    Engine-\u003e\u003eEngine: 2. Decrypt AES-256-GCM (FLOW_SECURE_KEY)\n    Engine-\u003e\u003eEngine: 3. Parse AST \u0026 Execute Workflow\n```\n\n**Step-by-step commands:**\n\n```bash\n# 1. Encrypt the pipeline definition\ncrypto_tool -action encrypt -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.enc -key-file prod.key\n\n# 2. Sign the encrypted package into a unified FLOWSIGNED envelope\ncrypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml.enc -out pipeline.xml.signed -private-key release_priv.pem -wrap\n\n# 3. Import the signed package into SQL Server repository\ndb_importer -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password@db:1433?database=ETL\" -table pipeline -name \"NIGHTLY_SYNC\" -file pipeline.xml.signed\n\n# 4. Execute on production server (verifies signature first, then decrypts)\nexport FLOW_SECURE_KEY=\"YourSecurePassphrase\"\nflow.exe \\\n  -file \"sql://sqlserver@db:1433?database=ETL#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name=\u0027NIGHTLY_SYNC\u0027\" \\\n  -public-key \"/etc/flow/release_pub.pem\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Recipe 2: Options Profile Overrides (`-options`)\n\nYou can create an XML options file conforming to `xsd/options.xsd` to pre-configure defaults for production hosts:\n\n```xml\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\n\u003cflow_cli_options\u003e\n    \u003coptions\u003e\n        \u003cformat\u003emarkdown\u003c/format\u003e\n        \u003cdebug\u003etrue\u003c/debug\u003e\n        \u003cverify-signature\u003etrue\u003c/verify-signature\u003e\n        \u003cpublic-key\u003eC:\\flow\\keys\\release_pub.pem\u003c/public-key\u003e\n        \u003cencrypted\u003etrue\u003c/encrypted\u003e\n    \u003c/options\u003e\n\u003c/flow_cli_options\u003e\n```\n\nExecute `flow.exe` referencing the options file:\n\n```powershell\n.\\flow.exe -options \"C:\\flow\\prod_options.xml\" -file \"C:\\flow\\pipelines\\job.xml\" -key-file \"C:\\secrets\\key.txt\"\n```\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e Any flag provided explicitly on the command line (e.g., `-format json`) will override the value defined inside `-options`.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Flag Cross-Reference Matrix\n\n| Capability | `flow.exe` | `crypto_tool` | `db_importer` |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| Specify Input File | `-file \u003cpath\\|uri\u003e` | `-in \u003cpath\u003e` | `-file \u003cpath\u003e` |\n| Specify Output File | `-out \u003cpath\u003e` *(XSLT)* | `-out \u003cpath\u003e` | `-file \u003cpath\u003e` *(Export)* |\n| Use Stdin / Stdout | Via `-export-file -` | `-in -` / `-out -` | `-file -` |\n| Symmetric Key File | `-key-file \u003cpath\u003e` | `-key-file \u003cpath\u003e` | `-key-file \u003cpath\u003e` |\n| Symmetric Key Flag | `-secure-key \u003cstr\u003e` | `-secure-key` / `-key` | `-secure-key \u003cstr\u003e` |\n| Env Var Fallback | `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`, `SECURE_KEY` | `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`, `SECURE_KEY` | `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`, `SECURE_KEY` |\n| Enable Encryption | `-encrypted` | `-action encrypt` | `-encrypted` |\n| Generate Symmetric Key | — | `-gen-key` | — |\n| Digital Signature File | `-signature \u003cpath\u003e` | `-signature` / `-sig` | — |\n| Digital Signing Key | — | `-private-key` / `-priv` | — |\n| Signature Verification Key | `-public-key \u003cpath\u003e` | `-public-key` / `-pub` | — |\n| X.509 Certificate | `-cert \u003cpath\u003e` | `-cert \u003cpath\u003e` | — |\n| Root CA Certificate | `-ca-cert \u003cpath\u003e` | `-ca-cert \u003cpath\u003e` | — |\n| OpenPGP Keyring | `-keyring \u003cpath\u003e` | `-keyring \u003cpath\u003e` | — |\n| Unified Signed Package | Transparently unwrapped | `-wrap` (create / extract) | Stored as text |\n| Target Database DSN | `-dsn \u003cstr\u003e` | — | `-dsn \u003cstr\u003e` |\n| Database Table Target | `-import-type` / `-export-type` | — | `-table \u003cpipeline\\|options\\|config\u003e` |\n| Visual Builder Server | `-builder`, `-builder-port` | — | — |\n","guides/CONFIG_HIERARCHY.md":"# Configuration \u0026 Execution Hierarchy\n\n`go-flow` uses a hierarchical configuration model to separate pipeline logic from environment-specific settings. This allows you to maintain a single pipeline definition while deploying it across different environments (Dev, Staging, Prod) simply by swapping configuration files or CLI arguments.\n\n## Configuration Hierarchy\n\nThe engine merges settings in a specific order of precedence. Later stages override earlier ones:\n\n**Base XML (`--file`)** $\\rightarrow$ **Options XML (`--options`)** $\\rightarrow$ **Config XML (`--config`)** $\\rightarrow$ **CLI Variables (`--vars`)**\n\n### Precedence Table\n\n| Level | Source | Purpose | Precedence | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Pipeline** | `--file` | Core logic, node definitions, default variables. | Lowest | `scripts.xml` |\n| **2. Options** | `--options` | Default CLI flag behavior (ports, formats). | Low | `defaults.xml` |\n| **3. Config** | `--config` | Env-specific overrides (DB strings, API keys). | High | `prod_env.xml` |\n| **4. Runtime** | `--vars` | Immediate, one-off overrides for a specific run. | Highest | `--vars \"Limit=10\"` |\n\n### Execution Flow Diagram\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Start Execution] --\u003e B{Load pipeline.xml}\n    B --\u003e C{Merge options.xml}\n    C --\u003e D{Merge config.xml}\n    D --\u003e E{Apply --vars CLI flags}\n    E --\u003e F[Final Resolved Context]\n    F --\u003e G[AST Validation]\n    G --\u003e H[Pipeline Execution]\n    \n    style A fill:#f9f,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px\n    style F fill:#bbf,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px\n    style H fill:#bfb,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px\n```\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Practical Examples\n\nAssume we have a core pipeline file `main.xml` that defines a variable `BatchSize` and a database connection `PrimaryDB`.\n\n### 1. Default Execution (Developer Mode)\nRuns using only the defaults defined in the script file.\n```bash\n./flow.exe --file main.xml\n```\n*   **BatchSize**: 500 (from `main.xml`)\n*   **DB**: `localhost` (from `main.xml`)\n\n### 2. Environment-Based Execution (Production)\nOverrides the base settings with a production configuration file.\n```bash\n./flow.exe --file main.xml --config prod_config.xml\n```\n*   **BatchSize**: 5000 (overridden by `prod_config.xml`)\n*   **DB**: `prod-sql-server.internal` (overridden by `prod_config.xml`)\n\n### 3. Ad-hoc Debugging Execution\nUses the production config but overrides a specific variable via CLI for a quick test.\n```bash\n./flow.exe --file main.xml --config prod_config.xml --vars \"BatchSize=10,DebugMode=true\"\n```\n*   **BatchSize**: 10 (overridden by `--vars`)\n*   **DB**: `prod-sql-server.internal` (from `prod_config.xml`)\n*   **DebugMode**: true (added via `--vars`)\n\n### 4. Standardized Runner (Using Options File)\nUses an options file to standardize the output format, port, and even the default config file used across all team members.\n\n**Example `corporate_defaults.xml`:**\n```xml\n\u003cflow_cli_options\u003e\n    \u003coption name=\"format\" value=\"jsonpretty\" /\u003e\n    \u003coption name=\"builder-port\" value=\"9000\" /\u003e\n    \u003coption name=\"config\" value=\"dev_config.xml\" /\u003e\n\u003c/flow_cli_options\u003e\n```\n\n**Execution:**\n```bash\n./flow.exe --file main.xml --options corporate_defaults.xml\n```\n*   **Format**: `jsonpretty` (from `corporate_defaults.xml`)\n*   **Port**: `9000` (from `corporate_defaults.xml`)\n*   **Config**: `dev_config.xml` (automatically loaded because it\u0027s specified in the options file)\n*   **DB**: `dev-db.local` (loaded via the config file specified in the options)\n\n**Override Example:**\nIf you want to use the corporate defaults but target Production instead:\n```bash\n./flow.exe --file main.xml --options corporate_defaults.xml --config prod_config.xml\n```\n*   **Config**: `prod_config.xml` (CLI flag `--config` overrides the value inside `corporate_defaults.xml`)\n\n\n---\n\n## Summary of File Roles\n\n- **`scripts.xml`**: The \"Source Code\". Defines *what* happens.\n- **`options.xml`**: The \"Tool Settings\". Defines *how* the CLI behaves.\n- **`config.xml`**: The \"Environment\". Defines *where* the data is.\n- **`--vars`**: The \"Tweak\". Defines *specifics* for this exact run.\n","guides/DATABASE_LOGGING_CONFIGURATION.md":"# Database Logging Setup for Pipeline Runs and Events\r\n\r\nIf package has a database configured with the name `log_db`, logging to that database will be automatically set up using the schemas defined below.\r\n\r\n## Example Usage\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase \r\n            name=\"log_db\" \r\n            driver=\"sqlserver\" \r\n            connection_string=\"sqlserver://sa:StrongPassword123!@localhost:1433?database=pipeline_telemetry\u0026amp;encrypt=disable\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Pipeline scripts --\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n## PostgreSQL Schema\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS pipeline_runs (\r\n    run_id VARCHAR(64) PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    file_path VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\r\n    config_path VARCHAR(255),\r\n    status VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\r\n    started_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\r\n    finished_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\r\n    duration_ms BIGINT NOT NULL,\r\n    task_count INT NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    os_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    db_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    spid BIGINT,\r\n    application_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    hostname VARCHAR(256),\r\n    options_path VARCHAR(2048),\r\n    error_class VARCHAR(128),\r\n    error_message TEXT,\r\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS pipeline_events (\r\n    id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    run_id VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    execution_id VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    sequence_num INT NOT NULL,\r\n    occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\r\n    event_type VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    node_kind VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    node_id VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,\r\n    status VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    os_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    db_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    spid VARCHAR(256),\r\n    application_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    hostname VARCHAR(256),\r\n    options_path VARCHAR(2048),\r\n    error_message TEXT,\r\n    rows_read BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_written BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_affected BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n    node_output TEXT\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n## MySQL / MariaDB\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS pipeline_runs (\r\n    run_id VARCHAR(64) PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    file_path VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\r\n    config_path VARCHAR(255),\r\n    status VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\r\n    started_at DATETIME(3) NOT NULL,\r\n    finished_at DATETIME(3) NOT NULL,\r\n    duration_ms BIGINT NOT NULL,\r\n    task_count INT NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    os_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    db_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    spid BIGINT,\r\n    application_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    hostname VARCHAR(256),\r\n    options_path VARCHAR(2048),\r\n    error_class VARCHAR(128),\r\n    error_message TEXT,\r\n    created_at DATETIME(3) DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(3)\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS pipeline_events (\r\n    id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    run_id VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    execution_id VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    sequence_num INT NOT NULL,\r\n    occurred_at DATETIME(3) NOT NULL,\r\n    event_type VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    node_kind VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    node_id VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,\r\n    status VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    os_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    db_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    spid VARCHAR(256),\r\n    application_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n    hostname VARCHAR(256),\r\n    options_path VARCHAR(2048),\r\n    error_message TEXT,\r\n    rows_read BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_written BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_affected BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n    node_output TEXT\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n## SQLite Schema\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS pipeline_runs (\r\n    run_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    file_path TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    config_path TEXT,\r\n    status TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    started_at TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    finished_at TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    duration_ms INTEGER NOT NULL,\r\n    task_count INTEGER NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name TEXT,\r\n    os_user_name TEXT,\r\n    db_user_name TEXT,\r\n    spid INTEGER,\r\n    application_name TEXT,\r\n    hostname TEXT,\r\n    options_path TEXT,\r\n    error_class TEXT,\r\n    error_message TEXT,\r\n    created_at TEXT DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS pipeline_events (\r\n    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\r\n    run_id TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    execution_id TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    sequence_num INTEGER NOT NULL,\r\n    occurred_at TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    event_type TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    node_kind TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    node_id TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    status TEXT NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name TEXT,\r\n    os_user_name TEXT,\r\n    db_user_name TEXT,\r\n    spid TEXT,\r\n    application_name TEXT,\r\n    hostname TEXT,\r\n    options_path TEXT,\r\n    error_message TEXT,\r\n    rows_read INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_written INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_affected INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\r\n    node_output TEXT\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Microsoft SQL Server\r\n\r\n```sql\r\n-- Create pipeline_runs table\r\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT * FROM sys.objects WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID(N\u0027[dbo].[pipeline_runs]\u0027) AND type in (N\u0027U\u0027))\r\nBEGIN\r\n    CREATE TABLE dbo.pipeline_runs (\r\n        run_id VARCHAR(64) PRIMARY KEY,\r\n        file_path VARCHAR(4000) NOT NULL,\r\n        config_path VARCHAR(4000),\r\n        status VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\r\n        started_at DATETIMEOFFSET NOT NULL,\r\n        finished_at DATETIMEOFFSET NOT NULL,\r\n        duration_ms BIGINT NOT NULL,\r\n        task_count INT NOT NULL,\r\n        user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        os_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        db_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        spid BIGINT,\r\n        application_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        hostname VARCHAR(256),\r\n        options_path VARCHAR(2048),\r\n        error_class VARCHAR(128),\r\n        error_message NVARCHAR(MAX),\r\n        created_at DATETIMEOFFSET DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\r\n    );\r\nEND;\r\nGO\r\n\r\n-- Create pipeline_events table\r\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT * FROM sys.objects WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID(N\u0027[dbo].[pipeline_events]\u0027) AND type in (N\u0027U\u0027))\r\nBEGIN\r\n    CREATE TABLE dbo.pipeline_events (\r\n        id BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1) PRIMARY KEY,\r\n        run_id VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n        execution_id VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n        sequence_num INT NOT NULL,\r\n        occurred_at DATETIMEOFFSET NOT NULL,\r\n        event_type VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n        node_kind VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,\r\n        node_id VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,\r\n        status VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\r\n        user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        os_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        db_user_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        spid VARCHAR(256),\r\n        application_name VARCHAR(256),\r\n        hostname VARCHAR(256),\r\n        options_path VARCHAR(2048),\r\n        error_message NVARCHAR(MAX),\r\n        rows_read BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n        rows_written BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n        rows_affected BIGINT DEFAULT 0,\r\n        node_output NVARCHAR(MAX),\r\n        created_at DATETIMEOFFSET DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\r\n    );\r\nEND;\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Oracle\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE pipeline_runs (\r\n    run_id VARCHAR2(64) PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    file_path VARCHAR2(255) NOT NULL,\r\n    config_path VARCHAR2(255),\r\n    status VARCHAR2(32) NOT NULL,\r\n    started_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE NOT NULL,\r\n    finished_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE NOT NULL,\r\n    duration_ms NUMBER(19) NOT NULL,\r\n    task_count NUMBER(10) NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    os_user_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    db_user_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    spid NUMBER(19),\r\n    application_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    hostname VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    options_path VARCHAR2(2048),\r\n    error_class VARCHAR2(128),\r\n    error_message CLOB,\r\n    created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\r\n);\r\n\r\nCREATE TABLE pipeline_events (\r\n    id NUMBER GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    run_id VARCHAR2(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    execution_id VARCHAR2(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    sequence_num NUMBER(10) NOT NULL,\r\n    occurred_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE NOT NULL,\r\n    event_type VARCHAR2(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    node_kind VARCHAR2(64) NOT NULL,\r\n    node_id VARCHAR2(128) NOT NULL,\r\n    status VARCHAR2(32) NOT NULL,\r\n    user_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    os_user_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    db_user_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    spid VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    application_name VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    hostname VARCHAR2(256),\r\n    options_path VARCHAR2(2048),\r\n    error_message CLOB,\r\n    rows_read NUMBER(19) DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_written NUMBER(19) DEFAULT 0,\r\n    rows_affected NUMBER(19) DEFAULT 0,\r\n    node_output CLOB\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Node Output Logging (`node_output`)\r\n\r\nBy default, `go-flow` does **not** persist node execution output to the `pipeline_events` table (`-node-output` is `false`).\r\n\r\nWhen enabled via the `-node-output` command-line flag, `\u003cnode-output\u003etrue\u003c/node-output\u003e` in an options XML file, or the **Log Node Output to DB (`-node-output`)** checkbox in the Visual Builder Runner tab:\r\n- If `log_db` is configured and the `pipeline_events` table contains a `node_output` column, `go-flow` records each node\u0027s execution output (`ScriptResult.ResultsString`) into `pipeline_events.node_output` on `\u0027node.finished\u0027` event rows.\r\n- Sensitive key-value patterns (`password`, `pwd`, `token`, `secret`, `authorization`) are automatically redacted (`[REDACTED]`) before writing to the database.\r\n- Oversized node outputs are UTF-8 safely truncated at 64 KB (`65536` bytes, or the detected database column length if narrower) with a `\\n...[TRUNCATED]` suffix so database `UPDATE` statements and UI payloads never fail due to size.\r\n- Other lifecycle events (`run.started`, `node.started`, `run.finished`) leave `node_output` as `NULL`.\r\n\r\n## Session Identity and Auditing Columns\r\n\r\nWhen logging pipeline events or runs to a database, the telemetry subsystem records session-level audit columns:\r\n\r\n| Column | Description | Dialect Resolution |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `os_user_name` / `user_name` | OS user executing the process (`go-flow`) | `user.Current()` / Windows environment |\r\n| `db_user_name` | Database user identity authenticated to the database. In `pipeline_runs`, captures the logging database connection user. In `pipeline_events`, captures the individual step\u0027s database connection user (or `source_user-\u003etarget_user` for cross-database `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` transfers). | MSSQL: `SYSTEM_USER`\u003cbr\u003ePostgreSQL: `CURRENT_USER`\u003cbr\u003eMySQL: `CURRENT_USER()`\u003cbr\u003eOracle: `SYS_CONTEXT(\u0027USERENV\u0027, \u0027SESSION_USER\u0027)`\u003cbr\u003eSQLite: `os.Getpid()` / `\"sqlite\"` |\r\n| `spid` / `sspid` | Server Process ID (SPID) or database session ID. In `pipeline_runs`, captures the logging database session SPID. In `pipeline_events`, captures the individual step\u0027s database session SPID (or `source_spid-\u003etarget_spid` for cross-database `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` transfers). | MSSQL: `@@SPID`\u003cbr\u003ePostgreSQL: `pg_backend_pid()`\u003cbr\u003eMySQL: `CONNECTION_ID()`\u003cbr\u003eOracle: `SYS_CONTEXT(\u0027USERENV\u0027, \u0027SID\u0027)`\u003cbr\u003eSQLite: `os.Getpid()` |\r\n| `application_name` / `app_name` | Application name executing the pipeline or connected to the database session. In `pipeline_runs`, captures the logging database session application name. In `pipeline_events`, captures the individual step\u0027s database session application name (or `source_app-\u003etarget_app` for cross-database `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` transfers). | MSSQL: `ISNULL(APP_NAME(), \u0027\u0027)`\u003cbr\u003ePostgreSQL: `COALESCE(current_setting(\u0027application_name\u0027, true), \u0027\u0027)`\u003cbr\u003eMySQL: `COALESCE(@@session.program_name, \u0027\u0027)`\u003cbr\u003eOracle: `COALESCE(SYS_CONTEXT(\u0027USERENV\u0027, \u0027MODULE\u0027), SYS_CONTEXT(\u0027USERENV\u0027, \u0027CLIENT_PROGRAM_NAME\u0027), \u0027\u0027)`\u003cbr\u003eFallback/SQLite: Binary executable name (`flow`) |\r\n| `node_output` | Execution output (`ResultsString`) of a completed node, populated on `\u0027node.finished\u0027` events only when `-node-output` is enabled. | Populated when `-node-output` is set and `node_output` column exists on `pipeline_events` |\r\n\r\n\u003e **Backward Compatibility**: If an existing database table was created with earlier schemas that lack `os_user_name`, `db_user_name`, `spid`, `sspid`, `application_name`, or `node_output`, `go-flow` automatically detects available columns and performs inserts without error. If `pipeline_events.spid` retains a legacy numeric type (`BIGINT`), cross-database bulk transfers automatically fall back to the numeric `source_spid` so database inserts never fail. When the columns exist, they are automatically populated.\r\n","guides/DATABASE_TRANSACTIONS.md":"# Database Transactions in Flow Pipelines\r\n\r\nFlow supports wrapping sequential SQL script execution inside an atomic database transaction. This ensures that a group of SQL scripts either succeeds together or rolls back completely if an error occurs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Configuration Syntax\r\n\r\nTransactions are declared at the `\u003cgroup\u003e` node level using two attributes:\r\n*   `transaction=\"true\"`: Enables transaction wrapping for all SQL script nodes inside the group.\r\n*   `db=\"...\"` (or `database=\"...\"`): Specifies the target database handle to run the transaction on.\r\n\r\n### Basic Example\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"sales_db\" driver=\"postgres\" connection_string=\"postgresql://...\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003cgroup id=\"update_sales_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n            \u003csql id=\"deduct_inventory\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n                UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - 1 WHERE item_id = 42;\r\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n            \u003csql id=\"record_sale\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n                INSERT INTO sales (item_id, qty) VALUES (42, 1);\r\n            \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n        \u003c/group\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Dynamic Loops \u0026 Nested Transactions (`\u003cforeach\u003e`)\r\n\r\nIf a group with `transaction=\"true\"` is placed inside a `\u003cforeach\u003e` loop, Flow begins, executes, and commits/rolls back a new transaction **per loop iteration**. \r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Loop driver gets item IDs --\u003e\r\n        \u003cforeach id=\"process_items\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT item_id, price FROM active_promotions;\r\n            \r\n            \u003c!-- A transaction is started and finished for each promotion processed --\u003e\r\n            \u003cgroup id=\"apply_promo_txn\" transaction=\"true\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n                \u003csql id=\"update_price\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n                    UPDATE products SET price = {{price}} WHERE id = {{item_id}};\r\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n                \u003csql id=\"log_history\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n                    INSERT INTO pricing_log (product_id, new_price) VALUES ({{item_id}}, {{price}});\r\n                \u003c/sql\u003e\r\n            \u003c/group\u003e\r\n        \u003c/foreach\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Parallel Execution \u0026 Isolation (`\u003cparallel\u003e`)\r\n\r\nWhen running concurrent tasks in a `\u003cparallel\u003e` block:\r\n*   Flow isolates each parallel branch inside its own execution context with a unique cloned executor.\r\n*   If a `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` is placed inside a parallel branch, its active transaction is completely isolated to that branch\u0027s worker connection.\r\n*   This prevents race conditions or shared transaction states across parallel threads.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cpipeline xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003cparallel max_threads=\"2\"\u003e\r\n            \u003c!-- Branch 1: isolated txn on sales_db --\u003e\r\n            \u003cgroup transaction=\"true\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n                \u003csql db=\"sales_db\"\u003eINSERT INTO logs VALUES (\u0027Thread A started\u0027);\u003c/sql\u003e\r\n                \u003csql db=\"sales_db\"\u003eUPDATE stats SET count = count + 1;\u003c/sql\u003e\r\n            \u003c/group\u003e\r\n\r\n            \u003c!-- Branch 2: isolated txn on sales_db --\u003e\r\n            \u003cgroup transaction=\"true\" db=\"sales_db\"\u003e\r\n                \u003csql db=\"sales_db\"\u003eINSERT INTO logs VALUES (\u0027Thread B started\u0027);\u003c/sql\u003e\r\n                \u003csql db=\"sales_db\"\u003eUPDATE stats SET count = count + 1;\u003c/sql\u003e\r\n            \u003c/group\u003e\r\n        \u003c/parallel\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3.5 Context Propagation \u0026 Automatic Rollback\r\n\r\nAll transactions in Flow are fully contextualized using Go\u0027s `context.Context` API:\r\n*   Transactions are initiated with `BeginTx(ctx, nil)`.\r\n*   If the pipeline\u0027s active context is cancelled or times out, the active transactions are automatically aborted and safely rolled back.\r\n*   This prevents database locks, stale transactions, and uncommitted orphaned states across timeouts and unexpected cancellations.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Interaction with SQL Server Bulk Copy (`StreamETL`)\r\n\r\nFlow has high-performance support for Microsoft SQL Server native TDS bulk streaming copy via the `StreamETL` function (`\u003csql_bulk target_table=\"...\"\u003e` tag). \r\n\r\n### How Bulk Copy Works\r\nThe MS SQL Server bulk copy utility loads data efficiently by writing data pages directly to the database without the overhead of standard row-by-row transaction logs. It utilizes the `mssql.CopyIn` interface.\r\n\r\n### Bulk Copy Batch-Level Transactions\r\nUnlike standard sequential SQL scripts, **SQL Server bulk copy manages its own internal transactions** to flush rows in high-speed chunks (defined by `batch_size`, which defaults to `10000` rows). Each batch flush:\r\n1. Opens a separate, native bulk transaction.\r\n2. Writes the chunk of rows.\r\n3. Commits that batch chunk immediately to disk.\r\n\r\n### Impact of Mixing Group Transactions and Bulk Copy\r\n\u003e [!WARNING]\r\n\u003e While a standard `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e` is excellent for atomic transactional integrity on conventional queries, you should avoid placing high-throughput bulk copy stream scripts (`\u003csql_bulk target_table=\"...\"\u003e`) inside a transaction-enabled group.\r\n\r\nHere is how configuration options impact bulk copy:\r\n\r\n1.  **Atomicity vs. Recovery**: \r\n    If a bulk copy script is wrapped inside an outer group transaction, standard driver behaviors might force all streamed batches to use the same underlying physical connection. However, because bulk copying relies on native SQL Server TDS fast-load streams, mixing outer group transactions with internal batch commits can cause locking, socket blocks, or driver-level command overlap errors.\r\n2.  **TABLOCK (Minimal Logging)**:\r\n    By default, bulk insert operations use the `tablock` option to lock the target table during inserts. This minimizes transactional logging and maximizes throughput. However, if a table lock is acquired within a long-running standard transaction, concurrent queries targeting that table are fully blocked until the entire transaction group is either committed or rolled back.\r\n3.  **Check Constraints \u0026 Triggers**:\r\n    By default, constraints checking (`check_constraints`) and triggers (`fire_triggers`) are turned off to speed up ingestion. Enabling them forces SQL Server to run validations, which increases logging overhead and log-write lock contention. Under an outer group transaction, this logging overhead can lead to rapid database transaction log depletion.\r\n\r\n### Summary Best Practice\r\n*   **Use `\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`** for transactional business logic, updates, inserts, and system state steps.\r\n*   **Do NOT use outer group transactions** for high-volume data streams (`StreamETL`). Instead, let the streaming engine handle batch-level transaction commits naturally.\r\n","guides/DEBUGGING_GUIDE.md":"# Comprehensive Pipeline Debugging Guide: Assert, Go \u0026 CSX Script Patterns\n\nThis guide provides an end-to-end reference for inspecting, debugging, pausing, and validating dynamic ETL workflows in Flow. It details the two primary scripting engines—**Embedded Go (`language=\"go\"`)** and **C# .NET Script (`language=\"csx\"`)**—demonstrating how to observe pipeline state, trace loop mutations, prompt operators interactively, and diagnose execution issues across platforms.\n\n---\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Architectural Overview \u0026 Scripting Engines](#1-architectural-overview--scripting-engines)\n2. [Variable Discovery \u0026 State Observation](#2-variable-discovery--state-observation)\n3. [Pattern 1: Inspect Specific Variables \u0026 Early Exit](#3-pattern-1-inspect-specific-variables--early-exit)\n4. [Pattern 2: Interactive Breakpoints \u0026 Operator Prompts](#4-pattern-2-interactive-breakpoints--operator-prompts)\n5. [Pattern 3: Per-Iteration Loop Inspection (`\u003cforeach\u003e`)](#5-pattern-3-per-iteration-loop-inspection-foreach)\n6. [Pattern 4: State Mutation \u0026 Polling Verification (`\u003cwhile\u003e`)](#6-pattern-4-state-mutation--polling-verification-while)\n7. [Pattern 5: Declarative Assertions \u0026 Diagnostic Dumps (`\u003cassert\u003e`)](#7-pattern-5-declarative-assertions--diagnostic-dumps-assert)\n8. [Engine Comparison: Go (`host/vars`) vs. CSX (`Environment`)](#8-engine-comparison-go-hostvars-vs-csx-environment)\n9. [CLI Diagnostic Flags \u0026 Best Practices](#9-cli-diagnostic-flags--best-practices)\n10. [Debugging Quick Reference Table](#10-debugging-quick-reference-table)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Overview \u0026 Scripting Engines\n\nFlow provides two distinct script execution models for pipeline logic, debugging, and data transformation:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Registry[(\"Pipeline Variable Registry\u003cbr/\u003e(Thread-Safe Key/Value Store)\")]\n\n    subgraph GoEngine [\"1. Yaegi Go Interpreter (In-Process)\"]\n        GoInterp[\"Yaegi Interpreter Context\"]\n        HostVars[\"\u0027host/vars\u0027 Synthetic Package\u003cbr/\u003evars.GetAll(), vars.GetString(), etc.\"]\n        GoStdout[\"Interpreter os.Stdout\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph CsxEngine [\"2. .NET Script CSX (Subprocess)\"]\n        DotnetScript[\"dotnet-script Process\"]\n        EnvInjection[\"OS Environment Injection\u003cbr/\u003eKey=Value pairs passed to cmd.Env\"]\n        CsxStdout[\"Console.Write / Console.WriteLine\"]\n    end\n\n    Registry \u003c--\u003e|Direct In-Memory Access| HostVars\n    HostVars \u003c--\u003e GoInterp\n    GoInterp --\u003e GoStdout\n\n    Registry --\u003e|Snapshot on Step Start| EnvInjection\n    EnvInjection --\u003e DotnetScript\n    DotnetScript --\u003e CsxStdout\n\n    GoStdout --\u003e|Captured by output_var| Registry\n    CsxStdout --\u003e|Captured by output_var| Registry\n```\n\n### The Two Execution Environments\n\n1. **Embedded Go (`language=\"go\"`)**:\n   - **Engine**: Traefik Yaegi Go interpreter running in-process.\n   - **Performance**: Near-instantaneous ($O(\\mu\\text{s})$ initialization) with zero process fork overhead.\n   - **Variable Interface**: Direct memory binding via the synthetic `\"host/vars\"` package.\n   - **Portability**: Completely self-contained within `flow.exe`; requires no external Go compiler or runtime.\n\n2. **C# .NET Script (`language=\"csx\"`)**:\n   - **Engine**: Cross-platform `dotnet-script` CLI executed via external process invocation.\n   - **Performance**: High-power runtime with access to full .NET Base Class Libraries (BCL), LINQ, `System.Text.Json`, and NuGet packaging.\n   - **Variable Interface**: Flow snapshots all registry variables and injects them as OS environment variables into the child process environment.\n   - **Portability**: Runs on any system where the .NET SDK / `dotnet-script` tool is installed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Variable Discovery \u0026 State Observation\n\nWhen debugging complex pipelines, inspecting the live state of all pipeline variables is essential.\n\n### Go: `vars.GetAll()`\n\nIn embedded Go scripts, the synthetic `\"host/vars\"` package provides `vars.GetAll()`, returning a copy of the current variable map:\n\n```go\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"os\"\n    \"sort\"\n    \"host/vars\"\n)\n\nallVars := vars.GetAll()\nkeys := make([]string, 0, len(allVars))\nfor k := range allVars {\n    keys = append(keys, k)\n}\nsort.Strings(keys)\n\nfor _, name := range keys {\n    fmt.Fprintf(os.Stdout, \"  %-20s = %v\\n\", name, allVars[name])\n}\n```\n\n### CSX: `Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable`\n\nIn C# scripts, all pipeline variables are injected into the child process environment. You can query specific variables by name or enumerate known pipeline variables in sorted order:\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\nvar pipelineVarNames = new[] {\n    \"DBPath\", \"DEPARTMENT\", \"FormattedRow\", \"ID\", \"LAST_OUTPUT\", \"LOOP_INDEX\",\n    \"MinSalary\", \"NAME\", \"SALARY\", \"department\", \"id\", \"log_db_cs\", \"name\", \"salary\"\n};\n\nvar keys = new List\u003cstring\u003e();\nforeach (var name in pipelineVarNames)\n{\n    if (Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name) != null)\n    {\n        keys.Add(name);\n    }\n}\nkeys.Sort(StringComparer.Ordinal);\n\nforeach (var name in keys)\n{\n    var val = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name);\n    Console.WriteLine($\"  {name,-20} = {val}\");\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Pattern 1: Inspect Specific Variables \u0026 Early Exit\n\n### Purpose\nWhen developing or troubleshooting a pipeline, you often need to verify that specific configuration variables (such as connection strings, file paths, or template outputs) were parsed correctly, and optionally halt before downstream destructive actions occur.\n\n### Baseline Implementations\n- **Go Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_specific_vars_and_exit_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_specific_vars_and_exit_pipeline.xml)\n- **CSX Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_specific_vars_and_exit_csx_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_specific_vars_and_exit_csx_pipeline.xml)\n\n### Side-by-Side Implementation\n\n#### Embedded Go (`language=\"go\"`)\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsAndExit\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        package main\n\n        import (\n            \"fmt\"\n            \"os\"\n            \"host/vars\"\n        )\n\n        func main() {\n            fmt.Println(\"========== Pipeline Variables ==========\")\n            fmt.Printf(\"  DBPath            = %v\\n\", vars.GetString(\"DBPath\"))\n            fmt.Printf(\"  MinSalary         = %v\\n\", vars.GetFloat(\"MinSalary\"))\n            fmt.Printf(\"  log_db_cs         = %v\\n\", vars.GetString(\"log_db_cs\"))\n            fmt.Printf(\"  FinalFormattedRow = \\n%v\\n\", vars.GetString(\"FormattedRow\"))\n            fmt.Println(\"========================================\")\n            /* os.Exit(0) */ // Uncomment for unconditional early exit\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n#### C# Script (`language=\"csx\"`)\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsAndExit\" language=\"csx\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        using System;\n\n        Console.WriteLine(\"========== Pipeline Variables ==========\");\n        Console.WriteLine($\"  DBPath            = {Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"DBPath\")}\");\n        Console.WriteLine($\"  MinSalary         = {Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"MinSalary\")}\");\n        Console.WriteLine($\"  log_db_cs         = {Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"log_db_cs\")}\");\n        Console.WriteLine($\"  FinalFormattedRow = \\n{Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"FormattedRow\")}\");\n        Console.WriteLine(\"========================================\");\n        /* Environment.Exit(0); */ // Uncomment for unconditional early exit\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n### Sample Output\n```text\n========== Pipeline Variables ==========\n  DBPath       = ./demo_employees.db\n  MinSalary    = 100000\n  log_db_cs    = sqlserver://db-server.example.com:1433?database=EXAMPLE_DB\u0026integrated+security=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\u0026app+name=Flow_Logger\n  FinalFormattedRow = \n==================================================\nEmployee ID: 103\nName:        Carol White\nDepartment:  DevOps\nSalary:      $132500.75\n==================================================\n========================================\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Pattern 2: Interactive Breakpoints \u0026 Operator Prompts\n\n### Purpose\nIn production troubleshooting or staging verifications, you may wish to inspect all live variables and prompt an operator whether to continue or abort. If aborted, the script halts execution and emits a structured JSON diagnostic payload.\n\n### Baseline Implementations\n- **Go Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_and_ask_continue_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_and_ask_continue_pipeline.xml)\n- **CSX Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_and_ask_continue_csx_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_and_ask_continue_csx_pipeline.xml)\n\n### Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Flow as Flow Engine\n    participant Script as Script (Go or CSX)\n    participant Console as Terminal / Operator\n\n    Flow-\u003e\u003eScript: Execute DumpVarsAndPause\n    Script-\u003e\u003eConsole: Print Table of All Variables\n    Script-\u003e\u003eConsole: \"Enter Y to cancel or N to continue [default: N]: \"\n    Console--\u003e\u003eScript: User Input (e.g., \u0027Y\u0027 or \u0027N\u0027 / Enter)\n    alt User Enters \u0027Y\u0027 (Cancel)\n        Script-\u003e\u003eConsole: \"Cancellation requested. Halting pipeline...\"\n        Script--\u003e\u003eFlow: Abort via panic() or throw Exception(jsonPayload)\n        Flow--\u003e\u003eConsole: Pipeline Halts with Error / Diagnostic Dump\n    else User Enters \u0027N\u0027 or Enter (Continue)\n        Script-\u003e\u003eConsole: \"Continuing pipeline...\"\n        Script--\u003e\u003eFlow: Return code 0 (Success)\n        Flow-\u003e\u003eFlow: Proceed to Next Pipeline Step\n    end\n```\n\n### Side-by-Side Implementation\n\n#### Embedded Go (`language=\"go\"`)\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsAndPause\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        package main\n\n        import (\n            \"bufio\"\n            \"encoding/json\"\n            \"fmt\"\n            \"os\"\n            \"sort\"\n            \"strings\"\n            \"host/vars\"\n        )\n\n        func main() {\n            fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, \"\\n========== Pipeline Variables ==========\")\n            allVars := vars.GetAll()\n            keys := make([]string, 0, len(allVars))\n            for k := range allVars {\n                keys = append(keys, k)\n            }\n            sort.Strings(keys)\n            for _, name := range keys {\n                fmt.Fprintf(os.Stdout, \"  %-20s = %v\\n\", name, allVars[name])\n            }\n            fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, \"========================================\")\n\n            fmt.Fprint(os.Stdout, \"\\nEnter Y to cancel or N to continue [default: N]: \")\n            os.Stdout.Sync()\n\n            reader := bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin)\n            rawInput, _ := reader.ReadString(\u0027\\n\u0027)\n            choice := strings.TrimSpace(strings.ToUpper(rawInput))\n\n            if choice == \"Y\" {\n                fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, \"Cancellation requested. Halting pipeline...\")\n                jsonBytes, _ := json.Marshal(allVars)\n                panic(string(jsonBytes))\n            }\n\n            fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, \"Continuing pipeline...\")\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n#### C# Script (`language=\"csx\"`)\n```xml\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsAndPause\" language=\"csx\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        using System;\n        using System.Collections.Generic;\n        using System.Text.Json;\n\n        Console.WriteLine(\"\\n========== Pipeline Variables ==========\");\n\n        var pipelineVarNames = new[] {\n            \"DBPath\", \"DEPARTMENT\", \"FormattedRow\", \"ID\", \"LAST_OUTPUT\", \"LOOP_INDEX\",\n            \"MinSalary\", \"NAME\", \"SALARY\", \"department\", \"id\", \"log_db_cs\", \"name\", \"salary\"\n        };\n\n        var keys = new List\u003cstring\u003e();\n        foreach (var name in pipelineVarNames)\n        {\n            if (Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name) != null)\n            {\n                keys.Add(name);\n            }\n        }\n        keys.Sort(StringComparer.Ordinal);\n\n        var allVars = new Dictionary\u003cstring, string\u003e();\n        foreach (var name in keys)\n        {\n            string val = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name) ?? \"\";\n            allVars[name] = val;\n            Console.WriteLine($\"  {name,-20} = {val}\");\n        }\n        Console.WriteLine(\"========================================\");\n\n        Console.Write(\"\\nEnter Y to cancel or N to continue [default: N]: \");\n        string rawInput = Console.ReadLine() ?? \"\";\n        string choice = rawInput.Trim().ToUpper();\n\n        if (choice == \"Y\")\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine(\"Cancellation requested. Halting pipeline...\");\n            string jsonString = JsonSerializer.Serialize(allVars);\n            throw new Exception(jsonString);\n        }\n\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Continuing pipeline...\");\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Pattern 3: Per-Iteration Loop Inspection (`\u003cforeach\u003e`)\n\n### Purpose\nIn cursor-driven database loops (`\u003cforeach\u003e`), data bugs often occur on specific rows (e.g., unexpected nulls, boundary values, or character encoding mismatches). Placing an inspection script inside the loop captures variable state on every single pass.\n\n### Baseline Implementations\n- **Go Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_and_exit_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_and_exit_pipeline.xml)\n- **CSX Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_and_exit_csx_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_and_exit_csx_pipeline.xml)\n\n### Side-by-Side Implementation\n\n#### Embedded Go (`language=\"go\"`)\n```xml\n\u003cforeach id=\"SelectEmployees\" db=\"sample_db\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        SELECT id, name, department, salary\n        FROM employees\n        WHERE salary \u003e= {{MinSalary}}\n        ORDER BY id;\n    ]]\u003e\n    \u003ctemplate id=\"FormatEmployeeRow\" output_var=\"FormattedRow\"\u003e\n        Employee ID: {{.id}} | Name: {{.name}} | Salary: ${{.salary}}\n    \u003c/template\u003e\n    \n    \u003c!-- In-loop debugging script --\u003e\n    \u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsPerLoop\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n        \u003c![CDATA[\n            package main\n\n            import (\n                \"fmt\"\n                \"os\"\n                \"sort\"\n                \"host/vars\"\n            )\n\n            func main() {\n                loopIdx := vars.GetInt(\"LOOP_INDEX\")\n                header := fmt.Sprintf(\"\\n========== Variables at LOOP_INDEX %d ==========\", loopIdx)\n                fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, header)\n\n                allVars := vars.GetAll()\n                keys := make([]string, 0, len(allVars))\n                for k := range allVars {\n                    keys = append(keys, k)\n                }\n                sort.Strings(keys)\n                for _, name := range keys {\n                    fmt.Fprintf(os.Stdout, \"  %-20s = %v\\n\", name, allVars[name])\n                }\n                fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, \"=================================================\")\n            }\n        ]]\u003e\n    \u003c/script\u003e\n\u003c/foreach\u003e\n```\n\n#### C# Script (`language=\"csx\"`)\n```xml\n\u003cforeach id=\"SelectEmployees\" db=\"sample_db\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        SELECT id, name, department, salary\n        FROM employees\n        WHERE salary \u003e= {{MinSalary}}\n        ORDER BY id;\n    ]]\u003e\n    \u003ctemplate id=\"FormatEmployeeRow\" output_var=\"FormattedRow\"\u003e\n        Employee ID: {{.id}} | Name: {{.name}} | Salary: ${{.salary}}\n    \u003c/template\u003e\n    \n    \u003c!-- In-loop debugging script --\u003e\n    \u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsPerLoop\" language=\"csx\"\u003e\n        \u003c![CDATA[\n            using System;\n            using System.Collections.Generic;\n\n            int loopIdx = int.TryParse(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"LOOP_INDEX\"), out var li) ? li : 0;\n            Console.WriteLine($\"\\n========== Variables at LOOP_INDEX {loopIdx} ==========\");\n\n            var pipelineVarNames = new[] {\n                \"DBPath\", \"DEPARTMENT\", \"FormattedRow\", \"ID\", \"LAST_OUTPUT\", \"LOOP_INDEX\",\n                \"MinSalary\", \"NAME\", \"SALARY\", \"department\", \"id\", \"log_db_cs\", \"name\", \"salary\"\n            };\n\n            var keys = new List\u003cstring\u003e();\n            foreach (var name in pipelineVarNames)\n            {\n                if (Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name) != null) keys.Add(name);\n            }\n            keys.Sort(StringComparer.Ordinal);\n\n            foreach (var name in keys)\n            {\n                var val = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name);\n                Console.WriteLine($\"  {name,-20} = {val}\");\n            }\n            Console.WriteLine(\"=================================================\");\n        ]]\u003e\n    \u003c/script\u003e\n\u003c/foreach\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Pattern 4: State Mutation \u0026 Polling Verification (`\u003cwhile\u003e`)\n\n### Purpose\n`\u003cwhile\u003e` loops are commonly used for polling asynchronous jobs or processing paginated batches. Debugging while loops requires:\n1. Observing variables at each `WHILE_INDEX` iteration pass.\n2. Verifying accumulator logic (e.g. `ProcessedCount = ProcessedCount + 25`).\n3. Ensuring termination conditions correctly evaluate and break the loop.\n\n### Baseline Implementations\n- **Go Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_while_loop_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_while_loop_pipeline.xml)\n- **CSX Reference**: [`../../examples/debug_while_loop_csx_pipeline.xml`](../../examples/debug_while_loop_csx_pipeline.xml)\n\n### While Loop Workflow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Start([Enter while: JobStatus == \u0027RUNNING\u0027]) --\u003e Step1[\"Step 1: DumpVarsInWhile\u003cbr/\u003eInspect state at WHILE_INDEX\"]\n    Step1 --\u003e Step2[\"Step 2: IncrementProcessedCount\u003cbr/\u003eoutput_var=\u0027ProcessedCount\u0027 (+25)\"]\n    Step2 --\u003e Step3{\"Step 3: UpdateLoopStatus\u003cbr/\u003eIs WHILE_INDEX \u003e= 2?\"}\n    Step3 -- \"No (Index 0, 1)\" --\u003e KeepRunning[\"Output \u0027RUNNING\u0027 to JobStatus\"]\n    Step3 -- \"Yes (Index 2)\" --\u003e MarkCompleted[\"Output \u0027COMPLETED\u0027 to JobStatus\"]\n    KeepRunning --\u003e LoopCheck{\"Condition Check:\u003cbr/\u003eJobStatus == \u0027RUNNING\u0027?\"}\n    MarkCompleted --\u003e LoopCheck\n    LoopCheck -- \"True\" --\u003e Step1\n    LoopCheck -- \"False\" --\u003e ExitLoop[\"Exit While Loop\"]\n    ExitLoop --\u003e FinalDump[\"Step 4: DumpFinalVarsAfterWhile\u003cbr/\u003eAudit final mutated state\"]\n```\n\n### Side-by-Side Implementation\n\n#### Step 1: Dump Variables at Current `WHILE_INDEX`\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- Go Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsInWhile\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        package main\n        import (\"fmt\"; \"os\"; \"sort\"; \"host/vars\")\n\n        func main() {\n            idx := vars.GetInt(\"WHILE_INDEX\")\n            fmt.Fprintf(os.Stdout, \"\\n========== Variables at WHILE_INDEX %d ==========\\n\", idx)\n            allVars := vars.GetAll()\n            // ... Sort \u0026 Print Keys ...\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- CSX Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpVarsInWhile\" language=\"csx\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        using System;\n        using System.Collections.Generic;\n\n        int idx = int.TryParse(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"WHILE_INDEX\"), out var i) ? i : 0;\n        Console.WriteLine($\"\\n========== Variables at WHILE_INDEX {idx} ==========\");\n        // ... Sort \u0026 Print Keys ...\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Simulate Processing \u0026 Mutate Accumulator via `output_var`\n\nFlow captures anything written to standard output (`stdout`) and updates the variable specified by `output_var`:\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- Go Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"IncrementProcessedCount\" language=\"go\" output_var=\"ProcessedCount\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        package main\n        import (\"fmt\"; \"host/vars\")\n\n        func main() {\n            count := vars.GetInt(\"ProcessedCount\") + 25\n            fmt.Print(count) // Captured directly by ProcessedCount\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- CSX Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"IncrementProcessedCount\" language=\"csx\" output_var=\"ProcessedCount\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        using System;\n\n        int count = int.TryParse(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"ProcessedCount\"), out var c) ? c : 0;\n        count += 25;\n        Console.Write(count); // Captured directly by ProcessedCount\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Update Loop Termination Condition via `output_var`\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- Go Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"UpdateLoopStatus\" language=\"go\" output_var=\"JobStatus\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        package main\n        import (\"fmt\"; \"host/vars\")\n\n        func main() {\n            idx := vars.GetInt(\"WHILE_INDEX\")\n            if idx \u003e= 2 {\n                fmt.Print(\"COMPLETED\")\n            } else {\n                fmt.Print(\"RUNNING\")\n            }\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- CSX Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"UpdateLoopStatus\" language=\"csx\" output_var=\"JobStatus\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        using System;\n\n        int idx = int.TryParse(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"WHILE_INDEX\"), out var i) ? i : 0;\n        if (idx \u003e= 2) {\n            Console.Write(\"COMPLETED\");\n        } else {\n            Console.Write(\"RUNNING\");\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n#### Step 4: Final State Audit After While Loop\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- Go Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpFinalVarsAfterWhile\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        package main\n        import (\"fmt\"; \"os\"; \"sort\"; \"host/vars\")\n\n        func main() {\n            fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, \"\\n========== Final Pipeline Variables After While Loop ==========\")\n            // ... Print all final variables ...\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- CSX Version --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"DumpFinalVarsAfterWhile\" language=\"csx\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        using System;\n        using System.Collections.Generic;\n\n        Console.WriteLine(\"\\n========== Final Pipeline Variables After While Loop ==========\");\n        // ... Print all final variables ...\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Pattern 5: Declarative Assertions \u0026 Diagnostic Dumps (`\u003cassert\u003e`)\n\n### Purpose\nWhile embedded scripts offer maximum flexibility, Flow\u0027s native `\u003cassert\u003e` tag provides a zero-code declarative mechanism for fail-fast debugging. By asserting expected state invariants between steps, you prevent silent data corruption, catch logic regressions immediately, and trigger automated diagnostic dumps when unexpected values arise.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    ExecStep[\"Execute Pipeline Step\u003cbr/\u003e(e.g. While Loop or SQL)\"] --\u003e CheckAssert{\"\u0026lt;assert\u0026gt; Evaluation\u003cbr/\u003eDoes \u0027var\u0027 match \u0027equals\u0027?\"}\n\n    CheckAssert -- \"Match (Passed)\" --\u003e Proceed[\"Continue Normal Pipeline Execution\"]\n    CheckAssert -- \"Mismatch (Failed)\" --\u003e Policy{\"on_failure Policy\"}\n\n    Policy -- \"warn\" --\u003e LogWarn[\"Log Warning Message\"]\n    LogWarn --\u003e SetDirty[\"Set fail_var = fail_val\u003cbr/\u003e(e.g. DEBUG_DIRTY = true)\"]\n    SetDirty --\u003e Proceed\n\n    Policy -- \"halt (default)\" --\u003e HasOnFailure{\"Nested \u0026lt;on_failure\u0026gt; Defined?\"}\n    HasOnFailure -- \"Yes\" --\u003e RunDiag[\"Execute Diagnostic Scripts\u003cbr/\u003e(Go or CSX state dump)\"]\n    RunDiag --\u003e HaltPipeline[/\"Halt Execution (Exit Code != 0)\"/]\n    HasOnFailure -- \"No\" --\u003e HaltPipeline\n```\n\n### Key `\u003cassert\u003e` Attributes for Debugging\n\n| Attribute | Options / Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `var` | *(required)* | Name of the pipeline registry variable to evaluate. |\n| `equals` / `value` | *(required)* | Expected value (case-insensitive string comparison). |\n| `message` | `\"\"` | Informative error or diagnostic message logged upon failure. |\n| `on_failure` | `halt` (default) \\| `warn` | `halt` stops the pipeline immediately; `warn` logs the failure and continues. |\n| `fail_var` | `\"\"` | Optional variable name to set upon failure (e.g., `DEBUG_ALERT`). |\n| `fail_val` | `\"true\"` | Value assigned to `fail_var` when assertion fails. |\n| `\u003con_failure\u003e` | *(child block)* | Container of child nodes (`\u003cscript\u003e`, `\u003csql\u003e`, etc.) executed exclusively if the assertion fails. |\n\n---\n\n### Example 1: Post-Loop Invariant Validation (Fail-Fast)\n\nVerify that the while loop terminated because `JobStatus` reached `\"COMPLETED\"` and that the expected batch count (`ProcessedCount == \"75\"`) was reached:\n\n```xml\n\u003cflow\u003e\n    \u003c!-- While Loop Execution --\u003e\n    \u003cwhile id=\"MonitorWhileLoop\" var=\"JobStatus\" equals=\"RUNNING\" max_iterations=\"5\"\u003e\n        \u003c!-- ... child steps mutating ProcessedCount and JobStatus ... --\u003e\n    \u003c/while\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- Fail-fast assertion: Ensure JobStatus is COMPLETED --\u003e\n    \u003cassert id=\"VerifyJobCompleted\" \n            var=\"JobStatus\" \n            equals=\"COMPLETED\" \n            message=\"State Error: Expected JobStatus to be COMPLETED after while loop!\" \n            on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- Fail-fast assertion: Verify exact processed record volume --\u003e\n    \u003cassert id=\"VerifyProcessedVolume\" \n            var=\"ProcessedCount\" \n            equals=\"75\" \n            message=\"Volume Mismatch: Expected ProcessedCount to reach exactly 75!\" \n            on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e\n\u003c/flow\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Automated Diagnostic Dumps via `\u003con_failure\u003e`\n\nWhen an assertion fails, standard execution halts. By nesting an embedded Go or CSX `\u003cscript\u003e` inside `\u003con_failure\u003e`, you can automatically dump all live variables to `stderr` or generate a structured JSON post-mortem diagnostic report before the process exits.\n\n#### Embedded Go (`language=\"go\"`) inside `\u003con_failure\u003e`\n```xml\n\u003cassert id=\"ValidateBatchThreshold\" \n        var=\"ProcessedCount\" \n        equals=\"75\" \n        on_failure=\"halt\" \n        message=\"Critical Assertion Failed: ProcessedCount did not reach expected threshold!\"\u003e\n    \u003con_failure\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Automated diagnostic post-mortem dump --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"PostMortemDiagnosticGo\" language=\"go\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n                package main\n\n                import (\n                    \"encoding/json\"\n                    \"fmt\"\n                    \"os\"\n                    \"sort\"\n                    \"host/vars\"\n                )\n\n                func main() {\n                    fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"\\n========================================================\")\n                    fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \" [POST-MORTEM DIAGNOSTIC DUMP: ASSERTION FAILURE]\")\n                    fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"========================================================\")\n\n                    allVars := vars.GetAll()\n                    keys := make([]string, 0, len(allVars))\n                    for k := range allVars {\n                        keys = append(keys, k)\n                    }\n                    sort.Strings(keys)\n\n                    for _, name := range keys {\n                        fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"  %-22s = %v\\n\", name, allVars[name])\n                    }\n\n                    // Serialize failure context to JSON for log ingestion\n                    jsonPayload, _ := json.MarshalIndent(allVars, \"\", \"  \")\n                    fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"\\nSerialized State:\\n%s\\n\", string(jsonPayload))\n                    fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"========================================================\")\n                }\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/on_failure\u003e\n\u003c/assert\u003e\n```\n\n#### C# Script (`language=\"csx\"`) inside `\u003con_failure\u003e`\n```xml\n\u003cassert id=\"ValidateBatchThreshold\" \n        var=\"ProcessedCount\" \n        equals=\"75\" \n        on_failure=\"halt\" \n        message=\"Critical Assertion Failed: ProcessedCount did not reach expected threshold!\"\u003e\n    \u003con_failure\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Automated diagnostic post-mortem dump --\u003e\n        \u003cscript id=\"PostMortemDiagnosticCSX\" language=\"csx\"\u003e\n            \u003c![CDATA[\n                using System;\n                using System.Collections.Generic;\n                using System.Text.Json;\n\n                Console.Error.WriteLine(\"\\n========================================================\");\n                Console.Error.WriteLine(\" [POST-MORTEM DIAGNOSTIC DUMP: ASSERTION FAILURE]\");\n                Console.Error.WriteLine(\"========================================================\");\n\n                var knownKeys = new[] {\n                    \"DBPath\", \"JobStatus\", \"LAST_OUTPUT\", \"MinSalary\",\n                    \"ProcessedCount\", \"TargetBatch\", \"Threshold\", \"WHILE_INDEX\"\n                };\n\n                var report = new Dictionary\u003cstring, object\u003e {\n                    [\"TimestampUtc\"] = DateTime.UtcNow.ToString(\"o\"),\n                    [\"FailureReason\"] = \"ProcessedCount did not match expected threshold of 75\"\n                };\n\n                var varSnapshot = new Dictionary\u003cstring, string\u003e();\n                foreach (var key in knownKeys)\n                {\n                    string val = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(key) ?? \"(null)\";\n                    varSnapshot[key] = val;\n                    Console.Error.WriteLine($\"  {key,-22} = {val}\");\n                }\n                report[\"Variables\"] = varSnapshot;\n\n                string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(report, new JsonSerializerOptions { WriteIndented = true });\n                Console.Error.WriteLine(\"\\nStructured Diagnostic JSON:\\n\" + json);\n                Console.Error.WriteLine(\"========================================================\");\n            ]]\u003e\n        \u003c/script\u003e\n    \u003c/on_failure\u003e\n\u003c/assert\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Non-Halting Soft Assertions with Warning Flags (`on_failure=\"warn\"`)\n\nIn staging or non-critical diagnostic workflows, you can flag state anomalies without breaking execution. A downstream script checks the flag to decide whether to trigger deeper diagnostics:\n\n```xml\n\u003c!-- Log warning and set DEBUG_ANOMALY=true if salary threshold was altered --\u003e\n\u003cassert id=\"CheckExpectedSalaryFilter\" \n        var=\"MinSalary\" \n        equals=\"100000.00\" \n        on_failure=\"warn\" \n        message=\"Warning: MinSalary was overridden from default baseline!\" \n        fail_var=\"DEBUG_ANOMALY\" \n        fail_val=\"true\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- Downstream diagnostic step checks flag --\u003e\n\u003cscript id=\"ConditionalDiagnostics\" language=\"csx\"\u003e\n    \u003c![CDATA[\n        using System;\n\n        string isAnomaly = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"DEBUG_ANOMALY\");\n        if (isAnomaly == \"true\")\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine(\"[DEBUG NOTICE] Pipeline running under non-standard configuration!\");\n        }\n    ]]\u003e\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Engine Comparison: Go (`host/vars`) vs. CSX (`Environment`)\n\n| Feature / Behavior | Embedded Go (`language=\"go\"`) | C# .NET Script (`language=\"csx\"`) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Execution Architecture** | In-process Yaegi interpreter | Subprocess (`dotnet-script`) |\n| **Startup Overhead** | Negligible ($\\sim 1\\text{ ms}$) | Process startup ($\\sim 800 - 1000\\text{ ms}$) |\n| **External Dependencies** | None (embedded in `flow.exe`) | .NET SDK + `dotnet-script` tool |\n| **Read Pipeline Variable** | `vars.GetString(\"Key\")`\u003cbr/\u003e`vars.GetInt(\"Key\")`\u003cbr/\u003e`vars.GetFloat(\"Key\")`\u003cbr/\u003e`vars.GetBool(\"Key\")` | `Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"Key\")` |\n| **Enumerate All Variables** | `allVars := vars.GetAll()` | Query environment or iterate known pipeline variable list |\n| **Mutate Variable** | Print to stdout + `output_var=\"VarName\"` | `Console.Write(...)` + `output_var=\"VarName\"` |\n| **Interactive Console Read** | `bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin).ReadString(\u0027\\n\u0027)` | `Console.ReadLine()` |\n| **Halt with Error** | `panic(\"reason\")` or `os.Exit(1)` | `throw new Exception(\"reason\")` or `Environment.Exit(1)` |\n| **Declarative Assertion** | `\u003cassert var=\"K\" equals=\"V\" /\u003e` | `\u003cassert var=\"K\" equals=\"V\" /\u003e` |\n| **Assertion Failure Hook** | `\u003con_failure\u003e` with `\u003cscript language=\"go\"\u003e` | `\u003con_failure\u003e` with `\u003cscript language=\"csx\"\u003e` |\n| **Clean Normal Exit** | Normal function return | Normal script completion |\n| **Rich Library Access** | Standard Go packages (fmt, strings, time, etc.) | Full .NET BCL, LINQ, `System.Text.Json`, NuGet |\n\n---\n\n## 9. CLI Diagnostic Flags \u0026 Best Practices\n\nFlow provides command-line flags to assist with pipeline debugging without altering pipeline XML:\n\n### 1. Schema \u0026 Structure Validation (`-validate`)\nValidates that the XML conforms to the Flow XSD schema and that all required attributes are present without executing any queries or scripts:\n```bash\n./flow -file examples/debug_while_loop_csx_pipeline.xml -validate\n```\n\n### 2. Preflight Only (`-preflight`)\nExecutes only `\u003cpreflight\u003e` assertions (e.g. database connectivity, directory existence) and exits:\n```bash\n./flow -file my_pipeline.xml -preflight\n```\n\n### 3. Verbose Execution Logging (`-debug`)\nEnables internal engine debug logging, showing SQL query preparation, timing, and variable substitutions:\n```bash\n./flow -file examples/debug_and_exit_csx_pipeline.xml -debug\n```\n\n### 4. Overriding Variables at Runtime (`-vars`)\nInject test parameters or override thresholds dynamically without editing the file:\n```bash\n./flow -file examples/debug_and_ask_continue_csx_pipeline.xml -vars \"MinSalary=120000.00\"\n```\n\n### 5. Structured Output Inspection (`-format`)\nFormat step results as JSON, Pretty JSON, or Markdown for log aggregators or documentation:\n```bash\n./flow -file examples/debug_specific_vars_and_exit_csx_pipeline.xml -format jsonpretty\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Debugging Quick Reference Table\n\n| Objective | Go (`language=\"go\"`) Snippet | CSX (`language=\"csx\"`) Snippet |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Print single variable** | `fmt.Println(vars.GetString(\"VarName\"))` | `Console.WriteLine(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"VarName\"));` |\n| **Parse integer variable** | `val := vars.GetInt(\"LOOP_INDEX\")` | `int val = int.Parse(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"LOOP_INDEX\") ?? \"0\");` |\n| **Format multiline output** | `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stdout, \"Val: %s\\n\", val)` | `Console.WriteLine($\"Val: {val}\");` |\n| **Store script result** | `fmt.Print(result)` with `output_var=\"Target\"` | `Console.Write(result);` with `output_var=\"Target\"` |\n| **Prompt user** | `reader.ReadString(\u0027\\n\u0027)` | `string input = Console.ReadLine();` |\n| **Trigger failure halt** | `panic(\"Validation failed\")` | `throw new Exception(\"Validation failed\");` |\n| **Dump JSON payload** | `bytes, _ := json.Marshal(allVars); panic(string(bytes))` | `string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(vars); throw new Exception(json);` |\n| **Assert and fail-fast** | `\u003cassert var=\"Status\" equals=\"COMPLETED\" on_failure=\"halt\" /\u003e` | *(Declarative XML tag; language-agnostic)* |\n| **Assert with warning flag** | `\u003cassert var=\"Env\" equals=\"prod\" on_failure=\"warn\" fail_var=\"DIRTY\" /\u003e` | *(Declarative XML tag; language-agnostic)* |\n| **Post-mortem state dump** | `\u003con_failure\u003e` wrapping Go diagnostic dump | `\u003con_failure\u003e` wrapping CSX `System.Text.Json` dump |\n\n","guides/development_builder_modal_tooltips_guide.md":"# Developer Guide: Adding Modal Tooltips \u0026 Documentation Popups to Builder Nodes\n\nThis guide explains how to add **modal tooltips**, **interactive documentation popups**, and **context-aware help links** to nodes and form fields in the Flow Pipeline Builder.\n\nIt covers both lightweight HTML tooltips and full interactive modal dialogs (similar to the [Database Connection Strings Guide](database_connection_strings.md)), detailing the complete end-to-end architecture across catalog metadata, backend API endpoints, embedded files, frontend templates, markdown rendering, and automated testing.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Overview\n\nFlow Builder supports three tiers of node and attribute assistance:\n\n| Tier | Component Type | Interaction | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Tier 1: Inline Attribute Tooltips** | Native `title` \u0026 helper text | Mouse hover / subtitle | Brief attribute explanation (1–2 sentences), parameter defaults, variable substitution notes. |\n| **Tier 2: Field-Level Modal Links** | Interactive link / button | Click | Contextual popup trigger next to a specific field label (e.g., `📖 Connection String Guide` or `Driver Reference ↗`). |\n| **Tier 3: Section / Node-Level Popups** | Header action button | Click | Full documentation modal with real-time search, quick-jump pills, syntax examples, and one-click form insertion (`⚡ Use Example`). |\n\n### Component Flow Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Metadata [\"1. Component Catalog\"]\n        Catalog[\"builder/catalog.go\u003cbr/\u003eComponentMeta \u0026 ComponentField\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Backend [\"2. Backend Server \u0026 Assets\"]\n        DocFile[\"docs/your_topic.md\u003cbr/\u003eMarkdown Resource\"]\n        Embed[\"builder/templates.go\u003cbr/\u003e//go:embed\"]\n        Server[\"builder/server.go\u003cbr/\u003eGET /api/docs/{topic}\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Frontend [\"3. Builder UI Engine\"]\n        CanvasPartial[\"builder/tmpl/partials/canvas_nodes.html\u003cbr/\u003eSection Trigger Button\"]\n        NodeModal[\"builder/tmpl/index.html\u003cbr/\u003erenderModalFields() Helper Link\"]\n        DocModal[\"#your-docs-modal or #db-docs-modal\u003cbr/\u003eInteractive Modal Dialog\"]\n        Renderer[\"renderMarkdownToHtml()\u003cbr/\u003eMarkdown Parser \u0026 Syntax Styler\"]\n        Sync[\"builder/html_content.go\u003cbr/\u003eStandalone Embedded String\"]\n    end\n\n    Catalog --\u003e NodeModal\n    DocFile --\u003e Embed --\u003e Server\n    DocFile -. Dev Hot-Reload .-\u003e Server\n    CanvasPartial --\u003e DocModal\n    NodeModal --\u003e DocModal\n    DocModal --\u003e Server\n    Server --\u003e Renderer\n    Renderer --\u003e DocModal\n    DocModal -. \"⚡ Use Example\" .-\u003e NodeModal\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Step-by-Step Implementation Guide\n\nFollow these 7 steps to add a documentation modal or tooltip to any node (e.g., `\u003chttp\u003e`, `\u003csql\u003e`, `\u003ctemplate\u003e`, `\u003cassert\u003e`, `\u003cfile\u003e`, `\u003cjson_path\u003e`, `\u003cxml_path\u003e`).\n\n### Step 1: Define Field Tooltips in `builder/catalog.go`\n\nIn [`builder/catalog.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/catalog.go), every node is represented by a `ComponentMeta` struct, and every attribute is a `ComponentField`.\n\n1. Open [`builder/catalog.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/catalog.go).\n2. Locate your component (e.g. `http`, `sql`, `template`, `assert`).\n3. Set the `Description` attribute on each field. This string is automatically rendered as helper text under the input field and used in hover tooltips:\n\n```go\n{\n    Type:        \"http_client\",\n    Tag:         \"http_client\",\n    Name:        \"HTTP / REST Client\",\n    Category:    \"Integration \u0026 Web\",\n    Description: \"Executes HTTP GET/POST/PUT/DELETE requests with headers and payload handling.\",\n    Section:     \"flow\",\n    HasContent:  true,\n    ContentHelp: \"Request body content (for POST/PUT). Supports JSON and variable interpolation.\",\n    Fields: []ComponentField{\n        {Name: \"id\", Label: \"Step ID\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: true, Description: \"Unique step ID for logging and orchestration\"},\n        {Name: \"url\", Label: \"Endpoint URL\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: true, Description: \"Target endpoint URL (supports {{Variables}} \u0026 query params)\"},\n        {Name: \"method\", Label: \"HTTP Method\", Type: \"select\", Mandatory: true, Default: \"GET\", Options: []string{\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\", \"PATCH\", \"HEAD\", \"OPTIONS\"}, Description: \"HTTP request verb\"},\n        {Name: \"headers\", Label: \"Request Headers\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: false, Description: `Custom headers: {\"Authorization\":\"Bearer {{Token}}\"} or K1:V1,K2:V2`},\n        {Name: \"content_type\", Label: \"Content-Type\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: false, Description: \"Shortcut for Content-Type header (e.g. application/json, text/xml)\"},\n        {Name: \"into\", Label: \"Response Variable\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: false, Description: \"Variable name receiving the response body (alias output_var)\"},\n        {Name: \"status_into\", Label: \"Status Code Variable\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: false, Description: \"Variable name receiving HTTP status code (e.g. 200, 404)\"},\n        {Name: \"timeout\", Label: \"Timeout Duration\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: false, Description: \"Total request timeout duration (e.g. 10s, 30s, 2m)\"},\n        {Name: \"proxy\", Label: \"HTTP Proxy URL\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: false, Description: \"Proxy URL (e.g. http://proxy.internal:8080)\"},\n        {Name: \"tls_insecure_skip_verify\", Label: \"Skip TLS Verify\", Type: \"select\", Mandatory: false, Default: \"false\", Options: []string{\"false\", \"true\"}, Description: \"Disable SSL/TLS certificate validation checks\"},\n        {Name: \"follow_redirects\", Label: \"Follow Redirects\", Type: \"select\", Mandatory: false, Default: \"true\", Options: []string{\"true\", \"false\"}, Description: \"Follow HTTP 3xx redirect responses automatically\"},\n        {Name: \"description\", Label: \"Description\", Type: \"text\", Mandatory: false, Description: \"Step explanation displayed in logs and flowcharts\"},\n    },\n    DefaultXML: `\u003chttp_client id=\"FetchRemoteData\" url=\"https://api.example.com/data\" method=\"GET\" into=\"ApiResponse\" /\u003e`,\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Prepare the Markdown Documentation Resource\n\n1. Place or edit your canonical markdown file under `docs/` (for example, [`docs/http_client.md`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/docs/http_client.md) or [`docs/templates.md`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/docs/templates.md)).\n2. Make a copy under `builder/tmpl/` for standalone compilation:\n   ```powershell\n   Copy-Item docs/http_client.md builder/tmpl/docs_http_client.md\n   ```\n3. Update the `//go:embed` directive in [`builder/templates.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/templates.go) to include the new file:\n\n```go\n// In builder/templates.go:\n//go:embed tmpl/*.html tmpl/partials/*.html tmpl/docs_connection_strings.md tmpl/docs_http_client.md\nvar templateFS embed.FS\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add the Backend HTTP Endpoint in `builder/server.go`\n\nIn [`builder/server.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/server.go), implement an API handler that returns the documentation JSON. Always use the **dual-source strategy** (check disk first for live development changes, fall back to embedded filesystem for standalone binaries):\n\n```go\n// handleHttpClientDoc serves the HTTP Client documentation markdown.\nfunc (s *Server) handleHttpClientDoc(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n    if r.Method != http.MethodGet {\n        http.Error(w, \"Method Not Allowed\", http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)\n        return\n    }\n\n    docFilename := \"http_client.md\"\n    diskCandidates := []string{\n        filepath.Join(\"docs\", docFilename),\n        filepath.Join(\"..\", \"docs\", docFilename),\n        filepath.Join(\"builder\", \"tmpl\", \"docs_http_client.md\"),\n    }\n\n    var content []byte\n    var usedPath string\n\n    for _, p := range diskCandidates {\n        if b, err := os.ReadFile(p); err == nil \u0026\u0026 len(b) \u003e 0 {\n            content = b\n            usedPath = p\n            break\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Fall back to embedded filesystem for standalone binary\n    if len(content) == 0 {\n        if b, err := templateFS.ReadFile(\"tmpl/docs_http_client.md\"); err == nil \u0026\u0026 len(b) \u003e 0 {\n            content = b\n            usedPath = \"embedded:tmpl/docs_http_client.md\"\n        }\n    }\n\n    if len(content) == 0 {\n        http.Error(w, \"HTTP Client documentation not found\", http.StatusNotFound)\n        return\n    }\n\n    w.Header().Set(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json; charset=utf-8\")\n    json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(map[string]string{\n        \"filename\": docFilename,\n        \"path\":     usedPath,\n        \"content\":  string(content),\n    })\n}\n```\n\nRegister the endpoint in `registerRoutes` under the authentication middleware:\n\n```go\n// In builder/server.go under authenticated router:\ns.mux.Handle(\"/api/docs/http-client\", s.requireAuth(http.HandlerFunc(s.handleHttpClientDoc)))\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Wire Trigger Links \u0026 Dynamic Header Guide in `builder/tmpl/index.html`\n\nWhen a user adds or edits a node, [`renderModalFields`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/index.html) and modal openers dynamically construct the form inputs and configure header buttons.\n\n#### 4.1 Modal Header Guide Button (`#node-modal-guide-btn`)\n\nIn `#node-modal` header, add a dynamic guide button that adapts based on the active node type:\n\n```html\n\u003cbutton type=\"button\" id=\"node-modal-guide-btn\" class=\"hidden text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 border border-slate-700/80 rounded transition cursor-pointer flex items-center space-x-1 font-medium\"\u003e\n    \u003cspan\u003e📖 Guide\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003c/button\u003e\n```\n\nWire `updateNodeModalGuideButton(nodeType)` into `openAddNodeModal(type, ...)` and `openEditNode(id, type, ...)`:\n\n```javascript\nfunction updateNodeModalGuideButton(nodeType) {\n    const guideBtn = document.getElementById(\u0027node-modal-guide-btn\u0027);\n    if (!guideBtn) return;\n    if (nodeType === \u0027http_client\u0027) {\n        guideBtn.innerHTML = \u0027\u003cspan\u003e📖 HTTP Guide\u003c/span\u003e\u0027;\n        guideBtn.setAttribute(\u0027onclick\u0027, \u0027openHttpDocsModal()\u0027);\n        guideBtn.setAttribute(\u0027title\u0027, \u0027Open HTTP Client Guide (docs/http_client.md)\u0027);\n        guideBtn.classList.remove(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n    } else if (nodeType === \u0027database\u0027) {\n        guideBtn.innerHTML = \u0027\u003cspan\u003e📖 DB Guide\u003c/span\u003e\u0027;\n        guideBtn.setAttribute(\u0027onclick\u0027, \u0027openDbDocsModal()\u0027);\n        guideBtn.setAttribute(\u0027title\u0027, \u0027Open Database Connection Strings Guide\u0027);\n        guideBtn.classList.remove(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n    } else {\n        guideBtn.classList.add(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 4.2 Inline Field-Level Triggers in `renderModalFields()`\n\nTo add contextual helper links directly to specific form fields (such as `url` and `headers` for `http_client`):\n\n```javascript\n// Field-specific helper link next to the label\nlet labelExtra = \u0027\u0027;\nif (f.name === \u0027connection_string\u0027) {\n    labelExtra = \u0027\u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"openDbDocsModal(getModalCurrentDriver())\" class=\"text-[11px] text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 font-medium underline underline-offset-2 cursor-pointer flex items-center space-x-1\" title=\"View documentation and examples\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e📖 Connection String Guide\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/button\u003e\u0027;\n} else if (f.name === \u0027url\u0027 \u0026\u0026 (meta.type === \u0027http_client\u0027 || meta.tag === \u0027http_client\u0027)) {\n    labelExtra = \u0027\u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"openHttpDocsModal()\" class=\"text-[11px] text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 font-medium underline underline-offset-2 cursor-pointer flex items-center space-x-1\" title=\"View HTTP Client documentation, headers, and request examples from docs/http_client.md\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e📖 HTTP Client Guide\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/button\u003e\u0027;\n} else if (f.name === \u0027headers\u0027 \u0026\u0026 (meta.type === \u0027http_client\u0027 || meta.tag === \u0027http_client\u0027)) {\n    labelExtra = \u0027\u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"openHttpDocsModal(\\\u0027headers\\\u0027)\" class=\"text-[11px] text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 font-medium underline underline-offset-2 cursor-pointer flex items-center space-x-1\" title=\"Jump to Headers documentation\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e📖 Headers Ref\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/button\u003e\u0027;\n}\n\n// Assemble the label header\ndiv.innerHTML = \u0027\u003cdiv class=\"flex justify-between items-center\"\u003e\u0027 +\n    \u0027\u003clabel class=\"text-xs font-medium text-slate-300\"\u003e\u0027 + f.label + (f.mandatory ? \u0027 \u003cspan class=\"text-red-400\"\u003e*\u003c/span\u003e\u0027 : \u0027\u0027) + \u0027\u003c/label\u003e\u0027 +\n    \u0027\u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center space-x-2\"\u003e\u0027 + labelExtra + \u0027\u003cspan class=\"text-[10px] text-slate-500\"\u003e\u0027 + (f.description || \u0027\u0027) + \u0027\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u0027 +\n\u0027\u003c/div\u003e\u0027 + inputHtml;\n```\n\nAnd optionally add inline helper footers below inputs:\n\n```javascript\n} else if (f.name === \u0027url\u0027 \u0026\u0026 (meta.type === \u0027http_client\u0027 || meta.tag === \u0027http_client\u0027)) {\n    inputHtml = \u0027\u003cdiv class=\"space-y-1\"\u003e\u0027 +\n        \u0027\u003cinput type=\"text\" id=\"attr_\u0027 + f.name + \u0027\" name=\"attr_\u0027 + f.name + \u0027\" value=\"\u0027 + (val || \u0027\u0027).replace(/\"/g, \u0027\u0026quot;\u0027) + \u0027\" placeholder=\"https://api.example.com/v1/resource\" class=\"w-full bg-slate-950 border border-slate-700 rounded p-1.5 text-xs text-cyan-300 font-mono focus:outline-none focus:border-blue-500\"\u003e\u0027 +\n        \u0027\u003cdiv class=\"flex justify-between items-center text-[10px] text-slate-500\"\u003e\u0027 +\n            \u0027\u003cspan\u003eSupports \u0026#123;\u0026#123;Variables\u0026#125;\u0026#125; and query parameters\u003c/span\u003e\u0027 +\n            \u0027\u003ca href=\"javascript:void(0)\" onclick=\"openHttpDocsModal()\" class=\"text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 hover:underline cursor-pointer\"\u003eHTTP Reference \u0026amp; Headers ↗\u003c/a\u003e\u0027 +\n        \u0027\u003c/div\u003e\u0027 +\n    \u0027\u003c/div\u003e\u0027;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Add Guide Buttons to Canvas \u0026 Node Cards\n\n1. **Canvas Section Headers** ([`builder/tmpl/partials/canvas_nodes.html`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/partials/canvas_nodes.html)):\n   ```html\n   \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center space-x-1.5\"\u003e\n       \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"openDbDocsModal()\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 text-[11px] font-medium bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 border border-slate-700 rounded transition flex items-center space-x-1 cursor-pointer\" title=\"View documentation and connection string examples for all supported databases\"\u003e\n           \u003cspan\u003e📖 Connection Guide\u003c/span\u003e\n       \u003c/button\u003e\n       \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"openAddNodeModal(\u0027database\u0027, \u0027Database Connection\u0027, \u0027databases\u0027)\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 text-[11px] font-medium bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-500 text-white rounded transition flex items-center space-x-1 cursor-pointer\"\u003e\n           \u003cspan\u003e＋ Add Database\u003c/span\u003e\n       \u003c/button\u003e\n   \u003c/div\u003e\n   ```\n\n2. **Canvas Node Cards** ([`builder/tmpl/partials/node_card.html`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/partials/node_card.html)):\n   In leaf node headers, expose a direct documentation shortcut button:\n   ```html\n   {{if eq .NodeType \"http_client\"}}\n   \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"openHttpDocsModal()\" class=\"p-1 hover:bg-slate-700 rounded text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 transition text-[11px]\" title=\"Open HTTP Client Guide (docs/http_client.md)\"\u003e📖\u003c/button\u003e\n   {{else if eq .NodeType \"database\"}}\n   \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"openDbDocsModal()\" class=\"p-1 hover:bg-slate-700 rounded text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 transition text-[11px]\" title=\"Open Connection String Guide\"\u003e📖\u003c/button\u003e\n   {{end}}\n   ```\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Create the Popup Modal \u0026 Client-Side Logic\n\n#### Modal HTML Container Pattern\n\nAdd the modal structure right before `\u003c!-- EVENT DETAIL MODAL --\u003e` in [`builder/tmpl/index.html`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/index.html):\n\n```html\n\u003c!-- HTTP CLIENT DOCS MODAL --\u003e\n\u003cdiv id=\"http-docs-modal\" class=\"hidden fixed inset-0 bg-black/80 backdrop-blur-sm flex items-center justify-center p-4 z-[75]\" onclick=\"if(event.target===this)closeHttpDocsModal()\"\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl max-w-5xl w-full p-6 shadow-2xl flex flex-col space-y-4 max-h-[92vh]\"\u003e\n        \u003c!-- Header --\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-slate-800 pb-3\"\u003e\n            \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center space-x-3\"\u003e\n                \u003cspan class=\"text-2xl\"\u003e🌐\u003c/span\u003e\n                \u003cdiv\u003e\n                    \u003ch3 class=\"text-base font-bold text-white flex items-center space-x-2\"\u003e\n                        \u003cspan\u003eHTTP Client \u0026amp; API Guide\u003c/span\u003e\n                        \u003cspan class=\"text-[10px] font-mono px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-400 border border-slate-700\"\u003edocs/http_client.md\u003c/span\u003e\n                    \u003c/h3\u003e\n                    \u003cp class=\"text-xs text-slate-400\"\u003eREST methods, authentication headers, query parameters, and payload examples\u003c/p\u003e\n                \u003c/div\u003e\n            \u003c/div\u003e\n            \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center space-x-2\"\u003e\n                \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"copyHttpDocsRaw()\" id=\"btn-copy-raw-http-docs\" class=\"text-xs px-2.5 py-1 bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700 transition cursor-pointer\"\u003e📋 Copy Markdown\u003c/button\u003e\n                \u003cbutton onclick=\"closeHttpDocsModal()\" class=\"text-slate-400 hover:text-white text-xl leading-none px-2 py-1 cursor-pointer\"\u003e\u0026times;\u003c/button\u003e\n            \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Filter \u0026 Quick Jumps --\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 bg-slate-950/70 p-2.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-800\"\u003e\n            \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center space-x-2 flex-1 min-w-[200px]\"\u003e\n                \u003cspan class=\"text-xs text-slate-500\"\u003e🔍\u003c/span\u003e\n                \u003cinput type=\"text\" id=\"http-docs-filter\" oninput=\"filterHttpDocs(this.value)\" placeholder=\"Search endpoints, headers, auth...\" class=\"w-full bg-transparent text-xs text-white placeholder-slate-500 focus:outline-none\"\u003e\n            \u003c/div\u003e\n            \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center space-x-1 text-[11px] overflow-x-auto\"\u003e\n                \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"jumpToHttpSection(\u0027attributes\u0027)\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-800 hover:bg-blue-600 text-slate-300 hover:text-white transition cursor-pointer\"\u003eAttributes\u003c/button\u003e\n                \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"jumpToHttpSection(\u0027client\u0027)\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-800 hover:bg-emerald-600 text-slate-300 hover:text-white transition cursor-pointer\"\u003eClient Settings\u003c/button\u003e\n                \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"jumpToHttpSection(\u0027tls\u0027)\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-800 hover:bg-amber-600 text-slate-300 hover:text-white transition cursor-pointer\"\u003eTLS \u0026amp; Proxy\u003c/button\u003e\n                \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"jumpToHttpSection(\u0027payload\u0027)\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-800 hover:bg-purple-600 text-slate-300 hover:text-white transition cursor-pointer\"\u003ePayloads \u0026amp; Vars\u003c/button\u003e\n                \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"jumpToHttpSection(\u0027loops\u0027)\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-800 hover:bg-cyan-600 text-slate-300 hover:text-white transition cursor-pointer\"\u003eLoops \u0026amp; Parallel\u003c/button\u003e\n                \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"jumpToHttpSection(\u0027examples\u0027)\" class=\"px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-800 hover:bg-rose-600 text-slate-300 hover:text-white transition cursor-pointer\"\u003eExamples\u003c/button\u003e\n            \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Scrollable Body --\u003e\n        \u003cdiv id=\"http-docs-content\" class=\"flex-1 overflow-y-auto custom-scrollbar bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-4\"\u003e\n            \u003cdiv id=\"http-docs-loading\" class=\"flex items-center justify-center p-12 text-slate-400 space-x-2\"\u003e\n                \u003cspan class=\"animate-spin text-base\"\u003e⏳\u003c/span\u003e\n                \u003cspan\u003eLoading documentation...\u003c/span\u003e\n            \u003c/div\u003e\n            \u003cdiv id=\"http-docs-body\" class=\"hidden space-y-4\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n\n        \u003c!-- Footer --\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center justify-between pt-2 border-t border-slate-800\"\u003e\n            \u003cspan class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-500\"\u003eClick any snippet or button to insert or copy\u003c/span\u003e\n            \u003cbutton type=\"button\" onclick=\"closeHttpDocsModal()\" class=\"px-4 py-1.5 text-xs font-semibold bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded-md transition cursor-pointer\"\u003eClose\u003c/button\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n```\n\n#### JavaScript Controller Logic\n\n```javascript\nlet httpDocsLoaded = false;\nlet httpDocsRawContent = \u0027\u0027;\n\nfunction openHttpDocsModal(preferredSection) {\n    const modal = document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-modal\u0027);\n    if (!modal) return;\n    modal.classList.remove(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n\n    if (!httpDocsLoaded) {\n        document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-loading\u0027).classList.remove(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n        document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-body\u0027).classList.add(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n        fetch(\u0027/api/docs/http-client\u0027)\n            .then(res =\u003e {\n                if (!res.ok) throw new Error(\u0027HTTP \u0027 + res.status);\n                return res.json();\n            })\n            .then(data =\u003e {\n                httpDocsRawContent = data.content || \u0027\u0027;\n                const html = renderMarkdownToHtml(httpDocsRawContent);\n                const body = document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-body\u0027);\n                body.innerHTML = html;\n                document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-loading\u0027).classList.add(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n                body.classList.remove(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n                httpDocsLoaded = true;\n                if (preferredSection) setTimeout(() =\u003e jumpToHttpSection(preferredSection), 150);\n            })\n            .catch(err =\u003e {\n                document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-loading\u0027).innerHTML =\n                    \u0027\u003cdiv class=\"text-rose-400 p-4 border border-rose-900/50 rounded bg-rose-950/20\"\u003eFailed to load documentation: \u0027 + escapeHtml(err.message) + \u0027\u003c/div\u003e\u0027;\n            });\n    } else {\n        if (preferredSection) setTimeout(() =\u003e jumpToHttpSection(preferredSection), 50);\n    }\n}\n\nfunction closeHttpDocsModal() {\n    const modal = document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-modal\u0027);\n    if (modal) modal.classList.add(\u0027hidden\u0027);\n}\n\nfunction jumpToHttpSection(sec) {\n    if (!sec) return;\n    sec = sec.toLowerCase();\n    const map = {\n        \u0027attributes\u0027: \u0027sec-2-complete-attribute-specification\u0027,\n        \u0027core\u0027: \u0027sec-2-1-core-request-variable-attributes\u0027,\n        \u0027headers\u0027: \u0027sec-2-1-core-request-variable-attributes\u0027,\n        \u0027client\u0027: \u0027sec-2-2-go-http-client-settings\u0027,\n        \u0027timeout\u0027: \u0027sec-2-2-go-http-client-settings\u0027,\n        \u0027tls\u0027: \u0027sec-2-3-go-http-transport-tls-tuning\u0027,\n        \u0027proxy\u0027: \u0027sec-2-3-go-http-transport-tls-tuning\u0027,\n        \u0027payload\u0027: \u0027sec-3-payload-resolution-variable-interpolation\u0027,\n        \u0027payloads\u0027: \u0027sec-3-payload-resolution-variable-interpolation\u0027,\n        \u0027vars\u0027: \u0027sec-3-payload-resolution-variable-interpolation\u0027,\n        \u0027loops\u0027: \u0027sec-4-control-flow-loop-integration\u0027,\n        \u0027parallel\u0027: \u0027sec-4-3-concurrent-requests-parallel\u0027,\n        \u0027examples\u0027: \u0027sec-5-comprehensive-pipeline-examples\u0027\n    };\n    const targetId = map[sec] || (\u0027sec-\u0027 + sec);\n    const targetEl = document.getElementById(targetId);\n    if (targetEl) {\n        targetEl.scrollIntoView({ behavior: \u0027smooth\u0027, block: \u0027start\u0027 });\n        targetEl.classList.add(\u0027bg-blue-900/40\u0027, \u0027p-1\u0027, \u0027rounded\u0027, \u0027transition-colors\u0027);\n        setTimeout(() =\u003e {\n            targetEl.classList.remove(\u0027bg-blue-900/40\u0027, \u0027p-1\u0027, \u0027rounded\u0027, \u0027transition-colors\u0027);\n        }, 2000);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### The Escape Key Priority Rule\n\nIn [`builder/tmpl/index.html`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/index.html), ensure that pressing `Escape` closes the documentation modal **without** closing the parent node modal underneath:\n\n```javascript\ndocument.addEventListener(\u0027keydown\u0027, (e) =\u003e {\n    if (e.key === \u0027Escape\u0027) {\n        const httpModal = document.getElementById(\u0027http-docs-modal\u0027);\n        if (httpModal \u0026\u0026 !httpModal.classList.contains(\u0027hidden\u0027)) {\n            closeHttpDocsModal();\n            return;\n        }\n        const dbModal = document.getElementById(\u0027db-docs-modal\u0027);\n        if (dbModal \u0026\u0026 !dbModal.classList.contains(\u0027hidden\u0027)) {\n            closeDbDocsModal();\n            return;\n        }\n        closeFileBrowser();\n        closeNodeModal();\n        closeComponentPicker();\n        closeDatabaseModal();\n    }\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 7: Synchronizing `builder/html_content.go`\n\n\u003e [!CAUTION]\n\u003e **Zero Inconsistency Rule**: [`builder/html_content.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/html_content.go) contains `const IndexHTML = ` + \"`\u003cindex.html\u003e`\". It must remain identical to [`builder/tmpl/index.html`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/index.html) so that the standalone binary works without disk files.\n\n#### Two Critical Pitfalls to Avoid:\n\n1. **No Raw Backticks Inside `tmpl/index.html`**:\n   In Go, raw string literals (`` `...` ``) cannot contain a backtick, even when escaped.\n   - ❌ **Do not write**: ``text.replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, ...)``\n   - ❌ **Do not write**: ``if (line.startsWith(\u0027```\u0027))``\n   - ✅ **Write instead**:\n     ```javascript\n     const fence = String.fromCharCode(96, 96, 96);\n     const inlineCodeRegex = new RegExp(\u0027\\\\x60([^\\\\x60]+)\\\\x60\u0027, \u0027g\u0027);\n     ```\n\n2. **No Unescaped Go Template Actions `{{...}}`**:\n   Go\u0027s `html/template.Parse` parses any `{{...}}` sequence as a Go template variable or action.\n   - ❌ **Do not write**: `\u0027\u003cspan\u003eSupports {{Variables}}\u003c/span\u003e\u0027` (Go will error with `function \"Variables\" not defined`)\n   - ✅ **Write instead**: `\u0027\u003cspan\u003eSupports \u0026#123;\u0026#123;Variables\u0026#125;\u0026#125;\u003c/span\u003e\u0027`\n\n#### Safe Synchronization Command:\n\nUse this PowerShell one-liner to synchronize `builder/html_content.go`:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -Command \"\u0026 { `$h = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText(\u0027builder/tmpl/index.html\u0027); `$bt = [char]96; `$content = \u0027package builder\u0027 + [Environment]::NewLine + [Environment]::NewLine + \u0027const IndexHTML = \u0027 + `$bt + `$h + `$bt + [Environment]::NewLine; [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText(\u0027builder/html_content.go\u0027, `$content, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8); Write-Host \u0027html_content.go successfully synchronized.\u0027 }\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Form Autofill Pattern (`⚡ Use Example`)\n\nOne of the most useful features of Flow Builder modal guides is the ability to directly populate form inputs from code examples.\n\nWhen markdown code blocks are parsed by `renderMarkdownToHtml`, any snippet containing a URL, connection string, or `\u003chttp_client ...\u003e` XML node includes a **`⚡ Use Example`** action button:\n\n```javascript\nfunction useSnippetInInput(codeId) {\n    const el = document.getElementById(codeId);\n    if (!el) return;\n    const text = (el.innerText || el.textContent || \u0027\u0027).trim();\n\n    // 1. If Database Modal is open\n    const dbModal = document.getElementById(\u0027database-modal\u0027);\n    if (dbModal \u0026\u0026 !dbModal.classList.contains(\u0027hidden\u0027)) {\n        const connInput = document.getElementById(\u0027db-conn-str\u0027);\n        if (connInput) {\n            connInput.value = text;\n            closeDbDocsModal();\n            return;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. If Node Modal is open in Script Editor\n    const nodeModal = document.getElementById(\u0027node-modal\u0027);\n    if (nodeModal \u0026\u0026 !nodeModal.classList.contains(\u0027hidden\u0027)) {\n        // Direct URL insertion\n        if (text.startsWith(\u0027http://\u0027) || text.startsWith(\u0027https://\u0027)) {\n            const urlInput = document.querySelector(\u0027#modal-fields-container input[name=\"attr_url\"]\u0027);\n            if (urlInput) {\n                urlInput.value = text;\n                closeHttpDocsModal();\n                return;\n            }\n        }\n        // Full \u003chttp_client ...\u003e XML snippet extraction\n        if (text.startsWith(\u0027\u003chttp_client\u0027) || text.includes(\u0027\u003chttp_client\u0027)) {\n            const urlMatch = text.match(/url=\"([^\"]+)\"/);\n            const methodMatch = text.match(/method=\"([^\"]+)\"/);\n            if (urlMatch) {\n                const u = document.querySelector(\u0027#modal-fields-container input[name=\"attr_url\"]\u0027);\n                if (u) u.value = urlMatch[1];\n            }\n            if (methodMatch) {\n                const m = document.querySelector(\u0027#modal-fields-container select[name=\"attr_method\"]\u0027);\n                if (m) m.value = methodMatch[1];\n            }\n            closeHttpDocsModal();\n            return;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 3. Fallback: Copy to system clipboard\n    navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Writing Automated Verification Tests\n\nAdd automated tests in [`builder/builder_test.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/builder_test.go) to guarantee that:\n1. Unauthorized requests to the API are rejected (`401 Unauthorized`).\n2. Authorized requests return the JSON content with expected keywords.\n3. Both `builder/tmpl/index.html` and `builder/html_content.go` contain the required modal DOM elements and triggers.\n\n```go\nfunc TestHttpClientDocAPI(t *testing.T) {\n    tmpDir := t.TempDir()\n    storage, err := NewStorage(filepath.Join(tmpDir, \"test_http_docs.db\"))\n    if err != nil {\n        t.Fatalf(\"failed to create storage: %v\", err)\n    }\n    defer storage.Close()\n\n    srv, err := NewServer(storage, 0)\n    if err != nil {\n        t.Fatalf(\"failed to create server: %v\", err)\n    }\n    srv.authToken = \"test-token\"\n\n    // 1. Unauthorized request returns 401\n    unauthReq := httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, \"/api/docs/http-client\", nil)\n    unauthRec := httptest.NewRecorder()\n    srv.Handler().ServeHTTP(unauthRec, unauthReq)\n    if unauthRec.Code != http.StatusUnauthorized {\n        t.Errorf(\"expected 401, got %d\", unauthRec.Code)\n    }\n\n    // 2. Authorized request returns 200 with JSON payload\n    authReq := httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, \"/api/docs/http-client?token=test-token\", nil)\n    authRec := httptest.NewRecorder()\n    srv.Handler().ServeHTTP(authRec, authReq)\n    if authRec.Code != http.StatusOK {\n        t.Errorf(\"expected 200, got %d\", authRec.Code)\n    }\n\n    var resp struct {\n        Filename string `json:\"filename\"`\n        Content  string `json:\"content\"`\n    }\n    json.Unmarshal(authRec.Body.Bytes(), \u0026resp)\n    if resp.Filename != \"http_client.md\" || len(resp.Content) == 0 {\n        t.Errorf(\"invalid response payload: %+v\", resp)\n    }\n}\n\nfunc TestHttpClientDocUI(t *testing.T) {\n    indexBytes, err := os.ReadFile(\"tmpl/index.html\")\n    if err != nil {\n        t.Fatalf(\"failed to read index.html: %v\", err)\n    }\n    tmplStr := string(indexBytes)\n\n    required := []string{\n        `id=\"http-docs-modal\"`,\n        `openHttpDocsModal`,\n        `closeHttpDocsModal`,\n        `updateNodeModalGuideButton`,\n        `node-modal-guide-btn`,\n        `📖 HTTP Client Guide`,\n        `/api/docs/http-client`,\n    }\n    for _, snippet := range required {\n        if !strings.Contains(tmplStr, snippet) {\n            t.Errorf(\"expected tmpl/index.html to contain %q\", snippet)\n        }\n        if !strings.Contains(IndexHTML, snippet) {\n            t.Errorf(\"expected IndexHTML in html_content.go to contain %q\", snippet)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary Checklist for Adding New Node Modals\n\nWhen adding a tooltip or documentation modal to any other node, check off each step:\n\n- [ ] **Catalog Metadata** updated in [`builder/catalog.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/catalog.go) (`Description` on fields for hover tooltips).\n- [ ] **Markdown File** created under `docs/` and copied to `builder/tmpl/docs_\u003cname\u003e.md`.\n- [ ] **Embed Directive** updated in [`builder/templates.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/templates.go).\n- [ ] **HTTP Handler** implemented and registered in [`builder/server.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/server.go) under `/api/docs/\u003ctopic\u003e`.\n- [ ] **Trigger Buttons** wired in [`renderModalFields`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/index.html) and/or [`canvas_nodes.html`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/partials/canvas_nodes.html).\n- [ ] **Modal HTML \u0026 JS** defined in [`builder/tmpl/index.html`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/tmpl/index.html).\n- [ ] **Escape Key Listener** updated so `Escape` dismisses the documentation modal first.\n- [ ] **No Raw Backticks** (`\\x60`) and **No Unescaped `{{...}}`** (`\u0026#123;\u0026#123;...\u0026#125;\u0026#125;`) in `tmpl/index.html`.\n- [ ] **Synchronized** [`builder/html_content.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/html_content.go) with `builder/tmpl/index.html`.\n- [ ] **Unit Tests** added in [`builder/builder_test.go`](file:///c:/Users/U00001/source/repos/etl-madness/go-flow/builder/builder_test.go) and passing:\n  ```powershell\n  go test -v -run \"TestHttpClientDoc\" ./builder/...\n  go test ./builder/...\n  go build -o flow.exe .\n  ```\n","guides/development_execution_guide.md":"# Executing Flow Pipelines\r\n\r\nFlow provides two public execution methods on `Executor`:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nfunc (e *Executor) Execute(ctx context.Context, nodes []PipelineNode) ([]ScriptResult, error)\r\nfunc (e *Executor) ExecuteRun(ctx context.Context, nodes []PipelineNode) (RunResult, error)\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth methods execute the same nodes through the same executor implementation. They honor the same `context.Context`, execute the same sequential, parallel, conditional, loop, transaction, and leaf-node behavior, and return a non-nil Go error when the pipeline fails or is canceled.\r\n\r\nThe difference is the returned result model.\r\n\r\n## Choose an API\r\n\r\n| Need | Use |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| Existing caller that reads script output or return codes | `Execute` |\r\n| Flat results for a simple sequential pipeline | `Execute` |\r\n| Run ID, overall run status, or start/finish timestamps | `ExecuteRun` |\r\n| Structured failure diagnosis for nested, parallel, or looped nodes | `ExecuteRun` |\r\n| Row-count reporting for SQL and bulk ETL | `ExecuteRun` |\r\n| JSON events, tracing, metrics, or an audit trail | `ExecuteRun` with an `EventSink` |\r\n\r\n## `Execute`: Flat Results\r\n\r\n`Execute` returns a `[]ScriptResult`. Each result describes a script or leaf operation in a flat list:\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype ScriptResult struct {\r\n    ScriptID      string\r\n    ReturnCode    any\r\n    ResultsString string\r\n    Duration      string\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse it when application code needs the output from a step, such as a SQL query result saved to `ResultsString`, and does not need a complete execution graph.\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor := flow.NewExecutor(registry)\r\n\r\nresults, err := executor.Execute(context.Background(), config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"pipeline failed: %w\", err)\r\n}\r\n\r\nfor _, result := range results {\r\n    fmt.Printf(\r\n        \"step=%s code=%v duration=%s\\n%s\\n\",\r\n        result.ScriptID,\r\n        result.ReturnCode,\r\n        result.Duration,\r\n        result.ResultsString,\r\n    )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`Execute` is fully compatible with callers written before run-level observability was introduced. It still collects the same flat `ScriptResult` values and returns the same generic execution error on failure.\r\n\r\n### Handling a Failure with `Execute`\r\n\r\nWhen a pipeline fails, `Execute` returns the results accumulated before execution stopped plus a non-nil error:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nresults, err := executor.Execute(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    for _, result := range results {\r\n        if result.ReturnCode != 0 {\r\n            log.Printf(\"step %s failed: %v\", result.ScriptID, result.ReturnCode)\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n    return err\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis works well for a linear workflow, but it does not provide a run ID, parent/child links, typed error categories, or a stable way to distinguish repeated executions of the same node inside a loop.\r\n\r\n## `ExecuteRun`: Structured Run Result\r\n\r\n`ExecuteRun` returns one `RunResult`, including details for the entire execution and a `NodeResult` for every node that actually starts:\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype RunResult struct {\r\n    RunID        string\r\n    StartedAt    time.Time\r\n    FinishedAt   time.Time\r\n    Status       flow.RunStatus\r\n    ErrorClass   flow.ErrorClass\r\n    ErrorMessage string\r\n    Nodes        []flow.NodeResult\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse it for production execution hosts, job runners, monitoring services, and operational tools. The method returns a populated `RunResult` even when execution fails or the context is canceled.\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor := flow.NewExecutor(registry)\r\n\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(context.Background(), config.FlowNodes)\r\nlog.Printf(\r\n    \"run=%s status=%s nodes=%d duration=%s\",\r\n    run.RunID,\r\n    run.Status,\r\n    len(run.Nodes),\r\n    run.FinishedAt.Sub(run.StartedAt),\r\n)\r\n\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    log.Printf(\r\n        \"run=%s class=%s error=%s\",\r\n        run.RunID,\r\n        run.ErrorClass,\r\n        run.ErrorMessage,\r\n    )\r\n    return err\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Inspect Failed Nodes\r\n\r\nEach `NodeResult` has a unique `ExecutionID`, its configured XML `NodeID` when present, a readable `NodePath`, optional `ParentExecutionID`, status, attempts, error classification, and row counts.\r\n\r\n```go\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    for _, node := range run.Nodes {\r\n        if node.Status != flow.RunStatusFailed \u0026\u0026 node.Status != flow.RunStatusCanceled {\r\n            continue\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        log.Printf(\r\n            \"run=%s execution=%s node=%s path=%s class=%s error=%s\",\r\n            run.RunID,\r\n            node.ExecutionID,\r\n            node.NodeID,\r\n            node.NodePath,\r\n            node.ErrorClass,\r\n            node.ErrorMessage,\r\n        )\r\n    }\r\n    return err\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor a nested XML structure, `NodePath` gives a stable structural location and `ParentExecutionID` points to the enclosing group, branch, loop, or assertion handler. Use `ExecutionID`, not `NodeID`, as the identity in external systems: a loop can execute one XML node multiple times.\r\n\r\n### Read ETL Counts\r\n\r\nWhere the operation can measure them, `NodeResult.RowCounts` reports:\r\n\r\n- `Affected` for SQL DML when the driver provides rows affected.\r\n- `Read` and `Written` for bulk SQL ETL.\r\n\r\n```go\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    return err\r\n}\r\n\r\nfor _, node := range run.Nodes {\r\n    if node.NodeKind != \"sql_bulk\" {\r\n        continue\r\n    }\r\n    log.Printf(\r\n        \"bulk node %s streamed read=%d written=%d\",\r\n        node.NodeID,\r\n        node.RowCounts.Read,\r\n        node.RowCounts.Written,\r\n    )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAn omitted count is not a confirmed zero; it means that operation does not currently expose that measurement.\r\n\r\n## The Go Error Is Still Required\r\n\r\nDo not replace the returned Go error with `RunResult.Status`. Use both:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    switch run.Status {\r\n    case flow.RunStatusCanceled:\r\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"run %s canceled: %w\", run.RunID, err)\r\n    case flow.RunStatusFailed:\r\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"run %s failed (%s): %w\", run.RunID, run.ErrorClass, err)\r\n    default:\r\n        return err\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe error controls the caller\u0027s success path. `RunResult` supplies the structured context needed for logs, retries, alerting, or incident investigation.\r\n\r\n## Add Events Without Changing Execution Semantics\r\n\r\nBoth APIs emit events when an event sink is configured, because both delegate to the same execution core. The sink does not change which method should be chosen; choose the API based on the return value your caller needs.\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor := flow.NewExecutor(registry)\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(\u0026flow.JSONLineSink{Writer: os.Stdout})\r\n\r\n// Legacy caller: events are emitted, but the returned value is still []ScriptResult.\r\nresults, err := executor.Execute(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\n\r\n// Observability-aware caller: the same events are emitted and the returned value is RunResult.\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\n```\r\n\r\nDo not call both methods for the same work. Each call starts a new pipeline run with a new run ID and executes the nodes again. Use `ExecuteRun` as the single call when a caller needs both execution and run-level observability.\r\n\r\nSee [observability_usage.md](observability_usage.md) for JSON Lines, multiple sinks, and custom sink examples.\r\n","guides/development_observability_usage.md":"# Observability Usage Guide\r\n\r\nFlow can return a structured record for each pipeline run and emit lifecycle events while work is executing. Use `ExecuteRun` when your application needs run status, node relationships, failures, or row counts. Continue using `Execute` when the existing `[]ScriptResult` result is sufficient.\r\n\r\n## Run a Pipeline and Inspect Its Result\r\n\r\n`ExecuteRun` returns a `RunResult` whether the run succeeds, fails, or is canceled. The Go error remains the signal for control flow; inspect the result for the run ID, classification, and individual node outcomes.\r\n\r\n```go\r\nctx := context.Background()\r\nexecutor := flow.NewExecutor(registry)\r\n\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    log.Printf(\"run %s finished with %s: %s\", run.RunID, run.ErrorClass, run.ErrorMessage)\r\n}\r\n\r\nlog.Printf(\r\n    \"run=%s status=%s started=%s finished=%s nodes=%d\",\r\n    run.RunID,\r\n    run.Status,\r\n    run.StartedAt.Format(time.RFC3339),\r\n    run.FinishedAt.Format(time.RFC3339),\r\n    len(run.Nodes),\r\n)\r\n\r\nfor _, node := range run.Nodes {\r\n    log.Printf(\r\n        \"node=%s id=%s status=%s path=%s parent=%s error_class=%s error=%s\",\r\n        node.ExecutionID,\r\n        node.NodeID,\r\n        node.Status,\r\n        node.NodePath,\r\n        node.ParentExecutionID,\r\n        node.ErrorClass,\r\n        node.ErrorMessage,\r\n    )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe run status is one of:\r\n\r\n- `flow.RunStatusSucceeded`: every executed node completed successfully.\r\n- `flow.RunStatusFailed`: a node failed and the executor stopped the pipeline.\r\n- `flow.RunStatusCanceled`: the context was canceled or reached its deadline.\r\n\r\nEach `NodeResult` currently has exactly one `AttemptResult`. The attempts collection is present so retry support can add future attempts without changing the result format.\r\n\r\n## Configure `SetEventSink`\r\n\r\nCall `SetEventSink` before `Execute` or `ExecuteRun` to register one destination for structured lifecycle events:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor := flow.NewExecutor(registry)\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(sink)\r\n\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe sink receives `run.started` before node work begins and `run.finished` after the executor has finalized the `RunResult`. Each executed node also emits `node.started`, `attempt.started`, `attempt.finished`, and `node.finished` events. Sinks do not require any XML configuration.\r\n\r\n`SetEventSink` replaces every previously registered sink. Pass `nil` to disable event emission for later runs:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(\u0026flow.JSONLineSink{Writer: os.Stdout})\r\n_, _ = executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\n\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(nil)\r\n_, _ = executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes) // Does not emit events.\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor more than one destination, use `SetEventSinks` rather than calling `SetEventSink` repeatedly:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor.SetEventSinks(\r\n    \u0026flow.JSONLineSink{Writer: os.Stdout},\r\n    auditSink,\r\n)\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Print Failed Nodes Only\r\n\r\nThis sink sends a compact message to the standard logger only when an executed node fails or is canceled:\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype FailureLogSink struct{}\r\n\r\nfunc (FailureLogSink) Emit(_ context.Context, event flow.ExecutionEvent) error {\r\n    if event.Type != flow.EventNodeFinished || event.Status == flow.RunStatusSucceeded {\r\n        return nil\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    log.Printf(\r\n        \"run=%s node=%s id=%s status=%s class=%s error=%s\",\r\n        event.RunID,\r\n        event.ExecutionID,\r\n        event.NodeID,\r\n        event.Status,\r\n        event.ErrorClass,\r\n        event.ErrorMessage,\r\n    )\r\n    return nil\r\n}\r\n\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(FailureLogSink{})\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Capture Events in a Test\r\n\r\nCustom sinks may be invoked by concurrent pipeline branches. Protect mutable in-memory state with a mutex when collecting events:\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype MemorySink struct {\r\n    mu     sync.Mutex\r\n    events []flow.ExecutionEvent\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunc (s *MemorySink) Emit(_ context.Context, event flow.ExecutionEvent) error {\r\n    s.mu.Lock()\r\n    defer s.mu.Unlock()\r\n    s.events = append(s.events, event)\r\n    return nil\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunc (s *MemorySink) Events() []flow.ExecutionEvent {\r\n    s.mu.Lock()\r\n    defer s.mu.Unlock()\r\n    return append([]flow.ExecutionEvent(nil), s.events...)\r\n}\r\n\r\nsink := \u0026MemorySink{}\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(sink)\r\n\r\n_, err := executor.ExecuteRun(context.Background(), config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    t.Fatal(err)\r\n}\r\n\r\nevents := sink.Events()\r\nif events[0].Type != flow.EventRunStarted {\r\n    t.Fatalf(\"first event = %s, want %s\", events[0].Type, flow.EventRunStarted)\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAn error returned from `Emit` is intentionally ignored by the executor. This prevents a logging, tracing, or metrics outage from changing pipeline execution. A sink that requires reliable delivery should buffer or persist work internally and report its own health separately.\r\n\r\n## Write JSON Lines Events\r\n\r\n`JSONLineSink` writes one `ExecutionEvent` as a JSON object followed by a newline. This format works with common log collectors and makes it easy to filter by `run_id`, `node_kind`, `status`, or `error_class`.\r\n\r\n```go\r\nlogFile, err := os.OpenFile(\"flow-events.jsonl\", os.O_APPEND|os.O_CREATE|os.O_WRONLY, 0o644)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    return err\r\n}\r\ndefer logFile.Close()\r\n\r\nexecutor := flow.NewExecutor(registry)\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(\u0026flow.JSONLineSink{Writer: logFile})\r\n\r\nrun, err := executor.ExecuteRun(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\nif err != nil {\r\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"flow run %s failed: %w\", run.RunID, err)\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nTo write events to standard output, use `os.Stdout` as the writer:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(\u0026flow.JSONLineSink{Writer: os.Stdout})\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Control Event Output Format\r\n\r\n`SetEventSink` selects the component that receives events. With `JSONLineSink`, the output format is fixed: one JSON-encoded `ExecutionEvent` per line. `Writer` controls the destination, such as a file, standard output, or a network-backed writer; it does not alter the JSON field selection or indentation.\r\n\r\nTo change the output format, implement `flow.EventSink` and register it with `SetEventSink`.\r\n\r\n### Compact Text Output\r\n\r\nUse a custom sink to produce human-readable lines rather than JSON:\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype TextSink struct {\r\n    Writer io.Writer\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunc (s TextSink) Emit(_ context.Context, event flow.ExecutionEvent) error {\r\n    _, err := fmt.Fprintf(\r\n        s.Writer,\r\n        \"%s run=%s type=%s node=%s status=%s error=%s\\n\",\r\n        event.OccurredAt.Format(time.RFC3339),\r\n        event.RunID,\r\n        event.Type,\r\n        event.NodeID,\r\n        event.Status,\r\n        event.ErrorMessage,\r\n    )\r\n    return err\r\n}\r\n\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(TextSink{Writer: os.Stdout})\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Indented JSON Output\r\n\r\nFor formatted JSON, marshal each event with `json.MarshalIndent`. The mutex prevents parallel branches from interleaving writes:\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype PrettyJSONSink struct {\r\n    Writer io.Writer\r\n    mu     sync.Mutex\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunc (s *PrettyJSONSink) Emit(_ context.Context, event flow.ExecutionEvent) error {\r\n    s.mu.Lock()\r\n    defer s.mu.Unlock()\r\n\r\n    data, err := json.MarshalIndent(event, \"\", \"  \")\r\n    if err != nil {\r\n        return err\r\n    }\r\n    _, err = fmt.Fprintln(s.Writer, string(data))\r\n    return err\r\n}\r\n\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(\u0026PrettyJSONSink{Writer: os.Stdout})\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Reduced Audit JSON\r\n\r\nMap `ExecutionEvent` to an application-specific structure when downstream systems should receive only selected fields. This is also the appropriate place to add application metadata, as long as it does not contain sensitive values.\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype AuditEvent struct {\r\n    Time   time.Time      `json:\"time\"`\r\n    RunID  string         `json:\"run_id\"`\r\n    Event  flow.EventType `json:\"event\"`\r\n    NodeID string         `json:\"node_id,omitempty\"`\r\n    Status flow.RunStatus `json:\"status,omitempty\"`\r\n    Error  string         `json:\"error,omitempty\"`\r\n}\r\n\r\ntype AuditSink struct {\r\n    Writer io.Writer\r\n    mu     sync.Mutex\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunc (s *AuditSink) Emit(_ context.Context, event flow.ExecutionEvent) error {\r\n    audit := AuditEvent{\r\n        Time:   event.OccurredAt,\r\n        RunID:  event.RunID,\r\n        Event:  event.Type,\r\n        NodeID: event.NodeID,\r\n        Status: event.Status,\r\n        Error:  event.ErrorMessage,\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    s.mu.Lock()\r\n    defer s.mu.Unlock()\r\n    return json.NewEncoder(s.Writer).Encode(audit)\r\n}\r\n\r\nexecutor.SetEventSink(\u0026AuditSink{Writer: os.Stdout})\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse `SetEventSinks` to retain the built-in JSON Lines output while emitting a second representation:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor.SetEventSinks(\r\n    \u0026flow.JSONLineSink{Writer: jsonLogFile},\r\n    TextSink{Writer: os.Stdout},\r\n)\r\n```\r\n\r\nA typical event resembles this:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"sequence\": 6,\r\n  \"type\": \"node.finished\",\r\n  \"occurred_at\": \"2026-09-03T12:00:01Z\",\r\n  \"run_id\": \"4b7e3b2a5d4f...\",\r\n  \"execution_id\": \"c0a8c3f6452e...\",\r\n  \"parent_execution_id\": \"7683f0a2a9bd...\",\r\n  \"node_id\": \"load_customers\",\r\n  \"node_kind\": \"sql_bulk\",\r\n  \"node_path\": \"group[0]/sql_bulk[0]\",\r\n  \"attempt\": 1,\r\n  \"status\": \"succeeded\",\r\n  \"row_counts\": {\"read\": 50000, \"written\": 50000}\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nEvents are emitted in this lifecycle order for a run and each node:\r\n\r\n1. `run.started`\r\n2. `node.started`\r\n3. `attempt.started`\r\n4. `attempt.finished`\r\n5. `node.finished`\r\n6. `run.finished`\r\n\r\nThe `sequence` field increases for each event within a run. Parallel branches may complete in different wall-clock order, but the sequence provides a single ordering for a consumer.\r\n\r\n## Use Multiple Sinks\r\n\r\nRegister more than one destination with `SetEventSinks`:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nexecutor.SetEventSinks(\r\n    \u0026flow.JSONLineSink{Writer: os.Stdout},\r\n    auditSink,\r\n)\r\n```\r\n\r\nCalling `SetEventSink` or `SetEventSinks` replaces the previous sink configuration. Sinks are called for every event in registration order. A sink error is ignored so an unavailable telemetry destination cannot change pipeline execution; applications that need delivery guarantees should make the sink durable and monitor the sink independently.\r\n\r\n## Implement a Custom Sink\r\n\r\nImplement `EventSink` to forward events to a logging, tracing, or metrics system. An event sink can be called concurrently by parallel pipeline branches, so protect mutable state with a mutex, channel, or a concurrency-safe client.\r\n\r\n```go\r\ntype MetricsSink struct {\r\n    mu      sync.Mutex\r\n    finished map[flow.RunStatus]int64\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunc (s *MetricsSink) Emit(_ context.Context, event flow.ExecutionEvent) error {\r\n    if event.Type != flow.EventNodeFinished {\r\n        return nil\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    s.mu.Lock()\r\n    defer s.mu.Unlock()\r\n    s.finished[event.Status]++\r\n    return nil\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse the same pattern to create an OpenTelemetry bridge: start a span on `node.started`, key it by `ExecutionID`, use `ParentExecutionID` to find its parent span, attach non-sensitive fields such as `NodeKind`, `Status`, and `ErrorClass`, then end it on `node.finished`. The base `flow` package deliberately has no OpenTelemetry or metrics backend dependency.\r\n\r\n## Navigate Nested Results\r\n\r\n`RunResult.Nodes` is a flat collection because it remains easy to serialize and query. Reconstruct the execution tree by matching `ParentExecutionID` to `ExecutionID`.\r\n\r\n`NodePath` is a readable structural address. For example, `group[0]/parallel[1]/script[0]` describes a script beneath a parallel block inside a group. `NodeID` is the XML `id` when configured, while `ExecutionID` uniquely identifies a specific execution. Use `ExecutionID` rather than `NodeID` for loops and parallel work, where the same configured node can execute more than once.\r\n\r\n## Interpret Row Counts and Errors\r\n\r\n`RowCounts` contains only measurements known to the executed node:\r\n\r\n- SQL DML exposes `Affected` when the driver returns rows affected.\r\n- Bulk SQL ETL (`\u003csql_bulk\u003e`) exposes `Read` and `Written` from the number of streamed rows.\r\n- Key-Value operations (`\u003ckv\u003e`) expose `Affected` for writes and deletions (`put`, `set`, `delete`, `del`), and `Read` for lookups and prefix scans (`get`, `scan`, `list`).\r\n- Key-Value bulk transfers (`\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`) expose `Read` and `Written` from the number of streamed records transferred from SQL to the KV store.\r\n- Excel reads and writes expose `Read` and `Written` row metrics.\r\n- Other nodes currently leave row counts empty.\r\n\r\nAn omitted count means the executor did not measure that value. Do not treat it as a confirmed zero.\r\n\r\n`ErrorClass` is designed for filtering and alert routing. Current values include `canceled`, `validation`, `database`, `http`, `filesystem`, `script`, `template`, `data_format`, and `unknown`. Preserve the Go error returned by `ExecuteRun` for detailed handling; the classified fields are a summary rather than a replacement.\r\n\r\nEvents do not include SQL text, request/response bodies, headers, or pipeline variables. Common credential fragments in recorded error messages, such as `password=...` and `token=...`, are redacted. Applications should still avoid placing sensitive values in custom event fields or logs.\r\n\r\n## Keep Existing Callers Unchanged\r\n\r\nThe original API remains available and keeps its existing behavior:\r\n\r\n```go\r\nresults, err := executor.Execute(ctx, config.FlowNodes)\r\n```\r\n\r\nAdopt `ExecuteRun` where structured observability is needed. No XML changes are required for either API.\r\n","guides/development_themes_guide.md":"# Flow Builder Theme Customization Guide\n\nThis guide explains how the Flow Builder theming system works and provides step-by-step instructions for adding new themes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Theme Engine Architecture\n\nFlow Builder\u0027s theme engine operates via client-side DOM manipulation and CSS styling:\n\n1. **Root Attribute**: The theme is set on the root `\u003chtml\u003e` element using the `data-theme` attribute (e.g. `\u003chtml data-theme=\"soft-white\"\u003e`).\n2. **Persistence**: The chosen theme is automatically persisted in browser `localStorage` under the key `flow_builder_theme`. When the page loads, `loadSavedTheme()` retrieves and applies the stored theme immediately.\n3. **Template Synchronization**: Flow Builder uses two synchronized files for its UI markup:\n   - `builder/html_content.go`: Contains the compiled Go constant string `IndexHTML`.\n   - `builder/tmpl/index.html`: Contains the raw template counterpart.\n   *(Any theme added to one must also be added to the other to ensure consistency across build configurations and tests).*\n\n---\n\n## 2. Built-In Themes\n\nFlow Builder includes a broad range of themes to accommodate different environments and preferences:\n\n| Theme Key | Name | Type | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `slate` | **Slate (Default)** | Dark | Default deep slate palette (`#020617` / `#0f172a`) |\n| `midnight` | **Midnight Blue** | Dark | Deep navy with soft blue highlights |\n| `nord` | **Nord Frost** | Dark | Arctic ice-blue Nordic palette |\n| `emerald` | **Emerald Matrix** | Dark | High-tech emerald and mint palette |\n| `amber` | **Warm Amber** | Dark | Low-fatigue golden amber tones |\n| `cyberpunk` | **Cyberpunk Neon** | Dark | High-contrast neon cyan and magenta accents |\n| `vscode-dark-plus` | **VS Code Dark+** | Dark | Classic VS Code dark UI theme |\n| `vscode-light-plus` | **VS Code Light+** | Light | Classic VS Code light UI theme |\n| `vscode-monokai` | **VS Code Monokai** | Dark | Monokai code palette with warm accents |\n| `vscode-dracula` | **VS Code Dracula** | Dark | Gothic purple and vibrant pastel accents |\n| `visual-studio-dark` | **Visual Studio Dark** | Dark | Visual Studio IDE charcoal palette |\n| `visual-studio-light` | **Visual Studio Light** | Light | Classic Visual Studio light gray/blue palette |\n| `visual-studio-blue` | **Visual Studio Blue** | Custom | Iconic Visual Studio blue IDE theme |\n| `soft-white` | **Soft White (Blue \u0026 Black)** | Light | Gentle soft white background with deep black typography and vibrant blue accents |\n| `light` | **Clean Light** | Light | Inverted high-contrast light theme |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Theme Implementation Approaches\n\nThere are two approaches to theming in Flow Builder:\n\n### Approach A: CSS Filter / Variable Theming (Quick)\nUseful for subtle tone shifts or dark variants where the default dark structure remains intact:\n```css\nhtml[data-theme=\"nord\"] {\n    filter: hue-rotate(190deg) brightness(0.95) saturate(1.1);\n}\n```\n\n### Approach B: Explicit Tailwind Utility Overrides (Recommended for True Themes)\nRequired for light themes (such as **Soft White**) or distinct custom color schemes (such as **Visual Studio Blue**). This explicitly overrides Tailwind classes used across the studio:\n- `.bg-slate-950`: Canvas background, deep containers, inputs, terminal blocks.\n- `.bg-slate-900`: Sidebars, header, modals, section containers.\n- `.node-card`: Leaf steps and container cards on the canvas.\n- `.bg-slate-800`, `.bg-slate-700`: Buttons, subtle tags, inactive tab pills.\n- `.border-slate-800`, `.border-slate-700`: Structural borders and divider lines.\n- `.text-slate-100`, `.text-slate-200`, `.text-white`: Primary text, titles, headings.\n- `.text-slate-400`, `.text-slate-500`: Secondary text, metadata, descriptions.\n- `.text-cyan-400`, `.text-blue-400`: Accents, step tags, highlights, links.\n- `.bg-blue-600`: Primary action buttons and active tabs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Step-by-Step: Adding a New Theme\n\n### Step 1: Add Theme Root Declaration\nIn `\u003cstyle\u003e` in both `builder/html_content.go` and `builder/tmpl/index.html`:\n```css\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] {\n    --bg-body: #f8fafc;\n    filter: none;\n}\n```\n\n### Step 2: Add CSS Overrides\nTarget the core layout classes for your theme:\n```css\n/* Custom Theme: Soft White with Blue \u0026 Black */\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"],\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] body {\n    background-color: #f8fafc !important;\n    color: #0f172a !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .bg-slate-950 {\n    background-color: #f8fafc !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .bg-slate-900,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] header,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] aside {\n    background-color: #ffffff !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .node-card,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .component-card {\n    background-color: #ffffff !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .component-card:hover,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .node-card:hover {\n    background-color: #f8fafc !important;\n    border-color: #2563eb !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .border-slate-800,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .border-slate-700 {\n    border-color: #cbd5e1 !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .text-slate-100,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .text-slate-200,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .text-white {\n    color: #0f172a !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .text-slate-400,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .text-slate-500 {\n    color: #334155 !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .text-cyan-400,\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .text-blue-400 {\n    color: #1d4ed8 !important;\n}\nhtml[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"] .bg-blue-600 {\n    background-color: #1d4ed8 !important;\n    color: #ffffff !important;\n}\n```\n\n### Step 3: Add the Option to the Theme Selector\nAdd your `\u003coption\u003e` inside `\u003cselect id=\"theme-selector\"\u003e` in both files:\n```html\n\u003coption value=\"my-custom-theme\" class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-200\"\u003eMy Custom Theme\u003c/option\u003e\n```\n\n### Step 4: Add Automated Unit Tests\nAdd assertions in `builder/builder_test.go` to ensure that templates and the live HTTP handler contain the new theme:\n```go\nfunc TestMyCustomTheme(t *testing.T) {\n    requiredSnippets := []string{\n        `html[data-theme=\"my-custom-theme\"]`,\n        `\u003coption value=\"my-custom-theme\"`,\n        `My Custom Theme`,\n    }\n    // verify tmpl/index.html, IndexHTML, and http GET \"/\"\n}\n```\n\n### Step 5: Verify\nRun the test suite:\n```bash\ngo test ./builder -v\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Reference: The Soft White Theme\n\nThe **Soft White (Blue \u0026 Black)** theme (`soft-white`) serves as a prime reference implementation:\n\n- **Canvas Background**: `#f8fafc` (Slate 50 soft white).\n- **Cards \u0026 Surfaces**: `#ffffff` (Clean white) with `#cbd5e1` crisp borders.\n- **Primary Typography**: Deep black (`#0f172a`) and dark slate (`#334155`).\n- **Primary Accents \u0026 Actions**: Royal blue (`#1d4ed8` / `#2563eb`).\n- **Inputs \u0026 Terminal**: Pure white backgrounds with dark black text and blue focus rings.\n","guides/ENCRYPTION.md":"# Cross-Platform Encryption Guide for Flow\r\n\r\nFlow provides native, cross-platform AES-256-GCM encryption and decryption across **Windows**, **Linux**, **macOS**, and **FreeBSD** with zero external runtime dependencies.\r\n\r\nThis system allows you to protect sensitive pipeline configurations, connection strings, credentials, and XML option files both at rest in the filesystem and inside database repositories (`dbo.flow_pipeline_content`, `dbo.flow_options_content`, `dbo.flow_config_content`, or SQLite builder databases).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Cryptographic Architecture\r\n\r\n* **Cipher:** AES-256 in Galois/Counter Mode (**AES-256-GCM**), providing authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD).\r\n* **Key Derivation:** **PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256** with **100,000 iterations** and a cryptographically secure 16-byte random salt generated via `crypto/rand`.\r\n* **Tamper Proofing:** 128-bit (16-byte) authentication tag; any modification, bit flip, or truncated payload is automatically rejected with an authentication error before any processing occurs.\r\n* **Pure Go Implementation:** Implemented with Go\u0027s standard library (`crypto/aes`, `crypto/cipher`, `crypto/rand`, `crypto/sha256`) and pure-Go PBKDF2 (`golang.org/x/crypto/pbkdf2`). No CGO, Windows DPAPI, or macOS Keychain dependencies required.\r\n* **In-Memory Zeroization:** Derived symmetric AES key buffers are zeroed out (`ZeroBytes`) immediately upon cipher initialization to prevent plaintext key exposure in memory dumps.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Text-Safe Envelope Specification\r\n\r\nTo ensure encrypted data can be safely stored in database string columns (such as SQL Server `NVARCHAR(MAX)` or SQLite `TEXT`) without Unicode or byte-encoding corruption, Flow formats ciphertexts into an armored text envelope:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFLOWENC:v1:\u003cbase64(salt || nonce || ciphertext || auth_tag)\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n* `FLOWENC:v1:` Header prefix identifying the envelope version.\r\n* `salt`: 16-byte random salt used during PBKDF2 derivation.\r\n* `nonce`: 12-byte standard GCM initialization vector / nonce.\r\n* `ciphertext`: AES-256-GCM encrypted payload.\r\n* `auth_tag`: 16-byte authentication tag verifying payload integrity.\r\n\r\nThe envelope prefix enables `flow.exe`, `db_importer`, and `crypto_tool` to automatically detect encrypted files and provide helpful error messages if a key is missing.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Key Management \u0026 Precedence\r\n \r\nEncryption and decryption keys can be passed through key files, CLI flags, or environment variables:\r\n\r\n| Priority | Method | Description |\r\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **1 (Highest)** | `-key-file \u003cpath\u003e` | Path to a secure key file. **Recommended** to avoid exposing plaintext passphrases in OS process listings (`ps`, Task Manager). |\r\n| **2** | `-secure-key \u003ckey\u003e` | Command-line parameter supplied directly to `flow.exe`, `db_importer`, or `crypto_tool`. *(Note: emits a warning to stderr recommending `-key-file`)*. |\r\n| **3** | `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` | Environment variable (ideal for containerized and automated production runs). |\r\n| **4** | `SECURE_KEY` | Generic fallback environment variable. |\r\n\r\n\u003e [!TIP]\r\n\u003e For production automation and scheduled jobs, always supply keys via `-key-file` or `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`. Passing plaintext keys via CLI arguments (`-secure-key`) makes them visible to other local processes and unprivileged users inspecting process arguments.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Standalone Tool: `crypto_tool`\r\n\r\nFlow includes a dedicated CLI utility located in `crypto_tool/` to generate keys and perform offline encryption/decryption.\r\n\r\n### Generate a Cryptographically Secure Key\r\n```bash\r\n# Generate a new 256-bit base64-encoded key\r\ncrypto_tool -gen-key\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Encrypt a File\r\n```bash\r\n# Encrypt an XML pipeline to text-armored format\r\ncrypto_tool -action encrypt -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.enc -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n\r\n# Encrypt using an environment variable\r\nexport FLOW_SECURE_KEY=\"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\ncrypto_tool -action encrypt -in config.xml -out config.xml.enc\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Decrypt a File\r\n```bash\r\ncrypto_tool -action decrypt -in pipeline.xml.enc -out pipeline.xml -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Raw Binary Mode (Optional)\r\nIf you specifically need a raw binary file instead of the armored text envelope:\r\n```bash\r\ncrypto_tool -action encrypt -in secret.bin -out secret.enc -binary -secure-key \"Key123\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Database Importer: `db_importer`\r\n\r\nThe `db_importer` tool imports and exports pipelines, options, and configs to/from SQL Server repositories, with support for encryption at rest:\r\n\r\n### Import and Encrypt into SQL Server\r\n```bash\r\n# Encrypts the local file before inserting/merging into dbo.flow_pipeline_content\r\ndb_importer -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\" \\\r\n            -table pipeline \\\r\n            -name \"daily_customer_sync\" \\\r\n            -file \"pipelines/sync.xml\" \\\r\n            -encrypted \\\r\n            -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Export from SQL Server and Decrypt\r\n```bash\r\n# Fetches from dbo.flow_pipeline_content, decrypts in memory, and writes plain XML to disk\r\ndb_importer -action export \\\r\n            -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\" \\\r\n            -table pipeline \\\r\n            -name \"daily_customer_sync\" \\\r\n            -file \"sync_decrypted.xml\" \\\r\n            -encrypted \\\r\n            -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Running Encrypted Pipelines with `flow.exe`\r\n\r\n`flow.exe` can execute encrypted pipelines, options, and configuration overrides whether they reside on disk, an HTTP endpoint, or inside a SQL database.\r\n\r\n### Running Encrypted Files from Disk\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -file pipeline.xml.enc -encrypted -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Running Encrypted Pipelines from a Database\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -encrypted -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\" \\\r\n         -file \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=master#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name = \u0027daily_customer_sync\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Running Encrypted Options from a Database\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -encrypted -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\" \\\r\n         -options \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=master#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name = \u0027PROD_OPTIONS\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Auto-Detection\r\nIf `flow.exe` loads a file or database record beginning with `FLOWENC:v1:`, it automatically detects that the resource is encrypted and uses the configured key (`-secure-key` or `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`) without requiring the `-encrypted` flag.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. `flow.exe` Draft Import \u0026 Export\r\n\r\n`flow.exe` can also export and import drafts directly from the local SQLite builder database (`flow_builder.db`) or external databases:\r\n\r\n### Exporting an Encrypted Pipeline Draft\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -export-file pipeline_backup.xml.enc \\\r\n         -export-name \"daily_etl\" \\\r\n         -export-type pipeline \\\r\n         -encrypted \\\r\n         -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Importing an Encrypted Draft into Builder Storage\r\n```bash\r\n# Decrypts the draft on import so it can be visually inspected and edited in the builder\r\nflow.exe -import-file pipeline_backup.xml.enc \\\r\n         -import-name \"daily_etl\" \\\r\n         -import-type pipeline \\\r\n         -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 8. Digital Signatures \u0026 Security Architecture\r\n\r\nFlow includes comprehensive digital signature creation and verification supporting OpenSSL (RSA, ECDSA, Ed25519), OpenPGP / GPG, and PKCS#7 / CMS in pure Go with zero OS dependencies.\r\n\r\n* **Decoupled Security Pipeline:** Signature verification occurs prior to decryption (**Verify -\u003e Decrypt -\u003e Parse -\u003e Execute**), preventing tampered or unauthorized payloads from being processed.\r\n* **Full Documentation:** See [**`SIGNATURES.md`**](SIGNATURES.md) for complete instructions, key generation guides, and engine flags (`-verify-signature`, `-public-key`, `-cert`, `-ca-cert`, `-keyring`, `-signature`).\r\n\r\n","guides/FAILURE_RESPONSE.md":"# Pipeline Failure Response \u0026 Error Propagation\n\n**By default, `go-flow` uses fail-fast execution.** When a node fails, the remaining nodes in the pipeline will **not** continue.\n\nThis document details how failure propagation, cooperative cancellation, transactional rollbacks, and explicit recovery blocks work across every orchestration primitive in the `flow` runtime engine.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Matrix\n\n| Orchestration Context | Default Behavior on Node Failure | Remaining Sibling Nodes | Downstream Pipeline Nodes | Special Recovery / Cleanup |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sequential (`\u003cflow\u003e`, `\u003cpreflight\u003e`, `\u003cgroup\u003e`, `\u003cif\u003e`)** | **Fail-Fast** | Skipped immediately | Skipped | None (unless inside `\u003cassert\u003e`) |\n| **Parallel (`\u003cparallel\u003e`)** | **Cooperative Cancel** | Queued tasks skipped; in-flight workers receive `ctx.Done()` | Skipped after `wg.Wait()` | Worker variables only merged after barrier |\n| **Loops (`\u003cforeach\u003e`, `\u003cwhile\u003e`)** | **Abort Loop** | Remaining children in current iteration \u0026 future iterations skipped | Skipped | Active DB cursor closed immediately |\n| **Transactional Group (`\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`)** | **Rollback \u0026 Halt** | Skipped immediately | Skipped | Automatic `ROLLBACK` (or `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` if nested) |\n| **Assertion (`\u003cassert on_failure=\"halt\"\u003e`)** | **Run Fallback, Then Halt** | Skipped | Skipped | Executes nested `\u003con_failure\u003e` child nodes before halting |\n| **Assertion (`\u003cassert on_failure=\"warn\"\u003e` / `\"continue\"`)** | **Warn \u0026 Continue** | **Executed normally** | **Executed normally** | Sets `fail_var`/`fail_val` and runs `\u003con_failure\u003e` children |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Sequential Nodes (`\u003cflow\u003e`, `\u003cpreflight\u003e`, `\u003cgroup\u003e`, `\u003cif\u003e`)\n\nIn `Executor.executeNodes`, pipeline nodes are evaluated sequentially inside a `for` loop. Each node handler returns a boolean `hasErr`:\n\n* As soon as any node returns `hasErr == true`, `executeNodes` emits the node failure event to registered sinks (`pipeline_events`), appends the `ScriptResult`, and immediately returns `true` to its caller.\n* All subsequent sibling nodes and parent containers are bypassed.\n* `Executor.executeRun` records the overall run status as `failed` in `pipeline_runs`, and `go-flow` exits with return code `1`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Start([\"Start Pipeline Run\"]) --\u003e N1[\"Execute Node 1\"]\n    N1 --\u003e Check1{\"hasErr == true?\"}\n    Check1 -- \"No (Success)\" --\u003e N2[\"Execute Node 2\"]\n    Check1 -- \"Yes (Failed)\" --\u003e EmitFail[\"Emit Node Failed Event\u003cbr/\u003e(pipeline_events)\"]\n    \n    N2 --\u003e Check2{\"hasErr == true?\"}\n    Check2 -- \"No (Success)\" --\u003e N3[\"Execute Node 3\"]\n    Check2 -- \"Yes (Failed)\" --\u003e EmitFail\n\n    N3 -. \"Skipped on Node 1/2 Failure\" .-\u003e EmitFail\n    EmitFail --\u003e FinishRun[\"Mark Run Status = \u0027failed\u0027\u003cbr/\u003e(pipeline_runs)\"]\n    FinishRun --\u003e Exit1([\"OS Exit(1)\"])\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Parallel Blocks (`\u003cparallel\u003e`)\n\nIn `Executor.executeParallelNode`, child nodes run concurrently across a bounded worker pool (`max_threads`, defaulting to `4`) sharing a derived cancellable context (`pCtx, cancel := context.WithCancel(ctx)`).\n\nWhen any child worker fails (`childErr == true`):\n1. **Immediate Context Cancellation**: The failing worker sets an atomic flag (`hasErr.Store(true)`) and invokes `cancel()` on `pCtx`.\n2. **Queued Siblings Skipped**: The dispatcher loop checks `hasErr.Load() || pCtx.Err() != nil` before acquiring the semaphore and immediately stops scheduling any remaining unstarted child nodes in the `\u003cparallel\u003e` block.\n3. **In-Flight Siblings Notified**: Currently executing sibling goroutines receive the cancellation signal via `pCtx.Done()`, which aborts context-aware operations (such as SQL queries via `QueryContext`/`ExecContext`, shell/CSX scripts via `exec.CommandContext`, HTTP requests, and loops).\n4. **Join Barrier \u0026 Halt**: `wg.Wait()` waits for all active workers to finish cleaning up. Because `executeParallelNode` returns `true`, **no downstream nodes after `\u003cparallel\u003e` will execute**.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Main as Main Executor\n    participant Pool as \u003cparallel\u003e Dispatcher (pCtx)\n    participant W1 as Worker 1 (Fails)\n    participant W2 as Worker 2 (In-Flight)\n    participant W3 as Worker 3 (Queued)\n\n    Main-\u003e\u003ePool: Enter \u003cparallel max_threads=\"2\"\u003e\n    Pool-\u003e\u003eW1: Spawn Worker 1 (pCtx)\n    Pool-\u003e\u003eW2: Spawn Worker 2 (pCtx)\n    Note over W3: Waiting for semaphore slot\n\n    W1--xPool: Node fails (childErr = true)\n    Pool-\u003e\u003ePool: hasErr.Store(true) \u0026 cancel(pCtx)\n    Pool--\u003e\u003eW2: pCtx.Done() signal delivered\n    Pool--xW3: Dispatcher breaks loop (Worker 3 never starts)\n    W2--\u003e\u003ePool: Aborts context-aware op \u0026 exits\n    Pool-\u003e\u003eMain: wg.Wait() completes -\u003e returns hasErr = true\n    Note over Main: Downstream pipeline nodes skipped\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Iterative Loops (`\u003cforeach\u003e`, `\u003cwhile\u003e`)\n\nBoth `\u003cforeach\u003e` (`Executor.executeForEachNode`, in both streaming and buffered modes) and `\u003cwhile\u003e` (`Executor.executeWhileNode`) evaluate their child nodes sequentially on each iteration via `e.executeNodes(ctx, node.Children, results)`.\n\n* If any child node fails during iteration $k$:\n  * Remaining child nodes in iteration $k$ are skipped.\n  * For `\u003cforeach\u003e`, the active database `sql.Rows` cursor is immediately closed (`rows.Close()`).\n  * Subsequent iterations ($k+1, \\dots, N$) are aborted.\n  * The error bubbles up and halts the rest of the pipeline.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    LoopStart([\"Enter \u003cforeach\u003e / \u003cwhile\u003e\"]) --\u003e Cond{\"Next Row /\u003cbr/\u003eCondition True?\"}\n    Cond -- \"No\" --\u003e LoopDone([\"Loop Complete (Continue Pipeline)\"])\n    Cond -- \"Yes\" --\u003e Child1[\"Iteration k: Child Node 1\"]\n    Child1 --\u003e CheckC1{\"hasErr?\"}\n    CheckC1 -- \"No\" --\u003e Child2[\"Iteration k: Child Node 2\"]\n    CheckC1 -- \"Yes\" --\u003e AbortLoop[\"Close DB Cursor (if \u003cforeach\u003e)\u003cbr/\u003eSkip Child Node 2 \u0026 Future Iterations\"]\n    Child2 --\u003e CheckC2{\"hasErr?\"}\n    CheckC2 -- \"No\" --\u003e Cond\n    CheckC2 -- \"Yes\" --\u003e AbortLoop\n    AbortLoop --\u003e Halt([\"Bubble Error Up \u0026 Halt Pipeline\"])\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Transactional Groups (`\u003cgroup transaction=\"true\"\u003e`)\n\nWhen a `\u003cgroup\u003e` has `transaction=\"true\"` and a target `db` configured:\n\n* **Top-Level Transaction**: Begins a database transaction (`dbConn.BeginTx`) and pushes it onto the executor\u0027s active transaction stack for that database.\n* **Nested Transaction on Same DB**: Creates a dialect-aware savepoint (`SAVE TRANSACTION sp_\u003ctimestamp\u003e` on SQL Server or `SAVEPOINT sp_\u003ctimestamp\u003e` on PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, Oracle).\n* **On Child Failure**:\n  1. Remaining child nodes inside the `\u003cgroup\u003e` are skipped.\n  2. The `defer` handler detects `!committed` and automatically executes `tx.Rollback()` (or `ROLLBACK TRANSACTION` / `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` for nested transactions).\n  3. The transaction is popped from the stack and the pipeline halts.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    EnterTx([\"Enter \u003cgroup transaction=\u0027true\u0027 db=\u0027target_db\u0027\u003e\"]) --\u003e CheckNested{\"Active Tx on\u003cbr/\u003e\u0027target_db\u0027?\"}\n    CheckNested -- \"No\" --\u003e BeginTx[\"dbConn.BeginTx()\"]\n    CheckNested -- \"Yes\" --\u003e CreateSP[\"Create Savepoint\u003cbr/\u003e(SAVEPOINT / SAVE TRANSACTION)\"]\n\n    BeginTx --\u003e RunChildren[\"Execute Group Child Nodes\"]\n    CreateSP --\u003e RunChildren\n\n    RunChildren --\u003e ChildCheck{\"Any Child Failed?\"}\n    ChildCheck -- \"No (All Succeeded)\" --\u003e CommitTx[\"Commit Tx / Release Savepoint\"]\n    CommitTx --\u003e ContinuePipe([\"Continue Pipeline\"])\n\n    ChildCheck -- \"Yes (Child Failed)\" --\u003e RollbackChoice{\"Is Nested?\"}\n    RollbackChoice -- \"No\" --\u003e RollbackTx[\"tx.Rollback()\"]\n    RollbackChoice -- \"Yes\" --\u003e RollbackSP[\"Rollback to Savepoint\"]\n    RollbackTx --\u003e HaltPipe([\"Skip Remaining Nodes \u0026 Halt Pipeline\"])\n    RollbackSP --\u003e HaltPipe\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Exceptions: When Nodes *Do* Continue (`\u003cassert\u003e`)\n\nThe `\u003cassert\u003e` component (`Executor.executeAssertNode`) is the only built-in node that provides explicit control over whether the pipeline halts or continues when a check fails, as well as a dedicated `\u003con_failure\u003e` fallback block.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    StartAssert([\"Evaluate \u003cassert\u003e Condition\"]) --\u003e Eval{\"Condition Passed?\"}\n    Eval -- \"Yes\" --\u003e Pass([\"ReturnCode = 0\u003cbr/\u003eContinue Pipeline\"])\n\n    Eval -- \"No (Assertion Failed)\" --\u003e CheckFailVar{\"fail_var specified?\"}\n    CheckFailVar -- \"Yes\" --\u003e SetVar[\"Set fail_var = fail_val\u003cbr/\u003e(default \u0027true\u0027)\"]\n    CheckFailVar -- \"No\" --\u003e CheckBlock{\"Has \u003con_failure\u003e\u003cbr/\u003echild nodes?\"}\n    SetVar --\u003e CheckBlock\n\n    CheckBlock -- \"Yes\" --\u003e RunFallback[\"Execute \u003con_failure\u003e Child Nodes\u003cbr/\u003e(e.g., Cleanup SQL, Slack Alert)\"]\n    CheckBlock -- \"No\" --\u003e Policy{\"on_failure attribute?\"}\n    RunFallback --\u003e Policy\n\n    Policy -- \"\u0027warn\u0027 or \u0027continue\u0027\" --\u003e WarnContinue([\"Log Warning (ReturnCode = 0)\u003cbr/\u003eCONTINUE Remaining Pipeline Nodes\"])\n    Policy -- \"\u0027halt\u0027 or default\" --\u003e HaltPipe([\"Log Critical Failure\u003cbr/\u003eHALT Remaining Pipeline Nodes\"])\n```\n\n### Option A: Continue Execution on Failure (`on_failure=\"warn\"` or `\"continue\"`)\nSetting `on_failure=\"warn\"` or `on_failure=\"continue\"` logs a warning message, optionally sets a flag variable (`fail_var` / `fail_val`), executes any nested `\u003con_failure\u003e` child nodes, and **allows all subsequent pipeline nodes to continue**.\n\n```xml\n\u003cassert id=\"check_optional_feed\"\n        var=\"FEED_STATUS\"\n        equals=\"200\"\n        on_failure=\"warn\"\n        fail_var=\"USING_CACHED_FEED\"\n        fail_val=\"true\"\n        message=\"External feed unavailable; falling back to cache.\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- Remaining nodes WILL continue executing --\u003e\n\u003cif var=\"USING_CACHED_FEED\" equals=\"true\"\u003e\n    \u003cthen\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"load_from_cache\" db=\"staging_db\"\u003e\n            INSERT INTO active_rates SELECT * FROM cached_rates;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/then\u003e\n\u003c/if\u003e\n```\n\n### Option B: Execute Cleanup Nodes Before Halting (`\u003con_failure\u003e` with `on_failure=\"halt\"`)\nEven when `on_failure=\"halt\"` (the default), any child nodes nested inside `\u003con_failure\u003e...\u003c/on_failure\u003e` **will** execute when the assertion fails, allowing you to run cleanup SQL or send alerts before the main pipeline halts.\n\n```xml\n\u003cassert id=\"verify_row_threshold\"\n        var=\"ROW_COUNT\"\n        equals=\"1000\"\n        on_failure=\"halt\"\n        message=\"Row count mismatch!\"\u003e\n    \u003con_failure\u003e\n        \u003c!-- These fallback nodes WILL execute before the pipeline halts --\u003e\n        \u003csql id=\"cleanup_staging\" db=\"staging_db\"\u003e\n            TRUNCATE TABLE stg_import;\n        \u003c/sql\u003e\n    \u003c/on_failure\u003e\n\u003c/assert\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- This node will NOT execute because on_failure=\"halt\" --\u003e\n\u003csql id=\"promote_to_prod\" db=\"prod_db\"\u003e\n    INSERT INTO fact_orders SELECT * FROM stg_import;\n\u003c/sql\u003e\n```\n","guides/FLOW_WITH_REMOTE_FILE_SOURCE.md":"# Flow CLI remote source guide using examples for file, HTTP, and SQL DSN inputs\r\n\r\nThe `flow.exe` command supports loading pipeline source content from multiple source types:\r\n\r\n- Local filesystem paths\r\n- HTTP and HTTPS URLs\r\n- SQL / database DSNs using `sql://...` or `db://...`\r\n\r\nThis is useful for the main pipeline file, the options file, and the config override file.\r\n\r\n## Supported source formats\r\n\r\n### Local filesystem\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe --file .\\examples\\CONFIG.xml\r\n.\\flow.exe --file .\\examples\\github_ai_credit_usage.xml --options .\\examples\\options_github_ai_credit_usage.xml\r\n.\\flow.exe --file .\\examples\\CONFIG.xml --config .\\examples\\production_config.xml\r\n```\r\n\r\n### HTTP / HTTPS\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe --file \"https://example.com/pipeline.xml\"\r\n.\\flow.exe --file \"https://example.com/pipeline.xml\" --options \"https://example.com/options.xml\"\r\n.\\flow.exe --file \"https://example.com/pipeline.xml\" --config \"https://example.com/config.xml\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor Basic auth, the `Authorization` header value can be embedded directly in the URL itself instead of using `user:pass` syntax. Example:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe --file \"https://Basic%20dXNlcjpwYXNz@example.com/pipeline.xml\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is translated to an HTTP header of:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAuthorization: Basic dXNlcjpwYXNz\r\n```\r\n\r\n### SQL / database DSN\r\n\r\nThe SQL URI format is:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nsql://\u003cdriver\u003e@\u003cdsn\u003e#\u003cSELECT ...\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe --file \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT XmlContent FROM dbo.flow_pipeline WHERE Name = \u0027PIPELINE_MAIN\u0027\"\r\n\r\n.\\flow.exe --options \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name = \u0027OPTIONS_PROD\u0027\"\r\n\r\n.\\flow.exe --config \"sql://postgres@postgres://app_user:Password123!@prod-db:5432/appdb?sslmode=require#SELECT config_xml FROM flow_config WHERE name = \u0027PROD\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nSQL Server with Windows integrated security / trusted connection:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe --options \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sqlserver.example.com:1433?database=MYDB\u0026integrated+security=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name = \u0027OPTIONS_GITHUB_AI_CREDIT_USAGE\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis pattern is useful when the SQL Server instance is running on a named host and you want to authenticate using a trusted Windows login instead of a username/password in the DSN itself.\r\n\r\n### Database alias form\r\n\r\nThe repository also supports a `db://` alias form:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe --file \"db://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT XmlContent FROM dbo.flow_pipeline WHERE Name = \u0027PIPELINE_MAIN\u0027\"\r\n\r\n.\\flow.exe --options \"db://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name = \u0027OPTIONS_GLOBAL\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Example with all three in one command\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe `\r\n  --file \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT XmlContent FROM dbo.flow_pipeline WHERE Name = \u0027PIPELINE_MAIN\u0027\" `\r\n  --config \"https://example.com/config/prod.xml\" `\r\n  --options \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name = \u0027OPTIONS_PROD\u0027\" `\r\n  --debug\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Notes\r\n\r\n- Put DSN/URI values in double quotes so `\u0026`, `?`, and `#` are preserved correctly by the shell.\r\n- Use `sql://` or `db://` for database-backed XML content.\r\n- Use `http://` or `https://` for remote XML content.\r\n- Use a plain path for local files.\r\n- The `options`, `config`, and `file` arguments all follow the same source-loading rules.\r\n\r\n## Typical production usage\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\n.\\flow.exe --file \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sqlprod:1433?database=ETL\u0026encrypt=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT XmlContent FROM dbo.flow_pipeline WHERE Name = \u0027PIPELINE_FINANCE\u0027\" --config \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sqlprod:1433?database=ETL\u0026encrypt=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT ConfigXML FROM dbo.flow_config WHERE Name = \u0027PROD\u0027\" --options \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sqlprod:1433?database=ETL\u0026encrypt=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name = \u0027OPTIONS_FINANCE\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n","guides/README_DATABASE_XML.md":"# Database-backed Pipeline, Config, and Options Storage\r\n\r\nThis project can load pipeline XML, config XML, and CLI option XML content directly from a database instead of a local file path.\r\n\r\nThe runtime supports SQL-backed resources using the format:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nsql://\u003cdriver\u003e@\u003cdsn\u003e#SELECT ...\r\n```\r\n\r\nor:\r\n\r\n```text\r\ndb://\u003cdriver\u003e@\u003cdsn\u003e#SELECT ...\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe loader accepts a SQL query that returns the XML document as a single value. This makes it possible to store each artifact in a database table and execute the pipeline by reference.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Supported resource types\r\n\r\nThe application resolves XML inputs from the database in the same way it resolves local files:\r\n\r\n- `-file` for the main pipeline XML\r\n- `-config` for the override config XML\r\n- `-options` for default CLI options XML\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n```shell\r\nflow.exe -file \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://localhost:1433?database=example_db\u0026integrated+security=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name = \u0027MY_PIPELINE\u0027\"\r\nflow.exe -config \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://localhost:1433?database=example_db\u0026integrated+security=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT ConfigXML FROM dbo.flow_config_content WHERE Name = \u0027CONFIG_DEV\u0027\"\r\nflow.exe -options \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://localhost:1433?database=example_db\u0026integrated+security=true\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML FROM dbo.flow_options_content WHERE Name = \u0027OPTIONS_DEV\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe loader also accepts `mssql`, `postgresql`, and `sqlite3` aliases and normalizes them internally to the proper Go SQL driver names.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Database table pattern\r\n\r\nThe importer uses this table layout:\r\n\r\n```sql\r\nCREATE TABLE dbo.flow_pipeline_content\r\n(\r\n    [Id] BIGINT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    [Name] VARCHAR(120),\r\n    [Tag] VARCHAR(4000), -- \u0027;\u0027 seperated tags\r\n    [Description] VARCHAR(256), \r\n    [CreatedOnUTC] DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\r\n    [Enabled] BIT DEFAULT 1, -- 0 = False, 1 = True, NULL = Unknown\r\n    [PipelineXML] NVARCHAR(MAX)  NOT NULL\r\n);\r\nCREATE TABLE dbo.flow_options_content\r\n(\r\n    [Id] BIGINT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    [Name] VARCHAR(120),\r\n    [Tag] VARCHAR(4000), -- \u0027;\u0027 seperated tags\r\n    [Description] VARCHAR(256), \r\n    [CreatedOnUTC] DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\r\n    [Enabled] BIT DEFAULT 1, -- 0 = False, 1 = True, NULL = Unknown\r\n    [OptionsXML] NVARCHAR(MAX)  NOT NULL\r\n);\r\nCREATE TABLE dbo.flow_config_content\r\n(\r\n    [Id] BIGINT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\r\n    [Name] VARCHAR(120),\r\n    [Tag] VARCHAR(4000), -- \u0027;\u0027 seperated tags\r\n    [Description] VARCHAR(256), \r\n    [CreatedOnUTC] DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),\r\n    [Enabled] BIT DEFAULT 1, -- 0 = False, 1 = True, NULL = Unknown\r\n    [ConfigXML] NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nA single XML document is stored as raw text in the corresponding column, and the database query returns that value directly to the loader.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Save a pipeline to the database\r\n\r\nUse the importer helper under `db_importer/mssql_importer.go`:\r\n\r\n```shell\r\ncd db_importer\r\ngo run mssql_importer.go \\\r\n  -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\" \\\r\n  -table pipeline \\\r\n  -name \"github_ai_credit_usage\" \\\r\n  -file \"../examples/github_ai_credit_usage.xml\" \\\r\n  -desc \"GitHub Copilot Billing Usage Pipeline\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis reads the XML file, opens the SQL Server connection, and performs a MERGE/UPSERT into `dbo.flow_pipeline_content`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Save config overrides to the database\r\n\r\n```shell\r\ncd db_importer\r\ngo run mssql_importer.go \\\r\n  -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\" \\\r\n  -table config \\\r\n  -name \"CONFIG_DEV_GITHUB\" \\\r\n  -file \"../examples/CONFIG.xml\" \\\r\n  -desc \"GitHub dev config override\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis stores the config XML in `dbo.flow_config_content` under the key `CONFIG_DEV_GITHUB`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Save CLI options to the database\r\n\r\n```shell\r\ncd db_importer\r\ngo run mssql_importer.go \\\r\n  -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\" \\\r\n  -table options \\\r\n  -name \"OPTIONS_GITHUB_AI_CREDIT_USAGE\" \\\r\n  -file \"../db_importer/OPTIONS_GITHUB_AI_CREDIT_USAGE.xml\" \\\r\n  -desc \"Options for GitHub billing pipeline\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe options file can contain runtime arguments and also reference database-backed XML content via `file` and `config` entries.\r\n\r\nExample option file:\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\r\n\u003coptions\u003e\r\n  \u003cfile\u003esql://sqlserver@sqlserver://localhost:1433?database=example_db\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\u0026amp;trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT PipelineXML from dbo.flow_pipeline_content where Name = \u0027github_ai_credit_usage\u0027\u003c/file\u003e\r\n  \u003cconfig\u003esql://sqlserver@sqlserver://localhost:1433?database=example_db\u0026amp;integrated+security=true\u0026amp;trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT ConfigXML from dbo.flow_config_content where Name = \u0027CONFIG_DEV_GITHUB\u0027\u003c/config\u003e\r\n  \u003cvars\u003eEnvironment=dev,RunID=nightly\u003c/vars\u003e\r\n\u003c/options\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis allows a single database-stored options document to bootstrap the pipeline, config, and runtime variables without a local filesystem dependency.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Run the pipeline from the database\r\n\r\n### Run the pipeline XML from a database row\r\n\r\n```shell\r\nflow.exe -file \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT PipelineXML from dbo.flow_pipeline_content where Name = \u0027github_ai_credit_usage\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Run with a database-backed config override\r\n\r\n```shell\r\nflow.exe \\\r\n  -file \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT PipelineXML from dbo.flow_pipeline_content where Name = \u0027github_ai_credit_usage\u0027\" \\\r\n  -config \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT ConfigXML from dbo.flow_config_content where Name = \u0027CONFIG_DEV_GITHUB\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Run with a database-backed options document\r\n\r\n```shell\r\nflow.exe \\\r\n  -options \"sql://sqlserver@sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true#SELECT OptionsXML from dbo.flow_options_content where Name = \u0027OPTIONS_GITHUB_AI_CREDIT_USAGE\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `-options` path itself can point to a database row; the XML inside that options document can in turn point at database-stored pipeline and config content.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Typical production pattern\r\n\r\nA common pattern is:\r\n\r\n1. Save the pipeline XML to `dbo.flow_pipeline_content`\r\n2. Save the environment-specific config XML to `dbo.flow_config_content`\r\n3. Save the runtime defaults to `dbo.flow_options_content`\r\n4. Execute with `-options` pointing to the database document that selects the right pipeline/config values for the environment\r\n\r\nThis keeps XML artifacts in versioned database tables while still allowing the command-line runtime to behave like a standard file-based execution model.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 8. Encrypted Storage and Execution\r\n\r\nPipelines, options, and configs stored in the database can be encrypted at rest using `crypto_tool` or `db_importer -encrypted`.\r\n\r\nBecause ciphertexts are formatted in Flow\u0027s text-safe armored envelope (`FLOWENC:v1:...`), they fit directly into existing `NVARCHAR(MAX)` or `TEXT` database columns without altering any table schemas.\r\n\r\n### Importing Encrypted Content\r\n```bash\r\ndb_importer -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\" \\\r\n            -table pipeline \\\r\n            -name \"production_daily_etl\" \\\r\n            -file \"pipelines/daily.xml\" \\\r\n            -encrypted \\\r\n            -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Executing Encrypted Content\r\nRun `flow.exe` specifying the `-encrypted` flag and `-secure-key` (or setting the `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` environment variable):\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -encrypted -secure-key \"MySecretPassphrase123!\" \\\r\n         -file \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=master#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name = \u0027production_daily_etl\u0027\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor full encryption architecture, PBKDF2 parameters, and offline utilities, see [ENCRYPTION.md](ENCRYPTION.md).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 9. Notes\r\n\r\n- The database query must return exactly one XML value.\r\n- If the query returns no rows or an empty value, the loader returns an error.\r\n- Use `-validate` and other standard Flow flags the same way you would with local XML files.\r\n- Database-backed inputs are resolved by the same loader path used for HTTP and filesystem inputs.\r\n\r\nFor a quick example using the helper importer and the sample GitHub billing XML files, see `db_importer/README_DB_IMPORTER.md`.\r\n","guides/README_UTF.md":"# UTF-8 \u0026 UTF-16 Encoding Guide for Go-Flow\n\nThis guide explains how to use **Go-Flow** with UTF-8 and UTF-16 encoded XML pipelines, environment configuration overrides, pre-baked CLI options, and database-resident XML storage (e.g., Microsoft SQL Server `NVARCHAR(MAX)`).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why UTF-16 in Enterprise ETL?\n\nModern enterprise environments frequently combine Unix/Linux microservices and Microsoft Windows infrastructure:\n* **Microsoft SQL Server**: Stores Unicode strings in `NVARCHAR(n)` and `NVARCHAR(MAX)` columns using native 16-bit encoding (UCS-2 / UTF-16LE).\n* **PowerShell**: Default redirection and `Out-File` operators on Windows PowerShell produce UTF-16LE files with a Byte Order Mark (`0xFF 0xFE`).\n* **Legacy SSIS \u0026 DTS Packages**: XML configuration packages exported from Microsoft ETL tools often default to UTF-16 declarations (`\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-16\"?\u003e`).\n\n**Go-Flow automatically detects and normalizes all UTF-8 and UTF-16 encoding variants at runtime.** You do not need to convert files manually before running pipelines or validating schemas.\n\n---\n\n## 2. CLI Execution with UTF-16 Files\n\nGo-Flow transparently handles UTF-16 files across all CLI inputs (`-file`, `-config`, `-options`).\n\n### Running a UTF-16 Pipeline File\n```shell\nflow.exe -file \"C:\\pipelines\\nightly_billing.xml\"\n```\nEven if `nightly_billing.xml` is saved as UTF-16LE with BOM and begins with `\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-16\"?\u003e`, Go-Flow normalizes the byte stream and executes all nodes cleanly.\n\n### Running with UTF-16 Config Overrides\n```shell\nflow.exe -file \"pipeline.xml\" -config \"C:\\configs\\config_prod_utf16.xml\"\n```\nEnvironment variable overrides and database connection strings in UTF-16 config files are parsed and merged into the active pipeline registry.\n\n### Pre-Baked CLI Options in UTF-16\n```shell\nflow.exe -options \"C:\\profiles\\options_utf16.xml\"\n```\nOptions files (which can specify `-file`, `-config`, and `-vars`) may also be authored and saved in UTF-16.\n\n### Validating UTF-16 Pipelines with `-validate`\n```shell\nflow.exe -file \"C:\\pipelines\\nightly_billing.xml\" -validate\n```\nGo-Flow uses `xmllint` under the hood. It normalizes UTF-16 bytes into standard UTF-8 and pipes them directly to `xmllint`\u0027s standard input. Schema validation succeeds without altering the file on disk.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Database-Resident XML (SQL Server `NVARCHAR(MAX)`)\n\nWhen storing XML pipelines directly in a relational database, queries return string data over the database driver protocol:\n\n```shell\nflow.exe -file \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=analytics\u0026integrated+security=true#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name=\u0027copilot_usage\u0027\"\n```\n\n### Supported Table Layout\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE dbo.flow_pipeline_content (\n    Name NVARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,\n    Description NVARCHAR(4000) NULL,\n    PipelineXML NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL\n);\n```\n\n### How Database Loading Works\n1. Go-Flow executes the SQL query using `database/sql`.\n2. The SQL Server TDS driver fetches the `NVARCHAR(MAX)` column into a Go UTF-8 string or raw byte slice.\n3. Go-Flow\u0027s resource loader inspects the byte payload. If the string contains an `encoding=\"UTF-16\"` XML declaration or BOM units, it normalizes the stream into valid UTF-8 and updates the declaration to `encoding=\"UTF-8\"`.\n4. Go\u0027s standard XML parser compiles the pipeline AST without error.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Importing Files into the Database (`mssql_importer.go`)\n\nThe helper tool located in `db_importer/mssql_importer.go` allows you to upload local XML files into SQL Server tables.\n\n```shell\ncd db_importer\ngo run mssql_importer.go \\\n  -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password123!@localhost:1433?database=master\u0026trustServerCertificate=true\" \\\n  -table pipeline \\\n  -name \"github_ai_credit_usage\" \\\n  -file \"../examples/github_ai_credit_usage.xml\" \\\n  -desc \"GitHub Copilot Billing Usage Pipeline\"\n```\n\n### Avoiding TDS Driver Double-Encoding\nWhen importing files saved in UTF-16LE, passing raw bytes directly into SQL Server driver parameter bindings (`@p3`) causes the driver to encode the already 16-bit bytes as an `NVARCHAR` string, producing corrupted Chinese/mojibake characters in SQL Server.\n\n`mssql_importer.go` includes automatic text decoding:\n* It inspects the input file for UTF-16 LE/BE BOM markers.\n* It decodes the file into a native Go string before passing it to `db.ExecContext`.\n* SQL Server receives clean Unicode text and stores it accurately in `NVARCHAR(MAX)`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Converting File Encodings (PowerShell Reference)\n\nIf you need to convert files between UTF-8 and UTF-16 manually in your scripts:\n\n### Convert to UTF-16LE (Unicode)\n```powershell\n# Windows PowerShell 5.1 \u0026 PowerShell 7+\nGet-Content -Path \"pipeline_utf8.xml\" -Raw | Set-Content -Path \"pipeline_utf16.xml\" -Encoding Unicode\n```\n\n### Convert to UTF-8 (No BOM)\n```powershell\n# PowerShell 7+\nGet-Content -Path \"pipeline_utf16.xml\" -Raw | Set-Content -Path \"pipeline_utf8.xml\" -Encoding utf8NoBOM\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Troubleshooting Common Issues\n\n### Issue 1: `XML syntax error on line 1: invalid UTF-8`\n* **Cause**: An application or custom script passed raw UTF-16 bytes (starting with `0xFF 0xFE`) directly into Go\u0027s standard `xml.NewDecoder` without transcoding.\n* **Resolution**: Use `flow.NormalizeXMLBytes(data)` or `flow.ParseXMLConfig(data)`, which automatically normalizes UTF-16 bytes to UTF-8.\n\n### Issue 2: `xmllint: parser error : Blank needed here` on `pipeline.xsd`\n* **Cause**: Declaring `encoding=\"UTF-16\"` on line 1 of an XSD file that is stored as 8-bit UTF-8 text on disk. `xmllint` treats every 2 ASCII characters as a 16-bit code unit, corrupting the schema compilation.\n* **Resolution**: Always keep `\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e` on line 1 of `xsd/pipeline.xsd` and `xsd/options.xsd`. Pipeline files validated *against* the schema may still declare `encoding=\"UTF-16\"`.\n\n### Issue 3: Corrupted Mojibake / Chinese characters in SQL Server\n* **Cause**: UTF-16 byte slices were converted directly via `string(bytes)` instead of being decoded via `unicode/utf16`.\n* **Resolution**: Use `flow.DecodeTextBytes(bytes)` or the updated `mssql_importer.go`.\n","guides/SECURITY_ORDER_AND_OPTIONS.md":"# Security Architecture: Cryptographic Order of Operations \u0026 Options Guide\r\n\r\nThis document details the exact **order of operations** for combining digital signatures and encryption in `go-flow`, explains the cryptographic design rationale, and provides a complete reference for all security options and CLI flags across `flow.exe`, `crypto_tool`, `db_importer`, and XML configuration files.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Quick Reference: Which Order Should You Use?\r\n\r\n| Security Pattern | Creation Order | Runtime Execution Order | Primary Advantage | Best Used When |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Encrypt-then-Sign**\u003cbr\u003e*(Recommended Standard)* | 1. Encrypt XML\u003cbr\u003e2. Sign Ciphertext | 1. Verify Signature\u003cbr\u003e2. Decrypt Ciphertext\u003cbr\u003e3. Parse \u0026 Run | **Zero Attack Surface:** Untrusted/tampered inputs are rejected before decryption keys or memory are touched. | Standard production workflows, public/untrusted transport, CI/CD distribution, and cloud deployments. |\r\n| **Sign-then-Encrypt**\u003cbr\u003e*(Confidential Signer)* | 1. Sign XML\u003cbr\u003e2. Encrypt Package | 1. Decrypt Ciphertext\u003cbr\u003e2. Verify Signature\u003cbr\u003e3. Parse \u0026 Run | **Signer Privacy:** The digital signature, certificate, and signer identity are encrypted and hidden from external observers. | Sensitive corporate environments where the identity of the signer or build server must remain confidential. |\r\n| **Detached Signature** | Sign file to `.sig` / `.p7s` / `.asc` | 1. Verify Detached Sig\u003cbr\u003e2. Decrypt (if needed)\u003cbr\u003e3. Parse \u0026 Run | **Non-Invasive:** Original XML or encrypted file remains completely untouched. | Auditing, compliance regimes, and external repository verification without wrapper envelopes. |\r\n\r\n### Architectural Comparison\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    subgraph EncryptThenSign[\"Workflow A: Encrypt-then-Sign (Recommended Standard)\"]\r\n        direction TB\r\n        A1[\"Plaintext XML\"] --\u003e|\"1. AES-256-GCM\"| A2[\"FLOWENC Ciphertext\"]\r\n        A2 --\u003e|\"2. Sign Outer\"| A3[\"FLOWSIGNED Package\"]\r\n        A3 --\u003e|\"3. flow.exe: Verify Sig First\"| A4[\"Verified Ciphertext\"]\r\n        A4 --\u003e|\"4. flow.exe: Decrypt Second\"| A5[\"Verified AST Execution\"]\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    subgraph SignThenEncrypt[\"Workflow B: Sign-then-Encrypt (Confidential Signer)\"]\r\n        direction TB\r\n        B1[\"Plaintext XML\"] --\u003e|\"1. Sign Plaintext\"| B2[\"FLOWSIGNED Envelope\"]\r\n        B2 --\u003e|\"2. AES-256-GCM\"| B3[\"FLOWENC Package\"]\r\n        B3 --\u003e|\"3. flow.exe: Decrypt First\"| B4[\"Decrypted FLOWSIGNED\"]\r\n        B4 --\u003e|\"4. flow.exe: Verify Sig Second\"| B5[\"Verified AST Execution\"]\r\n    end\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Order of Operations Deep Dive\r\n\r\n### Pattern A: Encrypt-then-Sign (Recommended Standard)\r\n\r\nIn modern cryptography (TLS 1.3, IPSec, SSH, Signal), **Encrypt-then-Authenticate/Sign** is the industry standard. The ciphertext is signed so that the receiving engine can verify data authenticity and author identity **before** attempting decryption.\r\n\r\n#### Creation Flow (`crypto_tool`):\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart LR\r\n    XML[\"Plaintext XML\u003cbr\u003e(pipeline.xml)\"] --\u003e|\"Step 1: AES-256-GCM\u003cbr\u003e(-action encrypt)\"| ENC[\"Ciphertext\u003cbr\u003eFLOWENC:v1:...\"]\r\n    ENC --\u003e|\"Step 2: Sign Ciphertext\u003cbr\u003e(-action sign -wrap)\"| SIGNED[\"Signed Package\u003cbr\u003eFLOWSIGNED:v1:...\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. **Step 1: Encrypt Plaintext XML**\r\n   ```bash\r\n   crypto_tool -action encrypt -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.enc -secure-key \"SecretPassphrase\"\r\n   ```\r\n   *Result:* Creates a `FLOWENC:v1:...` text-armored ciphertext.\r\n\r\n2. **Step 2: Sign Ciphertext into Unified Package**\r\n   ```bash\r\n   crypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml.enc -out pipeline.xml.signed -private-key priv.pem -wrap\r\n   ```\r\n   *Result:* Wraps the ciphertext into `FLOWSIGNED:v1:OPENSSL-RSA-SHA256:\u003cbase64-signature\u003e:\u003cbase64-FLOWENC-ciphertext\u003e`.\r\n\r\n#### Runtime Execution Flow (`flow.exe`):\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    INPUT[\"Input Package\u003cbr\u003e(pipeline.xml.signed)\"] --\u003e V_SIG{\"1. Verify Digital Signature\u003cbr\u003e(Public Key / Certificate)\"}\r\n    \r\n    V_SIG --\u003e|\"Signature Valid\"| DEC{\"2. Decrypt AES-256-GCM\u003cbr\u003e(PBKDF2 Key Derivation)\"}\r\n    V_SIG --\u003e|\"Signature Invalid / Untrusted\"| ABORT1[\"Immediate Abort (Exit 1)\u003cbr\u003eDecryption key never touched\"]\r\n    \r\n    DEC --\u003e|\"Key Valid \u0026 Authenticated\"| PARSE[\"3. Parse XML Schema \u0026 AST\"]\r\n    DEC --\u003e|\"Wrong Key / Tampered Tag\"| ABORT2[\"Immediate Abort (Exit 1)\u003cbr\u003eDecryption Failed\"]\r\n    \r\n    PARSE --\u003e EXEC[\"Execute Flow Workflows\"]\r\n\r\n    classDef pass fill:#e6fffa,stroke:#047857,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    classDef fail fill:#ffebe9,stroke:#cf222e,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    classDef stage fill:#f0f9ff,stroke:#0284c7,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    class V_SIG,DEC,PARSE stage;\r\n    class EXEC pass;\r\n    class ABORT1,ABORT2 fail;\r\n```\r\n\r\n*Command to execute:*\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -file pipeline.xml.signed -public-key pub.pem -secure-key \"SecretPassphrase\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Pattern B: Sign-then-Encrypt (Confidential Signatures)\r\n\r\nIn this pattern, the author signs the plaintext XML first, and then the entire signed package is encrypted. Only entities possessing the decryption key can view the pipeline **and** verify who signed it.\r\n\r\n#### Creation Flow (`crypto_tool`):\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart LR\r\n    XML[\"Plaintext XML\u003cbr\u003e(pipeline.xml)\"] --\u003e|\"Step 1: Sign XML\u003cbr\u003e(-action sign -wrap)\"| SIGNED[\"Signed Envelope\u003cbr\u003eFLOWSIGNED:v1:...\"]\r\n    SIGNED --\u003e|\"Step 2: AES-256-GCM\u003cbr\u003e(-action encrypt)\"| ENC[\"Encrypted Package\u003cbr\u003eFLOWENC:v1:...\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. **Step 1: Sign Plaintext XML into Unified Package**\r\n   ```bash\r\n   crypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.signed -private-key priv.pem -wrap\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n2. **Step 2: Encrypt the Signed Package**\r\n   ```bash\r\n   crypto_tool -action encrypt -in pipeline.xml.signed -out pipeline.xml.enc -secure-key \"SecretPassphrase\"\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n#### Runtime Execution Flow (`flow.exe`):\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    INPUT[\"Encrypted Resource\u003cbr\u003e(FLOWENC:v1:...)\"] --\u003e DEC{\"1. Decrypt Outer Envelope\u003cbr\u003e(AES-256-GCM with -secure-key)\"}\r\n    \r\n    DEC --\u003e|\"Decryption Successful\"| DETECT{\"Detect Inner Content\"}\r\n    DEC --\u003e|\"Wrong Key / Tampered Tag\"| ABORT1[\"Immediate Abort (Exit 1)\u003cbr\u003eDecryption Failed\"]\r\n    \r\n    DETECT --\u003e|\"Inner Content is FLOWSIGNED:v1\"| V_SIG{\"2. Verify Digital Signature\u003cbr\u003e(Public Key / Certificate)\"}\r\n    DETECT --\u003e|\"Inner Content is Plaintext XML\"| PARSE[\"3. Parse XML Schema \u0026 AST\"]\r\n    \r\n    V_SIG --\u003e|\"Signature Valid\"| PARSE\r\n    V_SIG --\u003e|\"Signature Invalid / Untrusted\"| ABORT2[\"Immediate Abort (Exit 1)\u003cbr\u003eSignature Verification Failed\"]\r\n    \r\n    PARSE --\u003e EXEC[\"Execute Flow Workflows\"]\r\n\r\n    classDef pass fill:#e6fffa,stroke:#047857,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    classDef fail fill:#ffebe9,stroke:#cf222e,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    classDef stage fill:#f0f9ff,stroke:#0284c7,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    class DEC,DETECT,V_SIG,PARSE stage;\r\n    class EXEC pass;\r\n    class ABORT1,ABORT2 fail;\r\n```\r\n\r\n*Command to execute:*\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -file pipeline.xml.enc -secure-key \"SecretPassphrase\" -public-key pub.pem\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Pattern C: Detached Signatures (Unwrapped Files)\r\n\r\nIn this pattern, signatures are kept as standalone companion files (`.sig`, `.asc`, or `.p7s`) alongside the target file.\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    TARGET[\"Pipeline Resource\u003cbr\u003e(pipeline.xml)\"] --\u003e CHECK{\"Check Companion Files\"}\r\n    CHECK --\u003e|\"Found pipeline.xml.sig\"| VER_SIG[\"Verify via OpenSSL / Raw Sig\"]\r\n    CHECK --\u003e|\"Found pipeline.xml.p7s\"| VER_P7S[\"Verify via Windows PKCS#7\"]\r\n    CHECK --\u003e|\"Found pipeline.xml.asc\"| VER_PGP[\"Verify via OpenPGP Keyring\"]\r\n    CHECK --\u003e|\"No Companion File Found\"| EXPLICIT{\"-signature Flag Specified?\"}\r\n    \r\n    EXPLICIT --\u003e|\"Yes\"| VER_EXP[\"Verify Explicit Signature File\"]\r\n    EXPLICIT --\u003e|\"No\"| RUN_DIRECT[\"Execute Unsigned (if allowed)\"]\r\n    \r\n    VER_SIG \u0026 VER_P7S \u0026 VER_PGP \u0026 VER_EXP --\u003e DEC_CHECK{\"Is Content Encrypted?\"}\r\n    DEC_CHECK --\u003e|\"Yes (FLOWENC:v1)\"| DEC[\"Decrypt AES-256-GCM\"]\r\n    DEC_CHECK --\u003e|\"No (Plaintext)\"| PARSE[\"Parse Schema \u0026 AST\"]\r\n    DEC --\u003e PARSE\r\n    PARSE --\u003e EXEC[\"Execute Flow Workflows\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n* **Create Detached Signature:**\r\n  ```bash\r\n  # Sign plaintext or encrypted file\r\n  crypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.sig -private-key priv.pem\r\n  ```\r\n\r\n* **Execute with Companion Auto-Detection:**\r\n  `flow.exe` automatically looks for `pipeline.xml.sig`, `pipeline.xml.asc`, or `pipeline.xml.p7s` when `-public-key` or `-cert` is specified:\r\n  ```bash\r\n  flow.exe -file pipeline.xml -public-key pub.pem\r\n  ```\r\n\r\n* **Execute with Explicit Signature Path:**\r\n  ```bash\r\n  flow.exe -file pipeline.xml -signature /path/to/custom_signature.sig -public-key pub.pem\r\n  ```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Comprehensive Options \u0026 Flags Matrix\r\n\r\n### Runtime Engine: `flow.exe`\r\n\r\n| CLI Flag | Type | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| `-file` | `string` | `scripts.xml` | Path to pipeline XML or `sql://...` database URI. |\r\n| `-encrypted` | `bool` | `false` | Explicitly declares that resource is encrypted. *(Note: Auto-detected if payload starts with `FLOWENC:v1:`)*. |\r\n| `-key-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Path to file containing decryption key/passphrase (preferred over CLI flag). |\r\n| `-secure-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Passphrase or key for AES-256 decryption (CLI warning logged; prefer `-key-file` or `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`). Falls back to `FLOW_SECURE_KEY` or `SECURE_KEY`. |\r\n| `-verify-signature` | `bool` | `false` | Enforce signature verification before decrypting or running pipeline. |\r\n| `-public-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Public key file path (RSA, ECDSA, Ed25519 PEM or OpenPGP keyring) for verification. |\r\n| `-cert` | `string` | `\"\"` | X.509 certificate file path (PEM or DER) for digital signature verification. |\r\n| `-ca-cert` | `string` | `\"\"` | Root CA certificate for certificate chain verification. |\r\n| `-keyring` | `string` | `\"\"` | OpenPGP armored public keyring file path for signature verification. |\r\n| `-signature` | `string` | `\"\"` | Explicit detached digital signature file path (auto-checks `\u003cin\u003e.sig`, `\u003cin\u003e.asc`, `\u003cin\u003e.p7s` if omitted). |\r\n| `-options` | `string` | `\"\"` | Path to XML options file (can be encrypted, signed, or plain). |\r\n| `-config` | `string` | `\"\"` | Path to config override XML file (can be encrypted, signed, or plain). |\r\n| `-import-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Import file into database storage (auto-verifies signatures and decrypts on import). |\r\n| `-export-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Export pipeline/options/config from database storage (supports `-encrypted`). |\r\n| `-dsn` | `string` | `\"\"` | External database DSN for import/export directly to external DB. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Security Utility: `crypto_tool`\r\n\r\n| CLI Flag | Type | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| `-action` | `string` | `encrypt` | Action to execute: `encrypt`, `decrypt`, `gen-key`, `sign`, `verify`, or `gen-keypair`. |\r\n| `-in` | `string` | `\"\"` | Input file path (or `-` / empty for `stdin`). |\r\n| `-out` | `string` | `\"\"` | Output file path (or `-` / empty for `stdout`). |\r\n| `-key-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Path to file containing key/passphrase (preferred to avoid process table exposure). |\r\n| `-secure-key`, `-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Key or passphrase for encryption/decryption (CLI warning logged). |\r\n| `-binary` | `bool` | `false` | Emit/read raw binary ciphertext instead of armored text (`FLOWENC:v1:`). |\r\n| `-sig-type` | `string` | `openssl` | Signature format for `-action sign`: `openssl`, `pgp`, or `pkcs7`. |\r\n| `-signature`, `-sig` | `string` | `\"\"` | Detached signature file path for `-action verify`. |\r\n| `-public-key`, `-pub` | `string` | `\"\"` | Public key file path for verification. |\r\n| `-private-key`, `-priv` | `string` | `\"\"` | Private key file path for signing. |\r\n| `-cert` | `string` | `\"\"` | X.509 certificate file path (PEM or DER) for signing (PKCS#7) or verification. |\r\n| `-ca-cert` | `string` | `\"\"` | Root CA certificate for chain validation. |\r\n| `-keyring` | `string` | `\"\"` | OpenPGP armored public keyring file path. |\r\n| `-wrap` | `bool` | `false` | When signing, bundle signature and content into unified `FLOWSIGNED:v1:...` envelope. |\r\n| `-keypair-type` | `string` | `rsa` | Key type for `-action gen-keypair`: `rsa`, `ecdsa`, `ed25519`, or `pgp`. |\r\n| `-bits` | `int` | `2048` | RSA key size in bits (`2048` or `4096`). |\r\n| `-out-priv` | `string` | `private_key.pem` | Generated private key output file path. |\r\n| `-out-pub` | `string` | `public_key.pem` | Generated public key output file path. |\r\n| `-out-cert` | `string` | `\"\"` | Optional output file path for self-signed X.509 certificate. |\r\n| `-cn` | `string` | `Flow Signer` | Common Name for self-signed certificate or OpenPGP identity. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Database Importer: `db_importer`\r\n\r\n| CLI Flag | Type | Default | Description |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\r\n| `-action` | `string` | `import` | Action to perform: `import` or `export`. |\r\n| `-dsn` | `string` | *(default)* | SQL Server connection string / DSN. |\r\n| `-table` | `string` | `pipeline` | Target table type: `pipeline`, `options`, or `config`. |\r\n| `-name` | `string` | `\"\"` | Repository key name for the imported/exported item. |\r\n| `-file` | `string` | `\"\"` | Local file to import, or destination path when exporting. |\r\n| `-desc` | `string` | *(default)* | Description of the XML content (import only). |\r\n| `-encrypted` | `bool` | `false` | Encrypts content before importing, or decrypts content after exporting. |\r\n| `-secure-key` | `string` | `\"\"` | Passphrase/key for AES-256-GCM encryption/decryption. |\r\n| `-export` | `bool` | `false` | Shortcut flag to export record from database to file. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### XML Options Configuration (`options.xml`)\r\n\r\nYou can define security options inside XML options profiles conforming to [`../xsd/options.xsd`](../xsd/options.xsd):\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\r\n\u003cflow_cli_options\u003e\r\n    \u003coptions\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Signature Verification Options --\u003e\r\n        \u003cverify-signature\u003etrue\u003c/verify-signature\u003e\r\n        \u003cpublic-key\u003e/etc/flow/keys/production_pub.pem\u003c/public-key\u003e\r\n        \u003ccert\u003e/etc/flow/certs/code_signing.crt\u003c/cert\u003e\r\n        \u003cca-cert\u003e/etc/flow/certs/root_ca.crt\u003c/ca-cert\u003e\r\n        \u003ckeyring\u003e/etc/flow/keys/pgp_keyring.asc\u003c/keyring\u003e\r\n        \u003csignature\u003e/var/signatures/pipeline.sig\u003c/signature\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Symmetric Encryption Options --\u003e\r\n        \u003cencrypted\u003etrue\u003c/encrypted\u003e\r\n        \u003csecure-key\u003eProductionSecretKey2026\u003c/secure-key\u003e\r\n    \u003c/options\u003e\r\n\u003c/flow_cli_options\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Key \u0026 Secret Precedence Hierarchy\r\n\r\nWhen multiple sources provide keys, `flow.exe` and `crypto_tool` resolve them strictly in the following order:\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    P0[\"1. Key File\u003cbr\u003e(-key-file \u0026lt;path\u0026gt;)\"]\r\n    P1[\"2. Explicit CLI Flag\u003cbr\u003e(-secure-key / -key)\"]\r\n    P2[\"3. XML Options Profile\u003cbr\u003e(\u0026lt;secure-key\u0026gt; in -options)\"]\r\n    P3[\"4. FLOW_SECURE_KEY\u003cbr\u003e(Environment Variable)\"]\r\n    P4[\"5. SECURE_KEY\u003cbr\u003e(Generic Fallback Env Var)\"]\r\n\r\n    P0 --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P1\r\n    P1 --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P2\r\n    P2 --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P3\r\n    P3 --\u003e|\"If not provided\"| P4\r\n\r\n    classDef high fill:#e6fffa,stroke:#047857,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    classDef item fill:#f0f9ff,stroke:#0284c7,stroke-width:2px;\r\n    class P0,P1 high;\r\n    class P2,P3,P4 item;\r\n```\r\n\r\n\u003e [!TIP]\r\n\u003e **Process Table Security:** Supplying keys directly via `-secure-key` or `-key` on the CLI exposes the secret to other users via `ps aux` or Windows Task Manager. In automated and shared environments, always supply secrets via `-key-file` or `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`. A warning is logged to `stderr` whenever a plaintext key is passed via CLI flags.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. End-to-End Practical Recipes\r\n\r\n### Recipe 1: Production CI/CD Release (Encrypt-then-Sign)\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# 1. Generate release keypair and certificate (one time setup)\r\ncrypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type rsa -bits 2048 \\\r\n  -out-priv release_priv.pem -out-pub release_pub.pem \\\r\n  -out-cert release_cert.pem -cn \"Release Build Agent\"\r\n\r\n# 2. Encrypt the production pipeline with a random 256-bit AES key\r\nKEY=$(crypto_tool -gen-key)\r\ncrypto_tool -action encrypt -in pipeline.xml -out pipeline.xml.enc -secure-key \"$KEY\"\r\n\r\n# 3. Sign the encrypted pipeline using the private key into a single package\r\ncrypto_tool -action sign -in pipeline.xml.enc -out pipeline.xml.signed -private-key release_priv.pem -wrap\r\n\r\n# 4. Deploy pipeline.xml.signed to production servers. Run with:\r\nexport FLOW_SECURE_KEY=\"$KEY\"\r\nflow.exe -file pipeline.xml.signed -public-key release_pub.pem\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Recipe 2: Windows Authenticode / PKCS#7 CMS Workflow (`.p7s`)\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# 1. Sign using an X.509 certificate and private key (creates standard PKCS#7 detached signature)\r\ncrypto_tool -action sign \\\r\n  -in scripts.xml \\\r\n  -out scripts.xml.p7s \\\r\n  -private-key signer_key.pem \\\r\n  -cert signer_cert.pem \\\r\n  -sig-type pkcs7\r\n\r\n# 2. Execute on Windows, Linux, macOS, or FreeBSD (pure Go parses PKCS#7 ASN.1 structure)\r\nflow.exe -file scripts.xml -cert signer_cert.pem\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Recipe 3: Storing Signed \u0026 Encrypted Pipelines in SQL Server\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# 1. Import pipeline package into SQL Server repository (storing encrypted at rest)\r\nflow.exe -dsn \"sqlserver://sa:Password@localhost:1433?database=ETL\" \\\r\n         -import-file pipeline.xml.signed \\\r\n         -import-name \"NightlyBillingJob\" \\\r\n         -import-type pipeline \\\r\n         -encrypted \\\r\n         -secure-key \"$KEY\"\r\n\r\n# 2. Execute directly from SQL Server URI:\r\nflow.exe \\\r\n  -file \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=ETL#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name=\u0027NightlyBillingJob\u0027\" \\\r\n  -public-key release_pub.pem \\\r\n  -secure-key \"$KEY\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Security Guarantees \u0026 Failure Handling\r\n\r\n* **Tampered Signature or Payload:** If any byte of the signature, ciphertext, or plaintext is altered, verification fails immediately with `exit code 1`. Zero child processes or downstream pipeline database connections are spawned.\r\n* **Missing or Invalid Key:** If an encrypted file is provided without a key, execution halts with `exit code 1` with a descriptive message prompting for `-secure-key` or `FLOW_SECURE_KEY`.\r\n* **Zero Binary Dependencies:** No system installations of `openssl`, `gpg`, or `signtool.exe` are invoked at runtime. The execution behavior and security boundaries are 100% identical on Windows, Linux, macOS, and FreeBSD.\r\n","guides/SIGNATURES.md":"# Cross-Platform Digital Signatures Guide\r\n\r\n`go-flow` and its companion utility `crypto_tool` provide pure Go, zero-dependency digital signature creation and verification. This architecture ensures complete cross-platform parity across **Windows**, **Linux**, **macOS**, and **FreeBSD** without requiring OpenSSL, GPG, or Windows CryptoAPI / PowerShell binaries to be installed on target systems.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Supported Signature Standards\r\n\r\n| Standard | Algorithms \u0026 Key Types | Formats | Verification Compatibility |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **OpenSSL / PKI X.509** | RSA (2048/4096-bit PKCS#1v1.5 \u0026 PSS), ECDSA (P-256, P-384, P-521), Ed25519 | PEM Private/Public Keys, X.509 Certificates (`.pem`, `.crt`, `.cer`) | Cross-platform |\r\n| **PKCS#7 / CMS** | RSA, ECDSA (RFC 2315 / RFC 5652 SignedData) | Detached CMS / PKCS#7 (`.p7s`), PEM or DER format | Cross-platform (pure Go ASN.1 parser) |\r\n| **GPG / OpenPGP** | RSA, Ed25519 (RFC 4880 OpenPGP) | ASCII-armored detached signatures (`.asc`, `.sig`), Armored Keyrings | Cross-platform (pure Go via `gopenpgp/v2`) |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Envelope Formats\r\n\r\nTo safely store signatures alongside XML pipelines or in SQL databases without binary corruption or character encoding issues, `go-flow` supports both detached signatures and self-contained armored envelopes:\r\n\r\n### Detached Armor Envelope (`FLOWSIG:v1:`)\r\nUsed when storing signatures as plain text:\r\n```text\r\nFLOWSIG:v1:OPENSSL-RSA-SHA256:\u003cbase64-signature\u003e\r\n```\r\nOr for Windows PKCS#7 / GPG:\r\n```text\r\nFLOWSIG:v1:PKCS7:\u003cbase64-pkcs7-der\u003e\r\nFLOWSIG:v1:PGP:\u003cbase64-pgp-sig\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Unified Signed Package (`FLOWSIGNED:v1:`)\r\nBundles both the cryptographic signature and the raw payload (XML or ciphertext) into a single text block:\r\n```text\r\nFLOWSIGNED:v1:\u003cFORMAT\u003e:\u003cbase64-signature\u003e:\u003cbase64-content\u003e\r\n```\r\nWhen `go-flow` encounters a `FLOWSIGNED:v1:` payload, it automatically extracts and validates the signature against the specified public key or certificate before passing the content to decryption or execution.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Decoupled Pipeline Lifecycle\r\n\r\n`go-flow` executes a strict three-stage security pipeline:\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    A[\"Input Resource (File, DB, HTTP)\"]\r\n    A --\u003e B[\"Stage 1: Signature Verify\u003cbr/\u003e(OpenSSL, PKCS#7, or GPG)\u003cbr/\u003eValidates author authenticity \u0026 payload integrity.\u003cbr/\u003eRejects tampered, altered, or unsigned resources.\"]\r\n    B --\u003e|Verified Payload| C[\"Stage 2: AES-256 Decrypt\u003cbr/\u003e(PBKDF2 + SHA-256)\u003cbr/\u003eDecrypts AES-256-GCM authenticated payload.\u003cbr/\u003eSkips if payload was already unencrypted.\"]\r\n    C --\u003e|Plaintext XML| D[\"Stage 3: Parse \u0026 Execute\u003cbr/\u003e(go-flow Engine)\u003cbr/\u003eValidates XML schema \u0026 AST, checks preflight,\u003cbr/\u003eand executes pipeline workflows.\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth **Sign-then-Encrypt** and **Encrypt-then-Sign** workflows are supported transparently.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Key \u0026 Certificate Generation (`crypto_tool`)\r\n\r\nThe `crypto_tool` utility can generate asymmetric keypairs and self-signed X.509 code-signing certificates out of the box.\r\n\r\n### Generate RSA 2048-bit Keypair \u0026 X.509 Certificate\r\n```bash\r\n# Generates private_key.pem, public_key.pem, and cert.pem\r\n./crypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type rsa -bits 2048 -out-priv priv.pem -out-pub pub.pem -out-cert cert.pem -cn \"Production Flow Signer\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Generate ECDSA (P-256) Keypair\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type ecdsa -out-priv ec_priv.pem -out-pub ec_pub.pem\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Generate Ed25519 Keypair\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type ed25519 -out-priv ed_priv.pem -out-pub ed_pub.pem\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Generate OpenPGP / GPG Armored Keypair\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action gen-keypair -keypair-type pgp -out-priv gpg_priv.asc -out-pub gpg_pub.asc -cn \"Release Operator\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Signing Pipelines (`crypto_tool`)\r\n\r\n### A. OpenSSL Detached Signature\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action sign \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml \\\r\n  -out pipeline.xml.sig \\\r\n  -private-key priv.pem \\\r\n  -sig-type openssl\r\n```\r\n\r\n### B. PKCS#7 / CMS Detached Signature (`.p7s`)\r\nCreates an RFC 2315 / RFC 5652 PKCS#7 SignedData structure embedding the X.509 signer certificate:\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action sign \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml \\\r\n  -out pipeline.xml.p7s \\\r\n  -private-key priv.pem \\\r\n  -cert cert.pem \\\r\n  -sig-type pkcs7\r\n```\r\n\r\n### C. OpenPGP / GPG ASCII-Armored Signature (`.asc`)\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action sign \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml \\\r\n  -out pipeline.xml.asc \\\r\n  -private-key gpg_priv.asc \\\r\n  -sig-type pgp\r\n```\r\n\r\n### D. Self-Contained Signed Package (`-wrap`)\r\nEmbeds signature and content into a single file suitable for database storage or direct distribution:\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action sign \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml \\\r\n  -out pipeline.xml.signed \\\r\n  -private-key priv.pem \\\r\n  -sig-type openssl \\\r\n  -wrap\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Verifying Signatures (`crypto_tool`)\r\n\r\n### Verify Detached Signature\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action verify \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml \\\r\n  -signature pipeline.xml.sig \\\r\n  -public-key pub.pem\r\n```\r\n*(If `-signature` is omitted, `crypto_tool` automatically searches for companion files `\u003cin\u003e.sig`, `\u003cin\u003e.asc`, or `\u003cin\u003e.p7s`)*.\r\n\r\n### Verify PKCS#7 / CMS Signature\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action verify \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml \\\r\n  -signature pipeline.xml.p7s \\\r\n  -cert cert.pem\r\n```\r\n*(Note: To prevent untrusted/self-signed certificates embedded inside `.p7s` payloads from being blindly accepted, PKCS#7 verification enforces that an explicit trust anchor is supplied via `-cert` or `-ca-cert`)*.\r\n\r\n### Verify OpenPGP Armored Signature\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action verify \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml \\\r\n  -signature pipeline.xml.asc \\\r\n  -keyring gpg_pub.asc\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Verify and Extract Content from Unified Envelope\r\n```bash\r\n./crypto_tool -action verify \\\r\n  -in pipeline.xml.signed \\\r\n  -public-key pub.pem \\\r\n  -out verified_pipeline.xml\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Engine Execution with `flow.exe`\r\n\r\n### Command Line Flags\r\n\r\n| Flag | Description |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `-verify-signature` | Enforces signature verification prior to execution or decryption |\r\n| `-public-key \u003cfile\u003e` | PEM public key path (RSA, ECDSA, Ed25519) |\r\n| `-cert \u003cfile\u003e` | X.509 certificate path (PEM or DER) |\r\n| `-ca-cert \u003cfile\u003e` | Root CA certificate for certificate chain validation |\r\n| `-keyring \u003cfile\u003e` | OpenPGP public keyring path |\r\n| `-signature \u003cfile\u003e` | Explicit detached signature file path |\r\n\r\n### Examples\r\n\r\n#### 1. Execute Pipeline with Companion Signature\r\nWhen `pipeline.xml.sig`, `pipeline.xml.asc`, or `pipeline.xml.p7s` exists alongside `pipeline.xml`, `flow.exe` detects and verifies it automatically:\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -file pipeline.xml -public-key pub.pem\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### 2. Execute Combined Signed and Encrypted Database Pipeline\r\nVerifies the digital signature on the encrypted package, decrypts with AES-256-GCM, and runs the workflow:\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe \\\r\n  -file \"sql://sqlserver@localhost:1433?database=ETLFlow#SELECT PipelineXML FROM dbo.flow_pipeline_content WHERE Name=\u0027ProductionBilling\u0027\" \\\r\n  -verify-signature \\\r\n  -public-key /etc/flow/signer_pub.pem \\\r\n  -encrypted \\\r\n  -secure-key \"ProductionPassphrase2026\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### 3. Enforce Signatures via XML Options File (`options.xml`)\r\nOptions files can enforce signature checks across automated pipelines:\r\n```xml\r\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\r\n\u003cflow_cli_options\u003e\r\n    \u003coptions\u003e\r\n        \u003cverify-signature\u003etrue\u003c/verify-signature\u003e\r\n        \u003cpublic-key\u003e/etc/flow/signer_pub.pem\u003c/public-key\u003e\r\n        \u003cencrypted\u003etrue\u003c/encrypted\u003e\r\n        \u003csecure-key\u003eProductionPassphrase2026\u003c/secure-key\u003e\r\n    \u003c/options\u003e\r\n\u003c/flow_cli_options\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\nExecute using:\r\n```bash\r\nflow.exe -options options.xml -file pipeline.xml.signed\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 8. Cross-Platform Parity\r\n\r\nAll signature features are implemented in standard Go and actively maintained libraries:\r\n- `crypto/rsa`, `crypto/ecdsa`, `crypto/ed25519`, `crypto/x509`\r\n- `github.com/ProtonMail/gopenpgp/v2` (modern OpenPGP support)\r\n- `encoding/asn1`, `encoding/pem`\r\n\r\nNo OS-specific tools (`openssl`, `gpg`, `signtool.exe`, or `certutil`) are invoked at runtime. Binaries compiled for Windows, Linux, macOS, or FreeBSD can seamlessly verify signatures generated on any other operating system.\r\n","guides/SUMMARY_OF_AVAILABLE_OPTIONS.md":"# `\u003cpipeline\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cpipeline\u003e` element defines a sequence of steps to be executed in order. It serves as the top-level container for all pipeline components, including templates, SQL steps, and bulk SQL operations.\r\n\r\n## `\u003cdatabases\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cdatabases\u003e` element declares database connections through nested `\u003cdatabase\u003e` elements.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `databases.description` | No | Human-readable description of the database collection. |\r\n| `databases` child element | No | A database connection definition. Any number of database child elements may be included. |\r\n| `databases.id` | No | Optional identifier for an individual database definition. |\r\n| `databases.name` | Yes | Unique connection name used by database-aware pipeline nodes. |\r\n \r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `\u003cdatabases\u003e` is optional and may occur once within a `\u003cpipeline\u003e`.\r\n- Reference a configured connection by its `database.name`, for example with `db=\"reporting_db\"` on a database-aware node.\r\n\r\n\r\n### `\u003cdatabase\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cdatabase\u003e` element defines a named database connection and its connection pool settings within a `\u003cdatabases\u003e` container.\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Optional identifier for the database definition. |\r\n| `name` | Yes | Unique database handle name referenced by `db`, `database`, `target_db`, and related attributes. |\r\n| `driver` | No | Database driver name: relational SQL (`sqlite`, `postgres`, `mysql`, `sqlserver`, `oracle`) or Key-Value (`bbolt`/`bolt`, `badger`, `redis`/`valkey`, `etcd`). Defaults to `sqlserver` when omitted. |\r\n| `type` | No | Alias for `driver`. |\r\n| `connection_string` | Yes | Driver-specific connection string, DSN, directory path, or URL used to open the database. Supports `{{VariableName}}` variable interpolation. |\r\n| `max_open_conns` | No | Maximum number of simultaneous open connections in the pool. Defaults to `25` if omitted or `\u003c= 0`. |\r\n| `max_idle_conns` | No | Maximum number of idle connections retained in the pool for reuse. Defaults to `10` if omitted or `\u003c= 0`. Automatically clamped to `max_open_conns` if greater. |\r\n| `conn_max_lifetime_seconds` | No | Maximum duration a connection may be reused before being recycled. Accepts duration strings (e.g., `5m`, `300s`) or integer seconds. Defaults to `5m` (`300s`) if omitted or `\u003c= 0`. |\r\n| `workload` | No | Optional tuning profile metadata label describing runtime usage patterns (`oltp`, `bulk`, `analytics`, `batch`). |\r\n| `description` | No | Human-readable description of the database connection. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Declare `\u003cdatabase\u003e` elements inside the `\u003cdatabases\u003e` container.\r\n- Attribute names are case-insensitive and support snake_case, kebab-case (`-`), and dot (`.`) separators.\r\n- Connection strings support variable placeholders using `{{VariableName}}`.\r\n- When pool tuning attributes are omitted, the runtime applies default pool values (`max_open_conns=25`, `max_idle_conns=10`, `conn_max_lifetime=5m`).\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n  \u003cdatabase\r\n    id=\"analytics-db\"\r\n    name=\"analytics_db\"\r\n    driver=\"postgres\"\r\n    connection_string=\"host=localhost port=5432 user=postgres password={{DB_PASSWORD}} dbname=analytics sslmode=disable\"\r\n    max_open_conns=\"50\"\r\n    max_idle_conns=\"20\"\r\n    conn_max_lifetime_seconds=\"300\"\r\n    workload=\"analytics\"\r\n    description=\"Analytics reporting replica database\" /\u003e\r\n\u003c/databases\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n## `\u003cvariables\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cvariables\u003e` element declares pipeline variables through nested `\u003cvariable\u003e` elements.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `description` | No | Human-readable description of the variables collection. |\r\n| `variable` child element | No | A variable definition. Any number of variable child elements may be included. |\r\n| `variable.id` | No | Optional identifier for an individual variable definition. |\r\n| `variable.name` | Yes | Name used to access the variable throughout the pipeline. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `\u003cvariables\u003e` is optional and may occur once within a `\u003cpipeline\u003e`.\r\n- Initialized variables are available to pipeline nodes and may be interpolated where supported using `{{VariableName}}`.\r\n\r\n\r\n### `\u003cvariable\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cvariable\u003e` element defines an initial pipeline variable within a `\u003cvariables\u003e` container.\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Optional identifier for the variable definition. |\r\n| `name` | Yes | Variable name used to retrieve or interpolate the value throughout the pipeline. |\r\n| `type` | No | Value type. Defaults to `string`; supported values are `string`, `int` / `integer`, `bool` / `boolean`, `float` / `double` / `float64`, `datetime`, and `date`. |\r\n| `value` | Yes | Initial variable value. Flow parses it according to `type`. |\r\n| `description` | No | Human-readable description of the variable. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Declare `\u003cvariable\u003e` elements inside the `\u003cvariables\u003e` container.\r\n- Variable names can be interpolated in supported fields using `{{VariableName}}`.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## `\u003cpreflight\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cpreflight\u003e` element contains setup and validation nodes that can be run before the main `\u003cflow\u003e` pipeline.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| attributes | No | The `\u003cpreflight\u003e` element does not define any attributes. |\r\n| pipeline nodes | No | Zero or more executable child elements used for environment checks, initialization, connectivity validation, or other preparation steps. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `\u003cpreflight\u003e` is optional and may occur once, before `\u003cflow\u003e`, within a `\u003cpipeline\u003e`.\r\n- Preflight and flow nodes are parsed into separate collections. The caller chooses whether to execute preflight nodes, flow nodes, or preflight followed by flow.\r\n\r\n## `\u003cflow\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cflow\u003e` element is the pipeline\u0027s main execution container. Its child nodes are parsed and executed in document order.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| attributes | No | The `\u003cflow\u003e` element does not define any attributes. |\r\n| pipeline nodes | No | Zero or more executable child elements, including `sql`, `sql_bulk`, `assert`, `http_client`, `template`, file, Excel, path-extraction, group, conditional, loop, and parallel nodes. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `\u003cflow\u003e` is optional in the pipeline schema, but it is normally used to contain the pipeline\u0027s executable work.\r\n- Child nodes execute sequentially unless a control-flow element such as `\u003cparallel\u003e`, `\u003cforeach\u003e`, or `\u003cwhile\u003e` changes that behavior.\r\n\r\n## Child Actions allowed under `\u003cflow\u003e` or `\u003cpreflight\u003e`\r\n\r\n### Conditional Execution and Logical Grouping\r\n#### `\u003cforeach\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cforeach\u003e` element runs its nested pipeline nodes once for every row returned by its SQL driver query.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the foreach step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `foreach_N`. |\r\n| `language` | No | Driver-query language. Defaults to `sql`; the current foreach runtime executes SQL drivers. |\r\n| `lang` | No | Alias for `language`. |\r\n| `db` | Yes | Name of the configured database connection used to execute the driver query. |\r\n| `database` | No | Alias for `db`. |\r\n| `var` | No | Name of a pipeline variable that supplies the SQL driver query. When it resolves to non-empty content, it takes precedence over inline query content. |\r\n| `variable` | No | Alias for `var`. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline SQL driver query placed before nested pipeline elements. Required when `var` or `variable` does not resolve to a non-empty SQL query. |\r\n| nested pipeline nodes | No | Child steps executed sequentially for each returned row. The query\u0027s columns are available as pipeline variables in their original, lowercase, and uppercase forms. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Each iteration sets `LOOP_INDEX` to the zero-based row index before executing child nodes.\r\n- The foreach result reports the number of driver rows and completed iterations.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cgroup\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cgroup\u003e` element organizes nested pipeline nodes into a sequential block. It can execute conditionally and can optionally wrap its child nodes in a database transaction.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the group. |\r\n| `var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable used for conditional execution. Alias for `if_var`. |\r\n| `if_var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable used for conditional execution. |\r\n| `equals` | No | Expected value for the condition. Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `value` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if_val` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if_equals` | No | Expected value required for the group to execute when `var` or `if_var` is set. |\r\n| `condition` | No | Inline condition expression. Used as the condition variable when `var` and `if_var` are not set. |\r\n| `cond` | No | Alias for `condition`. |\r\n| `transaction` | No | Boolean that wraps child-node execution in a database transaction. Defaults to `false`. |\r\n| `db` | Conditional | Name of the configured database connection for the transaction. Required when `transaction` is `true`. |\r\n| `database` | Conditional | Alias for `db`. |\r\n| nested pipeline nodes | No | Child steps executed sequentially when the condition passes, or unconditionally when no condition is supplied. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- If a transaction child step fails, Flow rolls back the transaction; otherwise it commits after all child steps complete.\r\n- When a condition is present but does not pass, the group and its child nodes are skipped.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cwhile\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cwhile\u003e` element repeatedly executes its nested pipeline nodes while its condition evaluates to true.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the while step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `while_N`. |\r\n| `var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable evaluated for each iteration. Alias for `if_var`. |\r\n| `if_var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable evaluated for each iteration. |\r\n| `equals` | No | Expected condition value. Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `val` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `value` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if_val` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if_equals` | No | Expected condition value used with `var` or `if_var`. |\r\n| `condition` | Conditional | Inline condition expression. Used as the condition variable when `var` and `if_var` are not set. |\r\n| `cond` | Conditional | Alias for `condition`. |\r\n| `max_iterations` | No | Positive maximum iteration count. Defaults to `1000` to prevent unbounded loops. |\r\n| `max_loops` | No | Alias for `max_iterations`. |\r\n| nested pipeline nodes | No | Child steps executed sequentially during each iteration. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Each iteration sets `WHILE_INDEX` to its zero-based index before executing child nodes.\r\n- Execution stops when the condition is false, the maximum iteration limit is reached, a child step fails, or the pipeline context is cancelled.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cif\u003e`, `\u003cthen\u003e`, `\u003celse\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cif\u003e` element conditionally executes one of two child branches. `\u003cthen\u003e` contains the branch for a passing condition, and `\u003celse\u003e` contains the branch for a failing condition.\r\n\r\n| Entity / attribute | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `\u003cif\u003e` | No | Conditional container that evaluates a pipeline variable or condition expression. |\r\n| `if.id` | No | Optional identifier for the conditional step. |\r\n| `if.var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable evaluated by the condition. Alias for `if_var`. |\r\n| `if.if_var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable evaluated by the condition. |\r\n| `if.equals` | No | Expected condition value. Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if.val` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if.value` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if.if_val` | No | Alias for `if_equals`. |\r\n| `if.if_equals` | No | Expected condition value used with `var` or `if_var`. |\r\n| `if.condition` | Conditional | Inline condition expression, used when `var` and `if_var` are not set. |\r\n| `if.cond` | Conditional | Alias for `condition`. |\r\n| `if.description` | No | Human-readable description of the conditional step. |\r\n| `\u003cthen\u003e` | No | Container for nodes executed when the condition passes. It does not define attributes. |\r\n| `\u003celse\u003e` | No | Container for nodes executed when the condition fails. It does not define attributes. |\r\n| inline child nodes | No | Child nodes placed directly inside `\u003cif\u003e` are treated as part of the then branch. |\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cparallel\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cparallel\u003e` element executes nested child nodes concurrently across a pool of worker goroutines. Variable states are thread-isolated during branch execution and safely merged upon completion with automatic collision namespacing (`WORKER_\u003cid\u003e_\u003ckey\u003e`).\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `max_threads` | No | Maximum number of concurrent worker threads. Defaults to unbounded concurrency (`0` or omitted runs all children concurrently). |\r\n| `threads` | No | Alias for `max_threads`. |\r\n| `concurrency` | No | Alias for `max_threads`. |\r\n| nested pipeline nodes | No | Child steps executed concurrently in parallel goroutines. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Context cancellation propagates to all active parallel child nodes if any branch errors or the pipeline is stopped.\r\n- Memory and thread-safe registry snapshots ensure concurrent writes do not race.\r\n\r\n### Currently Supported Tasks under `\u003cflow\u003e` and `\u003cpreflight\u003e`\r\nSupport under `\u003cflow\u003e` and `\u003cpreflight\u003e` including tasks within the conditional branches, groups and loops.\r\nSome tasks may support their own sub-tasks or nested structure, such as `\u003cassert\u003e`.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003ctemplate\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003ctemplate\u003e` element renders Go text templates using the current pipeline variables. It can read the template from an external file or from inline XML content, and it can optionally store the rendered output in a variable.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | Yes | Unique identifier for the template node. This is the logical name used in execution results and diagnostics. |\r\n| `name` | No | Friendly name for the template when it is parsed by Go\u0027s template engine. If omitted, the engine still works with a default internal name. |\r\n| `file` | No | Path to an external template file. If provided, the file contents are used as the template source. This is optional when the template is provided inline. |\r\n| `engine` | No | Declares the template engine. In the current implementation, execution uses Go `text/template`; this attribute is accepted for compatibility and documentation purposes. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the rendered output. This is the primary output variable name. |\r\n| `var` | No | Alias for `output_var`. If `output_var` is not set, this value is used instead. |\r\n| `mode` | No | Output behavior for the result. `summary` returns a brief success message; any other value (including unset) returns the rendered template text itself. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline template body text located between the opening and closing `\u003ctemplate\u003e` tags. This is required when `file` is not set. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `file` and inline `content` are mutually exclusive sources for the template body.\r\n- The template is rendered with the current registry variables available in the pipeline.\r\n- If both `output_var` and `var` are empty, the rendered text is still generated but not stored into a named variable. \r\n\r\n\r\n#### `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e` element renders Go HTML templates using the current pipeline variables. It can read the template from an external file or from inline XML content, and it can optionally store the rendered output in a variable.\r\n\r\nThe `\u003ctemplate\u003e` element renders Go text templates using the current pipeline variables. It can read the template from an external file or from inline XML content, and it can optionally store the rendered output in a variable.\r\n\r\nFor `\u003ctemplate_html\u003e`, the Go `html/template` engine is used, which provides automatic HTML escaping to help prevent XSS vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | Yes | Unique identifier for the template node. This is the logical name used in execution results and diagnostics. |\r\n| `name` | No | Friendly name for the template when it is parsed by Go\u0027s template engine. If omitted, the engine still works with a default internal name. |\r\n| `file` | No | Path to an external template file. If provided, the file contents are used as the template source. This is optional when the template is provided inline. |\r\n| `engine` | No | Declares the template engine. In the current implementation, execution uses Go `text/template`; this attribute is accepted for compatibility and documentation purposes. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the rendered output. This is the primary output variable name. |\r\n| `var` | No | Alias for `output_var`. If `output_var` is not set, this value is used instead. |\r\n| `mode` | No | Output behavior for the result. `summary` returns a brief success message; any other value (including unset) returns the rendered template text itself. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline template body text located between the opening and closing `\u003ctemplate\u003e` tags. This is required when `file` is not set. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `file` and inline `content` are mutually exclusive sources for the template body.\r\n- The template is rendered with the current registry variables available in the pipeline.\r\n- If both `output_var` and `var` are empty, the rendered text is still generated but not stored into a named variable. \r\n\r\n#### `\u003csql\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003csql\u003e` element executes a SQL statement against a configured database connection. The statement can be supplied inline or read from a pipeline variable.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the SQL step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `sql_N`. |\r\n| `db` | Yes | Name of the configured database connection on which to execute the SQL statement. |\r\n| `database` | No | Alias for `db`. |\r\n| `var` | No | Name of a pipeline variable that supplies the SQL statement. When its value is non-empty, it takes precedence over inline content. |\r\n| `variable` | No | Alias for `var`. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the query output. |\r\n| `out_var` | No | Alias for `output_var`. |\r\n| `output_variable` | No | Alias for `output_var`. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | SQL statement placed between the opening and closing `\u003csql\u003e` tags. Required when `var` or `variable` does not resolve to a non-empty SQL statement. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- SQL output is always stored in `LAST_OUTPUT`; specify an output attribute to also store it under a named variable.\r\n- Use either `db` or `database`, and use either inline `content` or a non-empty `var` / `variable` SQL source.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003csql_bulk\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` element streams the rows returned by a source SQL query into a destination table.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the bulk SQL step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `sql_bulk_N`. |\r\n| `db` | Yes | Name of the configured source database connection that executes the query. |\r\n| `database` | No | Alias for `db`. |\r\n| `target_db` | No | Name of the configured destination database connection. If omitted, the source database is used. |\r\n| `target_database` | No | Alias for `target_db`. |\r\n| `target_table` | Yes | Destination table that receives the streamed rows. Pipeline variable placeholders are supported. |\r\n| `batch_size` | No | Maximum number of rows written per batch. The underlying ETL operation uses its default when this is not specified. |\r\n| `var` | No | Name of a pipeline variable that supplies the source SQL query. When its value is non-empty, it takes precedence over inline content. |\r\n| `variable` | No | Alias for `var`. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the number of rows copied. |\r\n| `out_var` | No | Alias for `output_var`. |\r\n| `tablock` | No | Enables or disables a table lock during SQL Server bulk insert. Defaults to `true`. |\r\n| `check_constraints` | No | Whether to evaluate destination table constraints during SQL Server bulk insert. Defaults to `false`. |\r\n| `fire_triggers` | No | Whether to execute destination table triggers during SQL Server bulk insert. Defaults to `false`. |\r\n| `keep_nulls` | No | Whether explicit `NULL` values are preserved during SQL Server bulk insert. Defaults to `false`. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Source SQL query placed between the opening and closing `\u003csql_bulk\u003e` tags. Required when `var` or `variable` does not resolve to a non-empty SQL query. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `target_table` is required because bulk SQL streams query results rather than returning them as a result set.\r\n- The number of copied rows is always stored in `LAST_OUTPUT`; specify an output attribute to also store it under a named variable.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003ckv\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003ckv\u003e` element executes atomic key-value store operations (`get`, `put` / `set`, `delete` / `del`, `scan` / `list`) against configured embedded or server-based Key-Value databases (`bbolt`, `badger`, `redis`, `etcd`).\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the KV step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `kv_N`. |\r\n| `db` | Yes | Name of the configured database connection on which to execute the KV operation. |\r\n| `database` | No | Alias for `db`. |\r\n| `bucket` | No | Target bucket name (`bbolt`, `badger`) or namespace key prefix (`redis`, `etcd`). Defaults to `default`. |\r\n| `op` | No | Operation type: `get`, `put` / `set`, `delete` / `del`, `scan` / `list`. Can also be supplied via inline DSL text. |\r\n| `key` | No | Target key name. Can also be supplied via inline DSL text. |\r\n| `value` | No | Value payload to store during write operations. Can also be supplied via inline DSL text. |\r\n| `var` | No | Name of a pipeline variable supplying the inline DSL operation string. |\r\n| `variable` | No | Alias for `var`. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the retrieved value or scan output. |\r\n| `out_var` | No | Alias for `output_var`. |\r\n| `output_variable` | No | Alias for `output_var`. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline DSL operation command (e.g., `set user:101 Alice` or `get user:101`). |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Output is always stored in `LAST_OUTPUT`; specify an output attribute to also retain it under a named variable.\r\n- Operations can be defined via XML attributes (`op=\"put\" key=\"k1\" value=\"v1\"`) or via natural inline DSL command text (`set k1 v1`).\r\n\r\n#### `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e` element streams rows directly from a source relational SQL query into a target Key-Value bucket/namespace in high-throughput batches.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the bulk KV step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `kv_bulk_N`. |\r\n| `db` | Yes | Name of the configured source SQL database connection executing the query. |\r\n| `database` | No | Alias for `db`. |\r\n| `target_db` | Yes | Name of the configured destination Key-Value database connection. |\r\n| `target_database` | No | Alias for `target_db`. |\r\n| `target_bucket` | Yes | Destination Key-Value bucket or namespace prefix receiving the streamed key-value pairs. |\r\n| `bucket` | No | Alias for `target_bucket`. |\r\n| `batch_size` | No | Maximum number of key-value records written per batch chunk. Defaults to `10000`. |\r\n| `var` | No | Name of a pipeline variable that supplies the source SQL query. |\r\n| `variable` | No | Alias for `var`. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the total count of transferred records. |\r\n| `out_var` | No | Alias for `output_var`. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Source SQL query placed between the opening and closing `\u003ckv_bulk\u003e` tags. Required when `var` or `variable` is not set. Must return at least two columns: key and value. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- The enclosed SQL query must project at least two columns: column 1 becomes the key and column 2 becomes the value.\r\n- The total number of transferred records is stored in `LAST_OUTPUT` and in `output_var` when specified.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cassert\u003e` \r\n\r\nThe `\u003cassert\u003e` element checks whether a pipeline variable meets an expected condition and can halt the pipeline, continue with a warning, set a failure variable, or run fallback nodes when the check fails.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the assertion step. |\r\n| `var` | Yes | Name of the pipeline variable evaluated by the assertion. |\r\n| `equals` | Conditional | Expected value for the assertion. Use this or `value` when testing equality. `equals` takes precedence when both are set. |\r\n| `value` | Conditional | Alias for the expected equality value. Used only when `equals` is empty. |\r\n| `operator` | No | Declares a comparison operator. This attribute is accepted by the configuration model; assertion evaluation uses the variable and expected-value condition supported by Flow. |\r\n| `message` | No | Custom message included in the assertion result when the check fails. A default message is generated when omitted. |\r\n| `on_failure` | No | Failure action. `halt` (the default) stops the pipeline; `warn` and `continue` record a warning and allow it to continue. Any unrecognized value also halts the pipeline. |\r\n| `fail_var` | No | Name of a pipeline variable set when the assertion fails. |\r\n| `fail_val` | No | Value assigned to `fail_var` on failure. Defaults to `true` when omitted. |\r\n| `description` | No | Human-readable description of the assertion step. |\r\n| `on_failure` child element | No | Nested pipeline nodes to execute only when the assertion fails, such as cleanup or notification steps. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- `var` is mandatory; use `equals` or `value` to define an equality expectation.\r\n- Failure child nodes execute before the `on_failure` action determines whether the pipeline stops or continues.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cfile_read\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cfile_read\u003e` element loads a local file\u0027s contents into a pipeline variable.\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the file-read step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `file_read_N`. |\r\n| `file` | Conditional | Path of the file to read. This is the preferred path attribute and supports pipeline variable interpolation. |\r\n| `path` | Conditional | Alias for `file`, used only when `file` is empty. |\r\n| `filename` | Conditional | Alias for `file`, used only when both `file` and `path` are empty. |\r\n| `var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the file contents. This output alias has the highest precedence. |\r\n| `variable` | Conditional | Alias for `var`, used when `var` is empty. |\r\n| `output_var` | Conditional | Alias for the output variable, used when `var` and `variable` are empty. |\r\n| `output_variable` | Conditional | Alias for the output variable, used when earlier output aliases are empty. |\r\n| `out_var` | Conditional | Alias for the output variable, used when all earlier output aliases are empty. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Provide exactly one path attribute (`file`, `path`, or `filename`) and one output attribute (`var`, `variable`, `output_var`, `output_variable`, or `out_var`).\r\n- The file contents are stored in the selected output variable and in `LAST_OUTPUT`.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cfile_save\u003e`\r\n\r\nFlow implements file writing with the `\u003cfile_save\u003e` element. It writes content from a pipeline variable or from its inline body to a local file.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the file-write step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `file_save_N`. |\r\n| `file` | Conditional | Destination file path. This is the preferred path attribute and supports pipeline variable interpolation. |\r\n| `path` | Conditional | Alias for `file`, used only when `file` is empty. |\r\n| `filename` | Conditional | Alias for `file`, used only when both `file` and `path` are empty. |\r\n| `var` | No | Name of a pipeline variable that supplies the content to write. When set, its value takes precedence over inline content. |\r\n| `variable` | No | Alias for `var`. |\r\n| `append` | No | Boolean controlling write mode. `true` appends to the existing file; omitted or `false` overwrites it. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline text placed between the opening and closing `\u003cfile_save\u003e` tags. Used when `var` and `variable` are not set. Pipeline variable interpolation is supported. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Provide one path attribute (`file`, `path`, or `filename`) and either a variable source or inline content.\r\n- Parent directories are created automatically before the file is written.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cexcel_write\u003e`\r\n\r\nFlow implements Excel saving with the `\u003cexcel_write\u003e` element. It creates or updates an `.xlsx` workbook and can populate a worksheet with the rows returned by an inline SQL query.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the Excel-save step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `excel_write_N`. |\r\n| `file` | Yes | Destination `.xlsx` file path. Pipeline variable interpolation is supported. An existing workbook is updated; otherwise a new workbook is created. |\r\n| `sheet` | No | Name of the worksheet to create or update. Defaults to `Sheet1`. |\r\n| `db` | Conditional | Name of the configured database connection used to execute the inline query. Required when supplying a query. |\r\n| `var` | No | Accepted by the configuration model for compatibility, but the Excel write runtime currently populates sheets from inline query content. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline SQL query placed between the opening and closing `\u003cexcel_write\u003e` tags. Required with `db` to export query results into the worksheet. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- When both `db` and inline query content are provided, query columns become the first worksheet row and returned rows are written beneath them.\r\n- Parent directories are created automatically before the workbook is saved.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cexcel_read\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cexcel_read\u003e` element reads an `.xlsx` worksheet and serializes its rows as a JSON array of objects.\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the Excel-read step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `excel_read_N`. |\r\n| `file` | Yes | Path of the `.xlsx` workbook to read. Pipeline variable interpolation is supported. |\r\n| `sheet` | No | Worksheet name to read. Defaults to the workbook\u0027s first worksheet. |\r\n| `header` | No | Boolean that controls whether the first row contains column names. Defaults to `true`; the first row becomes the JSON object keys. |\r\n| `output_var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the serialized JSON. This is the preferred output variable name. |\r\n| `var` | Conditional | Alias for `output_var`, used when `output_var` is empty. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Provide `output_var` or `var` to retain the JSON under a named pipeline variable; the value is also stored in `LAST_OUTPUT`.\r\n- With `header=\"false\"`, the current runtime produces an empty JSON array because it serializes rows only when a header row is enabled.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cjson_path\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cjson_path\u003e` element extracts values or nodes from JSON supplied by a file or pipeline variable.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the JSON-path step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `json_path_N`. |\r\n| `file` | Conditional | Path to the source JSON file. Supports pipeline variable interpolation and takes precedence over `var`. |\r\n| `var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable containing source JSON. Used when `file` is not set. |\r\n| `path` | Conditional | JSONPath query expression. Takes precedence over `jsonpath`; required when query content is not supplied inline. |\r\n| `jsonpath` | Conditional | Alias for `path`, used when `path` is empty. |\r\n| `mode` | No | Output format: `value` (default) joins matched values with newlines; `json` returns a single match as JSON or multiple matches as an array; `json_array` always returns matches as a JSON array. |\r\n| `output_var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the extracted value. This is the preferred output variable name. |\r\n| `out_var` | Conditional | Alias for `output_var`, used when `output_var` is empty. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline JSONPath query placed between the opening and closing `\u003cjson_path\u003e` tags. Used when neither `path` nor `jsonpath` is set. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Supply one JSON source (`file` or `var`), one JSONPath expression, and one output attribute (`output_var` or `out_var`).\r\n- Extracted output is stored in the selected output variable and in `LAST_OUTPUT`.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cyaml_path\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003cyaml_path\u003e` element extracts values or nodes from YAML supplied by a file or pipeline variable.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the YAML-path step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `yaml_path_N`. |\r\n| `file` | Conditional | Path to the source YAML file. Supports pipeline variable interpolation and takes precedence over `var`. |\r\n| `var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable containing source YAML. Used when `file` is not set. |\r\n| `path` | Conditional | Path query expression evaluated against the YAML converted to normalized JSON. Takes precedence over `yamlpath`; required when query content is not supplied inline. |\r\n| `yamlpath` | Conditional | Alias for `path`, used when `path` is empty. |\r\n| `mode` | No | Output format: `value` (default) joins matched values with newlines; `json` returns a single match as JSON or multiple matches as an array; `json_array` always returns matches as a JSON array; `yaml` serializes matched nodes as YAML. |\r\n| `output_var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the extracted value. This is the preferred output variable name. |\r\n| `out_var` | Conditional | Alias for `output_var`, used when `output_var` is empty. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline path query placed between the opening and closing `\u003cyaml_path\u003e` tags. Used when neither `path` nor `yamlpath` is set. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Supply one YAML source (`file` or `var`), one path expression, and one output attribute (`output_var` or `out_var`).\r\n- Extracted output is stored in the selected output variable and in `LAST_OUTPUT`.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003cxml_xpath\u003e`\r\n\r\nFlow implements XML-path extraction with the `\u003cxml_xpath\u003e` element. It evaluates an XPath expression against XML supplied by a file or pipeline variable.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the XML-path step. |\r\n| `file` | Conditional | Path to the source XML file. Supports pipeline variable interpolation and takes precedence over `var`. |\r\n| `var` | Conditional | Name of the pipeline variable containing source XML. Used when `file` is not set. |\r\n| `xpath` | Conditional | XPath expression to evaluate. Required when the expression is not supplied as inline content. |\r\n| `mode` | No | Output format: `text` (default) joins each matched node\u0027s inner text with newlines; `xml` joins serialized matched nodes with newlines; `json_array` returns the matched outputs as a JSON array of strings. |\r\n| `output_var` | Yes | Name of the pipeline variable that receives the extracted output. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline XPath expression placed between the opening and closing `\u003cxml_xpath\u003e` tags. Used when `xpath` is not set. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Supply one XML source (`file` or `var`), one XPath expression, and `output_var`.\r\n- Extracted output is stored in `output_var` and in `LAST_OUTPUT`.\r\n\r\n#### `\u003chttp_client\u003e`\r\n\r\nThe `\u003chttp_client\u003e` element sends an HTTP request and can store its response body and status code in pipeline variables.\r\n\r\n| Attribute / field | Mandatory | Description |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `id` | No | Unique identifier for the HTTP-client step. If omitted, Flow generates an identifier in the form `http_N`. |\r\n| `uri` | Conditional | Target request URL. Takes precedence over `url`; one endpoint attribute is required. |\r\n| `url` | Conditional | Alias for `uri`, used when `uri` is empty. |\r\n| `method` | No | HTTP method. Defaults to `GET`. |\r\n| `data` | No | Request payload. Takes precedence over inline content and supports pipeline variable interpolation. |\r\n| `content` | Conditional | Inline request payload placed between the opening and closing `\u003chttp_client\u003e` tags. Used when `data` is empty. |\r\n| `headers` | No | Custom headers as comma-, semicolon-, or newline-separated `Name: Value` pairs. |\r\n| `content_type` | No | Sets the request `Content-Type` header. |\r\n| `var` | No | Response-body variable name. This alias has the highest precedence. |\r\n| `variable` | No | Alias for the response-body variable, used when `var` is empty. |\r\n| `output_var` | No | Alias for the response-body variable, used when `var` and `variable` are empty. |\r\n| `output_variable` | No | Alias for the response-body variable, used when earlier aliases are empty. |\r\n| `out_var` | No | Alias for the response-body variable, used when all earlier aliases are empty. |\r\n| `status_code_var` | No | Variable name that receives the HTTP status code. This alias has the highest precedence. |\r\n| `status_code_variable` | No | Alias for `status_code_var`. |\r\n| `status_var` | No | Alias for `status_code_var`. |\r\n| `status_variable` | No | Alias for `status_code_var`. |\r\n| `timeout` | No | Total request timeout as a Go duration, such as `30s` or `2m`. |\r\n| `follow_redirects` | No | Whether to automatically follow redirect responses. |\r\n| `max_redirects` | No | Maximum number of redirects permitted before the request fails. |\r\n| `cookie_jar` | No | Enables an in-memory cookie jar for the request client. |\r\n| `proxy` | No | HTTP proxy URL. |\r\n| `tls_insecure_skip_verify` | No | Disables TLS certificate verification. Use only for trusted development or test endpoints. |\r\n| `tls_handshake_timeout` | No | Maximum TLS handshake duration. |\r\n| `tls_server_name` | No | Server name used for TLS SNI and certificate verification. |\r\n| `tls_min_version` / `tls_max_version` | No | Minimum or maximum permitted TLS version. |\r\n| `disable_keep_alives` / `disable_compression` | No | Disable connection reuse or automatic compression handling. |\r\n| `max_idle_conns` / `max_idle_conns_per_host` / `max_conns_per_host` | No | Connection-pool limits. |\r\n| `idle_conn_timeout` / `response_header_timeout` / `expect_continue_timeout` | No | Transport timeouts expressed as Go durations. |\r\n| `max_response_header_bytes` | No | Maximum response-header size in bytes. |\r\n| `write_buffer_size` / `read_buffer_size` | No | HTTP transport buffer sizes in bytes. |\r\n| `force_attempt_http2` | No | Forces HTTP/2 negotiation when supported by the transport. |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Endpoint, header, content type, and payload values support pipeline variable interpolation.\r\n- The response body is always stored in `LAST_OUTPUT`; provide a response output alias to store it under a named variable.\r\n\r\n","guides/USING_DESCRIPTIONS.md":"# New Pipeline Description Example\r\n## Example Pipeline Description\r\n\r\nThis section provides an example of how to describe a pipeline, including its purpose, the databases it interacts with, and the scripts it executes. A well-documented pipeline helps in understanding its workflow and facilitates maintenance and collaboration.\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?\u003e\r\n\u003cpipeline description=\"Monthly Sales and Inventory Report Pipeline\" xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\r\n          xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/etl-madness/flow/main/xsd/pipeline.xsd\"\u003e\r\n    \u003cdatabases\u003e\r\n        \u003cdatabase name=\"sales_db\" \r\n                  driver=\"postgres\" \r\n                  connection_string=\"host=localhost port=5432 user=postgres password=secret dbname=sales sslmode=disable\" \r\n                  description=\"Primary Analytics Database\" /\u003e\r\n    \u003c/databases\u003e\r\n\r\n    \u003cflow\u003e\r\n        \u003c!-- Tab 1: Executive Summary --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"export_summary\" \r\n                     file=\"reports/Monthly_Executive_Report.xlsx\" \r\n                     sheet=\"Summary KPI\" \r\n                     db=\"sales_db\"\r\n                     description=\"Writes top-level monthly KPIs to the first tab\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT \r\n                DATE_TRUNC(\u0027month\u0027, order_date) AS month,\r\n                COUNT(order_id) AS total_orders,\r\n                SUM(total_amount) AS total_revenue\r\n            FROM orders\r\n            GROUP BY 1\r\n            ORDER BY 1 DESC;\r\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Tab 2: Customer Breakdown --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"export_customers\" \r\n                     file=\"reports/Monthly_Executive_Report.xlsx\" \r\n                     sheet=\"Customer Details\" \r\n                     db=\"sales_db\"\r\n                     description=\"Appends customer breakdown to a second tab\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT \r\n                customer_id,\r\n                company_name,\r\n                country,\r\n                total_spent\r\n            FROM customer_summary\r\n            WHERE total_spent \u003e 10000;\r\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\r\n\r\n        \u003c!-- Tab 3: Regional Inventory --\u003e\r\n        \u003cexcel_write id=\"export_inventory\" \r\n                     file=\"reports/Monthly_Executive_Report.xlsx\" \r\n                     sheet=\"Inventory Status\" \r\n                     db=\"sales_db\"\r\n                     description=\"Appends inventory levels to a third tab\"\u003e\r\n            SELECT \r\n                warehouse_id,\r\n                product_sku,\r\n                stock_on_hand,\r\n                reorder_level\r\n            FROM warehouse_inventory;\r\n        \u003c/excel_write\u003e\r\n    \u003c/flow\u003e\r\n\u003c/pipeline\u003e\r\n```"};
